:92,"766":93,"767":81,"768":89,"769":86,"770":93,"771":86,"772":87,"773":88,"774":95,"775":90,"776":90,"777":411,"778":88,"779":90,"780":94,"781":93,"782":223,"783":92,"784":93,"785":90,"786":91,"787":85,"788":87,"789":90,"790":85,"791":102,"792":425,"793":94,"794":91,"795":80,"796":93,"797":91,"798":88,"799":93,"800":95,"801":97,"802":90,"803":94,"804":85,"805":90,"806":97,"807":88,"808":89,"809":89,"810":91,"811":96,"812":90,"813":94,"814":86,"815":90,"816":94,"817":91,"818":99,"819":165,"820":81,"821":400,"822":82,"823":79,"824":85,"825":82,"826":85,"827":79,"828":83,"829":76,"830":74,"831":81,"832":83,"833":80,"834":84,"835":87,"836":79,"837":82,"838":79,"839":77,"840":75,"841":85,"842":84,"843":82,"844":89,"845":80,"846":81,"847":75,"848":82,"849":84,"850":79,"851":86,"852":81,"853":73,"854":83,"855":88,"856":82,"857":79,"858":79,"859":402,"860":80,"861":83,"862":81,"863":90,"864":86,"865":70,"866":86,"867":85,"868":87,"869":76,"870":87,"871":87,"872":80,"873":88,"874":81,"875":79,"876":85,"877":84,"878":80,"879":83,"880":79,"881":84,"882":75,"883":93,"884":89,"885":85,"886":89,"887":83,"888":95,"889":408,"890":390,"891":334,"892":483,"893":383,"894":185,"895":524},"keywords":{}},"unstoredValues":{},"unstoredTerms":{}},"docs":{"docs":{"1":{"title":"Capabilities","description":"All 844 capabilities, one page each.","url":"/docs/capabilities","content":"Every capability has its own page with plain limits, the exact platform details and a link to the data file. <Card title=\"Native apps (Android and iPhone)\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/\">361 capabilities, Android and iPhone side by side.</Card> <Card title=\"Websites (7 browsers)\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/\">483 capabilities with support in 7 browsers.</Card>"},"2":{"title":"Linking an app to a website","description":"Proves the app and website are owned together, enabling app links and shared passwords.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/app-associated-domains","content":"<Chips /> Can open web links in the app and offer saved passwords once linked Proves the app and website are owned together, enabling app links and shared passwords. Domain verification file on the website, not a user prompt. Also feeds password autofill. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One check at install, then per link\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing at install. You later tap a web link or a password field Open web links from that company directly in its app Offer your saved website passwords ins"},"3":{"title":"In-car apps (CarPlay and Android Auto)","description":"Shows an app on the car dashboard within strict templates.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/app-carplay","content":"<Chips /> Can show the app on your car screen, but only in a few fixed layouts Shows an app on the car dashboard within strict templates. Cars are safety critical, so both vendors restrict app categories and UI. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While connected to the car\" /> **What the user does:** You plug in or connect your phone to the car and tap the app on the car screen Show a simple screen on the dashboard in an approved layout Play audio, give directions, or show charging and parking info if it i"},"4":{"title":"Home accessories (HomeKit and Matter)","description":"Reads and controls the person's smart home: lights, locks, cameras.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/app-homekit","content":"<Chips /> Can see and control your smart home devices, only after you allow it Reads and controls the person's smart home: lights, locks, cameras. Shows when people are home, which doors are locked and camera feeds; it can also control physical devices. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your whole home, once allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow when the app asks for your home data, or approve a Google Home sheet List your lights, locks, cameras and sensors Turn devices on or off, lock doors, "},"5":{"title":"Purpose strings and review rejection","description":"Every iOS privacy prompt needs a written reason in the app, or the app is rejected or crashes.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/app-purpose-strings","content":"<Chips /> Can't ask for iPhone access without a written reason shown in the prompt Every iOS privacy prompt needs a written reason in the app, or the app is rejected or crashes. The string is the only text the person sees about why. Apple reviewers reject vague ones, and a missing key crashes the app. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One sentence you see before choosing\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. You see the reason when the app first asks for a camera, location or photos Show you a sentence exp"},"6":{"title":"Siri and voice shortcuts","description":"Lets Siri send the app voice requests and read the app's shortcuts.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/app-siri","content":"<Chips /> Can accept your Siri voice requests and show shortcuts, after you allow Siri Lets Siri send the app voice requests and read the app's shortcuts. Siri forwards what was spoken and learned patterns; apps expose actions to the assistant. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only when you ask Siri\" /> **What the user does:** You say a command to Siri, or tap Allow for Siri and Dictation Run an action when you ask Siri by voice Suggest shortcuts to you based on how you use the app Receive the words you "},"7":{"title":"Usage access","description":"Lets the app see which apps are used and for how long.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/apps-usage-stats","content":"<Chips /> Can see which apps you open and for how long, after you switch it on Lets the app see which apps are used and for how long. Shows habits, schedules and which apps are installed. Powers digital wellbeing, parental control and market research. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"All your app use, anytime\" /> **What the user does:** You open Settings and switch on Usage access for the app List which apps you used and how many minutes each Show screen time charts and set limits for parents or wellbein"},"8":{"title":"Probing for specific installed apps","description":"Android lets an app declare apps to see; iOS limits canOpenURL to 50 declared schemes.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/det-app-probing","content":"<Chips /> Can check if a few named apps are installed, but not list them all Android lets an app declare apps to see; iOS limits canOpenURL to 50 declared schemes. Lets an app check for a specific partner app (banking, wallet, rival) without a full list. Both platforms cap the surface. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only the apps it named\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app starts or when it checks for a partner app Check whether a specific bank, wallet or partner app is on the"},"9":{"title":"Full installed-app list (QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES)","description":"Android can list every installed app only with a special permission Play restricts; iOS cannot at all.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/det-installed-apps-all","content":"<Chips /> Can't list every app on iPhone; on Android only with special store approval Android can list every installed app only with a special permission Play restricts; iOS cannot at all. The installed-app list reveals religion, health, finance, dating and politics. Android 11 hides it by default; Play approves QUERY\\_ALL\\_PACKAGES for a short list of uses. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked on iPhone, approval only on Android\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens in the background when "},"10":{"title":"Custom URL schemes and app discovery","description":"A custom scheme (myapp://) opens an app; apps can probe which other apps are installed.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/eco-custom-url-schemes","content":"<Chips /> Can open another app by its link name and guess which apps you have A custom scheme (myapp://) opens an app; apps can probe which other apps are installed. Used for deep links and to guess which apps the person has. Both systems now limit probing. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only the apps it named\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app starts or you tap a link like myapp:// Open another app by its link name Check up to 50 named apps on iPhone Guess which apps you use "},"11":{"title":"Universal Links and Android App Links","description":"Verified web links that open the app directly, proven by a file on the website.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/eco-universal-app-links","content":"<Chips /> Can open the app directly from a website link, with the full link and campaign tags Verified web links that open the app directly, proven by a file on the website. Lets a site and its app share links and attribution params. The click lands in the app with the URL, including any campaign tags. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One link at a time\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a web link from that company in a message, email or browser Open the app on the exact screen the link points to Recei"},"12":{"title":"Other apps on the phone","description":"Seeing and talking to other installed apps. 29 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem","content":"Seeing and talking to other installed apps. 29 capabilities in this group. | Capability                    | Kind                 | In one line                                                                   |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Account deletion requirements | Works without asking | Can't keep your account forever, since apps must let you delete it in the app | | Capability                 | Kind                 | In one line                                                   |\n| -------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| App Clips and Instant Apps | Works without asking | Can run a tiny part of an app from a link or tag, iPhone only | | Capability                            | Kind                 | In one line                                                         |\n| ------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| App Review 5.1.1 and 5.1.2 data rules | Works without asking | Can't collect data or track you in ways that break App Store review | | Capability                         | Kind                 | In one line                                                       |\n| ---------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| App Store privacy nutrition labels | Works without asking | Can tell you what the app says it collects, on its App Store page | | Capability                           | Kind                 | In one line                                                         |\n| ------------------------------------ | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Custom URL schemes and app discovery | Works without asking | Can open another app by its link name and guess which apps you have | | Capability                                     | Kind          | In one line                                                                 |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Full installed-app list (QUERY\\_ALL\\_PACKAGES) | Gets silently | Can't list every app on iPhone; on Android only with special store approval | | Capability                  | Kind                 | In one line                                                                 |\n| --------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Google Play Age Signals API | Works without asking | Can learn if you are a verified adult, or your age range, not your birthday | | Capability                      | Kind                 | In one line                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Google Play Data safety section | Works without asking | Can tell you what the app says it collects and shares, on its Play Store page | | Capability                            | Kind           | In one line                                                          |\n| ------------------------------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Home accessories (HomeKit and Matter) | Asks you first | Can see and control your smart home devices, only after you allow it | | Capability          | Kind                 | In one line                                                                        |\n| ------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Home screen widgets | Works without asking | Can show live info on your home screen, but only refresh a limited number of times | | Capability                             | Kind                 | In one line                                                          |\n| -------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| In-car apps (CarPlay and Android Auto) | Works without asking | Can show the app on your car screen, but only in a few fixed layouts | | Capability                  | Kind                 | In one line                                                         |\n| --------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Linking an app to a website | Works without asking | Can open web links in the app and offer saved passwords once linked | | Capability       | Kind             | In one line                                              |\n| ---------------- | ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Other apps' data | Blocked for apps | Can't open another app's files, messages or saved logins | | Capability              | Kind             | In one line                                     |\n| ----------------------- | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| Other running processes | Blocked for apps | Can't list the other apps running on your phone | | Capability                           | Kind                 | In one line                                                     |\n| ------------------------------------ | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Play AccessibilityService API policy | Works without asking | Can see and control your screen once you turn it on in Settings | | Capability                      | Kind                 | In one line                                                                        |\n| ----------------"},"13":{"title":"Other apps' data","description":"Each app's files and memory are sealed from other apps on both platforms.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/nx-other-app-data","content":"<Chips /> Can't open another app's files, messages or saved logins Each app's files and memory are sealed from other apps on both platforms. Sandboxing keeps one app from reading another's messages, tokens or files. Only user-picked files, shared groups or content providers cross the line. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It is blocked by the phone itself See a file you pick through the file picker and share to it Share data through a group or shared area that"},"14":{"title":"Other running processes","description":"Blocked: apps cannot list what else is running.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/nx-processes","content":"<Chips /> Can't list the other apps running on your phone Blocked: apps cannot list what else is running. The foreground app list reveals habits. Android narrowed process lists from 5.0; a Usage Access special permission is the only broad proxy. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked, apart from Usage access on Android\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It is blocked by the phone itself See how long each app was in front, if you switched on Usage access on Android See its own running tasks List other"},"15":{"title":"App Clips and Instant Apps","description":"App Clips run a small part of an app without installing; Google shut Instant Apps down in December 2025.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/os-app-clips","content":"<Chips /> Can run a tiny part of an app from a link or tag, iPhone only App Clips run a small part of an app without installing; Google shut Instant Apps down in December 2025. A lightweight launch from a link, QR or NFC tag, before any install. App Clips are limited to 15 MB (50 MB for digital only launches) and lose most background and sensor access. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only while you use the clip\" /> **What the user does:** You scan a QR code, tap an NFC tag, or tap a link or banner Run a"},"16":{"title":"Home screen widgets","description":"Small live panels on the home screen that the person adds themselves; refresh rates are capped on both systems.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/os-widgets","content":"<Chips /> Can show live info on your home screen, but only refresh a limited number of times Small live panels on the home screen that the person adds themselves; refresh rates are capped on both systems. Widgets keep an app visible without opening it. Android refreshes on a timer (30 min minimum) or by push; iOS gives each widget a daily budget of about 40 to 70 refreshes. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Small, spaced out, after you add it\" /> **What the user does:** You add the widget yourself from th"},"17":{"title":"Play AccessibilityService API policy","description":"Accessibility services see and control the screen, so Play limits them to real accessibility use.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-accessibility-api","content":"<Chips /> Can see and control your screen once you turn it on in Settings Accessibility services see and control the screen, so Play limits them to real accessibility use. A powerful hook used by malware for fraud. Play requires a declaration and bars use for call recording, ads or remote control unless the app is an accessibility tool. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Everything on screen, anytime\" /> **What the user does:** You go to Settings, then Accessibility, switch the app on, and accept a warning"},"18":{"title":"Account deletion requirements","description":"Both stores require an in-app way to delete an account and its data if the app lets you create one.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-account-deletion","content":"<Chips /> Can't keep your account forever, since apps must let you delete it in the app Both stores require an in-app way to delete an account and its data if the app lets you create one. Apple required in-app deletion from 30 June 2022 (plus Sign in with Apple token revocation). Google required a deletion path in the app and a web link from 8 December 2023, with Data safety disclosure, and enforcement from 2024. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One account at a time\" /> **What the user does:** You open "},"19":{"title":"App Review 5.1.1 and 5.1.2 data rules","description":"Apple's review rules: collect only needed data, say why, ask consent, and do not track without ATT.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-app-review-5-1","content":"<Chips /> Can't collect data or track you in ways that break App Store review Apple's review rules: collect only needed data, say why, ask consent, and do not track without ATT. 5.1.1 covers privacy policy, consent, data minimization, permission purpose strings and account deletion. 5.1.2 covers data use and sharing, ATT and the ban on building profiles or fingerprinting. Reviews are the real enforcement. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Rules, not data\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing for you to do. "},"20":{"title":"Play background location policy","description":"Apps that read location while closed must justify it to Google with a video and form.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-background-location","content":"<Chips /> Can follow your location while closed, only if Google approves and you pick Always Apps that read location while closed must justify it to Google with a video and form. Background location is the most sensitive mobile signal. Play requires a feature that needs it, a prominent disclosure, and approval. iOS shows 'Always Allow' reminders instead. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Background location, anytime\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Always allow, or change it in Settings after a first W"},"21":{"title":"Play READ_CONTACTS declaration","description":"Google adds a Contacts permission declaration so apps use the Contact Picker when possible.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-contacts-declaration","content":"<Chips /> Can only access all your contacts on Android if Google approves the reason Google adds a Contacts permission declaration so apps use the Contact Picker when possible. Contact lists are valuable for growth hacks and ads. Policy effective 28 October 2026 asks apps to justify broad access, mirroring photo and video rules. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Contacts you pick, or all if approved\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Pick contacts, or Allow contacts when the app asks Show a contact picke"},"22":{"title":"Play Families policy and Apple Kids category","description":"Child directed apps get stricter rules: no ads tracking, no personal data to third parties, and certified SDKs.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-families-kids","content":"<Chips /> Can't track children or share their data with ad companies in kids apps Child directed apps get stricter rules: no ads tracking, no personal data to third parties, and certified SDKs. Apple's Kids category bans behavioral ads and limits third-party analytics. Play Families requires certified ad SDKs, no AAID use, and a safe design. Both align with COPPA and similar laws. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Rules, not data\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The rules apply when a child uses a kid"},"23":{"title":"Play photo and video permissions policy","description":"Apps may not request broad photo or video access unless it is their core purpose; the Photo Picker is the default.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-photo-video","content":"<Chips /> Can open only the photos you pick, unless Google approves broad photo access Apps may not request broad photo or video access unless it is their core purpose; the Photo Picker is the default. Google's 2024 policy forces most apps to the Photo Picker. Broad READ\\_MEDIA\\_IMAGES or READ\\_MEDIA\\_VIDEO needs a declaration and approval. iOS offers a limited library and PHPicker. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only what you pick, unless approved\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Add photo and choo"},"24":{"title":"Google Play Age Signals API","description":"A Play API that tells an app whether a user is verified as an adult or in an age band, to help meet state laws.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-play-age-signals","content":"<Chips /> Can learn if you are a verified adult, or your age range, not your birthday A Play API that tells an app whether a user is verified as an adult or in an age band, to help meet state laws. Beta from late 2025. Google supplies age verification status for users in places with age laws (Texas SB2420, Utah). It returns a status and an age range, not a birth date. Law dates were contested in court. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"An age band, on Android only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The "},"25":{"title":"Google Play Data safety section","description":"A Play Store listing section where developers declare data collected, shared, and security practices.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-play-data-safety","content":"<Chips /> Can tell you what the app says it collects and shares, on its Play Store page A Play Store listing section where developers declare data collected, shared, and security practices. Required for all apps since 20 July 2022. Developers must count SDK collection too. Google may reject or remove apps whose behavior does not match. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A label on the store page\" /> **What the user does:** You open the app page in Google Play and scroll to Data safety List what data types "},"26":{"title":"App Store privacy nutrition labels","description":"A label on each App Store page that lists what data the app collects and whether it is linked to you or used to track you.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-privacy-labels","content":"<Chips /> Can tell you what the app says it collects, on its App Store page A label on each App Store page that lists what data the app collects and whether it is linked to you or used to track you. Declared by the developer in App Store Connect with no technical check, so accuracy depends on honesty. Labels went live 14 December 2020. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A label, filled in by the developer\" /> **What the user does:** You open the app page in the App Store and scroll to App Privacy List data"},"27":{"title":"Privacy manifests (PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy)","description":"A file in each app and listed SDK that states what data it collects and why, checked by Apple at upload.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-privacy-manifests","content":"<Chips /> Can list what the app and its add on tools collect, in a file Apple checks A file in each app and listed SDK that states what data it collects and why, checked by Apple at upload. Apple turned self-declared privacy into machine-readable files. Xcode merges them into a privacy report, and tracking domains get blocked for ATT-denied users. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A file checked at upload\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It is checked when the developer uploads the app, and shown in a"},"28":{"title":"Play QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES policy","description":"Apps that want to list every installed app must justify it to Google.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-query-all-packages","content":"<Chips /> Can see every installed app on Android only if Google approves the reason Apps that want to list every installed app must justify it to Google. Since Android 11 apps see only a filtered set of other apps. QUERY\\_ALL\\_PACKAGES gives a full list, so Play requires a declared core purpose such as security, device management or file managers. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"All your apps, if approved\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app asks for the full list in the backgrou"},"29":{"title":"Required reason APIs","description":"Apple lists APIs that can be used to fingerprint a device and requires the app to state an approved reason.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-required-reason-apis","content":"<Chips /> Can't use fingerprint prone phone details on iPhone without an approved reason Apple lists APIs that can be used to fingerprint a device and requires the app to state an approved reason. Five categories: file timestamp, system boot time, disk space, active keyboards, and UserDefaults. An unapproved reason is a rejection, so fingerprinting uses are fenced in. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Rules, not data\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Apple checks the reasons when the developer submits "},"30":{"title":"SDK signatures and the Play SDK Index","description":"Apple requires signed copies of popular SDKs, and Google publishes data practices for listed SDKs.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-sdk-signatures","content":"<Chips /> Can check that popular add on tools were not changed, and show what they collect Apple requires signed copies of popular SDKs, and Google publishes data practices for listed SDKs. Cuts supply chain tampering and puts SDK data use on public record. Apple's list includes common ad and analytics SDKs. Google's SDK Index shows permissions and Data safety info per SDK. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A check at build time\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It is checked when the app is built and "},"31":{"title":"Play SMS and Call Log permission policy","description":"Apps can read SMS or call logs only if they are the default handler or qualify for a narrow exception.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Other apps on the phone"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/policy-sms-call-log","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your texts or call history unless it is your default texting or phone app Apps can read SMS or call logs only if they are the default handler or qualify for a narrow exception. Since 9 March 2019 Google limits these permissions to default SMS, Phone or Assistant apps and a few declared exceptions. It stops mass harvesting for contact lists and ads. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked, unless default app\" /> **What the user does:** You set the app as your default SMS or Phone app,"},"32":{"title":"Google advertising ID (AAID/GAID)","description":"A resettable Google ID apps use for ads, which the person can delete, giving apps zeros.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-aaid","content":"<Chips /> Can use a resettable Google ad ID to link your activity across apps A resettable Google ID apps use for ads, which the person can delete, giving apps zeros. The cross-app join key on Android: install attribution, retargeting, frequency capping. Users can reset or delete it; kids apps must not send it. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Follows you across apps, anytime\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app or its ad tool starts Join your activity across apps that use the sam"},"33":{"title":"AdAttributionKit re-engagement conversions","description":"Measures whether an ad brought an existing user back into the app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-aak-reengagement","content":"<Chips /> Can count whether an ad brought you back to an app you already have, on iPhone Measures whether an ad brought an existing user back into the app. SKAN could only count new installs. Re-engagement attribution lets advertisers measure retargeting without an IDFA. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A delayed count, no ID\" /> **What the user does:** You tap an ad, then open the app you already installed Tell the advertiser an ad led you to reopen the app Count it without an ad ID Work with overlappin"},"34":{"title":"AdAttributionKit (iOS 17.4)","description":"Apple's successor to SKAdNetwork that also covers re-engagement and alternative app stores.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-adattributionkit","content":"<Chips /> Can tell an ad network an ad led to an install, on iPhone, with delays Apple's successor to SKAdNetwork that also covers re-engagement and alternative app stores. A privacy-preserving way for ad networks to measure installs, reopens and re-downloads. Compatible with SKAN postbacks, adds marketplaces beyond the App Store. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A delayed, coarse count\" /> **What the user does:** You tap an ad, then install or reopen the app Tell the ad network an ad led to an install, "},"35":{"title":"In-app consent platforms (TCF/CMP)","description":"A consent screen the developer shows (in addition to the OS prompt) for EEA, UK and some US laws.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-consent-cmp","content":"<Chips /> Can show an extra consent screen on top of the phone prompt A consent screen the developer shows (in addition to the OS prompt) for EEA, UK and some US laws. Recorded consent decides what ad SDKs may do. Since 2024 Google requires a certified CMP for ads in the EEA. On iOS it adds a second prompt beside ATT, which Italy called double consent. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your choice, passed to the app\" /> **What the user does:** You see a Consent screen the first time you open the app, ofte"},"36":{"title":"Meta (Facebook) SDK and app events","description":"Meta's SDK logs installs and in-app events and can send hashed email or phone to match people.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-facebook-sdk","content":"<Chips /> Can send your app activity to Meta and match it to your account Meta's SDK logs installs and in-app events and can send hashed email or phone to match people. Ties activity to a Meta account for ads. Can send hashed email or phone (advanced matching). Logs events even for signed-out Facebook users. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Ongoing, tied to a Meta account\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app opens or you do something in it Send installs and in app actions, such as"},"37":{"title":"Fingerprinting ban and ATT enforcement","description":"Apple forbids deriving a device identity from signals to track someone who said no under ATT.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-fingerprinting-ban","content":"<Chips /> Can't rebuild your identity from phone details after you refuse tracking on iPhone Apple forbids deriving a device identity from signals to track someone who said no under ATT. Stops apps and SDKs from rebuilding an ID from IP, model, locale and similar signals after the person refused tracking. It is enforced mainly at App Review and by required reason API gating. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked by rule on iPhone\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. You said no on the tracking prompt;"},"38":{"title":"Google Analytics for Firebase","description":"Google's free analytics SDK that gives every install an ID and logs automatic events.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-firebase-analytics","content":"<Chips /> Can give each install an ID and log your taps and screens for Google Google's free analytics SDK that gives every install an ID and logs automatic events. Builds an app-level event stream and links it to Google Ads and BigQuery. Also provides the identifiers Google ad products use. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, tied to the install\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It starts when the app opens and runs while you use it Count sessions, screens and purchases for the developer Give e"},"39":{"title":"Google Ads app campaign measurement","description":"Google Play services and the Firebase SDK report installs and in-app events straight to Google Ads.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-google-ads-app-measurement","content":"<Chips /> Can tell Google Ads about your installs and in app actions Google Play services and the Firebase SDK report installs and in-app events straight to Google Ads. Google already knows the click, the install and the account. The SDK adds in-app events, closing the loop with no third-party MMP. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, linked to Google Ads\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a Google ad, install the app, then use it Tell Google an ad led to an install, using the Play install referrer"},"40":{"title":"IDFA (Identifier for Advertisers)","description":"A resettable device ad ID that apps can read only if the person allowed tracking.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-idfa","content":"<Chips /> Can use your iPhone ad ID only if you tap Allow on the tracking prompt A resettable device ad ID that apps can read only if the person allowed tracking. A stable cross-app key that joins a person's activity across apps for targeting, frequency caps and install attribution. Without a yes it is all zeros, so apps must fall back to aggregate methods. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Follows you across apps, if allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow or Ask App Not to Track on the trackin"},"41":{"title":"IDFV and App Set ID (developer-scoped IDs)","description":"A per-developer app ID that needs no prompt but cannot cross to other companies' apps.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-idfv","content":"<Chips /> Can recognise your phone across one company's apps, but not other companies' apps A per-developer app ID that needs no prompt but cannot cross to other companies' apps. Lets one developer recognize the same device across its own apps for analytics, fraud and fair-use limits. Not allowed for ad targeting on Android and not usable for third-party tracking on iOS. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Across one company's apps\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app starts Tell tha"},"42":{"title":"Adjust SDK","description":"A third-party SDK for install attribution, SKAN management, deep links and fraud filtering.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-mmp-adjust","content":"<Chips /> Can link your install to its ad and watch what you do in the app A third-party SDK for install attribution, SKAN management, deep links and fraud filtering. Collects device IDs, IP, UA, events; supports probabilistic matching on Android and for consenting iOS users. Part of AppLovin. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Ongoing, across ad networks\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It starts when the app opens, after you tapped an ad and installed Match an install to an ad through the Play instal"},"43":{"title":"AppsFlyer SDK","description":"A third-party SDK that attributes installs, runs deep links, and sends events to ad networks.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-mmp-appsflyer","content":"<Chips /> Can link your install to an ad and watch what you do in the app A third-party SDK that attributes installs, runs deep links, and sends events to ad networks. Sees device IDs, IP, user agent, in-app events for every customer app, and uses fraud and cohort tools. OneLink handles deferred deep links. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Ongoing, across its customers' apps\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It starts when the app opens, after you tapped an ad and installed Match an install to an ad u"},"44":{"title":"Branch SDK","description":"A SDK known for deferred deep links that also does attribution and clipboard-based matching.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-mmp-branch","content":"<Chips /> Can open the right screen after install and link the install to an ad A SDK known for deferred deep links that also does attribution and clipboard-based matching. Creates links that open the right in-app screen after install. Uses IDs, IP, device signals; on iOS it reads clipboard if the link was copied. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"A link and an install, then ongoing\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a Branch link, install the app, then open it Open the right screen after install from a "},"45":{"title":"Singular SDK","description":"A SDK that attributes installs and also merges ad spend and SKAN data into one ROI view.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-mmp-singular","content":"<Chips /> Can link your install to an ad and show spending and results in one place A SDK that attributes installs and also merges ad spend and SKAN data into one ROI view. Receives events, IDs and cost data; handles SKAN configuration and Android Install Referrer. Competes with AppsFlyer and Adjust. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Ongoing, across ad networks\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It starts when the app opens, after you tapped an ad and installed Match an install to an ad using the instal"},"46":{"title":"Private Click Measurement (Safari to app)","description":"Safari can report that an ad click on a web page led to an app install or purchase, without IDs.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-pcm","content":"<Chips /> Can tell a site an ad click led to an install, without a device ID Safari can report that an ad click on a web page led to an app install or purchase, without IDs. Lets a publisher site and an app measure click-to-install conversions through Apple's own delays and limited data bits. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A delayed, anonymous count\" /> **What the user does:** You tap an ad in Safari, then install or buy in the app Report that a click led to an install or purchase Send a few small bits"},"47":{"title":"Attribution Reporting API on Android","description":"A built-in way for ad tech to count conversions with noise and limits, without ad IDs.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-privacy-sandbox-attribution-reporting","content":"<Chips /> Can't count on this Android ad counting tool, since Google announced it is retiring it A built-in way for ad tech to count conversions with noise and limits, without ad IDs. Event-level and summary reports with caps and delays; Google announced in Oct 2025 it would retire it while standards work continues at W3C. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Being retired\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It was a background feature, and Google announced its retirement on 17 Oct 2025 Have counted ad resu"},"48":{"title":"Protected Audience on Android (FLEDGE)","description":"On-device ad auctions for remarketing, without sending a person's history to ad servers.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-privacy-sandbox-protected-audience","content":"<Chips /> Can't count on Android ad auctions on your phone, since Google is retiring them On-device ad auctions for remarketing, without sending a person's history to ad servers. Would have replaced retargeting with local interest groups and auctions. Retired together with Topics. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Being retired\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It was a background feature, and Google announced its retirement on 17 Oct 2025 Have kept ad interest groups on the phone (Android 13 and later"},"49":{"title":"Third-party SDK data sharing","description":"Any SDK in an app can read what the app can read and send it to its own servers.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-sdk-data-sharing","content":"<Chips /> Can share anything the app can see with its add on tools, who send it on Any SDK in an app can read what the app can read and send it to its own servers. Third-party code inherits all of the app's permissions, so one SDK can collect location or IDs for a data broker. Stores respond with manifests, disclosure rules and bans. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Everything the app can access, anytime\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens whenever the app runs Collect location, IDs and other"},"50":{"title":"SDK Runtime (privacy sandbox)","description":"Would have run ad SDKs in their own process so they could not read the host app's data.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-sdk-runtime","content":"<Chips /> Can't run ad tools in a separate box on Android, since Google retired it Would have run ad SDKs in their own process so they could not read the host app's data. Isolation of third-party SDKs from host app memory and identifiers. Never reached general availability; retired in the Oct 2025 announcement. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Retired\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It was a background feature that never reached wide release Have kept ad tools away from the app's data (Android 14, n"},"51":{"title":"Server-side conversions APIs","description":"The advertiser's server sends purchase or signup events to ad platforms, with hashed email or phone.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-server-side-conversions","content":"<Chips /> Can send your purchases and signups from its server to ad platforms The advertiser's server sends purchase or signup events to ad platforms, with hashed email or phone. Bypasses on-device tracking limits: the server sends identifiers and click IDs directly, so the platform matches events to ad clicks. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Ongoing, from the app's server\" /> **What the user does:** You buy, sign up, or tap an ad, then the app's server reports it Send events to ad platforms with a scra"},"52":{"title":"SKAdNetwork 4 (postbacks)","description":"Apple sends the ad network a signed, delayed, anonymized report that an ad led to an install.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-skadnetwork","content":"<Chips /> Can tell an ad network an ad led to an install, in a delayed report Apple sends the ad network a signed, delayed, anonymized report that an ad led to an install. Gives ad networks proof an ad drove an install without a device ID. Reports are coarse, delayed and may be dropped for small campaigns. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A delayed, anonymous count\" /> **What the user does:** You tap an ad, install the app, then use it for a few days Send a signed report that an ad led to an install Send"},"53":{"title":"SKAN conversion values (fine and coarse)","description":"A 6-bit number the app sets after install so the ad network learns how valuable the user was.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-skan-conversion-values","content":"<Chips /> Can send the ad network a small score about what you did after install A 6-bit number the app sets after install so the ad network learns how valuable the user was. Encodes early behavior (purchase, level) into at most 64 values, which is the only per-campaign performance signal left for non-consenting iOS users. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One small score per install\" /> **What the user does:** You use the app, for example buy something or reach a level Set a number from 0 to 63 that sums"},"54":{"title":"SKAN crowd anonymity tiers","description":"Apple withholds detail from postbacks when too few installs came from a campaign.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-skan-crowd-anonymity","content":"<Chips /> Can't get much detail when too few people installed from an ad Apple withholds detail from postbacks when too few installs came from a campaign. Protects individuals in small groups: low install counts get less detail. Small advertisers lose signal first. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Detail depends on campaign size\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Apple applies the rules by itself Send full detail for large campaigns Send a 3 or 4 digit campaign ID in higher tiers Send a detailed score "},"55":{"title":"SKAN postback windows and timers","description":"Postbacks arrive days after install, in three time windows with random delays.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-skan-timers","content":"<Chips /> Can only get install reports days later, in three windows with random delays Postbacks arrive days after install, in three time windows with random delays. Delays break the link between a user's behavior and the postback, so an ad network cannot tell which person it was. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Delayed, in three windows\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Apple sends the reports after you install and use the app Send the first report after days 0 to 2, plus a random delay of 24 to 48 "},"56":{"title":"Topics API on Android","description":"Android would have told ad SDKs a few interest topics from the person's recent app use.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-topics-android","content":"<Chips /> Can't tell ad tools your interest topics on Android, since Google retired it Android would have told ad SDKs a few interest topics from the person's recent app use. Coarse interest labels (about 470 topics) for ad targeting without an ID. Retired in Oct 2025 after low adoption. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Retired\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It was a background feature, retired in October 2025 Have told ad tools a few topics from about 470 labels (Android 13 and later) Have worked "},"57":{"title":"Uninstall tracking via push","description":"Apps or MMPs detect an uninstall by noticing a push token has stopped working.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/ads-uninstall-tracking","content":"<Chips /> Can tell when you delete the app by noticing that its push messages fail Apps or MMPs detect an uninstall by noticing a push token has stopped working. A silent push fails after uninstall, so the vendor learns when someone dropped the app. Used for retention and retargeting audiences. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"After you delete the app\" /> **What the user does:** You delete the app. The company learns within days Notice a push message fails after you delete the app Use that signal to send"},"58":{"title":"Install referrer","description":"The Play Store hands the app the campaign link that led to its install, readable for 90 days.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/attr-install-referrer","content":"<Chips /> Can learn which ad or link led to your install, for 90 days after The Play Store hands the app the campaign link that led to its install, readable for 90 days. Tells an app which ad or link produced the install, with click and install timestamps. No permission is needed, so it is the standard Android attribution signal. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One signal, for 90 days\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you first open the app after installing from Google Play Find the c"},"59":{"title":"SKAdNetwork and AdAttributionKit","description":"Apple's privacy preserving attribution: ad networks learn campaign results from a delayed, noisy postback.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/attr-skadnetwork","content":"<Chips /> Can tell an ad network an ad led to an install, in a delayed report Apple's privacy preserving attribution: ad networks learn campaign results from a delayed, noisy postback. Replaces device level tracking with coarse conversion values after a 0 to 2, 3 to 7 or 8 to 35 day window. AdAttributionKit (iOS 17.4) extends it to alternative stores and re-engagement. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A delayed, anonymous count\" /> **What the user does:** You tap an ad, install the app, then use it for a"},"60":{"title":"App Tracking Transparency","description":"A system prompt that asks whether the app may track the person across other companies' apps and sites.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/attrib-att","content":"<Chips /> Can track you across other companies' apps and sites only if you tap Allow A system prompt that asks whether the app may track the person across other companies' apps and sites. Gates the IDFA and cross-company tracking; a No returns an all zero IDFA. Rules also forbid workarounds. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Across apps, only if you allow it\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow or Ask App Not to Track on the system prompt Use the iPhone ad ID once you tap Allow Link your activity acr"},"61":{"title":"AD_ID permission","description":"Android 13 apps must declare AD_ID in the manifest to read the advertising ID.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/id-ad-id-permission","content":"<Chips /> Can use the Android ad ID only if the app lists the AD\\_ID permission Android 13 apps must declare AD\\_ID in the manifest to read the advertising ID. If missing, the ID reads as zeros. Cuts ad tracking in apps that did not declare it. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, if listed by the app\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It is switched on automatically when the app installs Use the Google advertising ID if the app lists AD\\_ID Get it with no prompt, since it is auto granted Give zer"},"62":{"title":"Advertising ID (IDFA and AAID)","description":"A resettable ad ID: Android gives it by default, iOS only after the person taps Allow in the ATT prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/id-advertising-id","content":"<Chips /> Can use a resettable ad ID, by default on Android and only after Allow on iPhone A resettable ad ID: Android gives it by default, iOS only after the person taps Allow in the ATT prompt. The cross-app profile key for ad targeting. ATT (iOS 14.5) cut iOS opt-in to roughly 25 percent in industry surveys; Android lets users delete it in Settings. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Follows you across apps, anytime\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing on Android. On iPhone you tap Allow on the tracking "},"63":{"title":"App Set ID","description":"A resettable per-developer ID on Android, not for ads.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/id-app-set-id","content":"<Chips /> Can recognise your Android phone across one developer's apps, not for ads A resettable per-developer ID on Android, not for ads. Links installs of one developer's apps for analytics and fraud with no prompt and no ad use. Resets after 13 months idle. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Across one developer's apps\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app starts Link installs from the same developer for analytics and fraud checks Keep the same ID across reinstalls while another a"},"64":{"title":"Ads and install tracking","description":"Measuring which ad led to an install or sale. 37 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads","content":"Measuring which ad led to an install or sale. 37 capabilities in this group. | Capability        | Kind           | In one line                                                           |\n| ----------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| AD\\_ID permission | Asks you first | Can use the Android ad ID only if the app lists the AD\\_ID permission | | Capability                  | Kind              | In one line                                                            |\n| --------------------------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| AdAttributionKit (iOS 17.4) | Used to track you | Can tell an ad network an ad led to an install, on iPhone, with delays | | Capability                                 | Kind              | In one line                                                                    |\n| ------------------------------------------ | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| AdAttributionKit re-engagement conversions | Used to track you | Can count whether an ad brought you back to an app you already have, on iPhone | | Capability | Kind              | In one line                                                      |\n| ---------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Adjust SDK | Used to track you | Can link your install to its ad and watch what you do in the app | | Capability                     | Kind              | In one line                                                                      |\n| ------------------------------ | ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Advertising ID (IDFA and AAID) | Used to track you | Can use a resettable ad ID, by default on Android and only after Allow on iPhone | | Capability | Kind              | In one line                                                               |\n| ---------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| App Set ID | Used to track you | Can recognise your Android phone across one developer's apps, not for ads | | Capability                | Kind              | In one line                                                                |\n| ------------------------- | ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| App Tracking Transparency | Used to track you | Can track you across other companies' apps and sites only if you tap Allow | | Capability    | Kind              | In one line                                                     |\n| ------------- | ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| AppsFlyer SDK | Used to track you | Can link your install to an ad and watch what you do in the app | | Capability                              | Kind                 | In one line                                                                  |\n| --------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| ATT regulatory actions and alternatives | Works without asking | Can show a different tracking prompt in the EU after Apple's regulator deals | | Capability                           | Kind              | In one line                                                                            |\n| ------------------------------------ | ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Attribution Reporting API on Android | Used to track you | Can't count on this Android ad counting tool, since Google announced it is retiring it | | Capability | Kind              | In one line                                                           |\n| ---------- | ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Branch SDK | Used to track you | Can open the right screen after install and link the install to an ad | | Capability                          | Kind              | In one line                                                                    |\n| ----------------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Data sharing with Microsoft for ads | Used to track you | Can use more of your LinkedIn data for Microsoft ads, unless you switch it off | | Capability                             | Kind             | In one line                                                                        |\n| -------------------------------------- | ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Fingerprinting ban and ATT enforcement | Blocked for apps | Can't rebuild your identity from phone details after you refuse tracking on iPhone | | Capability                          | Kind              | In one line                                                |\n| ----------------------------------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Google Ads app campaign measurement | Used to track you | Can tell Google Ads about your installs and in app actions | | Capability                        | Kind              | In one line                                                         |\n| --------------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Google advertising ID (AAID/GAID) | Used to track you | Can use a resettable Google ad ID to link your activity across apps | | Capability                    | Kind              | In one line                                                          |\n| ----------------------------- | ----------------- | --------------------------"},"65":{"title":"Data sharing with Microsoft for ads","description":"LinkedIn shares more member data with Microsoft to personalize ads.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/linkedin-microsoft-ad-sharing","content":"<Chips /> Can use more of your LinkedIn data for Microsoft ads, unless you switch it off LinkedIn shares more member data with Microsoft to personalize ads. Profile data, feed activity and ad engagement can feed Microsoft advertising. Applies per account across web and mobile. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your whole account, ongoing\" /> **What the user does:** You open Ad settings in the app or on the web and change the toggle Use your profile, feed activity and ad clicks to personalize Microsoft ads"},"66":{"title":"Store privacy labels","description":"What LinkedIn tells Apple and Google it collects.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/linkedin-store-privacy-labels","content":"<Chips /> Can tell you what LinkedIn says it collects, on its store pages What LinkedIn tells Apple and Google it collects. Self-reported lists, not audits. Differences between the two labels show how much each store lets a developer say. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A label on the store page\" /> **What the user does:** You open the app page in the App Store or Google Play Show a list of data LinkedIn says it collects Show whether data is linked to you (iPhone) or shared with third parties (Android) "},"67":{"title":"Identifiers across reinstall (IDFV, App Set ID)","description":"Vendor scoped ids stay stable across reinstalls until every app from that developer is removed.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/os-reinstall-identifiers","content":"<Chips /> Can recognise your phone after you reinstall, across one developer's apps Vendor scoped ids stay stable across reinstalls until every app from that developer is removed. IDFV and App Set ID let one developer recognise a device across its own apps. They reset when the last app from that vendor is deleted, and App Set ID also after long disuse. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Across one developer's apps\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app starts Keep the same ID after yo"},"68":{"title":"ATT regulatory actions and alternatives","description":"Competition regulators are fining Apple over ATT, and Apple has agreed to change how it works in the EU.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/policy-att-enforcement","content":"<Chips /> Can show a different tracking prompt in the EU after Apple's regulator deals Competition regulators are fining Apple over ATT, and Apple has agreed to change how it works in the EU. France fined Apple 150M euros (March 2025), Italy 98.6M euros (December 2025). Germany's settlement (September 2026) points to an alternative EU prompt from iOS 27.2. Regulators say ATT favors Apple's own ads. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A prompt that may change\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow or Ask "},"69":{"title":"Required Reason APIs and privacy manifest","description":"Apple requires apps to declare why they use 5 fingerprint-prone API groups, in a privacy manifest file.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Ads and install tracking"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/rr-regime","content":"<Chips /> Can only use five fingerprint prone phone details with an approved reason on iPhone Apple requires apps to declare why they use 5 fingerprint-prone API groups, in a privacy manifest file. Apps must list approved reasons per group in PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy or App Store Connect rejects the upload. Reasons limit use; fingerprinting is banned even with consent. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Rules, not data\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Apple checks the reasons when the developer uploads th"},"70":{"title":"Camera","description":"Asks to use the camera for photos, video, scanning or video calls.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/camera-mic-screen/camera-camera","content":"<Chips /> Can use your camera while the app is open, after you tap Allow Asks to use the camera for photos, video, scanning or video calls. Sees the room, the face and documents in front of the lens. Apps can capture without a shutter sound in some modes, so both OSes add on-screen indicators. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the app is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Take photo, Scan, or Start video call, then tap Allow Show what the camera sees and take photos or video Scan codes and doc"},"71":{"title":"Global camera and mic toggles","description":"System switches that turn the camera or mic off for all apps.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/camera-mic-screen/camera-global-toggles","content":"<Chips /> Can show you a blank camera and silence when you flip a phone wide switch System switches that turn the camera or mic off for all apps. A blank feed for the camera and silence for the mic. Android 12 only; support depends on the device. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One switch, all apps\" /> **What the user does:** You switch off Camera access or Mic access in Quick Settings Give blank frames from the camera when the switch is off (Android 12) Give silence from the microphone Slow down motion"},"72":{"title":"Camera and microphone indicators","description":"A dot or chip that shows the camera or mic is active.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/camera-mic-screen/camera-indicators","content":"<Chips /> Can't use the camera or mic without a dot or chip showing A dot or chip that shows the camera or mic is active. Warns the user in real time. Android 12 shows a green chip; iOS 14 shows green and orange dots. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Shown while in use\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The dot or chip shows when an app uses the camera or microphone Show a green chip for the camera on Android 12 and later; tap it for the app name Show a green dot for the camera and orange dot for the m"},"73":{"title":"Microphone","description":"Asks to record sound from the microphone.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/camera-mic-screen/camera-microphone","content":"<Chips /> Can listen through your microphone while in use, after you tap Allow Asks to record sound from the microphone. Hears conversations, music, ambient sounds and the user's voice. A top concern because capture can run without any visible UI. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"While the app is open, sometimes more\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Record, Voice message, or Join a call, then tap Allow Record sound and your voice while the app is open Hear music and background noise as well Keep recor"},"74":{"title":"Recording indicator dot","description":"A green or orange dot shows when an app is using the camera or microphone.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/camera-mic-screen/camera-recording-indicator","content":"<Chips /> Can't use the camera or mic without a green or orange dot showing A green or orange dot shows when an app is using the camera or microphone. System drawn, so apps cannot hide it. Android also adds hardware toggles that cut access. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Shown while in use\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The dot shows by itself when the camera or mic is in use Show a green dot for the camera and an orange dot for the mic Show the app name in Control Center (iPhone) Show a chip wit"},"75":{"title":"Speech recognition","description":"Turns the person's speech or audio files into text, possibly on Apple servers.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/camera-mic-screen/camera-speech-recognition","content":"<Chips /> Can turn your speech into text, sometimes on Apple's servers Turns the person's speech or audio files into text, possibly on Apple servers. Reveals what is said near the phone. iOS warns that audio may go to Apple servers; on-device modes avoid that. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While you speak, per request\" /> **What the user does:** You tap the microphone button and tap Allow for speech recognition Turn your speech or audio files into text Use on device recognition on Android 13 and later"},"76":{"title":"Camera extensions and hardware camera controls","description":"Uses vendor camera modes (night, HDR, bokeh) and hardware buttons from inside an app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/camera-mic-screen/hw-camera-extensions","content":"<Chips /> Can use phone camera modes such as night and HDR, after you allow the camera Uses vendor camera modes (night, HDR, bokeh) and hardware buttons from inside an app. Lets third-party apps match the stock camera's processing. The photos still need camera permission; the extras mainly affect image quality, not access. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the camera is in use\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow for the camera, then choose a mode such as Night Use Night, HDR, Bokeh, Face Retou"},"77":{"title":"Camera, mic and screen","description":"Seeing, hearing and recording. 7 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/camera-mic-screen","content":"Seeing, hearing and recording. 7 capabilities in this group. | Capability | Kind           | In one line                                                    |\n| ---------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Camera     | Asks you first | Can use your camera while the app is open, after you tap Allow | | Capability                       | Kind          | In one line                                               |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Camera and microphone indicators | Gets silently | Can't use the camera or mic without a dot or chip showing | | Capability                                     | Kind                 | In one line                                                                  |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Camera extensions and hardware camera controls | Works without asking | Can use phone camera modes such as night and HDR, after you allow the camera | | Capability                    | Kind                 | In one line                                                               |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Global camera and mic toggles | Works without asking | Can show you a blank camera and silence when you flip a phone wide switch | | Capability | Kind           | In one line                                                          |\n| ---------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Microphone | Asks you first | Can listen through your microphone while in use, after you tap Allow | | Capability              | Kind          | In one line                                                       |\n| ----------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Recording indicator dot | Gets silently | Can't use the camera or mic without a green or orange dot showing | | Capability         | Kind           | In one line                                                  |\n| ------------------ | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Speech recognition | Asks you first | Can turn your speech into text, sometimes on Apple's servers |"},"78":{"title":"In-app browser","description":"Opens outside links in a browser inside the app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/app-eco-in-app-browser","content":"<Chips /> Can show web pages inside the app and, in some versions, watch what you do Opens outside links in a browser inside the app. The host app can see page loads and may inject script. Isolated tabs like SFSafariViewController block that; plain WebViews do not. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only pages opened inside the app\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a link inside the app and it opens without leaving the app Show a web page inside the app window with no prompt Watch which pages you open an"},"79":{"title":"Firebase installation ID","description":"A random per-install ID created by the Firebase SDK.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/id-firebase-iid","content":"<Chips /> Can recognize this one install of the app with a random ID, until it is removed A random per-install ID created by the Firebase SDK. Fresh per app install; many SDKs use it as a first-party ID and link it to other IDs server-side. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"One install, ongoing\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The ID is created the first time the app connects to the internet Tell one install of the app apart from another with a 22 character random ID Link usage, crashes and messages t"},"80":{"title":"Identifier for Vendor (IDFV)","description":"A per-vendor iOS ID that is the same across one developer's apps.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/id-idfv","content":"<Chips /> Can recognize you across apps from the same developer on iPhone, but not other developers A per-vendor iOS ID that is the same across one developer's apps. Lets a developer link their own apps on one phone with no prompt. Resets when the last app by that vendor is deleted. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"One developer's apps, ongoing\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Any app can ask for it as soon as it opens Use one shared ID across all of one developer's apps on your iPhone Count how many"},"81":{"title":"Android ID (SSAID)","description":"A 64-bit ID per app signing key and user on Android; changes on factory reset.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/id-ssaid","content":"<Chips /> Can recognize your Android phone with a long ID, separate for each app maker A 64-bit ID per app signing key and user on Android; changes on factory reset. Gives a stable per-developer device ID with no prompt. Since Android 8 apps signed by different developers see different values. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"One developer's apps, ongoing\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Any Android app can ask for it with no prompt Use a 16 character ID to tell your phone and user profile apart Keep"},"82":{"title":"IDs that survive uninstall (Keychain, Block Store)","description":"iOS Keychain items and Android Block Store data can outlive an uninstall.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/id-survive-uninstall","content":"<Chips /> Can keep a small stored ID that may come back after you delete and reinstall iOS Keychain items and Android Block Store data can outlive an uninstall. Lets an app re-recognize a returning person after delete and reinstall. Keychain items often remain after app deletion; Block Store also restores to a new phone. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Survives uninstall and reinstall\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app saves it quietly while you use it Save a secret or ID in the iPhone Keychai"},"83":{"title":"Widevine device ID (MediaDrm)","description":"A DRM device ID that some apps read as a stable fingerprint.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/id-widevine","content":"<Chips /> Can tell your Android phone apart using a hidden video protection ID, but not on iPhone A DRM device ID that some apps read as a stable fingerprint. Used for DRM licensing; some builds return a device-unique value that can survive factory reset. Recent Android restricts it per app. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Stable phone ID, on Android\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. An app can ask for it silently when it starts Get a 32 byte ID tied to your phone's video protection system on Android"},"84":{"title":"Tracking across apps and sites","description":"IDs and tricks that follow you around. 19 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking","content":"IDs and tricks that follow you around. 19 capabilities in this group. | Capability         | Kind              | In one line                                                                  |\n| ------------------ | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Android ID (SSAID) | Used to track you | Can recognize your Android phone with a long ID, separate for each app maker | | Capability                  | Kind              | In one line                                                                       |\n| --------------------------- | ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Clipboard-based attribution | Used to track you | Can check the clipboard for an install code, with a warning shown on newer phones | | Capability          | Kind                 | In one line                                                                     |\n| ------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Deferred deep links | Works without asking | Can send you to the right screen after install, using how you reached the store | | Capability                    | Kind              | In one line                                                                     |\n| ----------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Device and SDK fingerprinting | Used to track you | Can guess who you are by combining small facts about your phone, with no prompt | | Capability                             | Kind              | In one line                                                                            |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Facebook SDK (login, share, analytics) | Used to track you | Can include Meta's tracking tools in the LinkedIn app, though active use is not proven | | Capability               | Kind              | In one line                                                                     |\n| ------------------------ | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Firebase installation ID | Used to track you | Can recognize this one install of the app with a random ID, until it is removed | | Capability                                            | Kind              | In one line                                                                         |\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Hashed email and phone identity graphs (UID2, RampID) | Used to track you | Can match you across apps and sites using your email or phone, scrambled into an ID | | Capability                              | Kind             | In one line                                                                          |\n| --------------------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| iCloud Private Relay and in-app traffic | Blocked for apps | Can't hide your IP in most iPhone app traffic, only in Safari and some other traffic | | Capability                   | Kind              | In one line                                                                               |\n| ---------------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Identifier for Vendor (IDFV) | Used to track you | Can recognize you across apps from the same developer on iPhone, but not other developers | | Capability                                         | Kind              | In one line                                                                  |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| IDs that survive uninstall (Keychain, Block Store) | Used to track you | Can keep a small stored ID that may come back after you delete and reinstall | | Capability     | Kind                 | In one line                                                                |\n| -------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| In-app browser | Works without asking | Can show web pages inside the app and, in some versions, watch what you do | | Capability                                            | Kind              | In one line                                                                     |\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Keychain and Block Store persistence across reinstall | Used to track you | Can recognize you after a reinstall using an ID that stays behind on your phone | | Capability                                           | Kind             | In one line                                                                     |\n| ---------------------------------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Mail Privacy Protection and Link Tracking Protection | Blocked for apps | Can't see when you open an email in Apple Mail, and some link tags get stripped | | Capability                                 | Kind              | In one line                                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------------------ | ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Microsoft App Center analytics and"},"85":{"title":"Microsoft App Center analytics and crashes","description":"Microsoft's analytics and crash SDK, retired by Microsoft in 2025.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/linkedin-app-center","content":"<Chips /> Can send usage and crash reports to Microsoft's retired tracking service from the Android app Microsoft's analytics and crash SDK, retired by Microsoft in 2025. Sent device model, OS, app version and crash traces to Microsoft. Retirement means newer builds should have replaced it. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the app runs, usage and crashes\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It runs when the app starts or crashes Send crash reports and usage counts over the internet Run with no prom"},"86":{"title":"Facebook SDK (login, share, analytics)","description":"Meta's SDK modules found inside the LinkedIn Android app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/linkedin-facebook-sdk","content":"<Chips /> Can include Meta's tracking tools in the LinkedIn app, though active use is not proven Meta's SDK modules found inside the LinkedIn Android app. Facebook Login, Share and Analytics code can read ad IDs and send app events to Meta unless disabled, linking activity across apps. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Present in code, use unconfirmed\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. These tools live in the app code and need no prompt Offer Facebook login, sharing and analytics if the app turns them o"},"87":{"title":"Qualtrics survey SDK","description":"Survey and feedback SDK found in recent LinkedIn Android builds.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/linkedin-qualtrics","content":"<Chips /> Can show survey and feedback forms inside the LinkedIn Android app, in some versions Survey and feedback SDK found in recent LinkedIn Android builds. Shows in-app surveys and can attach device or session context to answers. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only while surveys run\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. A survey may pop up while you use the app Show survey and feedback questions inside the app Send your survey answers over the internet Work with no prompt after install Appear in eve"},"88":{"title":"Clipboard-based attribution","description":"A web page copies an install token to the clipboard, and the app reads it on first launch.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/xtrack-clipboard-attribution","content":"<Chips /> Can check the clipboard for an install code, with a warning shown on newer phones A web page copies an install token to the clipboard, and the app reads it on first launch. A trick for iOS: the landing page puts a campaign token on the clipboard, then the app picks it up after install. It was blunted by paste banners and prompts. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Once, at first launch\" /> **What the user does:** You tap an ad or link, install the app, then open it for the first time Check text y"},"89":{"title":"Deferred deep links","description":"A link that sends a new user to the right in-app screen after they install the app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/xtrack-deferred-deep-link","content":"<Chips /> Can send you to the right screen after install, using how you reached the store A link that sends a new user to the right in-app screen after they install the app. A click happened before install, so something must carry the target and campaign through the store. Methods: Install Referrer, clipboard, or probabilistic match. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Once, after install\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a link, install the app, then open it for the first time Open the exact in-app scree"},"90":{"title":"Device and SDK fingerprinting","description":"Combining small device signals (model, OS, language, fonts, sensors) into a likely-unique identity.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/xtrack-device-fingerprinting","content":"<Chips /> Can guess who you are by combining small facts about your phone, with no prompt Combining small device signals (model, OS, language, fonts, sensors) into a likely-unique identity. A way to track when IDs are denied. Apple rejects apps and SDKs that do this to refuse-tracking users. Android has no ban. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, from many small signals\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app or an ad tool inside it starts Combine your phone model, system versi"},"91":{"title":"Hashed email and phone identity graphs (UID2, RampID)","description":"Ad firms turn a hashed email or phone into a shared ID that follows people across apps and sites.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/xtrack-hashed-email-identity","content":"<Chips /> Can match you across apps and sites using your email or phone, scrambled into an ID Ad firms turn a hashed email or phone into a shared ID that follows people across apps and sites. When device IDs are blocked, a login or email is the best key. Graphs link logins to ads across publishers, and they rely on the person having signed in. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Follows you across apps and sites\" /> **What the user does:** You sign in or give your email or phone to an app or site Turn your "},"92":{"title":"Sign in with Apple and Hide My Email","description":"Apple signs the person in and can give the app a random relay email instead of the real one.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/xtrack-hide-my-email","content":"<Chips /> Can sign you in on iPhone and give the app a random relay email instead Apple signs the person in and can give the app a random relay email instead of the real one. Stops the app from using email as a cross-site key; hash matching in UID2 and RampID fails against relay addresses. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One choice, at sign in\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in with Apple and choose Share My Email or Hide My Email Sign you in with your Apple account Give the app your real email"},"93":{"title":"iCloud Private Relay and in-app traffic","description":"iCloud Private Relay hides the IP in Safari and some cleartext traffic, but not most app traffic.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/xtrack-icloud-private-relay","content":"<Chips /> Can't hide your IP in most iPhone app traffic, only in Safari and some other traffic iCloud Private Relay hides the IP in Safari and some cleartext traffic, but not most app traffic. Click-side IPs from Safari differ from the app's own IP, hurting probabilistic matching. Apps still see the real IP in their own requests. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Limited cover, mostly Safari\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. You turn iCloud Private Relay on in iPhone settings Hide your real IP in Safar"},"94":{"title":"Shared Keychain access groups and app groups","description":"Apps from one developer team can share secrets and files; other developers cannot join.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/xtrack-keychain-access-groups","content":"<Chips /> Can share saved secrets and files between apps from the same developer on iPhone Apps from one developer team can share secrets and files; other developers cannot join. Lets one company recognize the same person across its own apps. It cannot see another company's keychain items. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"One developer's apps, ongoing\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The developer sets this up before the app ships Share a login or token between apps from one Apple developer team Shar"},"95":{"title":"Keychain and Block Store persistence across reinstall","description":"A stored ID that survives uninstall, so a reinstalled app recognizes the person.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/xtrack-keychain-reinstall","content":"<Chips /> Can recognize you after a reinstall using an ID that stays behind on your phone A stored ID that survives uninstall, so a reinstalled app recognizes the person. iOS Keychain entries remain after the app is deleted. Android needs Block Store or Auto Backup. Used for fraud and abuse limits, and sometimes tracking. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Survives uninstall and reinstall\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app saves it quietly the first time you use it Keep an ID in the iPhone Keycha"},"96":{"title":"Mail Privacy Protection and Link Tracking Protection","description":"Apple Mail preloads email images through a proxy and Safari and Mail strip known tracking parameters from links.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/xtrack-mail-privacy-protection","content":"<Chips /> Can't see when you open an email in Apple Mail, and some link tags get stripped Apple Mail preloads email images through a proxy and Safari and Mail strip known tracking parameters from links. Open pixels look like opens even when no human saw the email, so email-to-app funnels are noisy. Link Tracking Protection (iOS 17) removes known click IDs from URLs in Mail, Messages and Safari private browsing. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocks some tracking, not all\" /> **What the user does:** Not"},"97":{"title":"Probabilistic install matching (IP plus user agent)","description":"An MMP guesses which ad click led to an install by matching IP, device model and OS version within a short time window.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/xtrack-probabilistic-matching","content":"<Chips /> Can guess which ad led to your install by matching your IP, phone model and timing An MMP guesses which ad click led to an install by matching IP, device model and OS version within a short time window. Without IDs, the vendor matches the click (IP, UA, language, OS) with the first app open from the same signals, usually within hours. Apple treats this as fingerprinting for ATT-denied users. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One guess, near first open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap an ad, i"},"98":{"title":"Debugger, hooks and developer mode flags","description":"Apps can read whether ADB, developer options or a debugger are on.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/det-debugger-adb","content":"<Chips /> Can check if developer mode or a debugger is turned on, with no prompt on Android Apps can read whether ADB, developer options or a debugger are on. Used in anti-tamper and fraud checks. Reveals a technical person or a reverse-engineer. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A quick check at one moment\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it starts or before a sensitive step Check if Android USB debugging is on Check if Android developer options are on Check if a debugger is attac"},"99":{"title":"Emulator and simulator detection","description":"Apps read build fields and hardware traits to spot emulators.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/det-emulator","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if the app is running on a fake phone used for testing Apps read build fields and hardware traits to spot emulators. Used to block bots and farms. A real phone with unusual build fields can be flagged by mistake. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A quick check at one moment\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it starts or when you sign in Spot the official iPhone Simulator reliably Guess an Android emulator from build names and hardware clues Block or limit features"},"100":{"title":"Root and jailbreak detection","description":"Apps infer root or jailbreak from files, props and attestation; neither OS gives a direct flag.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/det-root-jailbreak","content":"<Chips /> Can guess if your phone is rooted or jailbroken, but cannot be sure Apps infer root or jailbreak from files, props and attestation; neither OS gives a direct flag. Rooted or jailbroken phones weaken the sandbox. Banking and game apps lock them out; privacy tool users get caught as false positives. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A quick check at one moment\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it opens, or when you sign in or pay Look for files and tools that rooted or jailb"},"101":{"title":"Battery health, temperature and cycle count","description":"Android 14 lets apps read battery cycle count and health; iOS shows none of this to apps.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-battery-health","content":"<Chips /> Can see battery health and charge cycles on Android 14 and newer, but not on iPhone Android 14 lets apps read battery cycle count and health; iOS shows none of this to apps. Reveals how old and worn a phone is (cycle count, capacity percent), which is a strong device-age and value signal and a stable fingerprint bit set. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Android 14 and newer only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it starts or on a timer See battery cycle count and health o"},"102":{"title":"Battery level and charging state","description":"Reads battery percent and whether the phone is plugged in, with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-battery-level","content":"<Chips /> Can see your battery percent and if the phone is charging, with no prompt Reads battery percent and whether the phone is plugged in, with no prompt. Shows routine (charging overnight at home) and pairs with other signals to track a device. Apps use it for power-aware sync; Olejnik et al. showed level and discharge time can tag a device briefly. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the app is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks while it is open See your battery level from "},"103":{"title":"Boot time and uptime","description":"Reads time since boot; the boot timestamp itself is a classic stable ID until restart.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-boot-time","content":"<Chips /> Can see how long your phone has been on, and on Android its restart count Reads time since boot; the boot timestamp itself is a classic stable ID until restart. now minus uptime gives a boot timestamp that stays constant until reboot and is near-unique across devices. That is why Apple made it a Required Reason API. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Quick check, until restart\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it starts See time since the last restart on Android, to the mil"},"104":{"title":"Screen brightness","description":"Reads the screen brightness setting with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-brightness","content":"<Chips /> Can check your screen brightness with no prompt, and change it while the app is open Reads the screen brightness setting with no prompt. Reveals indoor or outdoor use and whether the person left auto-brightness. Mostly a context hint. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the app is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing to check it. You tap a feature like Full brightness for a ticket or code See your brightness level, from 0 to 255 on Android See if brightness is set to automatic on Android"},"105":{"title":"Build fingerprint and kernel version","description":"Reads the full build ID string and kernel version.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-build-fingerprint","content":"<Chips /> Can see your exact phone software build, a long label often over 100 characters Reads the full build ID string and kernel version. Pins the exact firmware (model, build, patch) and so the OEM and carrier variant. Tight fingerprint; also tells where a phone was sold. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Always available, one label\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app can look it up as soon as it opens See the full Android build label, with tags and type See the kernel version on Android and "},"106":{"title":"CPU cores and ABI","description":"Reads core count and CPU architecture with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-cpu-cores","content":"<Chips /> Can see how many CPU cores your phone has and what chip type, with no prompt Reads core count and CPU architecture with no prompt. Identifies chipset class (4 to 10 cores). A device-model proxy: apps pick builds and thread pools with it, trackers add it to a fingerprint. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Always available, simple facts\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it starts, often to pick graphics or video settings See the number of cores, typically 4 to 10 on Android "},"107":{"title":"User-assigned device name","description":"Reads the name the person gave their phone: free on Android, locked to a generic name on iOS 16 and later.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-device-name","content":"<Chips /> Can see the name you gave your phone on Android, but a generic one on iPhone Reads the name the person gave their phone: free on Android, locked to a generic name on iOS 16 and later. A name like Jane's iPhone leaks a real name and maybe a family. Apple stopped this in iOS 16; Android still shows it. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Always available on Android\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing on Android. The app checks when it starts See the phone name you set on Android, often just the mode"},"108":{"title":"Low Power Mode and Battery Saver","description":"Tells an app whether the person turned on the system power-saving mode.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-low-power","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if Battery Saver or Low Power Mode is on, but cannot change it Tells an app whether the person turned on the system power-saving mode. Shows the person is low on battery or conserving it. Apps use it to cut background work; it is a minor context signal. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the app is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. You turn the mode on yourself, or your phone does at low battery Check if Low Power Mode (iPhone) or Battery Saver (Android) is on Get told when"},"109":{"title":"Device model and manufacturer","description":"Reads exact model and manufacturer on Android; iOS gives a model code via sysctl.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-model","content":"<Chips /> Can see your exact phone model and maker on Android, and a model code on iPhone Reads exact model and manufacturer on Android; iOS gives a model code via sysctl. Tells price tier, age, and often region. It is the biggest single device-fingerprint field: Android has thousands of models, iPhone has about 40 live models. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Always available, simple facts\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app can ask the moment it opens See your exact Android model and brand, su"},"110":{"title":"OS version and security patch level","description":"Reads OS version, API level and security patch date with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-os-version","content":"<Chips /> Can see your phone's system version and security patch date, with no prompt Reads OS version, API level and security patch date with no prompt. Shows how up to date the phone is and its exposure to known bugs. A strong fingerprint bit and a fraud and compatibility signal. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Always available, simple facts\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it opens, often to show an update message See your Android version, its build level and the security patc"},"111":{"title":"RAM size","description":"Reads total memory in bytes with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-ram-size","content":"<Chips /> Can see how much memory your phone has, down to the exact byte, with no prompt Reads total memory in bytes with no prompt. Maps almost directly to device price tier (2 to 16 GB) and is a stable fingerprint bit. Apps use it to size caches. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Always available, one number\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it starts to choose how heavy to run See total memory in bytes on Android and iPhone See the app's Android memory class, about 128 to 512 MB "},"112":{"title":"Display refresh rate and HDR","description":"Reads the screen refresh rate and HDR support with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-refresh-rate","content":"<Chips /> Can see your screen refresh rate and whether it shows HDR, with no prompt Reads the screen refresh rate and HDR support with no prompt. 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz plus HDR format list separates device tiers and models. Used for animation pacing. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Always available, simple facts\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Games and video apps check when they start See the refresh rate, such as 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz See which refresh rate options your screen supports on Android"},"113":{"title":"Ringer mode, Do Not Disturb and Focus","description":"Android shows ringer mode and DND state freely; iOS exposes only a Focus on/off flag after a prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-ringer-focus","content":"<Chips /> Can see ringer and Do Not Disturb on Android, but only Focus on iPhone Android shows ringer mode and DND state freely; iOS exposes only a Focus on/off flag after a prompt. Reveals when the person does not want to be disturbed: sleeping, working, driving. Android leaks more with no prompt; iOS gates it behind a Focus Status permission and returns one boolean. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the app is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing on Android. On iPhone you answer a permission p"},"114":{"title":"Screen size and density","description":"Reads pixel size, density and scale factor with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-screen-metrics","content":"<Chips /> Can see your screen size, sharpness and scale, with no prompt Reads pixel size, density and scale factor with no prompt. Identifies the device model almost uniquely on iPhone and narrows it on Android. Needed for layout; also a long-lived fingerprint field. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Always available, simple facts\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it opens, to lay out its screens See screen width and height, in pixels on Android and points on iPhone See sharpness, 1"},"115":{"title":"Free and total storage","description":"Reads exact free and total disk bytes with no prompt; on iOS this needs a declared reason.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-storage-space","content":"<Chips /> Can see free and total storage on Android, and on iPhone only with a stated reason Reads exact free and total disk bytes with no prompt; on iOS this needs a declared reason. Exact free bytes change slowly and are near-unique, so they are a classic fingerprint. It also reveals device tier (64 GB vs 512 GB) and how full the phone is. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Always available on Android\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it starts, or before a big download See exact f"},"116":{"title":"Thermal state and headroom","description":"Reports how hot the device is: 4 coarse levels on iOS, 7 levels plus a forecast on Android.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-thermal-state","content":"<Chips /> Can tell how hot your phone is, in 4 levels on iPhone and 7 on Android Reports how hot the device is: 4 coarse levels on iOS, 7 levels plus a forecast on Android. Shows the phone is throttled or in a hot place (car, sun, charging while gaming). Weak context signal; more useful to games that scale quality. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the app is running\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks while it is running, mostly games and video See iPhone heat as nominal, fair, seri"},"117":{"title":"Volume level per audio stream","description":"Reads the current media, ring and alarm volume without any prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/dev-volume-stream","content":"<Chips /> Can see how loud your phone is set, with no prompt Reads the current media, ring and alarm volume without any prompt. Shows whether the person is muted, at a meeting, or listening loud. Apps use it to warn or adapt; trackers read it as a weak behavioral signal. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the app is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks while it is open, such as a video or call app See media, ring and alarm volume on Android See the iPhone output volume while the a"},"118":{"title":"Extended virtual addressing","description":"Lets an app use a much larger virtual address space.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/device-extended-virtual-addressing","content":"<Chips /> Can use a larger memory address range on iPhone, but not on Android Lets an app use a much larger virtual address space. Not about people, but part of the entitlement tier: some capabilities are just a switch in the app. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A developer setting, no data\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The developer turns on a setting before the app ships Map more memory than the default limit on iPhone Handle very large files or models in heavy apps Use it with no prompt after "},"119":{"title":"App and device identifiers","description":"IDs the OS gives an app to recognize an install or a vendor's apps.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/device-identifiers","content":"<Chips /> Can use IDs your phone gives it to recognize this install or the developer's apps IDs the OS gives an app to recognize an install or a vendor's apps. Lets a company recognize the same install across sessions and link sessions without login. Some survive reinstall (iOS Keychain, backups). <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"One developer, ongoing\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app can get them as soon as it opens Use Android ID to recognize you across the same developer's apps Use the iPh"},"120":{"title":"Increased memory limit","description":"Raises the foreground memory cap for heavy apps.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/device-increased-memory-limit","content":"<Chips /> Can ask for more working memory for heavy tasks, with no prompt Raises the foreground memory cap for heavy apps. No privacy impact; shows how OS gives bigger resource caps to apps that ask in a manifest or entitlement. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A developer setting, no data\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The developer turns this on before the app ships Raise the app's memory cap on supported iPhones Ask for a large memory heap on Android with one setting Open big photos, video or ma"},"121":{"title":"Device name set by the person","description":"Reads the name a person gave the phone, such as a first name plus a model.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/device-user-assigned-device-name","content":"<Chips /> Can see the name you gave your phone on Android, but a generic one on iPhone Reads the name a person gave the phone, such as a first name plus a model. Often contains a real name, a strong identifier. iOS 16 hides it behind an Apple approved entitlement. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Always available on Android\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing on Android. On iPhone Apple must approve a form first See your phone name set in Android settings, often a first name plus a model See the Bluetoot"},"122":{"title":"Device details","description":"Model, memory, battery and other facts about the device. 24 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile","content":"Model, memory, battery and other facts about the device. 24 capabilities in this group. | Capability                 | Kind          | In one line                                                                       |\n| -------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| App and device identifiers | Gets silently | Can use IDs your phone gives it to recognize this install or the developer's apps | | Capability                                  | Kind          | In one line                                                                         |\n| ------------------------------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Battery health, temperature and cycle count | Gets silently | Can see battery health and charge cycles on Android 14 and newer, but not on iPhone | | Capability                       | Kind          | In one line                                                               |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Battery level and charging state | Gets silently | Can see your battery percent and if the phone is charging, with no prompt | | Capability           | Kind          | In one line                                                               |\n| -------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Boot time and uptime | Gets silently | Can see how long your phone has been on, and on Android its restart count | | Capability                           | Kind          | In one line                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Build fingerprint and kernel version | Gets silently | Can see your exact phone software build, a long label often over 100 characters | | Capability        | Kind          | In one line                                                                  |\n| ----------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| CPU cores and ABI | Gets silently | Can see how many CPU cores your phone has and what chip type, with no prompt | | Capability                               | Kind          | In one line                                                                       |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Debugger, hooks and developer mode flags | Gets silently | Can check if developer mode or a debugger is turned on, with no prompt on Android | | Capability                    | Kind          | In one line                                                                     |\n| ----------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Device model and manufacturer | Gets silently | Can see your exact phone model and maker on Android, and a model code on iPhone | | Capability                    | Kind          | In one line                                                                  |\n| ----------------------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Device name set by the person | Gets silently | Can see the name you gave your phone on Android, but a generic one on iPhone | | Capability                   | Kind          | In one line                                                               |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Display refresh rate and HDR | Gets silently | Can see your screen refresh rate and whether it shows HDR, with no prompt | | Capability                       | Kind          | In one line                                                     |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Emulator and simulator detection | Gets silently | Can tell if the app is running on a fake phone used for testing | | Capability                  | Kind                 | In one line                                                         |\n| --------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Extended virtual addressing | Works without asking | Can use a larger memory address range on iPhone, but not on Android | | Capability             | Kind          | In one line                                                                        |\n| ---------------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Free and total storage | Gets silently | Can see free and total storage on Android, and on iPhone only with a stated reason | | Capability             | Kind                 | In one line                                                     |\n| ---------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Increased memory limit | Works without asking | Can ask for more working memory for heavy tasks, with no prompt | | Capability                       | Kind          | In one line                                                             |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Low Power Mode and Battery Saver | Gets silently | Can tell if Battery Saver or Low Power Mode is on, but cannot change it | | Capability                          | Kind          | In one line                                                          "},"123":{"title":"Screenshot and screen recording detection","description":"Apps are told when the person takes a screenshot or records the screen.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/display-window/det-screen-capture","content":"<Chips /> Can tell when you take a screenshot or record the screen, but not stop you Apps are told when the person takes a screenshot or records the screen. Lets a chat, bank or media app react to capture. Reveals the person kept a copy. Android 14 added screenshot detection; iOS has had it since iOS 7. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the app is open\" /> **What the user does:** You take a screenshot or start a screen recording while the app is open Get told when you take a screenshot, on Android 1"},"124":{"title":"Draw over other apps","description":"Lets the app draw windows on top of other apps.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/display-window/display-overlay","content":"<Chips /> Can draw windows over other apps on Android if you flip a switch, never on iPhone Lets the app draw windows on top of other apps. Overlays power chat heads and screen filters but are the classic tool for tapjacking and fake login screens. Android 12 limits untrusted touches through overlays. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Anytime, once you allow it\" /> **What the user does:** You turn on Display over other apps for this app in Android settings Show a floating button or bubble above other apps"},"125":{"title":"Picture in picture","description":"Lets video keep playing in a small window while the user uses other apps.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/display-window/display-pip","content":"<Chips /> Can keep a video playing in a small window while you use other apps Lets video keep playing in a small window while the user uses other apps. A small floating window. Android lets users disable it per app; iOS supports PiP via AVKit. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the video plays\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Picture in Picture or swipe home while a video plays Keep a video or call in a small window above other apps Start the window on its own when you leave the app, on Android 12"},"126":{"title":"External display and casting output","description":"Draws a separate screen on a connected monitor or AirPlay target.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/display-window/hw-external-display","content":"<Chips /> Can show a separate screen on a connected monitor, TV or AirPlay device Draws a separate screen on a connected monitor or AirPlay target. An app can show different content on a second screen. Mostly a layout feature; it also lets an app know that a monitor is attached. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While connected\" /> **What the user does:** You plug in a monitor or choose Cast or AirPlay Show a different screen on a wired display with no prompt Show to a TV through the system Cast, Miracast"},"127":{"title":"Screen and windows","description":"Screen size, brightness, full screen, windows. 4 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/display-window","content":"Screen size, brightness, full screen, windows. 4 capabilities in this group. | Capability           | Kind           | In one line                                                                       |\n| -------------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Draw over other apps | Asks you first | Can draw windows over other apps on Android if you flip a switch, never on iPhone | | Capability                          | Kind                 | In one line                                                             |\n| ----------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| External display and casting output | Works without asking | Can show a separate screen on a connected monitor, TV or AirPlay device | | Capability         | Kind                 | In one line                                                         |\n| ------------------ | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Picture in picture | Works without asking | Can keep a video playing in a small window while you use other apps | | Capability                                | Kind          | In one line                                                                |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Screenshot and screen recording detection | Gets silently | Can tell when you take a screenshot or record the screen, but not stop you |"},"128":{"title":"Cache and temporary storage eviction","description":"The system may delete cache and temporary files when storage runs low; other files stay.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-cache-eviction","content":"<Chips /> Can store temporary files that the system may delete when your storage runs low The system may delete cache and temporary files when storage runs low; other files stay. Apps cannot rely on cache data staying. Android clears cache dirs and reports a cache quota; iOS purges Caches and tmp when space is needed, and flags files as purgeable. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Temporary, system may delete\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app saves cache files while you use it Save cache and te"},"129":{"title":"File data protection classes","description":"Files are encrypted at rest, and whether an app can read them while the phone is locked depends on a protection class.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-data-protection","content":"<Chips /> Can keep files locked until you unlock the phone, depending on how they are protected Files are encrypted at rest, and whether an app can read them while the phone is locked depends on a protection class. iOS has four data protection classes; Android has credential encrypted and device encrypted storage. This decides if background work can run before the first unlock. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Depends on the lock state\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. You unlock your phone as usual E"},"130":{"title":"Document picker","description":"System file chooser for one document, such as a resume.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-document-picker","content":"<Chips /> Can open one document you pick from the system file chooser System file chooser for one document, such as a resume. The app gets only the file picked. Resumes and CVs hold name, address, employers and sometimes IDs. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only the file you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Upload file, Attach, or Choose resume and pick a file Open the one file or files you choose Upload or attach the file you pick Let you browse folders, iCloud Drive or other storage in the sys"},"131":{"title":"All files access","description":"Gives the app access to nearly all files on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-manage-external-storage","content":"<Chips /> Can open almost every file on your Android phone if you turn on All files access Gives the app access to nearly all files on the device. Lets an app read and write most user files. Intended for file managers and backup tools; Play requires a declaration. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Nearly all files, anytime\" /> **What the user does:** You open Settings and switch on All files access for the app List and open files across your Android shared storage Change, move and delete files in those sh"},"132":{"title":"Read audio files","description":"Lets the app read music and audio files on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-media-audio","content":"<Chips /> Can list and play the music and audio files on your phone once you allow it Lets the app read music and audio files on the device. Reveals music taste and voice recordings stored as files. A separate permission group on Android 13. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"All audio, once you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow on the music and audio prompt, often in a player or editor app List and open the music and audio files in your shared storage on Android See your music library on iPh"},"133":{"title":"Read photos","description":"Lets the app read all photos in the shared gallery.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-media-images","content":"<Chips /> Can open all photos in your gallery, or only the ones you choose on newer phones Lets the app read all photos in the shared gallery. Exposes the person's whole photo library, including screenshots, documents and faces. Google Play requires apps to use the Photo Picker unless core functionality needs full access. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"All photos, or only those you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Add photo or Backup photos, then choose Allow or Select photos Open every photo i"},"134":{"title":"Music and media library","description":"Reads the person's own music library and listening history.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-media-library","content":"<Chips /> Can see your own music library and play your songs once you tap Allow Reads the person's own music library and listening history. Reveals taste, play counts and playlists; on iOS it also gates Apple Music playback control. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your music library, once allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow when a music or player app asks for your library List songs and playlists in your music library Play your music inside the app Use Apple Music playback on iPhone after "},"135":{"title":"Media location metadata","description":"Lets the app read the GPS location stored inside photos.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-media-location","content":"<Chips /> Can see where your photos were taken, if you allow location info in media Lets the app read the GPS location stored inside photos. Photo EXIF reveals where and when images were taken, including home and holiday locations. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Where photos were taken\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow on the Location in media prompt, often in a gallery or editing app See the GPS place saved inside photos, on Android once you allow it Ask for the original photo with its locatio"},"136":{"title":"Selected photos access","description":"Lets the user share only chosen photos and videos with the app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-media-selected","content":"<Chips /> Can open only the photos and videos you choose to share, not your whole library Lets the user share only chosen photos and videos with the app. A narrower grant than full library access. Android 14 lets people pick specific items; Android 16 pre-selects app-owned photos. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only what you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Select photos and videos (Android) or Select Photos (iPhone) in the prompt Open the specific photos and videos you picked Let you add more "},"137":{"title":"Read videos","description":"Lets the app read all videos in the shared gallery.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-media-video","content":"<Chips /> Can open every video in your gallery on Android, and on iPhone with the photos permission Lets the app read all videos in the shared gallery. Exposes recorded and downloaded videos. Same Play restrictions as photos. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"All videos, once you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow on the Photos and videos prompt List and open videos in your shared gallery on Android Play videos inside the app Open videos on iPhone with the same permission as photos Upload vid"},"138":{"title":"Photo picker","description":"A system gallery picker that returns only chosen photos with no permission.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-photo-picker","content":"<Chips /> Can open only the photos you pick from a system gallery, with no permission asked A system gallery picker that returns only chosen photos with no permission. Reveals only the items the person selects. Recommended by both platforms. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only what you pick, once\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Choose photo or Attach image and select photos in the system picker Open the one or more photos you pick Upload or edit the chosen photos Work with no permission prompt at a"},"139":{"title":"Photo library, add only","description":"Saves a photo or video into the library without being able to read any of it.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-photos-add-only","content":"<Chips /> Can save new photos and videos to your library, but cannot look at what is there Saves a photo or video into the library without being able to read any of it. Lets an app drop images in (camera, scanner, meme maker) with no view of what is already stored, a far smaller disclosure than reading the roll. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Save only, no browsing\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Save photo, Save to gallery or Download image Save a new photo or video into your library Save the app'"},"140":{"title":"Photo library, full access","description":"Reads and edits every photo and video in the shared library.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-photos-full-library","content":"<Chips /> Can see and change every photo and video in your library once you allow full access Reads and edits every photo and video in the shared library. Exposes the whole camera roll: faces, places (EXIF GPS), screenshots of IDs and bank pages, dates that map out a life. Apps rarely need all of it. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Whole library, anytime\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow access to all photos or Allow all, often for backup or editing List and open every photo and video Upload you"},"141":{"title":"Limited photo access (chosen items only)","description":"The person shares only hand-picked photos with the app, not the whole library.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-photos-limited","content":"<Chips /> Can see only the photos you hand pick, not your whole library The person shares only hand-picked photos with the app, not the whole library. Lets someone give an app a few images without exposing everything. The app sees only chosen assets and cannot tell what else exists. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only photos you hand pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Select Photos and choose a few in the prompt Open the photos and videos you chose Show a button to let you add more photos later o"},"142":{"title":"Scoped storage and shared files","description":"Android apps see their own folder and shared media through MediaStore; all files access needs a special grant.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-scoped-storage","content":"<Chips /> Can open its own folder and shared media only, not everyone's files, on Android Android apps see their own folder and shared media through MediaStore; all files access needs a special grant. Since Android 10, apps no longer roam the whole shared storage. Photos and files come through MediaStore, document picker or the all files grant. iOS apps see only their own sandbox. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Own folder plus what you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow on a photos prompt "},"143":{"title":"Legacy external storage","description":"Old permission for reading and writing shared files.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/files-storage-legacy","content":"<Chips /> Can't open shared files with the old storage permission on newer Android, and never on iPhone Old permission for reading and writing shared files. Opened all shared files to any app that asked. Replaced by scoped storage and granular media permissions. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Mostly blocked on new phones\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow on a storage prompt, which no longer does much Ask for the old storage permission on older Android Open shared files freely on Android 10 and "},"144":{"title":"Files and storage","description":"Saving, opening and keeping data. 19 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage","content":"Saving, opening and keeping data. 19 capabilities in this group. | Capability       | Kind           | In one line                                                                      |\n| ---------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| All files access | Asks you first | Can open almost every file on your Android phone if you turn on All files access | | Capability                           | Kind                 | In one line                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------------ | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Cache and temporary storage eviction | Works without asking | Can store temporary files that the system may delete when your storage runs low | | Capability      | Kind                   | In one line                                                 |\n| --------------- | ---------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Document picker | You pick what to share | Can open one document you pick from the system file chooser | | Capability                   | Kind                 | In one line                                                                           |\n| ---------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| File data protection classes | Works without asking | Can keep files locked until you unlock the phone, depending on how they are protected | | Capability              | Kind           | In one line                                                                                   |\n| ----------------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Legacy external storage | Asks you first | Can't open shared files with the old storage permission on newer Android, and never on iPhone | | Capability                               | Kind           | In one line                                                   |\n| ---------------------------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Limited photo access (chosen items only) | Asks you first | Can see only the photos you hand pick, not your whole library | | Capability              | Kind           | In one line                                                               |\n| ----------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Media location metadata | Asks you first | Can see where your photos were taken, if you allow location info in media | | Capability              | Kind           | In one line                                                           |\n| ----------------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Music and media library | Asks you first | Can see your own music library and play your songs once you tap Allow | | Capability              | Kind           | In one line                                                                      |\n| ----------------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Photo library, add only | Asks you first | Can save new photos and videos to your library, but cannot look at what is there | | Capability                 | Kind           | In one line                                                                         |\n| -------------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Photo library, full access | Asks you first | Can see and change every photo and video in your library once you allow full access | | Capability   | Kind                   | In one line                                                                       |\n| ------------ | ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Photo picker | You pick what to share | Can open only the photos you pick from a system gallery, with no permission asked | | Capability       | Kind           | In one line                                                                 |\n| ---------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Read audio files | Asks you first | Can list and play the music and audio files on your phone once you allow it | | Capability  | Kind           | In one line                                                                      |\n| ----------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Read photos | Asks you first | Can open all photos in your gallery, or only the ones you choose on newer phones | | Capability  | Kind           | In one line                                                                               |\n| ----------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Read videos | Asks you first | Can open every video in your gallery on Android, and on iPhone with the photos permission | | Capability                  | Kind                 | In one line                                                                            |\n| --------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Required Reason: disk space | Works without asking | Can check your free storage on iPhone only with a stated reason, and freely on Android | | Capability                       | Kind                 | In one line                                                                     |\n| -------------------------------- | --------------------"},"145":{"title":"Required Reason: disk space","description":"iOS free and total storage need 85F4.1, E174.1, 7D9E.1 or B728.1.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/rr-disk-space","content":"<Chips /> Can check your free storage on iPhone only with a stated reason, and freely on Android iOS free and total storage need 85F4.1, E174.1, 7D9E.1 or B728.1. Exact free bytes are near-unique. 85F4.1 shows space to the user, E174.1 checks room before writing, 7D9E.1 adds to a user-sent bug report, B728.1 is for health research apps. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A number, with a stated reason\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it starts or before a download Show you how much "},"146":{"title":"Required Reason: file timestamps","description":"iOS file creation and modification time needs a reason code: C617.1, 3B52.1, DDA9.1 or 0A2A.1.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/rr-file-timestamps","content":"<Chips /> Can check file dates on iPhone only with a stated reason, and freely on Android iOS file creation and modification time needs a reason code: C617.1, 3B52.1, DDA9.1 or 0A2A.1. File dates can reveal install time and OS age. Reasons limit who sees them: C617.1 app container, 3B52.1 user-granted files, DDA9.1 display, 0A2A.1 SDK wrapper. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A number, with a stated reason\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it opens or syncs Check dates on its own f"},"147":{"title":"Required Reason: UserDefaults","description":"iOS UserDefaults access needs a reason code such as CA92.1 (app only) or 1C8F.1 (app group).","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/rr-user-defaults","content":"<Chips /> Can keep its own saved settings on iPhone, but must give Apple a reason iOS UserDefaults access needs a reason code such as CA92.1 (app only) or 1C8F.1 (app group). Shared defaults can leak info across apps. CA92.1 is app-only data, 1C8F.1 is data shared in one app group, C56D.1 is an SDK wrapper, AC6B.1 reads managed configuration. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Its own settings\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app stores settings quietly as you use it Save its own settings and choic"},"148":{"title":"Body sensors in background","description":"Lets the app read heart rate and similar vitals while not on screen.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Health and fitness"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/health-fitness/health-body-sensors-background","content":"<Chips /> Can keep checking your heart rate and vitals while the app is not on screen Lets the app read heart rate and similar vitals while not on screen. Continuous health monitoring: sleep, stress and arrhythmia patterns. Replaced by READ\\_HEALTH\\_DATA\\_IN\\_BACKGROUND for apps targeting Android 16. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, but only vitals you allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You first allow body sensors, then turn on background access in Settings See heart rate and similar vitals in"},"149":{"title":"Body sensors","description":"Asks to read heart rate and similar body sensor data while the app is in use.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Health and fitness"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/health-fitness/health-body-sensors","content":"<Chips /> Can see your heart rate and similar body signals while the app is open Asks to read heart rate and similar body sensor data while the app is in use. Heart rate and related vitals reveal fitness, stress and health conditions. Android 16 replaces this permission with granular health permissions for apps targeting 36. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the app is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Start workout or Measure heart rate and then Allow See live heart rate from the phone or a "},"150":{"title":"Clinical health records (FHIR)","description":"Reads medical records such as conditions, allergies and immunizations in FHIR form.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Health and fitness"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/health-fitness/health-clinical-records","content":"<Chips /> Can see your medical records, such as conditions and allergies, if you share them Reads medical records such as conditions, allergies and immunizations in FHIR form. Lab results, diagnoses and vaccines are among the most sensitive data. Both platforms use separate permissions. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Medical records you share\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect health records and approve a separate sheet See conditions, allergies and immunizations you choose to share Use record"},"151":{"title":"Health Connect permissions","description":"A set of per-data-type read and write permissions for the health data store.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Health and fitness"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/health-fitness/health-health-connect","content":"<Chips /> Can see and add health data, one type at a time, from Health Connect or Health A set of per-data-type read and write permissions for the health data store. Each Health Connect data type (steps, heart rate, sleep, weight and more) has its own read and write grant plus background and history options. A privacy policy rationale page is mandatory. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Only the data types you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect to Health Connect (Android) or Allow in the He"},"152":{"title":"Health data (HealthKit and Health Connect)","description":"Reads and writes health records such as steps, heart rate and sleep, one data type at a time.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Health and fitness"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/health-fitness/health-healthkit","content":"<Chips /> Can see and add health data such as steps and sleep, one type at a time Reads and writes health records such as steps, heart rate and sleep, one data type at a time. Sleep, heart rate and cycle data show conditions and habits. Both OSes grant per data type. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Only the data types you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect health data and switch on the types you want to share See the data types you allow, such as steps, heart rate and sleep Add new record"},"153":{"title":"Health information (declared, not sensor data)","description":"Health data LinkedIn declares on its Play label, with no health sensor access.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Health and fitness"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/health-fitness/health-self-declared","content":"<Chips /> Can't use your health sensors in LinkedIn; health info is only on the store label Health data LinkedIn declares on its Play label, with no health sensor access. A disability or health related profile field or message can become special category data under privacy law. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Declared only, no sensor access\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. No health permission is asked List health information on its store label as optional Collect health details you type in yourself"},"154":{"title":"SensorKit research sensors","description":"Reads broad passive sensor data for approved research studies only.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Health and fitness"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/health-fitness/health-sensorkit","content":"<Chips /> Can't collect broad sensor data like research studies do, unless Apple approves a study Reads broad passive sensor data for approved research studies only. Gives researchers keyboard metrics, ambient light, device usage and more over months, a deep behavioral record. Apple limits it to reviewed studies. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Research studies only\" /> **What the user does:** You join an approved research study and approve each sensor on a consent sheet Collect passive sensor data for "},"155":{"title":"Health and fitness","description":"Steps, heart rate, workouts, health records. 7 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/health-fitness","content":"Steps, heart rate, workouts, health records. 7 capabilities in this group. | Capability   | Kind           | In one line                                                            |\n| ------------ | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Body sensors | Asks you first | Can see your heart rate and similar body signals while the app is open | | Capability                 | Kind           | In one line                                                                 |\n| -------------------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Body sensors in background | Asks you first | Can keep checking your heart rate and vitals while the app is not on screen | | Capability                     | Kind           | In one line                                                                       |\n| ------------------------------ | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Clinical health records (FHIR) | Asks you first | Can see your medical records, such as conditions and allergies, if you share them | | Capability                 | Kind           | In one line                                                                    |\n| -------------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Health Connect permissions | Asks you first | Can see and add health data, one type at a time, from Health Connect or Health | | Capability                                 | Kind           | In one line                                                             |\n| ------------------------------------------ | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Health data (HealthKit and Health Connect) | Asks you first | Can see and add health data such as steps and sleep, one type at a time | | Capability                                     | Kind          | In one line                                                                       |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Health information (declared, not sensor data) | Gets silently | Can't use your health sensors in LinkedIn; health info is only on the store label | | Capability                 | Kind                 | In one line                                                                             |\n| -------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| SensorKit research sensors | Works without asking | Can't collect broad sensor data like research studies do, unless Apple approves a study |"},"156":{"title":"Biometric availability and enrollment change","description":"Reads if biometrics are enrolled; iOS also exposes a token that changes when enrollment changes.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/det-biometric-enrolled","content":"<Chips /> Can check whether you set up fingerprint or face unlock, but not who you are Reads if biometrics are enrolled; iOS also exposes a token that changes when enrollment changes. Reveals device security setup. The iOS domain state changes when a new finger or face is added, so it is a weak device-state fingerprint. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no answer\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app decides whether to offer biometric sign in Check if the phone has finger"},"157":{"title":"Device lock and passcode state","description":"Reads whether the phone has a screen lock and whether it is locked.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/det-lock-state","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if your phone has a screen lock and whether it is locked now Reads whether the phone has a screen lock and whether it is locked. A phone with no lock is a risk flag; banking apps change flows on it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A yes or no about your lock\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app checks device security Check if the phone is protected by a PIN, pattern, password or biometric Check if the screen is locked right now (Android) Use the answer to allow"},"158":{"title":"App Attest","description":"Cryptographic proof to a server that a request came from a genuine copy of the app on real Apple hardware.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/id-app-attest","content":"<Chips /> Can prove its requests come from the real app on a real iPhone Cryptographic proof to a server that a request came from a genuine copy of the app on real Apple hardware. Cuts bots and cloned apps; per-install key kept in the Secure Enclave. Can lock out jailbroken or modified devices. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Proof about the app, not you\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app signs in, buys, or sends something it wants to protect Create a key for this app install t"},"159":{"title":"Autofill (app side and service side)","description":"Lets the keyboard offer saved usernames, passwords, cards and addresses in app forms.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/id-autofill","content":"<Chips /> Can offer saved logins, cards and addresses in forms, only after you tap a suggestion Lets the keyboard offer saved usernames, passwords, cards and addresses in app forms. The autofill service sees which fields the app shows (labels, IDs) and can fill them. Legacy Android services read the whole view tree. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only what you choose to fill\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a username, password, card or address field and choose a suggestion Mark form fields so the k"},"160":{"title":"Credential provider role (third party password and passkey manager)","description":"An app registers as the person's password and passkey manager for the whole system.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/id-credential-provider","content":"<Chips /> Can act as your phone's password and passkey manager once you turn it on An app registers as the person's password and passkey manager for the whole system. The chosen manager can supply logins for every other app and site, and sees which services the person signs into. It is one of the most trusted roles on the phone. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Every login you store with it\" /> **What the user does:** You open Settings and choose this app as your passwords and passkeys provider Save pass"},"161":{"title":"DeviceCheck two bits","description":"Apple stores two bits per device per developer on its servers.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/id-devicecheck","content":"<Chips /> Can store two yes or no flags about your device on Apple's servers Apple stores two bits per device per developer on its servers. Lets a developer mark a device (for example, trial used) with 2 bits that persist across reinstall and device wipe; no other data. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Two flags that survive reinstall\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app's server asks Apple about your device Set or look up 2 bits on Apple's servers for this device and this develop"},"162":{"title":"Request an ID or credential from a wallet (verifier)","description":"Asks the phone's wallet for a verified age, name or license, with the person's consent.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/id-digital-credentials-verify","content":"<Chips /> Can ask your wallet to prove one fact, such as your age, once you approve Asks the phone's wallet for a verified age, name or license, with the person's consent. Shares only chosen attributes (age over 21, name, address) cryptographically signed by an issuer. Replaces ID photos and upload flows when it works. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One approved answer, one moment\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Verify my age or Verify my ID and approve the wallet prompt Ask the wallet for a specif"},"163":{"title":"Game services sign in (Play Games, Game Center)","description":"Signs the player into the platform game account for scores and saves.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/id-game-sign-in","content":"<Chips /> Can sign you in to your game account to save progress and post scores Signs the player into the platform game account for scores and saves. Gives a game a stable player ID, display name and friends list. It links gaming activity across titles for the same person. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your game profile only\" /> **What the user does:** You open a game and tap Sign in, or it signs you in on first launch Sign you in to Play Games or Game Center with the account already on the phone Show"},"164":{"title":"Hardware backed keys (Keystore and Secure Enclave)","description":"Creates cryptographic keys that never leave the phone's secure chip.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/id-hardware-keys","content":"<Chips /> Can make secret keys that stay locked inside your phone's security chip Creates cryptographic keys that never leave the phone's secure chip. A device-bound key proves that a request comes from one specific phone. The iOS Keychain can survive app deletion, which lets services recognise a returning device. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Keys only usable on this phone\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing, or you confirm with fingerprint or face when the app sets up secure sign in Create a key in "},"165":{"title":"Passkeys in an app (create and sign in)","description":"Creates and uses phishing resistant passkeys for sign in inside an app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/id-passkeys-app","content":"<Chips /> Can create and use passkeys inside the app after you approve with your device lock Creates and uses phishing resistant passkeys for sign in inside an app. Replaces passwords with device-held keys; an app learns only a public key, never the biometric. Passkeys sync through the platform keychain, so account recovery shifts to Google or Apple. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One approved sign in at a time\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Create a passkey or Sign in with a passkey, then confirm"},"166":{"title":"Play Integrity verdicts","description":"Google returns a signed verdict on the device, app, and account integrity.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/id-play-integrity","content":"<Chips /> Can ask Google whether the phone and app look genuine and unmodified Google returns a signed verdict on the device, app, and account integrity. Verdict levels (BASIC, DEVICE, STRONG) tell servers if a phone is rooted, an emulator, or running an altered app. STRONG (Android 13+) needs hardware-backed signals and a recent patch. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A verdict about the device and app\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you sign in, pay, or do something the app wants t"},"167":{"title":"Sign in with Google","description":"One tap sign in with a Google account that is already on the phone.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/id-sign-in-google","content":"<Chips /> Can sign you in with a Google account already on your phone after you tap One tap sign in with a Google account that is already on the phone. Links an app to the person's Google identity (email, name, photo). On Android it reads accounts from the phone through Google Play services without a separate permission. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only the account you choose\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in with Google and choose an account Show the Google accounts on the phone as a pick"},"168":{"title":"ID in Wallet (issue and provision)","description":"Adds a driver's license or passport as a digital ID to the phone wallet.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/id-wallet-id-issue","content":"<Chips /> Can add your driver's license or passport based ID to your phone's wallet Adds a driver's license or passport as a digital ID to the phone wallet. Puts a government ID inside the phone, so identity checks can be done by tapping or sharing from the Wallet app. Issuers and states choose when to launch. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One ID you choose to add\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Add to Wallet and scan your ID, then follow the issuer's checks Add a digital driver's license or passp"},"169":{"title":"Age range signal","description":"Asks the OS whether the user is in an age band, without giving a birth date.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/identity-age-range","content":"<Chips /> Can learn your age band, such as 13 to 15, but not your birth date Asks the OS whether the user is in an age band, without giving a birth date. Added to meet child safety laws. Both OSes return a range and a basis, not a date of birth. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"An age band, not a birth date\" /> **What the user does:** You approve sharing your age range, or a parent approves for a child Ask the phone for your age band (for example under 13, 13 to 15, or 18 and over) Learn whether a parent"},"170":{"title":"App Attest and Play Integrity","description":"Proves to a server that a request came from a genuine copy of the app on a genuine device.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/identity-app-attest","content":"<Chips /> Can prove its requests come from the real app on a genuine phone Proves to a server that a request came from a genuine copy of the app on a genuine device. Anti fraud tool with a privacy angle: it is a device level key, not a user id. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Proof about the app and phone\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you sign in, pay, or send something the app protects Get a signed statement that the app and device look genuine Sign requests with a key held in th"},"171":{"title":"Sign in with Apple and Credential Manager","description":"Account sign in that can hide the real email behind a relay address.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/identity-sign-in-with-apple","content":"<Chips /> Can sign you in with Apple and hide your real email if you choose Account sign in that can hide the real email behind a relay address. Apple required it where other social logins existed until January 2024. Android uses Credential Manager. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only what you choose to share\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in with Apple and choose Share or Hide My Email Create an account using your Apple ID with Face ID or Touch ID Get a steady user ID for your account Receiv"},"172":{"title":"Sign in and identity","description":"Face ID, passkeys, accounts and logins. 18 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth","content":"Face ID, passkeys, accounts and logins. 18 capabilities in this group. | Capability       | Kind          | In one line                                                        |\n| ---------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Age range signal | Gets silently | Can learn your age band, such as 13 to 15, but not your birth date | | Capability | Kind                 | In one line                                                    |\n| ---------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| App Attest | Works without asking | Can prove its requests come from the real app on a real iPhone | | Capability                    | Kind                 | In one line                                                      |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| App Attest and Play Integrity | Works without asking | Can prove its requests come from the real app on a genuine phone | | Capability                           | Kind                 | In one line                                                                           |\n| ------------------------------------ | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Autofill (app side and service side) | Works without asking | Can offer saved logins, cards and addresses in forms, only after you tap a suggestion | | Capability               | Kind                 | In one line                                                                |\n| ------------------------ | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Biometric authentication | Works without asking | Can ask the phone to check your fingerprint or face, without seeing either | | Capability                                   | Kind          | In one line                                                                  |\n| -------------------------------------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Biometric availability and enrollment change | Gets silently | Can check whether you set up fingerprint or face unlock, but not who you are | | Capability                                                          | Kind                 | In one line                                                              |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Credential provider role (third party password and passkey manager) | Works without asking | Can act as your phone's password and passkey manager once you turn it on | | Capability                     | Kind          | In one line                                                           |\n| ------------------------------ | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Device lock and passcode state | Gets silently | Can tell if your phone has a screen lock and whether it is locked now | | Capability           | Kind              | In one line                                                        |\n| -------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| DeviceCheck two bits | Used to track you | Can store two yes or no flags about your device on Apple's servers | | Capability                                      | Kind                 | In one line                                                           |\n| ----------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Game services sign in (Play Games, Game Center) | Works without asking | Can sign you in to your game account to save progress and post scores | | Capability                                         | Kind                 | In one line                                                             |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Hardware backed keys (Keystore and Secure Enclave) | Works without asking | Can make secret keys that stay locked inside your phone's security chip | | Capability                         | Kind                 | In one line                                                               |\n| ---------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| ID in Wallet (issue and provision) | Works without asking | Can add your driver's license or passport based ID to your phone's wallet | | Capability                                | Kind                 | In one line                                                             |\n| ----------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| LinkedIn ID verification (CLEAR, Persona) | Works without asking | Can check a government ID and a selfie through a partner inside the app | | Capability                              | Kind                 | In one line                                                                        |\n| --------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Passkeys in an app (create and sign in) | Works without asking | Can create and use passkeys inside the app after you approve with your device lock | | Capability              | Kind                 | In one line                                                          |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------- | -----------------------------------------------------------------"},"173":{"title":"LinkedIn ID verification (CLEAR, Persona)","description":"Checks a government ID and selfie through a partner inside the app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/linkedin-id-verification","content":"<Chips /> Can check a government ID and a selfie through a partner inside the app Checks a government ID and selfie through a partner inside the app. Links a real legal identity and face to the profile, then shows a verified badge. Vendor retention rules apply. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One scan, one moment\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Verify, take photos of your ID, and scan a selfie Use the camera to photograph your ID and your face Scan the chip in a passport or ID with NFC (Android and "},"174":{"title":"Biometric authentication","description":"Lets the app ask the system to verify the user with fingerprint or face.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/peripherals-biometric","content":"<Chips /> Can ask the phone to check your fingerprint or face, without seeing either Lets the app ask the system to verify the user with fingerprint or face. The app never sees the biometric; it gets a pass or fail. Face ID needs a purpose string. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A yes or no from the phone\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in, Approve payment, or Unlock and use your fingerprint or face Show the system fingerprint or face prompt Learn a yes or no answer after the check Fall back to"},"175":{"title":"Native apps (Android and iPhone)","description":"361 capabilities of installed apps, with Android and iPhone shown side by side.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile","content":"361 capabilities of installed apps, with Android and iPhone shown side by side. <Card title=\"Location and motion\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/\">Where you are and how you move. 14 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Camera, mic and screen\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/camera-mic-screen/\">Seeing, hearing and recording. 7 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Contacts and personal data\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/\">Your people, photos, calendar and more. 14 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Health and fitness\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/health-fitness/\">Steps, heart rate, workouts, health records. 7 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Sign in and identity\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/identity-auth/\">Face ID, passkeys, accounts and logins. 18 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Payments and shopping\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/payments-commerce/\">Paying, wallets and purchases. 9 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Notifications and background\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/\">Alerts and work that runs when the app is closed. 50 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Files and storage\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/files-storage/\">Saving, opening and keeping data. 19 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Hardware and accessories\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/\">Bluetooth, USB, NFC, controllers, sensors. 19 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Network and connection\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/\">Wi-Fi, cell signal, internet speed. 21 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Device details\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/device-system-profile/\">Model, memory, battery and other facts about the device. 24 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Screen and windows\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/display-window/\">Screen size, brightness, full screen, windows. 4 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Typing and clipboard\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/input-clipboard/\">Keyboard, copy and paste, touch and mouse. 4 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Language and settings\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/locale-preferences/\">Language, time zone, dark mode, text size. 9 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Speed and timing\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/timing-performance/\">How fast things load and run. 1 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"On-device AI\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/on-device-ai/\">AI that runs right on your device. 7 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Home screen and system links\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/\">Installing, widgets, shortcuts, sharing. 20 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"System control\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/\">Changing device settings and controlling other apps. 20 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Calls and texts\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/telephony-sms/\">Phone calls, SMS and carrier details. 9 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Other apps on the phone\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/app-ecosystem/\">Seeing and talking to other installed apps. 29 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Ads and install tracking\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/attribution-ads/\">Measuring which ad led to an install or sale. 37 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Tracking across apps and sites\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/cross-site-tracking/\">IDs and tricks that follow you around. 19 capabilities.</Card>"},"176":{"title":"Clipboard read","description":"Android shows a toast on read; iOS 16 asks Allow Paste; reading the change count is silent.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/input-clipboard/det-clipboard-read","content":"<Chips /> Can see what you copied while the app is open, and you may get a notice Android shows a toast on read; iOS 16 asks Allow Paste; reading the change count is silent. Clipboards hold passwords, 2FA codes and crypto addresses. Both OSes added visible signals so silent reads stop. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"What you last copied, while open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Paste, or the app checks your clipboard when it opens Look at the text or link you last copied while the app is in front"},"177":{"title":"Typing and clipboard","description":"Keyboard, copy and paste, touch and mouse. 4 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/input-clipboard","content":"Keyboard, copy and paste, touch and mouse. 4 capabilities in this group. | Capability              | Kind          | In one line                                                                       |\n| ----------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Clipboard access banner | Gets silently | Can't hide a clipboard check, because you see a banner or toast when an app looks | | Capability     | Kind          | In one line                                                             |\n| -------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Clipboard read | Gets silently | Can see what you copied while the app is open, and you may get a notice | | Capability                       | Kind           | In one line                                                            |\n| -------------------------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Paste permission and PasteButton | Asks you first | Can ask to paste from another app, and a paste button skips the prompt | | Capability                        | Kind                 | In one line                                                                            |\n| --------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Required Reason: active keyboards | Works without asking | Can list your keyboards on iPhone only for approved reasons; Android lists them freely |"},"178":{"title":"Clipboard access banner","description":"A banner or toast tells the person that an app read the clipboard.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/input-clipboard/input-clipboard-banner","content":"<Chips /> Can't hide a clipboard check, because you see a banner or toast when an app looks A banner or toast tells the person that an app read the clipboard. Transparency tool. Not a gate, but caught many apps reading on launch. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A warning, not a block\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The notice appears when an app looks at what you copied Show you a small notice when an app looks at your clipboard Show the source app name on iPhone in older versions Let you notice an"},"179":{"title":"Paste permission and PasteButton","description":"iOS asks when an app reads the clipboard from another app; a system paste button skips the prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/input-clipboard/input-paste-prompt","content":"<Chips /> Can ask to paste from another app, and a paste button skips the prompt iOS asks when an app reads the clipboard from another app; a system paste button skips the prompt. Added after apps were caught reading the clipboard on launch. Android has no prompt, only a toast. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One paste, only when you say\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Paste in a text box, or the app asks to look at the clipboard Ask to paste what you copied in another app (iPhone) Use the Paste but"},"180":{"title":"Required Reason: active keyboards","description":"iOS reading active input modes needs 3EC4.1 or 54BD.1.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/input-clipboard/rr-active-keyboards","content":"<Chips /> Can list your keyboards on iPhone only for approved reasons; Android lists them freely iOS reading active input modes needs 3EC4.1 or 54BD.1. Keyboard lists show languages and third-party keyboards. 3EC4.1 is for keyboard apps, 54BD.1 adjusts UI to active keyboards; neither may leave the device. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A list of installed keyboards\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app checks your keyboard setup Check the keyboards and input languages that are tu"},"181":{"title":"Language and settings","description":"Language, time zone, dark mode, text size. 9 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/locale-preferences","content":"Language, time zone, dark mode, text size. 9 capabilities in this group. | Capability                                 | Kind          | In one line                                                              |\n| ------------------------------------------ | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| 24-hour clock, calendar and number formats | Gets silently | Can see your 12 or 24 hour clock setting, calendar type and number style | | Capability                           | Kind          | In one line                                               |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Active keyboards and input languages | Gets silently | Can see which keyboards and input languages you turned on | | Capability          | Kind          | In one line                                         |\n| ------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| Dark mode and theme | Gets silently | Can see whether your phone is in dark or light mode | | Capability                         | Kind          | In one line                                                           |\n| ---------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Font size and Dynamic Type setting | Gets silently | Can see your text size setting, which may hint at age or vision needs | | Capability                 | Kind          | In one line                                                           |\n| -------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Installed and custom fonts | Gets silently | Can see which fonts your phone has, a small but telling device detail | | Capability                       | Kind          | In one line                                           |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| Language, region and locale list | Gets silently | Can see your language list and region, with no prompt | | Capability                                             | Kind          | In one line                                                                |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Reduce motion, contrast, color inversion and bold text | Gets silently | Can see whether Reduce Motion, high contrast, inversion or bold text is on | | Capability                                 | Kind          | In one line                                                             |\n| ------------------------------------------ | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Screen reader and assistive services flags | Gets silently | Can tell whether TalkBack, VoiceOver or similar helpers are switched on | | Capability | Kind          | In one line                                                   |\n| ---------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Timezone   | Gets silently | Can see your timezone and its offset from UTC, with no prompt |"},"182":{"title":"24-hour clock, calendar and number formats","description":"Reads 12 or 24 hour clock, first weekday, calendar type and number format.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/locale-preferences/loc-clock-calendar","content":"<Chips /> Can see your 12 or 24 hour clock setting, calendar type and number style Reads 12 or 24 hour clock, first weekday, calendar type and number format. Small habit signals that add fingerprint bits: 24 hour clock, Monday start, Hijri or Buddhist calendar. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A few display settings\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app shows dates, times or numbers Check for a 12 or 24 hour clock Find your calendar type and first day of the week Format numbers and"},"183":{"title":"Dark mode and theme","description":"Reads whether the system uses dark or light appearance.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/locale-preferences/loc-dark-mode","content":"<Chips /> Can see whether your phone is in dark or light mode Reads whether the system uses dark or light appearance. Weak habit signal; one bit, but often scheduled so it also hints at sunset time. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app opens or you switch themes Check if the system uses dark or light appearance Switch its own colors to match Change your phone's theme See your wallpaper, brightness or schedule Tell who you are on its o"},"184":{"title":"Reduce motion, contrast, color inversion and bold text","description":"Reads display accessibility toggles with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/locale-preferences/loc-display-accessibility","content":"<Chips /> Can see whether Reduce Motion, high contrast, inversion or bold text is on Reads display accessibility toggles with no prompt. A combination of 5 to 8 toggles gives a small fingerprint and hints at motion sensitivity or vision needs. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A handful of on or off settings\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app draws its screens Check if Reduce Motion is on and skip heavy animations Check for color inversion, bold text and similar toggles Adjust sc"},"185":{"title":"Font size and Dynamic Type setting","description":"Reads the system text size setting, which hints at age or low vision.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/locale-preferences/loc-font-scale","content":"<Chips /> Can see your text size setting, which may hint at age or vision needs Reads the system text size setting, which hints at age or low vision. Large text settings correlate with age and visual impairment, a sensitive inference the person never chose to share. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One size setting\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app lays out text Check how large your system text is Make its own text larger or smaller to match Use the size as one clue in a device"},"186":{"title":"Installed and custom fonts","description":"Lists fonts: Android reads the system font dir, iOS lists system families and installed profiles.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/locale-preferences/loc-installed-fonts","content":"<Chips /> Can see which fonts your phone has, a small but telling device detail Lists fonts: Android reads the system font dir, iOS lists system families and installed profiles. The set of fonts is a classic fingerprint. iOS 13 added user-installable fonts, so the list can differ per person. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A list of fonts\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app or page draws its text List the system fonts on the phone On iPhone, see fonts you installed yourself (iOS"},"187":{"title":"Active keyboards and input languages","description":"Lists enabled keyboards: open on Android, a Required Reason API on iOS.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/locale-preferences/loc-keyboards","content":"<Chips /> Can see which keyboards and input languages you turned on Lists enabled keyboards: open on Android, a Required Reason API on iOS. Third-party keyboards and extra input languages identify bilingual or non-native users and are a strong personalization fingerprint (Kurtz et al.). <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A list of installed keyboards\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app checks your keyboard setup List enabled keyboards and their languages on Android Check for a cust"},"188":{"title":"Language, region and locale list","description":"Reads the person's ordered language list and region with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/locale-preferences/loc-language-region","content":"<Chips /> Can see your language list and region, with no prompt Reads the person's ordered language list and region with no prompt. Tells native language, diaspora, expat status and often nationality. Apps localize with it; ad tech uses it for targeting and as a fingerprint bit. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Your language settings\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app or page loads Get your ordered list of languages (usually up to about 5) Find your region and country format Sho"},"189":{"title":"Screen reader and assistive services flags","description":"Tells whether TalkBack or VoiceOver, Switch Control and similar are running.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/locale-preferences/loc-screen-reader","content":"<Chips /> Can tell whether TalkBack, VoiceOver or similar helpers are switched on Tells whether TalkBack or VoiceOver, Switch Control and similar are running. Discloses disability: a special-category fact. Apps need it to adapt, but it is a sensitive inference and Play restricts using the Accessibility API for it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One setting per helper\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app draws or when you turn a helper on Check if a screen reader is on Check for "},"190":{"title":"Timezone","description":"Reads the timezone and UTC offset with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/locale-preferences/loc-timezone","content":"<Chips /> Can see your timezone and its offset from UTC, with no prompt Reads the timezone and UTC offset with no prompt. A coarse location proxy (about 400 IANA zones; usually the country or region) that survives a location permission denial. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A timezone name\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app shows dates and times Find your timezone name, such as America/Chicago See your offset from UTC in minutes Show times in your local zone See your exact loc"},"191":{"title":"Location and motion","description":"Where you are and how you move. 14 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion","content":"Where you are and how you move. 14 capabilities in this group. | Capability           | Kind           | In one line                                                                   |\n| -------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Activity recognition | Asks you first | Can tell if you walk, drive or cycle, and count steps, after you allow Motion | | Capability           | Kind          | In one line                                            |\n| -------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Ambient light sensor | Gets silently | Can measure room light on Android, but can't on iPhone | | Capability           | Kind           | In one line                                                  |\n| -------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Approximate location | Asks you first | Can see roughly where you are, in a region of a few to 20 km | | Capability          | Kind           | In one line                                                                    |\n| ------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Background location | Asks you first | Can follow your location when the app is closed, after you choose Always allow | | Capability             | Kind          | In one line                                                                 |\n| ---------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Barometer and altitude | Gets silently | Can measure air pressure and height change, after a Motion prompt on iPhone | | Capability                       | Kind                   | In one line                                                              |\n| -------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Location button (one time share) | You pick what to share | Can get your location once when you tap the Share button, with no prompt | | Capability                       | Kind          | In one line                                                                          |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Magnetometer and compass heading | Gets silently | Can use your phone's compass and magnetic field, with a location link for true north | | Capability                  | Kind           | In one line                                                                   |\n| --------------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Motion and fitness activity | Asks you first | Can see your steps and whether you walk, run, drive or cycle, after you allow | | Capability                       | Kind              | In one line                                                                     |\n| -------------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Motion sensors as a side channel | Used to track you | Can maybe pick up nearby speech or typing taps from motion sensors, in research | | Capability       | Kind           | In one line                                                        |\n| ---------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Precise location | Asks you first | Can see your exact GPS spot while you use the app, after you allow | | Capability       | Kind          | In one line                                                    |\n| ---------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Proximity sensor | Gets silently | Can tell if something is close to the screen, such as your ear | | Capability                      | Kind          | In one line                                            |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Raw accelerometer and gyroscope | Gets silently | Can see how your phone moves and tilts, with no prompt | | Capability                                | Kind              | In one line                                                                 |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Sensor calibration fingerprint (SensorID) | Used to track you | Can maybe recognize your phone from tiny sensor flaws, with no ID or prompt | | Capability                      | Kind          | In one line                                                          |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Sensor list and vendor metadata | Gets silently | Can list the sensors your phone has, with maker, range and precision |"},"192":{"title":"Activity recognition","description":"Asks to detect walking, driving, cycling and step counts.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/location-activity-recognition","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if you walk, drive or cycle, and count steps, after you allow Motion Asks to detect walking, driving, cycling and step counts. Motion classes reveal commutes, exercise habits and sleep schedules. On both OSes it is gated by a runtime prompt. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your movement, while allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow when the app asks for physical activity or motion access Detect walking, running, cycling and driving Count steps and walking distance Look back"},"193":{"title":"Approximate location","description":"Asks for a city-block level location instead of the exact spot.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/location-approximate","content":"<Chips /> Can see roughly where you are, in a region of a few to 20 km Asks for a city-block level location instead of the exact spot. Reveals the neighborhood but not the exact address. Useful for weather and local content; still shows commuting patterns over time. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A few kilometers, while in use\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow and choose Approximate location, or turn Precise off on iPhone Find your area within a few square kilometers on Android Find your region"},"194":{"title":"Background location","description":"Lets the app read location when it is closed or not on screen.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/location-background","content":"<Chips /> Can follow your location when the app is closed, after you choose Always allow Lets the app read location when it is closed or not on screen. Builds a continuous movement history: home, work, visits, trips. The strongest location grant, and the one platforms push users to avoid. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Everywhere you go, anytime\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Always Allow, or choose Allow all the time in Settings Keep your location updates running with the app closed Alert you whe"},"195":{"title":"Location button (one time share)","description":"A system button the person taps to share location once, with no prompt or settings.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/location-location-button","content":"<Chips /> Can get your location once when you tap the Share button, with no prompt A system button the person taps to share location once, with no prompt or settings. App gets one time precise location without long term permission. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One tap, one location\" /> **What the user does:** You tap the system Share Location button Get your location one time for this tap Use that spot to find nearby places or fill an address Do it with no permission prompt or settings trip on iPhone"},"196":{"title":"Motion and fitness activity","description":"Reads step counts and whether the person is walking, running, driving or cycling.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/location-motion-activity","content":"<Chips /> Can see your steps and whether you walk, run, drive or cycle, after you allow Reads step counts and whether the person is walking, running, driving or cycling. Reveals daily routine, commute mode and exercise habits, and works as a coarse movement log even without location. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your movement, while allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow for Motion and Fitness (iPhone) or Physical activity (Android) Count your steps and walking distance Tell whether you wa"},"197":{"title":"Raw accelerometer and gyroscope","description":"Reads raw motion sensor streams with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/location-motion-sensors-raw","content":"<Chips /> Can see how your phone moves and tilts, with no prompt Reads raw motion sensor streams with no prompt. Accelerometer and gyro readings can infer taps, typing, gait and a device-specific calibration fingerprint. Both OSes cap or restrict background use. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Motion data, while the app runs\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a game, map or app uses motion Get accelerometer and gyroscope readings (tilt and shake) Use motion for games, steps and compass"},"198":{"title":"Precise location","description":"Asks for the device's exact GPS position while the app is in use.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/location-precise","content":"<Chips /> Can see your exact GPS spot while you use the app, after you allow Asks for the device's exact GPS position while the app is in use. Reveals where a person is to within meters: home, work, routines, travel. Android's fine and iOS's precise grant are the most sensitive runtime permissions after background access. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Exact spot, while the app is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow While Using the App with Precise on Find your exact position within a few me"},"199":{"title":"Ambient light sensor","description":"Android reads lux with no prompt; iOS has no public ambient light API.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/sensor-ambient-light","content":"<Chips /> Can measure room light on Android, but can't on iPhone Android reads lux with no prompt; iOS has no public ambient light API. Shows whether the person is indoors, outdoors or in the dark. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Light level, Android only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. On Android it happens when the app uses auto brightness or a light meter feature Measure the light level around an Android phone, up to about 40000 lux Adjust screen colors or brightness to match the room (Android) "},"200":{"title":"Barometer and altitude","description":"Reads air pressure; iOS needs Motion and Fitness permission for relative altitude.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/sensor-barometer","content":"<Chips /> Can measure air pressure and height change, after a Motion prompt on iPhone Reads air pressure; iOS needs Motion and Fitness permission for relative altitude. Pressure reveals floor in a building and weather; combined with time it can aid location inference. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Pressure and height change\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing on Android. On iPhone you tap Allow for Motion and Fitness Measure air pressure in hectopascals (Android) or kilopascals (iPhone) Track relative"},"201":{"title":"Sensor calibration fingerprint (SensorID)","description":"Per-device factory calibration in accelerometer and gyro data identifies a phone with no ID API.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/sensor-calibration","content":"<Chips /> Can maybe recognize your phone from tiny sensor flaws, with no ID or prompt Per-device factory calibration in accelerometer and gyro data identifies a phone with no ID API. Factory calibration error is unique per unit. SensorID read about 42 bits on an iPhone 6S, survives resets and uninstalls, and also hit some Pixel phones. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"A hidden ID that follows the phone\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when an app or page collects motion sensor data Collect"},"202":{"title":"Motion sensors as a side channel","description":"Motion sensors can pick up nearby speech and typing taps.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/sensor-eavesdrop","content":"<Chips /> Can maybe pick up nearby speech or typing taps from motion sensors, in research Motion sensors can pick up nearby speech and typing taps. Gyroscope and accelerometer pick up speaker vibrations and taps, letting a research app recover digits or speech with no mic permission. This is why Android 12 capped the rate. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Side channel, mostly lab results\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when an app or page collects fast motion data Collect motion data at a"},"203":{"title":"Sensor list and vendor metadata","description":"Lists the sensors the phone has, with vendor, resolution and range.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/sensor-list-metadata","content":"<Chips /> Can list the sensors your phone has, with maker, range and precision Lists the sensors the phone has, with vendor, resolution and range. The set of sensors with vendor and power numbers differs by model and firmware and is a stable device identifier. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A list of hardware details\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app or game checks what hardware it can use List all sensors on Android, often 20 to 60 See each sensor's maker, resolution and ran"},"204":{"title":"Magnetometer and compass heading","description":"Reads magnetic field and heading; iOS true heading uses location services.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/sensor-magnetometer","content":"<Chips /> Can use your phone's compass and magnetic field, with a location link for true north Reads magnetic field and heading; iOS true heading uses location services. Reveals orientation and nearby magnetic objects (cars, metal), and is used for indoor positioning. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Compass and field, while in use\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing, or you allow location when a compass wants true north on iPhone Measure the magnetic field in microtesla Show compass heading toward magne"},"205":{"title":"Proximity sensor","description":"Android reads the distance; iOS gives only a near or far boolean.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/location-motion/sensor-proximity","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if something is close to the screen, such as your ear Android reads the distance; iOS gives only a near or far boolean. Shows the phone is at the ear or in a pocket. Low value as an ID. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Near or far\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you hold the phone to your ear on a call or voice message Check if the phone is near your face Turn off the screen during calls On some Android phones, get a distance in centimeters Get a distance on iPhone"},"206":{"title":"Network and connection","description":"Wi-Fi, cell signal, internet speed. 21 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity","content":"Wi-Fi, cell signal, internet speed. 21 capabilities in this group. | Capability                | Kind          | In one line                                                       |\n| ------------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Carrier name, MCC and MNC | Gets silently | Can see your carrier name and country code on Android, not iPhone | | Capability                          | Kind              | In one line                                                                     |\n| ----------------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Cedexis Radar (network measurement) | Used to track you | Can maybe measure how fast your connection is, as found inside LinkedIn Android | | Capability                         | Kind          | In one line                                                   |\n| ---------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Cellular radio technology (4G, 5G) | Gets silently | Can tell if you are on 3G, 4G or 5G, with a prompt on Android | | Capability                     | Kind           | In one line                                                               |\n| ------------------------------ | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Connected Wi-Fi name and BSSID | Asks you first | Can see your Wi-Fi name, but only with location access and extra approval | | Capability         | Kind             | In one line                                                                |\n| ------------------ | ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Device MAC address | Blocked for apps | Can't see your phone's real hardware address; apps get a fixed placeholder | | Capability      | Kind           | In one line                                                                        |\n| --------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Internet access | Asks you first | Can use the internet, which Android grants at install and iPhone allows by default | | Capability                      | Kind                 | In one line                                                             |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Join or suggest a Wi-Fi network | Works without asking | Can add a Wi-Fi network to your phone, usually to set up a smart device | | Capability                         | Kind          | In one line                                                                          |\n| ---------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Local IP addresses and DNS servers | Gets silently | Can see your phone's home network address and DNS servers, with limits in Android 17 | | Capability           | Kind           | In one line                                                             |\n| -------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Local network access | Asks you first | Can talk to devices on your home or office network, after you tap Allow | | Capability           | Kind                 | In one line                                                                |\n| -------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Multicast networking | Works without asking | Can send and receive shared messages to many devices on your local network | | Capability           | Kind           | In one line                                                                |\n| -------------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Nearby Wi-Fi devices | Asks you first | Can find and connect to nearby Wi-Fi devices without needing your location | | Capability                 | Kind          | In one line                                                                  |\n| -------------------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Nearby Wi-Fi networks scan | Gets silently | Can list nearby Wi-Fi networks on Android, with limits and a location prompt | | Capability    | Kind          | In one line                                                      |\n| ------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Network state | Gets silently | Can see if your phone is online and what kind of network it uses | | Capability                                  | Kind          | In one line                                                           |\n| ------------------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Network type, metered and constrained flags | Gets silently | Can tell if you are on Wi-Fi, cellular or VPN, and if data is metered | | Capability        | Kind          | In one line                                                                              |\n| ----------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Public IP address | Gets silently | Can't avoid showing your public address to any server it contacts, which hints at a city | | Capability                         | Kind             | In one line                                                    |\n| ---------------------------------- | ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Saved Wi-Fi networks and passwords | Blocked for apps | Can't see the Wi"},"207":{"title":"Cedexis Radar (network measurement)","description":"Real-user network measurement SDK found in LinkedIn Android builds.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/linkedin-cedexis-radar","content":"<Chips /> Can maybe measure how fast your connection is, as found inside LinkedIn Android Real-user network measurement SDK found in LinkedIn Android builds. Measures latency and throughput from the phone to many servers, building a map of network performance by carrier and place. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Connection speed, unverified use\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It may happen while the app is open and loading content Time how fast the network loads from different servers Send speed me"},"208":{"title":"Carrier name, MCC and MNC","description":"Android reads carrier and country codes with no prompt; iOS now returns placeholders.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/net-carrier","content":"<Chips /> Can see your carrier name and country code on Android, not iPhone Android reads carrier and country codes with no prompt; iOS now returns placeholders. Reveals the person's carrier, home country and roaming. Apple deprecated CTCarrier in iOS 16 and returns static values. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Carrier and country, Android only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app checks your network Get your carrier name on Android Find your mobile country and network codes (MC"},"209":{"title":"Local IP addresses and DNS servers","description":"Reads the phone's LAN addresses and DNS servers; Android 17 adds a local network permission.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/net-local-ip","content":"<Chips /> Can see your phone's home network address and DNS servers, with limits in Android 17 Reads the phone's LAN addresses and DNS servers; Android 17 adds a local network permission. LAN IP and DNS often reveal the router and network type; local network access also lets apps scan other devices at home (50 Ways to Leak Your Data). <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Home network info, with limits\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing, or you tap Allow when the app asks to find devices on your network See "},"210":{"title":"Network type, metered and constrained flags","description":"Reads whether the phone is on Wi-Fi, cellular or VPN, and if the connection is metered.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/net-network-type","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if you are on Wi-Fi, cellular or VPN, and if data is metered Reads whether the phone is on Wi-Fi, cellular or VPN, and if the connection is metered. Tells whether the person is at home or out, and whether data saving is on. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"The kind of connection\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app loads or before it downloads something big Check if the phone uses Wi-Fi, cellular, Ethernet, VPN or Bluetooth Check if the connection is metered or "},"211":{"title":"System proxy settings","description":"Reads the HTTP proxy configured on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/net-proxy","content":"<Chips /> Can see if your phone sends web traffic through a proxy Reads the HTTP proxy configured on the device. Shows corporate or debugging proxies and can indicate traffic inspection. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Proxy settings\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app opens a network connection See the proxy host, port and any PAC script address Route its traffic through the same proxy Spot a work or school network setup See the traffic that goes through the proxy Change your p"},"212":{"title":"Public IP address","description":"Any server the app contacts sees the public IP, which can be matched to a city.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/net-public-ip","content":"<Chips /> Can't avoid showing your public address to any server it contacts, which hints at a city Any server the app contacts sees the public IP, which can be matched to a city. IP gives city-level location and ISP, with no prompt. Apple's Private Relay and Google's IP Protection hide it for some traffic. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Public address, with every connection\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens whenever the app connects to the internet Let every server see your public IP addr"},"213":{"title":"Cellular radio technology (4G, 5G)","description":"Reads whether the phone is on LTE, 5G or 3G.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/net-radio-type","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if you are on 3G, 4G or 5G, with a prompt on Android Reads whether the phone is on LTE, 5G or 3G. Shows connection quality and device capability; low sensitivity. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"The cellular type\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow for Phone or Phone state on Android; iPhone shows nothing See whether the phone is on 3G, 4G LTE or 5G Check each SIM on iPhone with dual SIM Adapt video quality or downloads Get the exact radio type on Android 11 and newer without Ph"},"214":{"title":"VPN active detection","description":"Android shows a VPN flag with no prompt; iOS offers only indirect hints.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/net-vpn","content":"<Chips /> Can see if a VPN is on, directly on Android and by guessing on iPhone Android shows a VPN flag with no prompt; iOS offers only indirect hints. Reveals whether the person hides their IP or uses a corporate VPN; apps use it for fraud and geo-blocking decisions. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Whether a VPN is on\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app checks the connection Check a VPN flag on Android Guess a VPN from iPhone network hints Block or limit features when a VPN is"},"215":{"title":"Wi-Fi signal strength and link speed","description":"Android reads signal and link speed with no prompt; iOS gives signal strength only with the Wi-Fi entitlement.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/net-wifi-link","content":"<Chips /> Can see Wi-Fi signal strength and speed on Android; iPhone needs special approval Android reads signal and link speed with no prompt; iOS gives signal strength only with the Wi-Fi entitlement. Gives a rough distance from the router and speed tier. Can be used with BSSID to locate the person inside a home. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Signal and speed of your Wi-Fi\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing on Android. On iPhone only approved apps can do it Get signal strength in dBm on Android See"},"216":{"title":"Nearby Wi-Fi networks scan","description":"Android lists nearby access points with location permission and throttling; iOS has no public scan.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/net-wifi-scan","content":"<Chips /> Can list nearby Wi-Fi networks on Android, with limits and a location prompt Android lists nearby access points with location permission and throttling; iOS has no public scan. The list of nearby BSSIDs locates a phone within meters indoors and enables positioning, which is why scans are location-gated and throttled. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Nearby Wi-Fi list, with limits\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow for Location or Nearby devices when the app asks to scan Wi-Fi List nearby"},"217":{"title":"Join or suggest a Wi-Fi network","description":"Adds a Wi-Fi network to the phone, usually to onboard a smart device.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/network-hotspot-configuration","content":"<Chips /> Can add a Wi-Fi network to your phone, usually to set up a smart device Adds a Wi-Fi network to the phone, usually to onboard a smart device. Changes network state, so both OSes ask the person. Apple needs a capability and Android needs a normal permission. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One network, with your approval\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect or Set up device and approve the Wi-Fi join prompt Suggest or add a Wi-Fi network by name and password Join a device's setup Wi-Fi o"},"218":{"title":"Internet access","description":"Lets the app use the network; granted automatically on Android.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/network-internet","content":"<Chips /> Can use the internet, which Android grants at install and iPhone allows by default Lets the app use the network; granted automatically on Android. Lets an app send any data off the device. The permission is auto-granted so the real controls are network policies and user data toggles. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Open internet, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens whenever the app loads or sends something Send and receive data over Wi-Fi or cellular Load pages, sync acco"},"219":{"title":"Local network access","description":"Asks to talk to devices on the home or office network.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/network-local-network","content":"<Chips /> Can talk to devices on your home or office network, after you tap Allow Asks to talk to devices on the home or office network. Lets apps discover printers, TVs and smart-home devices and also map every device on the network. iOS has prompted since 14; Android adds it in 17. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Devices on your local network\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow when the app asks to find devices on your local network Find smart TVs, printers and speakers on your network Send data"},"220":{"title":"Multicast networking","description":"Sends and receives multicast and broadcast traffic on the local network.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/network-multicast","content":"<Chips /> Can send and receive shared messages to many devices on your local network Sends and receives multicast and broadcast traffic on the local network. Needed for custom device discovery; also a way to enumerate LAN devices, so Apple gates it by approval. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Local network broadcasts\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing, or you use device discovery or casting inside the app Listen for broadcast announcements on the local network Find devices that announce themselves (pri"},"221":{"title":"Nearby Wi-Fi devices","description":"Lets the app find and connect to nearby Wi-Fi devices without needing location.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/network-nearby-wifi","content":"<Chips /> Can find and connect to nearby Wi-Fi devices without needing your location Lets the app find and connect to nearby Wi-Fi devices without needing location. Reveals nearby access points and peers; the neverForLocation flag declares the app does not derive location. Also the interim grant for Android 16 local network protection. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Nearby devices, while in use\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow for Nearby devices when the app asks Find nearby devices for Wi-Fi "},"222":{"title":"Network state","description":"Lets the app see whether the device is online and what kind of network.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/network-network-state","content":"<Chips /> Can see if your phone is online and what kind of network it uses Lets the app see whether the device is online and what kind of network. Shows online status and network type; slightly useful for fingerprinting. Auto-granted. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Online status and type\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app loads or the network changes Check if the phone is online See the network type, metered state and rough speed estimate Notice Low Data Mode on iPhone React w"},"223":{"title":"Wi-Fi Aware peer discovery","description":"Finds and talks to nearby devices directly over Wi-Fi, with no router.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/network-wifi-aware","content":"<Chips /> Can find and talk to nearby devices directly over Wi-Fi, with no router Finds and talks to nearby devices directly over Wi-Fi, with no router. A broad nearby radio. Android treats it as a nearby devices permission and iOS 26 adds a system pairing flow. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Nearby devices, with approval\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow for Nearby devices or approve pairing in the system sheet Find nearby phones and gadgets directly Pair through a system pairing screen on iPh"},"224":{"title":"Connected Wi-Fi name and BSSID","description":"Reads the name and ID of the Wi-Fi network the phone is on, which gives away place.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/network-wifi-info","content":"<Chips /> Can see your Wi-Fi name, but only with location access and extra approval Reads the name and ID of the Wi-Fi network the phone is on, which gives away place. SSID and BSSID are lookup keys for geolocation databases, so apps need location permission to read them. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your Wi-Fi name, with location\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow Location when the app asks to see your Wi-Fi See the Wi-Fi name (SSID) and BSSID on Android after Location is allowed See the curr"},"225":{"title":"Wi-Fi state","description":"Lets the app see Wi-Fi status; the network name needs location permission.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/network-wifi-state","content":"<Chips /> Can see if Wi-Fi is on, and its name only with location access Lets the app see Wi-Fi status; the network name needs location permission. SSID and BSSID can place a person at home or work. Android 8.1 and later hide them without location permission. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Wi-Fi status, name needs location\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing for on or off. You tap Allow Location for the Wi-Fi name Check whether Wi-Fi is connected See the network name after Location is allowed (Android "},"226":{"title":"Device MAC address","description":"Blocked: apps see a fixed placeholder; Wi-Fi uses a randomized MAC per network.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/nx-mac-address","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your phone's real hardware address; apps get a fixed placeholder Blocked: apps see a fixed placeholder; Wi-Fi uses a randomized MAC per network. A MAC is a permanent hardware ID. Android returns 02:00:00:00:00:00 since 6.0 and randomizes per network since 10; iOS 14 added Private Wi-Fi Address. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked, a placeholder only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Apps may try when they set up networking, but it fails See a placeholder address (02:00:00:00:0"},"227":{"title":"Saved Wi-Fi networks and passwords","description":"Blocked: apps cannot list the networks the phone has joined before.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/network-connectivity/nx-saved-wifi","content":"<Chips /> Can't see the Wi-Fi networks or passwords your phone has saved Blocked: apps cannot list the networks the phone has joined before. Past networks reveal places the person has been. Android 10 removed list access; iOS never exposed it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked, own networks only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Apps may try during network setup, but it fails See only networks the app itself suggested See an empty list on Android 10 and newer See only networks the app set up it"},"228":{"title":"Background audio and media playback","description":"Apps can keep playing sound with the screen off; Android 17 adds new limits for apps that target it.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-background-audio","content":"<Chips /> Can keep playing sound with the screen off, with new limits in Android 17 Apps can keep playing sound with the screen off; Android 17 adds new limits for apps that target it. Used for music, podcasts and navigation prompts. Android 17 hardens background audio playback, focus and volume so quiet apps cannot hijack the speaker. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Sound while the app is hidden\" /> **What the user does:** You start music, a podcast or a call, then lock your phone or switch apps Keep a"},"229":{"title":"Background downloads and uploads","description":"A system service finishes downloads and uploads for the app even after it is closed.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-background-url-session","content":"<Chips /> Can finish downloads and uploads for the app even after you close it A system service finishes downloads and uploads for the app even after it is closed. Lets apps move large files without staying alive. The transfer survives termination but not a force quit, and the system picks the timing. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"File transfers while closed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Download or Upload, then leave the app Continue large downloads and uploads while the app is closed Wait for "},"230":{"title":"Bluetooth Low Energy in the background","description":"Apps can keep talking to Bluetooth devices when closed, with strict scan rules on iOS.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-ble-background","content":"<Chips /> Can keep talking to Bluetooth gadgets with the app closed, with strict scan rules Apps can keep talking to Bluetooth devices when closed, with strict scan rules on iOS. Used by wearables, trackers and beacons. iOS wakes apps for known peripherals; Android needs a foreground service or a companion device grant to scan reliably. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Bluetooth gadgets, even when closed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow for Bluetooth and connect a device, then leave the app Stay"},"231":{"title":"Doze and idle mode","description":"When the phone is still and unplugged, Android delays jobs, alarms and network to save battery.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-doze","content":"<Chips /> Can't force the phone to stay awake; Doze delays jobs, alarms and network When the phone is still and unplugged, Android delays jobs, alarms and network to save battery. Explains why background trackers go quiet at night. Apps get brief maintenance windows; only high priority push, exact alarms and foreground services run freely. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Limited by battery saving\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the phone sits still and unplugged Check if the phone i"},"232":{"title":"Why the process ended (exit reasons)","description":"Apps can read why they were killed: Android ApplicationExitInfo, iOS MetricKit exit metrics.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-exit-reasons","content":"<Chips /> Can learn why the system last ended the app, such as low memory Apps can read why they were killed: Android ApplicationExitInfo, iOS MetricKit exit metrics. Lets apps and SDKs learn whether the system, the user or a crash ended a session. Android 17 adds a memory limiter reason; it also exposes behavior to the person's device. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"About this app only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens the next time the app starts or a report arrives List recent reasons "},"233":{"title":"Expedited and user-initiated long jobs","description":"Jobs that start soon or run long after a user action: Android expedited and UIDT, iOS 26 continued processing.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-expedited-uidt","content":"<Chips /> Can run jobs soon or for a long time after you tap something, with daily limits Jobs that start soon or run long after a user action: Android expedited and UIDT, iOS 26 continued processing. These are the sanctioned ways to finish work the person started (upload, export). They need a visible progress surface, so apps can no longer quietly continue large jobs. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Long jobs you started\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Export, Upload or Process and the app keeps wo"},"234":{"title":"Background start restrictions","description":"Apps in the background cannot freely start foreground services or open screens.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-fgs-start-restrictions","content":"<Chips /> Can't freely start work or open screens from the background Apps in the background cannot freely start foreground services or open screens. Stops hidden activity from popping up or starting long tasks while the person is elsewhere. iOS goes further: apps can only launch for a few triggers, like location or push. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked unless an exemption applies\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when an app tries to start a service or screen while hidden Start a "},"235":{"title":"Foreground service timeouts (Android 15)","description":"Some foreground services now stop after 6 hours in 24, and can only restart after a user returns to the app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-fgs-timeouts","content":"<Chips /> Can keep some Android services running, but only about 6 hours in 24 Some foreground services now stop after 6 hours in 24, and can only restart after a user returns to the app. A hard cap on long background syncs and media processing. It limits always-running services; Android 17 adds memory and audio limits on top. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Hard daily time limit on Android 15\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when an app runs a long sync or media job in the background Kee"},"236":{"title":"Foreground service types (Android 14)","description":"Every foreground service must declare a purpose and hold the matching permission.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-fgs-types","content":"<Chips /> Can run long Android background tasks, but only for a declared purpose with a notification Every foreground service must declare a purpose and hold the matching permission. Forces apps to state why they keep running (location, camera, media, phone call). The type decides which hardware and permissions the service can use, so it shows purpose to reviewers. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, but tied to a stated purpose\" /> **What the user does:** You start something long, like playing mus"},"237":{"title":"Force stop and force quit","description":"A person can kill an app: Android force stop cancels alarms; iOS force quit stops background fetch and silent push.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-force-stop","content":"<Chips /> Can't keep running or wake itself after you force stop (Android) or swipe it away (iPhone) A person can kill an app: Android force stop cancels alarms; iOS force quit stops background fetch and silent push. Marks the clearest signal that someone does not want an app active. Both systems then block background starts until the person opens the app again. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Stopped until you open it\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Force stop in Android Settings, or swipe the app "},"238":{"title":"iOS background modes","description":"Xcode background modes (audio, location, VoIP, Bluetooth, fetch, push) decide what can run while closed.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-ios-modes","content":"<Chips /> Can run in the background on iPhone, but only for the kind of job Apple approved Xcode background modes (audio, location, VoIP, Bluetooth, fetch, push) decide what can run while closed. Each mode is a narrow exemption tied to a visible use. Using one for something else is an App Review rejection, so the list shows what a company claims to need. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, but only for approved jobs\" /> **What the user does:** You start playback, a navigation trip, a call, or conne"},"239":{"title":"iOS app refresh and processing tasks","description":"iOS lets apps request short refreshes (about 30 s) or longer idle processing; timing is up to the system.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-ios-refresh-processing","content":"<Chips /> Can ask iPhone for short refreshes of about 30 seconds, but the system picks the time iOS lets apps request short refreshes (about 30 s) or longer idle processing; timing is up to the system. The only system scheduled background work on iOS. Timing follows how often the person uses the app; dormant installs may never run. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Short bursts, timed by the system\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when iOS decides to wake the app, often after you use it a l"},"240":{"title":"Low Power Mode, Battery Saver and Background App Refresh state","description":"Apps can see whether the phone is in a power saving state and adapt, and iOS shows if background refresh is on.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-low-power-state","content":"<Chips /> Can see if your phone is saving power, and slow itself down to match Apps can see whether the phone is in a power saving state and adapt, and iOS shows if background refresh is on. Reveals whether the person is low on battery or has background activity disabled. Apps use it to defer work; it also hints at device habits. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A simple on or off check\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks this when it opens or before it starts background work Tell if Low "},"241":{"title":"OEM background killers","description":"Many Android makers add extra killers that stop apps beyond what Google's rules allow.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-oem-killers","content":"<Chips /> Can't count on running in the background on some Android phones, since brands add extra killers Many Android makers add extra killers that stop apps beyond what Google's rules allow. Explains why push and alarms fail on some phones: aggressive task killers, autostart lists and battery managers. Developers ask users to whitelist; no API can force it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Varies by phone brand\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The phone's maker stops the app on its own after you le"},"242":{"title":"Region monitoring and geofences","description":"Wakes the app when the device enters or leaves a circle: 20 regions on iOS, 100 geofences on Android.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-region-monitoring","content":"<Chips /> Can wake the app when you enter or leave set places, up to 20 on iPhone Wakes the app when the device enters or leaves a circle: 20 regions on iOS, 100 geofences on Android. Lets a retailer know when someone arrives at a store or leaves home. The caps force apps to rotate regions, and the OS keeps them even after the app is closed. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, but only for set places\" /> **What the user does:** You turn on place reminders, home arrival alerts, or a store offer feat"},"243":{"title":"Restricted bucket and battery restriction settings","description":"The user can set an app to Unrestricted, Optimized or Restricted; unused apps can fall into Restricted.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-restricted-battery-ui","content":"<Chips /> Can only run in the background as much as your battery setting allows The user can set an app to Unrestricted, Optimized or Restricted; unused apps can fall into Restricted. The strongest user lever over background activity. Restricted means one 10 minute job session a day, almost no alarms, and no network; people rarely change it, so defaults shape reach. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only what your battery setting allows\" /> **What the user does:** You open Settings, then Battery, and set "},"244":{"title":"Significant location change","description":"A low power way to learn that the device moved a long distance, even when the app is closed.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-significant-location","content":"<Chips /> Can learn that you moved a long way, about 500 meters, even with the app closed A low power way to learn that the device moved a long distance, even when the app is closed. Wakes the app after the person moves about 500 meters or changes cell towers, so it can rebuild a movement history cheaply. iOS relaunches apps for it; Android uses geofences and activity transitions. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, but only big moves\" /> **What the user does:** You allow Always location, or turn o"},"245":{"title":"App Standby Buckets","description":"Android sorts apps into five buckets that cap job time, alarms and network by how recently they were used.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-standby-buckets","content":"<Chips /> Can't run as much in the background if you rarely open the app Android sorts apps into five buckets that cap job time, alarms and network by how recently they were used. Rarely opened apps get tiny background budgets, so a retention team sees its sync silently slow down for dormant users. Active apps in the top bucket have almost none. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Limits grow tighter if unused\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Android sorts apps into groups by how recently you opened the"},"246":{"title":"Visit monitoring","description":"iOS reports the places a person stays at (arrival and departure) without constant GPS.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-visit-monitoring","content":"<Chips /> Can learn which places you stay at, with arrival and leave times, on iPhone iOS reports the places a person stays at (arrival and departure) without constant GPS. Infers home, work and frequent stops with low power. The app gets coarse arrival and departure times and places, a daily routine map. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, but only places you stay\" /> **What the user does:** You allow Always location for a feature that keeps a place history or journal Learn about stops, like home,"},"247":{"title":"Deferrable background work (WorkManager, JobScheduler)","description":"The standard way to run background tasks; periodic work cannot repeat faster than every 15 minutes.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/bg-workmanager-jobs","content":"<Chips /> Can run background jobs on Android, but not more often than every 15 minutes The standard way to run background tasks; periodic work cannot repeat faster than every 15 minutes. Defines how often a background sync can run for an idle install. The 15 minute minimum and bucket quotas make exact timing impossible, so apps lean on push for freshness. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, but limited and delayed\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app sets up a sync, upload o"},"248":{"title":"Notifications and background","description":"Alerts and work that runs when the app is closed. 50 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background","content":"Alerts and work that runs when the app is closed. 50 capabilities in this group. | Capability           | Kind           | In one line                                                                   |\n| -------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Alarms and reminders | Asks you first | Can set alarms at an exact time on Android, if you allow Alarms and reminders | | Capability                                            | Kind                 | In one line                                                                |\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Android Live Updates (promoted ongoing notifications) | Works without asking | Can show a live progress card on your Android lock screen, if you allow it | | Capability                                             | Kind                 | In one line                                                                |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| APNs delivery: payload, priority, expiration, collapse | Works without asking | Can send alerts to an iPhone through Apple, up to 4 KB of data per message | | Capability           | Kind                 | In one line                                            |\n| -------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| App icon badge count | Works without asking | Can show a number on the app icon, if you allow badges | | Capability          | Kind                 | In one line                                                    |\n| ------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| App Standby Buckets | Works without asking | Can't run as much in the background if you rarely open the app | | Capability                | Kind           | In one line                                                                  |\n| ------------------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Auto-granted exact alarms | Asks you first | Can set exact alarms by itself on Android, if it is an alarm or calendar app | | Capability                          | Kind                 | In one line                                                               |\n| ----------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Background audio and media playback | Works without asking | Can keep playing sound with the screen off, with new limits in Android 17 | | Capability                       | Kind                 | In one line                                                          |\n| -------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Background downloads and uploads | Works without asking | Can finish downloads and uploads for the app even after you close it | | Capability                    | Kind                 | In one line                                                 |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Background start restrictions | Works without asking | Can't freely start work or open screens from the background | | Capability                             | Kind                 | In one line                                                                       |\n| -------------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Bluetooth Low Energy in the background | Works without asking | Can keep talking to Bluetooth gadgets with the app closed, with strict scan rules | | Capability                                    | Kind                 | In one line                                                    |\n| --------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Communication notifications and conversations | Works without asking | Can show who a message is from, with their photo, in the alert | | Capability      | Kind                 | In one line                                                                           |\n| --------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Critical alerts | Works without asking | Can ring through Do Not Disturb, Focus and the mute switch, if Apple approves the app | | Capability                                             | Kind                 | In one line                                                                  |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Deferrable background work (WorkManager, JobScheduler) | Works without asking | Can run background jobs on Android, but not more often than every 15 minutes | | Capability         | Kind                 | In one line                                                               |\n| ------------------ | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Doze and idle mode | Works without asking | Can't force the phone to stay awake; Doze delays jobs, alarms and network | | Capability                             | Kind                 | In one line                                                                     |\n| -------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Ex"},"249":{"title":"Android Live Updates (promoted ongoing notifications)","description":"Android 16's progress-style notification that sits on the lock screen and status bar chip.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-android-live-updates","content":"<Chips /> Can show a live progress card on your Android lock screen, if you allow it Android 16's progress-style notification that sits on the lock screen and status bar chip. Gives rideshare, navigation and delivery apps a persistent live surface. Google limits it to specific styles and says it is not for ads, chat or package tracking. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While a task is running\" /> **What the user does:** You start a ride, delivery, timer or navigation, and the app shows a Live Update Show"},"250":{"title":"APNs delivery: payload, priority, expiration, collapse","description":"Apple's push channel: 4 KB payload (5 KB VoIP), priority 10 or 5, optional expiry and collapse id.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-apns-delivery","content":"<Chips /> Can send alerts to an iPhone through Apple, up to 4 KB of data per message Apple's push channel: 4 KB payload (5 KB VoIP), priority 10 or 5, optional expiry and collapse id. Apple holds only the latest push per collapse id and can drop or delay low priority ones. The company learns delivery only as best effort, so apps pair pushes with a fetch on open. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, whenever the app's server sends\" /> **What the user does:** You allow notifications when the app asks,"},"251":{"title":"Silent (background) push","description":"A push with no alert that wakes the app in the background; Apple asks for 2 or 3 per hour at most.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-apns-silent-push","content":"<Chips /> Can wake the app quietly with a push, but only a few times an hour A push with no alert that wakes the app in the background; Apple asks for 2 or 3 per hour at most. Lets a server trigger a data refresh without the person seeing anything, and also reveals whether the install is still alive. Apple throttles it, so it is a nudge, not a reliable timer. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, but rate limited\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app's server sends it, and you see nothing on s"},"252":{"title":"App icon badge count","description":"Shows an unread number on the app icon.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-badge","content":"<Chips /> Can show a number on the app icon, if you allow badges Shows an unread number on the app icon. A constant nudge to re-open the app. iOS needs alert-style permission for badges; Android launchers derive badges from active notifications, not from a number the app can set. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A single number on the icon\" /> **What the user does:** You allow notifications and the Badges switch when the app asks Show a count of unread items on the icon Clear or change the count when you"},"253":{"title":"Notification channels and categories","description":"Splits alerts into types the person can silence one by one.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-channels-categories","content":"<Chips /> Can sort its alerts into types you can silence one by one Splits alerts into types the person can silence one by one. Gives control over each kind of message (promotions vs receipts). It also stops an app turning off everything: a person can mute marketing and keep security alerts. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only what you switch on\" /> **What the user does:** You open the app's notification settings and switch a type on or off Split alerts into types, like messages, promotions and reminde"},"254":{"title":"Communication notifications and conversations","description":"Shows who a message is from with their photo, tied to the system's contact graph.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-communication-notifications","content":"<Chips /> Can show who a message is from, with their photo, in the alert Shows who a message is from with their photo, tied to the system's contact graph. Links alerts to a person so Focus can allow only certain people through. It also feeds the system's idea of who the user talks to; apps declare the intent for each message. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only the people in your messages\" /> **What the user does:** You allow notifications and get a message from someone in your contacts Show the sender"},"255":{"title":"FCM message delivery: payload, TTL, collapse","description":"Google's push channel: 4 KB payload, up to 4 weeks held, delivered through Play services.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-fcm-delivery","content":"<Chips /> Can send alerts to an Android phone through Google, up to 4 KB per message Google's push channel: 4 KB payload, up to 4 weeks held, delivered through Play services. A server can nudge an Android install with small data. Limits shape what a marketer can do: little payload, and queued messages expire or collapse, so apps fetch the real content after the nudge. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, whenever the app's server sends\" /> **What the user does:** You allow notifications on Android 1"},"256":{"title":"FCM priority and deprioritization","description":"High priority pushes wake a sleeping phone, but Android downgrades apps that abuse them.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-fcm-priority","content":"<Chips /> Can wake a sleeping Android phone with a high priority push, but misuse gets slowed down High priority pushes wake a sleeping phone, but Android downgrades apps that abuse them. High priority is the only way to reach a dozing phone fast. Apps that send high priority with no visible notification get demoted over a 7 day window, so engagement bots must show something. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, but downgraded if abused\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app's server picks the"},"257":{"title":"Live Activities and Dynamic Island","description":"Pins a live status (delivery, score, ride) to the lock screen and Dynamic Island for up to 8 hours.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-live-activities","content":"<Chips /> Can pin live status to your iPhone lock screen for up to 8 hours Pins a live status (delivery, score, ride) to the lock screen and Dynamic Island for up to 8 hours. A glanceable always-on channel that shows where a person's order or ride is. It runs a long way under the OS: 8 hours active, then up to 4 more on the lock screen. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While running, up to 8 hours\" /> **What the user does:** You start a ride, order, game or timer, and the app starts a Live Activity Show "},"258":{"title":"Live Activity push updates and budget","description":"Updates a Live Activity by push; priority 10 pushes count against an hourly budget.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-liveactivity-push","content":"<Chips /> Can update a Live Activity by push, but important updates count against an hourly budget Updates a Live Activity by push; priority 10 pushes count against an hourly budget. Lets a server start and update a lock screen widget while the app is closed. Apple budgets pushes, so heavy tracking apps must justify a frequent-updates entitlement. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Updates while an activity is live\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app's server sends updates while your Live Activity"},"259":{"title":"Ongoing and foreground service notifications","description":"A notification the person cannot easily swipe away that shows background work is running.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-ongoing-foreground","content":"<Chips /> Can show a notification you cannot easily swipe away while background work runs A notification the person cannot easily swipe away that shows background work is running. It makes background activity visible. Android 14 let people swipe most of these away, but the Task Manager (Android 13+) still lists the foreground app. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While work runs, visible to you\" /> **What the user does:** You start something long, like music, a download or navigation Show an ongoing aler"},"260":{"title":"Push token registration (FCM token, APNs device token)","description":"Gets an address that lets a server reach this app install, even when the app is closed.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-push-token-registration","content":"<Chips /> Can get an address so its server can reach your app, even when closed Gets an address that lets a server reach this app install, even when the app is closed. The token ties one install to a server-side profile and lets the company wake or message the person at any time. It rotates on reinstall, restore or data clear, so servers use it to detect churn. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"A stable address for this install\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app opens and registe"},"261":{"title":"Rich notification media attachments","description":"Attaches images, audio or video to an iOS alert: 10 MB, 5 MB and 50 MB limits.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-rich-media","content":"<Chips /> Can attach pictures, sound or video to an iPhone alert, within size limits Attaches images, audio or video to an iOS alert: 10 MB, 5 MB and 50 MB limits. Fetched media can reveal when and where an alert was shown through the request. Android notifications show images via BigPictureStyle with no published size cap. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One attachment per alert\" /> **What the user does:** You get a push with a photo, clip or audio attached Show an image up to 10 MB, like JPEG, GIF or "},"262":{"title":"Notification service extension and mutable content","description":"A small helper that edits an iOS push for about 30 seconds before it is shown.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-service-extension","content":"<Chips /> Can edit an iPhone push for about 30 seconds before you see it A small helper that edits an iOS push for about 30 seconds before it is shown. Lets a company decrypt message text, fetch images and personalize on the phone. Android does the same inside the messaging service; both give short windows to run code. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A short edit before the alert shows\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a push arrives and before the alert shows Download a picture or at"},"263":{"title":"Notification summary, grouping and cooldown","description":"The OS decides when and how alerts are bundled: iOS summary, Android groups and cooldown.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-summary-grouping","content":"<Chips /> Can't decide alone when your alerts arrive; the system groups them and holds some back The OS decides when and how alerts are bundled: iOS summary, Android groups and cooldown. Controls how often a person is nudged. Apps can only hint (relevance score, group keys); the system may hold alerts for a daily digest or mute rapid ones. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"The system decides what you see\" /> **What the user does:** You pick apps and times in Notification Summary on iPhone, or use adaptive"},"264":{"title":"Time Sensitive and DND-bypass alerts","description":"Marks an alert urgent enough to break through Focus or Do Not Disturb for an hour.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-time-sensitive","content":"<Chips /> Can mark an alert urgent so it breaks through Focus for about an hour Marks an alert urgent enough to break through Focus or Do Not Disturb for an hour. A way past the person's quiet time. iOS gates it with a capability and lets users switch it off per app; Android gates the equivalent behind Do Not Disturb access. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Urgent alerts only, if you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You allow Time Sensitive in iPhone Settings, or give Do Not Disturb access on Android Sh"},"265":{"title":"Uninstall detection through push failures","description":"Servers learn an app was removed when the push provider reports the token as invalid.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-uninstall-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can find out the app was removed, but only after hours or days Servers learn an app was removed when the push provider reports the token as invalid. A company can tell, days later, which people deleted the app and then retarget them in ads or email. No on-device code runs after uninstall; the signal is the push service reply. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A late hint that you removed it\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app's server learns it when a push fails Learn the app was remove"},"266":{"title":"VoIP push (PushKit) and call-style wakeups","description":"A special push that wakes the app for a call; iOS demands it shows a call UI right away.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notif-voip-push","content":"<Chips /> Can wake the app right away for a call, if it shows a call screen A special push that wakes the app for a call; iOS demands it shows a call UI right away. Used for real-time calls, the strongest wake-up either platform offers. Misuse for silent tracking got apps cut off on iOS 13, so both platforms now tie it to a visible call screen. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Wakes for calls only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when someone calls you through the app Wake the app for an i"},"267":{"title":"Ignore battery optimization","description":"Asks to be exempt from Doze and app standby.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notifications-battery-optimization","content":"<Chips /> Can ask to keep running in the background on Android, only if you agree Asks to be exempt from Doze and app standby. Lets the app run in the background more freely at the cost of battery. The user confirms with a dialog. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, but only after your OK\" /> **What the user does:** A system box asks if the app may run in the background and you tap Allow Ask you to turn off battery limits for the app Stay awake longer in the background once you allow it Use less st"},"268":{"title":"Run at startup","description":"Lets the app start in the background after the phone reboots.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notifications-boot-completed","content":"<Chips /> Can start itself in the background after your Android phone restarts Lets the app start in the background after the phone reboots. Lets apps resume tasks after reboot. Android 15 restricts launching certain foreground services from boot receivers. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Starts after every restart\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you restart the phone Start up again after a reboot to restore alarms, syncs or reminders Run light setup work once the phone is on Be all"},"269":{"title":"Alarms and reminders","description":"Lets the app fire alarms at an exact time.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notifications-exact-alarm","content":"<Chips /> Can set alarms at an exact time on Android, if you allow Alarms and reminders Lets the app fire alarms at an exact time. Wakes the phone at precise times. Revoking it cancels all future exact alarms and stops the app. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only when you allow it\" /> **What the user does:** You turn on the Alarms and reminders switch in Settings, or set an alarm in the app Fire an alarm or reminder at an exact minute Work again after you allow it, since Android tells the app about the"},"270":{"title":"Foreground service","description":"Lets the app run long tasks with a visible notification.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notifications-foreground-service","content":"<Chips /> Can run long tasks on Android with a visible notification, but only for a stated purpose Lets the app run long tasks with a visible notification. A visible, user-aware background task such as navigation or a call. Types and time limits keep abuse in check. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Long tasks with a notification\" /> **What the user does:** You start something long, like a download, a workout or a file sync Keep a long task going while the app is closed, with a notification showing Declar"},"271":{"title":"Full screen intent","description":"Lets a notification take over the screen like an incoming call or alarm.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notifications-full-screen-intent","content":"<Chips /> Can take over your Android screen for a call or alarm, only if you allow it Lets a notification take over the screen like an incoming call or alarm. Interrupts the user with a full-screen UI. Limited to calling and alarm apps on Android 14 and later. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only calls and alarms, unless you allow\" /> **What the user does:** A call comes in, or an alarm goes off, while your phone is locked Open a full screen alert for an incoming call or alarm Show it even when the phon"},"272":{"title":"Live Updates and Live Activities","description":"Shows a live, ongoing status on the lock screen and status bar.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notifications-live-updates","content":"<Chips /> Can show a live status on your lock screen, if you allow it for the app Shows a live, ongoing status on the lock screen and status bar. Live status cards for rides, deliveries and timers. They keep the app visible in prime screen space and are limited to defined use cases. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While a task is running\" /> **What the user does:** You start a ride, order, timer or trip and the app shows a live card Show live progress on the lock screen and status bar Use the Dynamic Is"},"273":{"title":"Notifications","description":"Asks to show alerts, banners and badges from the app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notifications-post-notifications","content":"<Chips /> Can show alerts, banners and badges, but only after you allow it Asks to show alerts, banners and badges from the app. Gives the app a direct line to the person's lock screen. Abuse is spam; legitimate uses are messages, delivery updates and security codes. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only after you say yes\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow when the app asks to send notifications Show alerts, banners, sounds and badges once you allow Ask at a good moment; apps for Android 13 or new"},"274":{"title":"Auto-granted exact alarms","description":"Auto-granted exact alarm access for alarm and calendar apps.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notifications-use-exact-alarm","content":"<Chips /> Can set exact alarms by itself on Android, if it is an alarm or calendar app Auto-granted exact alarm access for alarm and calendar apps. Same capability as the exact alarm grant but granted at install and not user-revocable. Play restricts it to alarm and calendar apps. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, set at install\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Alarm and calendar apps get this when you install them Fire alarms at exact times without a switch in Settings Run alarm clock featur"},"275":{"title":"Wake lock","description":"Keeps the CPU or screen awake.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notifications-wake-lock","content":"<Chips /> Can keep your phone's processor awake on Android, or the screen on iPhone Keeps the CPU or screen awake. Prevents sleep and drains battery. Android flags apps that hold long wake locks in Android vitals. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While a task is running\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens during a download, a video, a workout or a call Keep the screen on during a video or recipe on iPhone Keep the processor running so a task can finish on Android Be allowed at install without"},"276":{"title":"Critical alerts","description":"A notification that rings through Do Not Disturb, Focus and the mute switch.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notify-critical-alerts","content":"<Chips /> Can ring through Do Not Disturb, Focus and the mute switch, if Apple approves the app A notification that rings through Do Not Disturb, Focus and the mute switch. For emergencies like glucose alarms. Powerful enough that Apple demands justification. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only for the rare urgent alert\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow on a separate Critical Alerts prompt, or give Do Not Disturb access on Android Sound an alert even when Focus or the mute switch is on Warn you"},"277":{"title":"Provisional notification delivery","description":"Delivers notifications quietly without asking first, for the person to keep or turn off.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notify-provisional-authorization","content":"<Chips /> Can send quiet notifications without asking first, which you can keep or turn off Delivers notifications quietly without asking first, for the person to keep or turn off. Inverts the prompt: app earns trust by showing value first. Android 13 prompts but defaults channels. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Quiet alerts only, until you choose\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Your first notification shows up quietly in Notification Center Deliver alerts to Notification Center on iPhone with no "},"278":{"title":"Push to Talk","description":"Walkie talkie voice channels that start in the background from a push.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/notifications-background/notify-push-to-talk","content":"<Chips /> Can start walkie talkie voice from the background on iPhone, with microphone consent Walkie talkie voice channels that start in the background from a push. Gives apps a system managed channel to open the microphone from a push, with a clear UI. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While you talk in a channel\" /> **What the user does:** You open a channel and hold to talk, or someone starts talking to you Wake the app by push to start a live voice channel on iPhone Show a system screen that names th"},"279":{"title":"Image generation (Image Playground)","description":"Creates stylised images on device from prompts.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/on-device-ai/ai-image-gen","content":"<Chips /> Can offer picture creation through Apple's Image Playground sheet, and sees only the result Creates stylised images on device from prompts. iOS offers Image Playground and Genmoji through a system sheet. Android has no equivalent first party app API for images. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One picture you choose\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a button like Create image, then describe what you want in the Apple sheet Open Apple's Image Playground sheet inside the app Receive the finishe"},"280":{"title":"On-device language model (Foundation Models, Gemini Nano Prompt API)","description":"Runs a small language model on the phone with no cloud call.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/on-device-ai/ai-llm-prompt","content":"<Chips /> Can use a small AI model on your phone, with no internet needed Runs a small language model on the phone with no cloud call. Apps can prompt a system model for text, structured output and tool calls. Both are free and offline but only on eligible devices, and the models are small. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While you use the feature\" /> **What the user does:** You tap an AI feature like Summarize or Rewrite Answer, summarize or rewrite text on the phone Work offline and for free on iPhone"},"281":{"title":"On-device ML runtime (Core ML, LiteRT)","description":"Runs trained models on the phone's neural hardware.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/on-device-ai/ai-ml-runtime","content":"<Chips /> Can run trained AI models on your phone's own chip Runs trained models on the phone's neural hardware. Developers ship models with the app and run them on CPU, GPU or the neural chip. NNAPI is deprecated in favour of LiteRT, which targets vendor accelerators. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the feature runs\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens inside the app when you use a smart feature Run models for photos, speech or text using the phone's neural hardware Work offline Use fa"},"282":{"title":"Assistant screen context","description":"Lets the assistant see what is on screen to answer questions.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/on-device-ai/ai-screen-context","content":"<Chips /> Can see your screen when you call the assistant, if it is your chosen one Lets the assistant see what is on screen to answer questions. The system can capture the screen, or a camera view, and hand it to an assistant. Apps can opt out of capture on Android and iOS. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only when you ask the assistant\" /> **What the user does:** You set an assistant as default, or use Circle to Search or Visual Intelligence Look at the screen content to answer a question about it Sha"},"283":{"title":"System writing tools (Writing Tools, ML Kit Summarization and Rewriting)","description":"Offers rewrite, proofread and summarise inside any text field.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/on-device-ai/ai-text-tools","content":"<Chips /> Can offer rewrite, proofread and summarize inside text boxes, if Apple Intelligence is on Offers rewrite, proofread and summarise inside any text field. Writing Tools appear automatically in standard text views on iOS. On Android, apps call task APIs for summarize, proofread, rewrite and describe image. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only the text you select\" /> **What the user does:** You select text and tap Writing Tools, or an app's own rewrite button Rewrite, proofread or summarize text y"},"284":{"title":"On-device translation","description":"Translates text on the phone.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/on-device-ai/ai-translation","content":"<Chips /> Can translate text on your phone, after you download a language pack Translates text on the phone. Apps can translate with downloaded language packs. iOS 18 added the Translation framework; ML Kit offers the same on Android. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only the text you give it\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Translate and choose the languages Translate text into another language without the internet Use packs of about 30 MB per language on Android Translate calls and chats live with A"},"285":{"title":"Text recognition and visual look up (Live Text, Visual Look Up, ML Kit)","description":"Reads text in images and scans documents on device.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/on-device-ai/ai-visual-lookup-ocr","content":"<Chips /> Can pick out text in pictures and scan documents on your phone Reads text in images and scans documents on device. Apps can recognise text, scan pages and identify objects in photos. Visual Look Up and Live Text work inside the system apps with no code. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only what you scan or open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Scan document, point the camera at text, or open a photo Pick out the words in a photo or live camera view Scan a document without the camera permiss"},"286":{"title":"On-device AI","description":"AI that runs right on your device. 7 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/on-device-ai","content":"AI that runs right on your device. 7 capabilities in this group. | Capability               | Kind                 | In one line                                                               |\n| ------------------------ | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Assistant screen context | Works without asking | Can see your screen when you call the assistant, if it is your chosen one | | Capability                          | Kind                 | In one line                                                                                 |\n| ----------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Image generation (Image Playground) | Works without asking | Can offer picture creation through Apple's Image Playground sheet, and sees only the result | | Capability                                                           | Kind                 | In one line                                                     |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| On-device language model (Foundation Models, Gemini Nano Prompt API) | Works without asking | Can use a small AI model on your phone, with no internet needed | | Capability                             | Kind                 | In one line                                        |\n| -------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| On-device ML runtime (Core ML, LiteRT) | Works without asking | Can run trained AI models on your phone's own chip | | Capability            | Kind                 | In one line                                                          |\n| --------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| On-device translation | Works without asking | Can translate text on your phone, after you download a language pack | | Capability                                                               | Kind                 | In one line                                                                               |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| System writing tools (Writing Tools, ML Kit Summarization and Rewriting) | Works without asking | Can offer rewrite, proofread and summarize inside text boxes, if Apple Intelligence is on | | Capability                                                              | Kind                 | In one line                                                    |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Text recognition and visual look up (Live Text, Visual Look Up, ML Kit) | Works without asking | Can pick out text in pictures and scan documents on your phone |"},"287":{"title":"Install source and installer package","description":"Apps can see which store installed them.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/det-install-source","content":"<Chips /> Can see which store installed it, like Google Play or a side loaded file Apps can see which store installed them. Shows sideloaded, beta or store installs. Used for fraud and update messaging. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A single fact about itself\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app starts up Tell if it came from Google Play or another source on Android 11 or newer Check this to decide what to show you Learn a rough clue about the install source on iPhone See which"},"288":{"title":"App install and update time","description":"Android gives exact install and update timestamps; iOS gives them only indirectly.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/det-install-time","content":"<Chips /> Can see when it was installed and last updated, with exact times on Android Android gives exact install and update timestamps; iOS gives them only indirectly. Install time pins when a person got an app, and (with install referrer) which campaign. Android values are exact to the millisecond. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Two dates about itself\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app starts up Check the exact install and update times on Android Estimate the install date on"},"289":{"title":"Home screen and system links","description":"Installing, widgets, shortcuts, sharing. 20 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install","content":"Installing, widgets, shortcuts, sharing. 20 capabilities in this group. | Capability                                        | Kind                 | In one line                                                                    |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Alternative app marketplaces and web distribution | Works without asking | Can install apps from stores other than the main one, only in the EU on iPhone | | Capability                      | Kind                 | In one line                                                   |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Android Auto Backup and restore | Works without asking | Can save up to 25 MB of app data to your Google account daily | | Capability                                             | Kind                 | In one line                                                                          |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| App actions for assistants (App Intents, AppFunctions) | Works without asking | Can let Siri, Shortcuts or Gemini run actions inside the app, with your confirmation | | Capability                   | Kind                 | In one line                                                               |\n| ---------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| App archiving and offloading | Works without asking | Can remove an unused app but keep your data so a reinstall brings it back | | Capability                  | Kind          | In one line                                                                 |\n| --------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| App install and update time | Gets silently | Can see when it was installed and last updated, with exact times on Android | | Capability                              | Kind                          | In one line                                                                        |\n| --------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Apple notarization and app review gates | Does something on your device | Can install apps from outside the App Store in the EU and Japan after Apple checks | | Capability                         | Kind                 | In one line                                                            |\n| ---------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Block Store and credential restore | Works without asking | Can keep a small sign in token that follows you to a new Android phone | | Capability                         | Kind                 | In one line                                                                           |\n| ---------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Custom keyboards and input methods | Works without asking | Can replace the on screen keyboard, but only after you add it and approve its warning | | Capability                                | Kind                 | In one line                                                        |\n| ----------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Default browser and browser engine choice | Works without asking | Can open your web links, after you pick it as your default browser | | Capability                                          | Kind                 | In one line                                                                |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Default mail, navigation, dialer and messaging apps | Works without asking | Can open your email, maps, calls and texts, once you set it as the default | | Capability             | Kind                 | In one line                                                                        |\n| ---------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Developer verification | Works without asking | Can only install on many Android phones if the developer is registered with Google | | Capability                        | Kind                 | In one line                                                                 |\n| --------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| iCloud backup and key value store | Works without asking | Can save the app's folder to iCloud and keep 1 MB of values after uninstall | | Capability                           | Kind          | In one line                                                              |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Install source and installer package | Gets silently | Can see which store installed it, like Google Play or a side loaded file | | Capability           | Kind           | In one line                                                                     |\n| -------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Install unknown apps | Asks you first | Can install other apps from files on Android, only if you allow it for that "},"290":{"title":"Developer verification","description":"Android checks that sideloaded apps come from a registered developer.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/install-developer-verification","content":"<Chips /> Can only install on many Android phones if the developer is registered with Google Android checks that sideloaded apps come from a registered developer. Adds identity checks for apps outside Google Play on certified devices in four markets from Sep 30, 2026; global rollout planned for 2027 and later. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A check before the install\" /> **What the user does:** You open an app file from outside the store and try to install it Install apps from verified developers on ce"},"291":{"title":"Install unknown apps","description":"Lets the app install other apps from APK files.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/install-install-packages","content":"<Chips /> Can install other apps from files on Android, only if you allow it for that app Lets the app install other apps from APK files. The gateway for sideloaded malware. Android makes users approve each source app; Play restricts the permission. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only per app, after you allow it\" /> **What the user does:** You open an app file and switch on Install unknown apps for the app that opened it Install an app from a file after you allow it for that source Show you a system co"},"292":{"title":"Alternative app marketplaces and web distribution","description":"Installs apps from stores other than the official one.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-alt-marketplaces","content":"<Chips /> Can install apps from stores other than the main one, only in the EU on iPhone Installs apps from stores other than the official one. Android always allowed other sources. iOS added regional alternative marketplaces and web distribution in the EU (17.4), then Japan (26.2), but each still needs Apple authorisation or notarization. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only stores you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You open the other store's page and confirm the install on the system sheet Install "},"293":{"title":"App archiving and offloading","description":"Both systems can remove an unused app but keep its data, so a reinstall restores the old state.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-app-archiving","content":"<Chips /> Can remove an unused app but keep your data so a reinstall brings it back Both systems can remove an unused app but keep its data, so a reinstall restores the old state. Android 15 archiving and iOS Offload Unused Apps free storage while keeping user data. A later reinstall looks like a returning user, not a new one. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Data stays, the app is gone\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Archive or Offload Unused Apps, or the phone does it when storage is low Keep your "},"294":{"title":"App actions for assistants (App Intents, AppFunctions)","description":"Lets Siri, Shortcuts or Gemini run actions inside an app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-app-intents","content":"<Chips /> Can let Siri, Shortcuts or Gemini run actions inside the app, with your confirmation Lets Siri, Shortcuts or Gemini run actions inside an app. An app exposes verbs and data to the system assistant, so a voice request can act without opening the app. Modern assistants can chain these across apps. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only actions you ask for\" /> **What the user does:** You ask Siri or Gemini to do something, or tap an action in Shortcuts or Spotlight Run an action in the app by voice"},"295":{"title":"Launcher shortcuts and App Intents","description":"Apps add quick actions to the icon menu and expose actions to voice and automation.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-app-shortcuts","content":"<Chips /> Can add quick actions to the icon menu, and offer actions to voice and automation Apps add quick actions to the icon menu and expose actions to voice and automation. A long press on the icon shows a few actions; iOS App Intents also let Siri, Spotlight and Shortcuts run app actions. This puts app features into system surfaces. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only taps you choose\" /> **What the user does:** You long press the app icon, or use Siri and Shortcuts suggestions Show about 4 or 5 qui"},"296":{"title":"Android Auto Backup and restore","description":"Android copies up to 25 MB of app data to Google Drive about once a day and restores it at the next install.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-auto-backup","content":"<Chips /> Can save up to 25 MB of app data to your Google account daily Android copies up to 25 MB of app data to Google Drive about once a day and restores it at the next install. A reinstall or new phone can bring old app data back, including identifiers the developer stored. Android 16 QPR2 adds transfer of some data to iOS. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"A daily copy of app data\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when Android backup is on and the phone is idle Back up app settings and "},"297":{"title":"Block Store and credential restore","description":"Google Play services can hold a small token that follows a person to a new phone or a reinstall.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-block-store","content":"<Chips /> Can keep a small sign in token that follows you to a new Android phone Google Play services can hold a small token that follows a person to a new phone or a reinstall. Lets an app restore sign-in without a password. The data is end to end encrypted when a screen lock is set and can survive a reinstall; iOS has no direct equivalent apart from iCloud Keychain. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"A small token that follows you\" /> **What the user does:** You set up a new phone or reinstall the app an"},"298":{"title":"Custom keyboards and input methods","description":"Replaces the on-screen keyboard.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-custom-keyboards","content":"<Chips /> Can replace the on screen keyboard, but only after you add it and approve its warning Replaces the on-screen keyboard. A keyboard sees every keystroke. iOS sandboxes it and needs a separate Full Access toggle for network; Android IMEs have broader reach but show a risk warning. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Everything you type, if you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You open Settings and switch on a new keyboard, then accept the warning Type for you in any normal text field once you switch"},"299":{"title":"Default browser and browser engine choice","description":"Becomes the app that opens web links.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-default-browser","content":"<Chips /> Can open your web links, after you pick it as your default browser Becomes the app that opens web links. The default browser sees every tapped link. Android has long allowed any browser; iOS added it in 14 and the EU and Japan added choice screens and engine freedom. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Every web link, once you choose\" /> **What the user does:** You choose a browser in your default apps settings or on a choice screen Open web links you tap in other apps Use its own browser engine o"},"300":{"title":"Default mail, navigation, dialer and messaging apps","description":"Changes which app opens email, maps, calls, texts and passwords.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-default-nav-mail","content":"<Chips /> Can open your email, maps, calls and texts, once you set it as the default Changes which app opens email, maps, calls, texts and passwords. Android has had roles for dialer and SMS for years. iOS added default mail and browser in 14, then maps, messaging, calling, passwords and translation in later releases. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Every link of that kind, if chosen\" /> **What the user does:** You accept a role request dialog, or pick a default in Settings Open email links, map links, "},"301":{"title":"iCloud backup and key value store","description":"iCloud backs up the app container and offers a 1 MB key value store that survives uninstall.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-icloud-backup-kvs","content":"<Chips /> Can save the app's folder to iCloud and keep 1 MB of values after uninstall iCloud backs up the app container and offers a 1 MB key value store that survives uninstall. Documents and Application Support are in iCloud backup unless excluded; NSUbiquitousKeyValueStore keeps up to 1 MB and 1024 keys that sync across the person's devices. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"A regular copy of the app's folder\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when iCloud Backup is on and the phone is char"},"302":{"title":"Keychain across uninstall","description":"On iOS, small secrets stored in the Keychain stay on the device after the app is deleted and come back on reinstall.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-keychain-persistence","content":"<Chips /> Can keep small secrets on your iPhone that survive after you delete the app On iOS, small secrets stored in the Keychain stay on the device after the app is deleted and come back on reinstall. Developers use this to keep an anonymous id or login across reinstalls. It is a durable device level identifier unless the person wipes or restores to a new device. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"A stable ID that follows reinstalls\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you delete the app "},"303":{"title":"Apple notarization and app review gates","description":"Apple scans apps outside the App Store before install in the EU and Japan.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-notarization","content":"<Chips /> Can install apps from outside the App Store in the EU and Japan after Apple checks Apple scans apps outside the App Store before install in the EU and Japan. Apps distributed through alternative marketplaces or the web still pass Apple checks for malware and platform rules. The checks do not review content. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A check before the install\" /> **What the user does:** You install a marketplace or web app and see a sheet with the developer's name Install apps that Apple"},"304":{"title":"Share sheet and share extensions","description":"Receives or sends content through the system share menu.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-share-sheet","content":"<Chips /> Can send or receive content through the share menu, after you pick where it goes Receives or sends content through the system share menu. Any app can offer to take shared files or text. The system shows suggested people, so the app can learn who the sender usually shares with. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only what you share, one time\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Share and choose an app or person Send a link, picture or file to another app Receive things other apps share with it Show"},"305":{"title":"Sideloading and Android developer verification","description":"Android will require every installed app's developer to be verified starting 2026.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-sideloading-verification","content":"<Chips /> Can install apps from outside the store on Android, but the developer must be verified Android will require every installed app's developer to be verified starting 2026. Apps from any source on certified devices must be tied to a verified developer. A one time advanced flow lets experienced users install unverified apps. ADB installs are exempt. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A check before the install\" /> **What the user does:** You try to install an app file, and Android checks who made it "},"306":{"title":"System search index (Core Spotlight, AppSearch)","description":"Puts app content into system search.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-spotlight","content":"<Chips /> Can add its own content to your phone's search, like items, chats or notes Puts app content into system search. Apps feed items to the OS index so people can find them outside the app. The index stays on device but can reveal what content the app holds. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only the content the app adds\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app adds items; you see them when you search the phone Show app items in Spotlight and Siri Suggestions on iPhone Add searchable items throug"},"307":{"title":"What uninstall deletes and what survives","description":"Uninstall wipes app storage on both systems, but some data survives in backups, the cloud or the keychain.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/os-integration-install/os-uninstall-data-deletion","content":"<Chips /> Can't keep most data after uninstall, though backups, cloud and keychain may restore some Uninstall wipes app storage on both systems, but some data survives in backups, the cloud or the keychain. Residual data is how a company recognises a returning user. Survivors: iOS Keychain, iCloud data, Android backups, shared media and server records. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Local data goes, some data survives\" /> **What the user does:** You uninstall the app from Settings or delete it from the"},"308":{"title":"Payments and shopping","description":"Paying, wallets and purchases. 9 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/payments-commerce","content":"Paying, wallets and purchases. 9 capabilities in this group. | Capability                                  | Kind                 | In one line                                                                             |\n| ------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Contactless payments and NFC secure element | Works without asking | Can act as a contactless card or key on Android freely, but iPhone needs Apple approval | | Capability                      | Kind                 | In one line                                                             |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Default contactless payment app | Works without asking | Can answer when you tap to pay, after you pick it as the default wallet | | Capability                                          | Kind                 | In one line                                                                          |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| EU DMA alternative payments, links and distribution | Works without asking | Can offer other payment providers and web links in the EU, with a clear screen first | | Capability                                 | Kind                 | In one line                                                               |\n| ------------------------------------------ | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| In-app purchase (StoreKit 2, Play Billing) | Works without asking | Can sell digital items and subscriptions, after you approve each purchase | | Capability                                      | Kind                 | In one line                                                                  |\n| ----------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Japan MSCA payments and distribution (Dec 2025) | Works without asking | Can offer other app stores, payment options and web links to buyers in Japan | | Capability                                            | Kind                 | In one line                                                                |\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Tap to Pay (accept contactless payments on the phone) | Works without asking | Can accept tap payments from cards and wallets on the phone, once approved | | Capability                                                    | Kind                 | In one line                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| US external links and alternative billing (post Epic rulings) | Works without asking | Can send you to outside web checkout in the US, with fees still being settled | | Capability                                        | Kind                 | In one line                                                                        |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Wallet passes (boarding passes, tickets, loyalty) | Works without asking | Can add tickets, boarding passes and loyalty cards to your wallet when you tap Add | | Capability                                   | Kind                 | In one line                                                                    |\n| -------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Wallet payment sheet (Apple Pay, Google Pay) | Works without asking | Can charge you through Apple Pay or Google Pay without seeing your card number |"},"309":{"title":"Default contactless payment app","description":"The person picks which wallet app answers when they tap to pay.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/payments-commerce/pay-contactless-default","content":"<Chips /> Can answer when you tap to pay, after you pick it as the default wallet The person picks which wallet app answers when they tap to pay. Decides which company sits between the person and the terminal. Android lets any HCE wallet be default; iOS opened this in the EU. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only when you tap to pay\" /> **What the user does:** You choose a wallet in Settings, then hold your phone to a payment terminal Pay at a terminal with a tap Be chosen as default on Android in the co"},"310":{"title":"EU DMA alternative payments, links and distribution","description":"In the EU, apps can offer other payment providers and links to the web.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/payments-commerce/pay-eu-dma","content":"<Chips /> Can offer other payment providers and web links in the EU, with a clear screen first In the EU, apps can offer other payment providers and links to the web. Brings third party payment and link-out choices to EU users under the Digital Markets Act. Terms include new Apple fees from 2026. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A choice you make at checkout\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a button that takes you to a payment page or an outside offer Show other payment options or a link to the web in"},"311":{"title":"In-app purchase (StoreKit 2, Play Billing)","description":"Sells digital goods and subscriptions through the platform store.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/payments-commerce/pay-iap","content":"<Chips /> Can sell digital items and subscriptions, after you approve each purchase Sells digital goods and subscriptions through the platform store. The platform handles payment, receipts and refunds, and takes a commission. The store knows who buys what in every app. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One purchase at a time\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Buy or Subscribe and confirm with Face ID, a fingerprint or a passcode Charge you through the App Store or Google Play after you confirm Offer subs"},"312":{"title":"Japan MSCA payments and distribution (Dec 2025)","description":"Japan's smartphone act lets apps use other stores, payments and links from Dec 2025.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/payments-commerce/pay-japan-msca","content":"<Chips /> Can offer other app stores, payment options and web links to buyers in Japan Japan's smartphone act lets apps use other stores, payments and links from Dec 2025. Opens Japan to alternative app marketplaces, alternative payment processors and links. Apple still vets marketplaces; fees changed. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One purchase or install at a time\" /> **What the user does:** You see a choice screen, or tap a link to pay outside the main store Offer a different payment company at chec"},"313":{"title":"Tap to Pay (accept contactless payments on the phone)","description":"Turns the phone into a card reader for merchants.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/payments-commerce/pay-tap-to-pay","content":"<Chips /> Can accept tap payments from cards and wallets on the phone, once approved Turns the phone into a card reader for merchants. Lets a business accept taps from cards and wallets with no extra hardware. Reader code is certified by card schemes. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One tap at a time\" /> **What the user does:** A shop owner opens the payment app and asks the customer to tap a card or phone Take a contactless tap from a card, phone or watch Ask for a PIN when the payment needs one (iPhon"},"314":{"title":"US external links and alternative billing (post Epic rulings)","description":"In the US, apps can link out to web checkout and may offer other billing.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/payments-commerce/pay-us-external-links","content":"<Chips /> Can send you to outside web checkout in the US, with fees still being settled In the US, apps can link out to web checkout and may offer other billing. Court rulings forced both stores to allow links to outside payment. Fees on those purchases are still being decided in Apple's case. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One purchase at a time\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a Buy on the web style link inside an app Send you to a website to pay instead of using in app billing Offer a different b"},"315":{"title":"Wallet passes (boarding passes, tickets, loyalty)","description":"Adds tickets, loyalty cards and keys to the Wallet app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/payments-commerce/pay-wallet-passes","content":"<Chips /> Can add tickets, boarding passes and loyalty cards to your wallet when you tap Add Adds tickets, loyalty cards and keys to the Wallet app. Passes can appear on the lock screen at a time or place, using location and time relevance without the app running. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only passes you choose to add\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Add to Apple Wallet or Add to Google Wallet Save a pass into your wallet app Show the pass on the lock screen near a set time or place Update the"},"316":{"title":"Wallet payment sheet (Apple Pay, Google Pay)","description":"Takes a card payment through the OS wallet with biometric confirm.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/payments-commerce/pay-wallet-pay-sheet","content":"<Chips /> Can charge you through Apple Pay or Google Pay without seeing your card number Takes a card payment through the OS wallet with biometric confirm. A merchant gets a payment token, not the card number. The wallet also reveals shipping and contact details the person chooses to share. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One payment per confirmation\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Apple Pay or Google Pay at checkout and confirm with Face ID, fingerprint or PIN Receive a payment token instead of you"},"317":{"title":"Contactless payments and NFC secure element","description":"Lets an app act as a contactless card or key. Open on Android, entitlement gated on iOS.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/payments-commerce/payments-nfc-secure-element","content":"<Chips /> Can act as a contactless card or key on Android freely, but iPhone needs Apple approval Lets an app act as a contactless card or key. Open on Android, entitlement gated on iOS. Android has allowed host card emulation since 2013. Apple opened it first in the EU in 2024. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing once you choose it\" /> **What the user does:** You choose the app as your default payment app, then tap a reader Answer a payment terminal as a virtual card (Android, once you pick it as d"},"318":{"title":"Accessory pairing without broad Bluetooth access","description":"A system picker pairs one chosen accessory so the app never needs the full Bluetooth scan.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/hw-accessory-setup","content":"<Chips /> Can pair the one accessory you pick without seeing other Bluetooth devices nearby A system picker pairs one chosen accessory so the app never needs the full Bluetooth scan. Reduces what an app learns: it sees only the accessory the person picked, not every device nearby, so a pairing flow cannot double as a presence survey. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One accessory you choose\" /> **What the user does:** You tap your accessory in the system pairing sheet Show you a system picker listing onl"},"319":{"title":"Augmented reality world tracking","description":"Maps the surroundings and places digital objects on surfaces.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/hw-ar-world-tracking","content":"<Chips /> Can see through the camera and map your room while the app is open Maps the surroundings and places digital objects on surfaces. Builds a 3D map of a person's room and tracks where they look. Cloud and geospatial anchors can send visual data to the platform vendor. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the camera is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Try it in your room or open a camera based AR feature Find floors and tables through the camera and place digital objects on them Track whe"},"320":{"title":"Bluetooth LE scan and connect","description":"Scans for nearby BLE devices and connects to read or write their data.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/hw-ble-central","content":"<Chips /> Can find and connect to nearby Bluetooth gadgets once you allow Bluetooth Scans for nearby BLE devices and connects to read or write their data. Shows which gadgets, wearables, trackers and beacons are near the person. Scan results and device names can act as an indoor location or an identity of what the person owns. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Nearby gadgets, while you use it\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect to device or Scan for gadgets Scan for nearby Bluetooth LE devices suc"},"321":{"title":"Bluetooth Classic (RFCOMM, A2DP, HID)","description":"Pairs and talks to classic Bluetooth devices such as headsets, printers and car kits.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/hw-bluetooth-classic","content":"<Chips /> Can talk to headsets, printers and car kits on Android; iPhone allows only certified accessories Pairs and talks to classic Bluetooth devices such as headsets, printers and car kits. Classic links expose paired devices by name and address, and serial links can carry any data. iPhone apps cannot open raw Classic sockets, which blocks many hobby and industrial uses. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Paired devices, while connected\" /> **What the user does:** You pair a headset, printer or car kit,"},"322":{"title":"LiDAR and depth sensing","description":"Gets a depth map or room mesh of what the camera sees.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/hw-lidar-depth","content":"<Chips /> Can measure depth and room shape through the camera, on phones with the hardware Gets a depth map or room mesh of what the camera sees. Reveals the shape of rooms and faces. TrueDepth depth maps identify facial structure, so access is tied to the camera prompt. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the camera is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Scan room or Measure, and point the camera around Build a depth map of what the camera sees Make a 3D model of a room on iPhone Pro models with"},"323":{"title":"Host card emulation (HCE)","description":"Lets an app act as a contactless card or key that other readers tap.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/hw-nfc-hce","content":"<Chips /> Can act as a contactless card or key when you pick it as default Lets an app act as a contactless card or key that other readers tap. A phone that emulates a card can hold payment, transit or access credentials without the OS wallet. Opening it to apps changes who controls tap to pay. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing once you choose it\" /> **What the user does:** You choose the app in Settings under Tap and pay, then tap a reader Answer readers with a stored card, ticket or key Hold pay"},"324":{"title":"Secure Element and NFC platform access","description":"Reads and writes applets in the phone's secure chip for keys, badges and payments.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/hw-nfc-secure-element","content":"<Chips /> Can use the phone's secure chip only with special approval Reads and writes applets in the phone's secure chip for keys, badges and payments. The secure element holds the strongest credentials on the phone. Third-party access means banks, car makers and employers can issue keys without Apple or Google Wallet. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, but approval gated\" /> **What the user does:** You add a car key, work badge or bank credential from the issuer's app Store keys, badges and payme"},"325":{"title":"Flashlight (torch)","description":"Turns the camera LED on as a flashlight.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/hw-torch","content":"<Chips /> Can turn the flashlight on while the app is open, with no permission Turns the camera LED on as a flashlight. No personal data is read. Neither platform asks for permission, so any app can switch the light on while in the foreground. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"While the app is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap the flashlight button Switch the camera light on and off Set the brightness level (Android 13 and later, iPhone) Do it with no prompt See any of your personal data Keep the l"},"326":{"title":"USB host and accessories","description":"Talks to USB devices plugged into the phone.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/hw-usb","content":"<Chips /> Can talk to plugged in USB gear on Android; iPhone mostly limits apps to certified devices Talks to USB devices plugged into the phone. Reveals attached gear (drives, keyboards, radios, microcontrollers). Android lets an app drive raw USB; iPhone mostly limits apps to MFi devices and cameras. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Plugged in devices you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You plug in a USB device and tap Allow when asked Open a connection to a drive, keyboard, radio or board (Android) "},"327":{"title":"Wear OS and watchOS companion apps","description":"Pairs a watch app with the phone app and syncs data between them.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/hw-watch-companion","content":"<Chips /> Can sync data between the phone app and watch app; sensors keep their own prompts Pairs a watch app with the phone app and syncs data between them. Moves a person's health, location and messages to the wrist and back. Companion sync can carry more than the phone app would ask for directly. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing sync between your devices\" /> **What the user does:** You install the watch app and open it on your wrist Send messages and data between the phone and the watch Run a "},"328":{"title":"Hardware and accessories","description":"Bluetooth, USB, NFC, controllers, sensors. 19 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware","content":"Bluetooth, USB, NFC, controllers, sensors. 19 capabilities in this group. | Capability              | Kind                   | In one line                                                                         |\n| ----------------------- | ---------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Accessory pairing sheet | You pick what to share | Can pair a Bluetooth or Wi-Fi accessory you pick without scanning everything nearby | | Capability                                       | Kind                   | In one line                                                                       |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Accessory pairing without broad Bluetooth access | You pick what to share | Can pair the one accessory you pick without seeing other Bluetooth devices nearby | | Capability                       | Kind                 | In one line                                                        |\n| -------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Augmented reality world tracking | Works without asking | Can see through the camera and map your room while the app is open | | Capability | Kind           | In one line                                                                     |\n| ---------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Bluetooth  | Asks you first | Can scan for, connect to and broadcast as Bluetooth devices after you tap Allow | | Capability          | Kind           | In one line                                                                          |\n| ------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Bluetooth advertise | Asks you first | Can make the phone visible to nearby Bluetooth devices, mostly while the app is open | | Capability                            | Kind                 | In one line                                                                                      |\n| ------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Bluetooth Classic (RFCOMM, A2DP, HID) | Works without asking | Can talk to headsets, printers and car kits on Android; iPhone allows only certified accessories | | Capability        | Kind           | In one line                                                                 |\n| ----------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Bluetooth connect | Asks you first | Can talk to Bluetooth devices you already paired, after you allow Bluetooth | | Capability                    | Kind           | In one line                                                               |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Bluetooth LE scan and connect | Asks you first | Can find and connect to nearby Bluetooth gadgets once you allow Bluetooth | | Capability     | Kind           | In one line                                                 |\n| -------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Bluetooth scan | Asks you first | Can search for nearby Bluetooth devices after you tap Allow | | Capability         | Kind                          | In one line                                                          |\n| ------------------ | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Flashlight (torch) | Does something on your device | Can turn the flashlight on while the app is open, with no permission | | Capability                           | Kind                 | In one line                                                          |\n| ------------------------------------ | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Hardware drivers (DriverKit and USB) | Works without asking | Can run hardware drivers only on M series iPads, with Apple approval | | Capability                | Kind                 | In one line                                                      |\n| ------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Host card emulation (HCE) | Works without asking | Can act as a contactless card or key when you pick it as default | | Capability              | Kind                 | In one line                                                                      |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| LiDAR and depth sensing | Works without asking | Can measure depth and room shape through the camera, on phones with the hardware | | Capability | Kind           | In one line                                            |\n| ---------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| NFC        | Asks you first | Can scan NFC tags and cards that you hold to the phone | | Capability                             | Kind                 | In one line                                                |\n| -------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Secure Element and NFC platform access | Works without asking | Can use the phone's secure chip only with special approval | | Capability             | Kind           | In one line                                                                          |\n| ---------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------"},"329":{"title":"Accessory pairing sheet","description":"A system picker that pairs a Bluetooth or Wi-Fi accessory without a broad permission.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-accessory-setup-kit","content":"<Chips /> Can pair a Bluetooth or Wi-Fi accessory you pick without scanning everything nearby A system picker that pairs a Bluetooth or Wi-Fi accessory without a broad permission. The app learns only about the accessory the person picks, which avoids scanning for everything nearby. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One accessory you choose\" /> **What the user does:** You tap your accessory in the system pairing sheet Show a system chooser with only matching accessories Connect to the one accessory you cho"},"330":{"title":"Bluetooth advertise","description":"Lets the app make the phone discoverable to other Bluetooth devices.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-ble-advertise","content":"<Chips /> Can make the phone visible to nearby Bluetooth devices, mostly while the app is open Lets the app make the phone discoverable to other Bluetooth devices. Broadcasting makes the phone detectable by anyone nearby; useful for proximity features but also for tracking. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Broadcasting, mostly while open\" /> **What the user does:** You turn on a feature like Find nearby friends or Share with a device Broadcast that the phone is nearby Let other devices find and connect t"},"331":{"title":"Bluetooth connect","description":"Lets the app talk to Bluetooth devices that are already paired.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-ble-connect","content":"<Chips /> Can talk to Bluetooth devices you already paired, after you allow Bluetooth Lets the app talk to Bluetooth devices that are already paired. Exposes paired device names and lets the app read data from headphones, trackers and medical devices. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Devices you already paired\" /> **What the user does:** You open the app and tap Connect to your headphones, tracker or device List paired device names (Android 12 and later) Connect to headphones, trackers and medical device"},"332":{"title":"Bluetooth scan","description":"Lets the app search for nearby Bluetooth devices.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-ble-scan","content":"<Chips /> Can search for nearby Bluetooth devices after you tap Allow Lets the app search for nearby Bluetooth devices. Scanning can reveal nearby phones, beacons and wearables and can be used for indoor location. Android 12 split it out of location. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Nearby devices, while you use it\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Find devices or Scan nearby Find nearby phones, beacons and wearables Use scan results to guess indoor position Scan on iPhone with one prompt that covers s"},"333":{"title":"Bluetooth","description":"Scans for, connects to and advertises as Bluetooth devices.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-bluetooth","content":"<Chips /> Can scan for, connect to and broadcast as Bluetooth devices after you tap Allow Scans for, connects to and advertises as Bluetooth devices. Nearby device scans can map a home or store and identify accessories and people. Both OSes tie it to a prompt. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Nearby devices, while you use it\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a Bluetooth feature, then answer the Allow prompt Scan for nearby devices Connect to devices Advertise the phone to other devices Map a home or st"},"334":{"title":"Hardware drivers (DriverKit and USB)","description":"Runs user space drivers for hardware; on iPadOS only on M series iPads.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-driverkit","content":"<Chips /> Can run hardware drivers only on M series iPads, with Apple approval Runs user space drivers for hardware; on iPadOS only on M series iPads. Lets apps talk to USB and PCIe style accessories; power and risk justify Apple approval and user approval. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Devices you plug in and approve\" /> **What the user does:** You turn the driver on in Settings, then plug in the device Run a driver for a USB or similar accessory on iPad with M1 or newer (iPadOS 16 and later) Talk to"},"335":{"title":"NFC","description":"Lets the app read or emulate NFC tags and cards.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-nfc","content":"<Chips /> Can scan NFC tags and cards that you hold to the phone Lets the app read or emulate NFC tags and cards. Reads tags, payment-style cards and badges. Android grants it at install; iOS uses a capability and purpose string. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Tags you hold close\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Scan tag and hold the phone near an NFC tag or card Scan tags, badges and similar cards Scan tags in the background on iPhone (iOS 12 and later) Act as a contactless card on Android Scan on "},"336":{"title":"Ultra-wideband ranging","description":"Lets the app measure exact distance and direction to other UWB devices.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-uwb","content":"<Chips /> Can measure exact distance and direction to nearby UWB devices while the app is open Lets the app measure exact distance and direction to other UWB devices. Centimeter accurate nearby tracking: finding keys, car access, handoff. Part of Nearby devices on Android. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Nearby UWB devices, while open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Find my item or start a nearby handoff Measure distance to another UWB device, to within about 10 cm on iPhone Point you toward the dev"},"337":{"title":"Vibration and haptics","description":"Lets the app vibrate the phone.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-vibrate","content":"<Chips /> Can make the phone buzz, with no prompt and no personal data Lets the app vibrate the phone. A harmless-looking motor trigger; no sensitive data. Android requires a manifest permission; iOS needs none. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A buzz, nothing more\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app gives feedback, like a tap or an alert Buzz the phone Play tap style feedback through the Taptic engine (iPhone) Use a haptic pattern on Android 8 and later See any of your data Work"},"338":{"title":"Device accounts list","description":"Lets the app list the Google and other accounts signed in on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/contacts-accounts","content":"<Chips /> Can list only its own accounts on Android; iPhone shows no account list Lets the app list the Google and other accounts signed in on the device. Reveals the email addresses of accounts on the phone. Largely replaced by account pickers because the list is an easy way to build an identity profile. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Own accounts, or the one you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Choose account or Sign in with Google List the accounts the app itself owns (Android) Learn about o"},"339":{"title":"Calendar, full access","description":"Reads and edits every event in the person's calendars.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/contacts-calendar-full","content":"<Chips /> Can see, add and change every event in all your calendars once you allow full access Reads and edits every event in the person's calendars. Calendars show meetings, attendees, travel, doctor visits and routines. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"All your events, anytime\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow Full Access when the app asks about your calendar List meetings, places and attendees Change or delete events that already exist Add new events Check your calendar again later without ask"},"340":{"title":"Calendar, write only","description":"Adds events to the calendar without reading anything already there.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/contacts-calendar-write-only","content":"<Chips /> Can add events to your calendar without seeing anything already in it Adds events to the calendar without reading anything already there. Lets a ticketing or booking app add an event with no view of the person's schedule. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Adds events, sees none\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Add to calendar on a ticket or booking Open the system calendar editor with the event filled in, no permission needed Add events directly once you allow it (Android) or choose add only "},"341":{"title":"Calendar","description":"Lets the app read or write the user's calendar events.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/contacts-calendar","content":"<Chips /> Can see and add calendar events after you allow it, or only add them on iPhone Lets the app read or write the user's calendar events. Shows meetings, locations, attendees and routines; writes can create events. Reveals employer, health appointments and travel plans. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"All your events, once allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Add to calendar or Sync my calendar List events with times, places and attendees Create new events Add an event through the system ca"},"342":{"title":"Contacts, read and write","description":"Reads the whole address book: names, numbers, emails, addresses.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/contacts-full-access","content":"<Chips /> Can open your whole address book once you allow it, or only some on iPhone Reads the whole address book: names, numbers, emails, addresses. Exposes the person's social graph and other people's private data. A shared favorite for growth teams and a historic abuse vector. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Everything in your contacts\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Find friends or Import contacts List names, numbers, emails and addresses Add or change entries (Android with the extra permission)"},"343":{"title":"Limited contacts access","description":"The person lets the app see only chosen contacts, not the whole address book.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/contacts-limited-access","content":"<Chips /> Can see only the contacts you choose on iPhone; Android gives only a one time pick The person lets the app see only chosen contacts, not the whole address book. Shrinks contact exposure to a hand picked list; the app can also surface matching contacts inside its UI without a prompt. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only the contacts you choose\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Limit Access and choose contacts, or pick from a list Show the contacts you selected Suggest matching contacts inside"},"344":{"title":"Contact notes field","description":"Apps cannot read free-text notes on contacts without an Apple entitlement.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/contacts-notes-field-entitlement","content":"<Chips /> Can't see the Notes field on contacts on iPhone without Apple's special approval Apps cannot read free-text notes on contacts without an Apple entitlement. Notes often hold passwords, door codes and medical details. Apple walled them off after reports of misuse. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked on iPhone without approval\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It is blocked when the app asks for notes without the entitlement Open names, numbers and emails if you allowed contacts See notes "},"345":{"title":"Contact picker","description":"A system screen where the person selects contacts to share; no permission needed.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/contacts-picker","content":"<Chips /> Can get only the contacts and fields you pick in the system screen, no permission needed A system screen where the person selects contacts to share; no permission needed. Shares only the chosen contacts and fields, so an invite flow does not need the whole book. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only what you pick, once\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Invite friends and select people from the system list Receive the contacts you tick Ask for just certain fields, such as a phone number Set a "},"346":{"title":"Read contacts","description":"Lets the app read the whole address book.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/contacts-read","content":"<Chips /> Can see your whole address book once you tap Allow, or only some on iPhone Lets the app read the whole address book. Exposes names, numbers, emails, and who a person knows. Apps use it to match friends, which also lets them upload the entire social graph of the person's family and colleagues. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Everything in your contacts\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Find friends or Match my contacts List names, numbers and emails Upload the full list to match friends Take "},"347":{"title":"Reminders","description":"Reads and edits the Reminders app's lists.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/contacts-reminders","content":"<Chips /> Can see and change your Reminders lists on iPhone; Android has no shared list Reads and edits the Reminders app's lists. Task lists reveal chores, errands, health habits and relationships. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Everything in Reminders, once allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow Full Access when a task app asks for Reminders List tasks and lists (iPhone) Add, tick off and change reminders (iPhone) Set an alarm on Android with a normal permission Use an add only mode on iPh"},"348":{"title":"Adding contacts through the system editor","description":"An app creates or edits a contact by opening the system editor, not by writing silently.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/contacts-write-via-system-editor","content":"<Chips /> Can open the system contact screen so you add or edit a contact yourself An app creates or edits a contact by opening the system editor, not by writing silently. Prevents apps from planting or altering contacts in the background; the person sees and approves each change. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One contact you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Add to contacts and press Save in the system screen Open the system editor with details filled in Insert or edit a contact with no per"},"349":{"title":"Write contacts","description":"Lets the app add, edit or delete address book entries.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/contacts-write","content":"<Chips /> Can add, edit and delete address book entries once you tap Allow Lets the app add, edit or delete address book entries. Lets apps modify who is in the phone's book, which can plant fake contacts or merge entries. Backup and sync apps need it. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Everything in your contacts\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow when a backup, sync or contacts app asks Add new contacts Edit or merge existing entries Delete entries Change many entries in one batch (Android) Change"},"350":{"title":"Contacts and personal data","description":"Your people, photos, calendar and more. 14 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts","content":"Your people, photos, calendar and more. 14 capabilities in this group. | Capability                                | Kind                          | In one line                                                              |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Adding contacts through the system editor | Does something on your device | Can open the system contact screen so you add or edit a contact yourself | | Capability | Kind           | In one line                                                                    |\n| ---------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Calendar   | Asks you first | Can see and add calendar events after you allow it, or only add them on iPhone | | Capability            | Kind           | In one line                                                                          |\n| --------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Calendar, full access | Asks you first | Can see, add and change every event in all your calendars once you allow full access | | Capability           | Kind           | In one line                                                           |\n| -------------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Calendar, write only | Asks you first | Can add events to your calendar without seeing anything already in it | | Capability          | Kind             | In one line                                                                      |\n| ------------------- | ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Contact notes field | Blocked for apps | Can't see the Notes field on contacts on iPhone without Apple's special approval | | Capability     | Kind                   | In one line                                                                              |\n| -------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Contact picker | You pick what to share | Can get only the contacts and fields you pick in the system screen, no permission needed | | Capability               | Kind           | In one line                                                                |\n| ------------------------ | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Contacts, read and write | Asks you first | Can open your whole address book once you allow it, or only some on iPhone | | Capability                                 | Kind                 | In one line                                                                  |\n| ------------------------------------------ | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Data for Generative AI Improvement setting | Works without asking | Can use your posts and profile to train AI unless you switch the setting off | | Capability           | Kind           | In one line                                                             |\n| -------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Device accounts list | Asks you first | Can list only its own accounts on Android; iPhone shows no account list | | Capability              | Kind           | In one line                                                                        |\n| ----------------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Limited contacts access | Asks you first | Can see only the contacts you choose on iPhone; Android gives only a one time pick | | Capability                                     | Kind              | In one line                                                                              |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| People You May Know from contacts and calendar | Used to track you | Can suggest you to people from their uploaded contacts, even if you never uploaded yours | | Capability    | Kind           | In one line                                                                |\n| ------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Read contacts | Asks you first | Can see your whole address book once you tap Allow, or only some on iPhone | | Capability | Kind           | In one line                                                                   |\n| ---------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Reminders  | Asks you first | Can see and change your Reminders lists on iPhone; Android has no shared list | | Capability     | Kind           | In one line                                                      |\n| -------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Write contacts | Asks you first | Can add, edit and delete address book entries once you tap Allow |"},"351":{"title":"Data for Generative AI Improvement setting","description":"A LinkedIn account setting that controls using member content to train AI models.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/linkedin-gen-ai-data-setting","content":"<Chips /> Can use your posts and profile to train AI unless you switch the setting off A LinkedIn account setting that controls using member content to train AI models. Posts, profile data and job application data can train content-generating models. The toggle is forward looking; it cannot undo past training. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, your content on LinkedIn\" /> **What the user does:** You open Settings and turn Data for Generative AI Improvement on or off Use posts, profile and job app"},"352":{"title":"People You May Know from contacts and calendar","description":"LinkedIn uses uploaded contacts and others' uploads to suggest connections.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/personal-data-contacts/linkedin-people-you-may-know","content":"<Chips /> Can suggest you to people from their uploaded contacts, even if you never uploaded yours LinkedIn uses uploaded contacts and others' uploads to suggest connections. A person's number or email in someone else's address book can feed suggestions about them even if they never uploaded anything. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Ongoing, from other people's uploads\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing from you. It happens when someone else uploads their address book Match your number or email found i"},"353":{"title":"System control","description":"Changing device settings and controlling other apps. 20 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control","content":"Changing device settings and controlling other apps. 20 capabilities in this group. | Capability                                          | Kind                 | In one line                                                         |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Accessibility Service (read and control the screen) | Works without asking | Can see and tap other apps' screens on Android, once you turn it on | | Capability                               | Kind          | In one line                                                                      |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| App privacy report and Privacy Dashboard | Gets silently | Can show you which apps used sensors, and on iPhone which domains they contacted | | Capability                    | Kind                 | In one line                                                                          |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Content filters and DNS proxy | Works without asking | Can filter or block internet traffic and domains across the phone once you set it up | | Capability                                                  | Kind                 | In one line                                                       |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Device admin and Device Policy (work profile, device owner) | Works without asking | Can enforce rules and wipe a phone only when a company manages it | | Capability            | Kind           | In one line                                                                   |\n| --------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Do Not Disturb access | Asks you first | Can check and change Do Not Disturb on Android; iPhone shows only a yes or no | | Capability                      | Kind          | In one line                                                          |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Focus and Do Not Disturb status | Gets silently | Can learn only whether Focus or Do Not Disturb is on, not which mode | | Capability            | Kind                 | In one line                                                          |\n| --------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Kiosk and app pinning | Works without asking | Can lock the phone to one app when you pin it or a company allows it | | Capability                            | Kind                 | In one line                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Lockdown Mode and Advanced Protection | Works without asking | Can't use many risky features when Lockdown Mode or Advanced Protection is on | | Capability                               | Kind                 | In one line                                                                         |\n| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Mobile device management and supervision | Works without asking | Can control settings, apps and restrictions on the phone, once a company enrolls it | | Capability             | Kind           | In one line                                                                                  |\n| ---------------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Modify system settings | Asks you first | Can change brightness, ringtone and screen timeout on Android; iPhone allows only brightness | | Capability           | Kind                 | In one line                                                                       |\n| -------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| One-time permissions | Works without asking | Can use location, camera or mic only until you leave, if you pick one time access | | Capability                   | Kind                 | In one line                                                    |\n| ---------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Privacy dashboard and report | Works without asking | Can show you which apps used location, camera or mic, and when | | Capability                     | Kind                 | In one line                                                                      |\n| ------------------------------ | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Read other apps' notifications | Works without asking | Can see other apps' notifications only on Android after you allow it in Settings | | Capability                              | Kind                          | In one line                                                                          |\n| --------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Restricted settings for sideloaded apps | Does something on your device | Can't turn on powerful settings for apps installed outside stores until you allow it | | Capability                     | Kind                 |"},"354":{"title":"System logs","description":"Blocked: apps can read only their own logs.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/nx-system-logs","content":"<Chips /> Can't see other apps' logs, only its own Blocked: apps can read only their own logs. System logs once leaked other apps' data. Android 4.1 limited apps to their own logs; iOS gives a normal app only its own process logs. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only its own logs\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The phone limits each app to its own logs See the app's own log messages Check its own process logs on iPhone (iOS 15 and later) See logs from other apps Use the old log permission as a norm"},"355":{"title":"Accessibility Service (read and control the screen)","description":"Lets an app read other apps' screens and tap for the user.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/sys-accessibility-service","content":"<Chips /> Can see and tap other apps' screens on Android, once you turn it on Lets an app read other apps' screens and tap for the user. The most powerful non-system capability on Android: it can read text, click and type in any app. Criminal malware abuses it, so Play restricts it to real accessibility tools. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Everything on screen, once on\" /> **What the user does:** You open Settings, then Accessibility, pick the app and accept the full control warning See text on the sc"},"356":{"title":"Kiosk and app pinning","description":"Locks the phone to one app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/sys-app-pinning","content":"<Chips /> Can lock the phone to one app when you pin it or a company allows it Locks the phone to one app. Used for kiosks and exams. Android has screen pinning for users and lock task for device owners; iOS has Guided Access and supervised Single App Mode. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, until unpinned\" /> **What the user does:** You pin the screen, or triple click to start Guided Access Keep you inside one app, like a kiosk or an exam Hide the status bar and navigation (Android lock task, com"},"357":{"title":"Device admin and Device Policy (work profile, device owner)","description":"Lets a company app enforce policies and wipe a phone.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/sys-device-admin","content":"<Chips /> Can enforce rules and wipe a phone only when a company manages it Lets a company app enforce policies and wipe a phone. Controls passwords, app installs, wiping and cameras. Device admin is deprecated; device owner and work profile are the supported routes. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Broad, on managed phones\" /> **What the user does:** Your company has you scan a QR code or enroll the phone and accept a warning Set password rules Control app installs and cameras Wipe the phone Keep work d"},"358":{"title":"Mobile device management and supervision","description":"A company server controls the phone's settings, apps and restrictions.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/sys-mdm","content":"<Chips /> Can control settings, apps and restrictions on the phone, once a company enrolls it A company server controls the phone's settings, apps and restrictions. MDM can install apps, set Wi-Fi and VPN, block features and wipe the device. Supervised iOS devices allow tighter control, such as app lockdown and content filters. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Broad, on enrolled phones\" /> **What the user does:** You enroll the phone with your company by QR code, profile install or automatic setup Instal"},"359":{"title":"Content filters and DNS proxy","description":"Filters or blocks network traffic and domains system wide.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/sys-network-filter-dns","content":"<Chips /> Can filter or block internet traffic and domains across the phone once you set it up Filters or blocks network traffic and domains system wide. Lets an app block ads, malware and categories. iOS content filters see flows but not content on unsupervised devices; DNS settings are encrypted. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"All traffic, once enabled\" /> **What the user does:** You turn on a blocker app, pick a Private DNS, or tap Allow on the filter prompt Block ads, malware and categories of site"},"360":{"title":"Read other apps' notifications","description":"Reads what other apps show in the notification shade.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/sys-notification-listener","content":"<Chips /> Can see other apps' notifications only on Android after you allow it in Settings Reads what other apps show in the notification shade. Notification text can contain codes, messages and private data. Android needs a special setting; iOS exposes this only to paired accessories, with EU forwarding added in 26.3. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Every notification, once on\" /> **What the user does:** You open Settings, then Notifications, and switch the app on under Device and app notifications See"},"361":{"title":"Restricted settings for sideloaded apps","description":"Android blocks some powerful toggles for apps installed outside stores.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/sys-restricted-settings","content":"<Chips /> Can't turn on powerful settings for apps installed outside stores until you allow it Android blocks some powerful toggles for apps installed outside stores. Accessibility, notification listener and similar options are greyed out for sideloaded apps until the person opens App Info and allows restricted settings. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked until you unlock it\" /> **What the user does:** You open App info, tap the menu and choose Allow restricted settings Install outside a store on A"},"362":{"title":"App privacy report and Privacy Dashboard","description":"A log in Settings showing which apps used sensors and which domains they contacted.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/system-app-privacy-report","content":"<Chips /> Can show you which apps used sensors, and on iPhone which domains they contacted A log in Settings showing which apps used sensors and which domains they contacted. Android's dashboard shows sensor access. Apple's report adds network domains. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Shows a log, changes nothing\" /> **What the user does:** You open the Privacy dashboard in Settings, or turn on Record App Activity on iPhone List which apps used location, camera and microphone Show when each use happened "},"363":{"title":"Unused app permission reset","description":"Automatically removes permissions from apps not opened for months.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/system-auto-reset","content":"<Chips /> Can remove permissions from apps you have not opened for about three months, on Android Automatically removes permissions from apps not opened for months. Limits dormant exposure. Android resets runtime permissions after about three months unused; iOS has no automatic revocation. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Removes permissions from idle apps\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Android does it on its own and sends you a notice Reset the permissions of apps you stopped using Tell you when a"},"364":{"title":"Do Not Disturb access","description":"Lets the app read and change Do Not Disturb settings.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/system-dnd-access","content":"<Chips /> Can check and change Do Not Disturb on Android; iPhone shows only a yes or no Lets the app read and change Do Not Disturb settings. Reveals whether the user is in a focus mode and lets apps toggle it. iOS shows only a yes or no focus status. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your quiet mode, while allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You switch on Do Not Disturb access in Settings (Android), or tap Share Focus Status (iPhone) Check whether Do Not Disturb is on Change the interruption filter on An"},"365":{"title":"Screen Time and parental controls","description":"Limits apps and websites and reads usage summaries, with opaque identifiers on iOS.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/system-family-controls","content":"<Chips /> Can limit apps and websites and see usage summaries, once you or a parent allow it Limits apps and websites and reads usage summaries, with opaque identifiers on iOS. Shows app habits; iOS hides which apps behind tokens, Android returns package names. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing app habits, once allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You approve the Screen Time sheet on iPhone, or turn on Usage access in Settings on Android Set limits on apps and websites See how long apps were used, "},"366":{"title":"Focus and Do Not Disturb status","description":"Learns only whether the person has a Focus or Do Not Disturb mode switched on.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/system-focus-status","content":"<Chips /> Can learn only whether Focus or Do Not Disturb is on, not which mode Learns only whether the person has a Focus or Do Not Disturb mode switched on. iOS returns a single yes or no. Android returns the interruption filter level with no permission. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A yes or no, or a level\" /> **What the user does:** On iPhone you tap Allow on the Focus Status prompt; on Android nothing happens Tell whether a Focus is on, as a yes or no (iPhone) Check the Do Not Disturb level on And"},"367":{"title":"Lockdown Mode and Advanced Protection","description":"An extreme protection mode that turns off risky features for high risk people.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/system-lockdown-mode","content":"<Chips /> Can't use many risky features when Lockdown Mode or Advanced Protection is on An extreme protection mode that turns off risky features for high risk people. Cuts attack surface at the cost of features. Apps may see features missing. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocks features for you\" /> **What the user does:** You switch on Lockdown Mode (iPhone) or Advanced Protection (Android) in Settings Keep working with fewer features Notice that some features are missing Use just in time code and mo"},"368":{"title":"VPN and network extensions","description":"Routes or filters the device's network traffic as a VPN, DNS or content filter.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/system-network-extension","content":"<Chips /> Can route or filter all the phone's network traffic once you accept a VPN prompt Routes or filters the device's network traffic as a VPN, DNS or content filter. A tunnel app sees destination metadata and can read cleartext for all other apps. It is a powerful trust position. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"All traffic, anytime while on\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow on the Add VPN Configurations or connection request prompt See where your traffic goes See unencrypted content for all"},"369":{"title":"One-time permissions","description":"A prompt choice that grants access only until the user leaves the app.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/system-one-time-permission","content":"<Chips /> Can use location, camera or mic only until you leave, if you pick one time access A prompt choice that grants access only until the user leaves the app. Reduces long-term exposure for location, mic and camera. Android 11 introduced Only this time; iOS offers Allow Once for location. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Until you leave the app\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Only this time (Android) or Allow Once (iPhone location) Use the sensor while the app is open Ask again the next time you "},"370":{"title":"Privacy dashboard and report","description":"A system view that shows which apps used sensitive data and when.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/system-privacy-dashboard","content":"<Chips /> Can show you which apps used location, camera or mic, and when A system view that shows which apps used sensitive data and when. Lets users audit access to location, camera and mic. iOS also logs network contact. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Shows a log, changes nothing\" /> **What the user does:** You open the Privacy dashboard in Settings, or App Privacy Report on iPhone List when apps used location, camera and microphone Show a timeline of access Show the explanations apps add to their ac"},"371":{"title":"Safety Check and Safety Center","description":"A guided reset of everything shared with other people and apps.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/system-safety-check","content":"<Chips /> Can quickly shut off what you share with people and apps, with one guided reset A guided reset of everything shared with other people and apps. Built for people in unsafe relationships. Quickly shuts off access and sharing. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One reset at a time\" /> **What the user does:** You open Safety Center (Android) or Safety Check (iPhone) in Settings and choose a reset Stop sharing with other people and apps Review privacy controls and run scans Run Emergency Reset on iPho"},"372":{"title":"Two denial rule","description":"After two denials, Android stops showing the permission dialog.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/system-two-denial-rule","content":"<Chips /> Can ask for a permission only twice on Android, and once on iPhone After two denials, Android stops showing the permission dialog. Limits repeated prompts. iOS shows a permission prompt only once; later changes must be made in Settings. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One or two asks, then Settings\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Don't allow on a permission dialog Ask again after one denial on Android Send you to Settings with a link to change it Explain why it needs the permission before "},"373":{"title":"Modify system settings","description":"Lets the app change system settings like brightness and ringtone.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","System control"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/system-control/system-write-settings","content":"<Chips /> Can change brightness, ringtone and screen timeout on Android; iPhone allows only brightness Lets the app change system settings like brightness and ringtone. Allows changing display brightness, ringtones and timeouts. A minor privacy risk but often requested. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A few display and sound settings\" /> **What the user does:** You switch on Modify system settings for the app in Settings Change the screen brightness Change the ringtone and screen timeout (Android) Set b"},"374":{"title":"Calls and texts","description":"Phone calls, SMS and carrier details. 9 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/telephony-sms","content":"Phone calls, SMS and carrier details. 9 capabilities in this group. | Capability           | Kind             | In one line                                 |\n| -------------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| Call audio recording | Blocked for apps | Can't record the other side of a phone call | | Capability | Kind           | In one line                                                         |\n| ---------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Call log   | Asks you first | Can see your call history on Android, only for apps Google approves | | Capability                   | Kind             | In one line                                                       |\n| ---------------------------- | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| IMEI, MEID and serial number | Blocked for apps | Can't see the phone's IMEI, MEID or serial number as a normal app | | Capability       | Kind             | In one line                                                                                  |\n| ---------------- | ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Own phone number | Blocked for apps | Can't get the phone's own number on iPhone; Android allows it behind a restricted permission | | Capability              | Kind           | In one line                                                                 |\n| ----------------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Phone state and numbers | Asks you first | Can see call state, SIM and carrier info on Android; iPhone shows no number | | Capability             | Kind           | In one line                                                                       |\n| ---------------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Place and answer calls | Asks you first | Can start calls for you on Android after you allow it; iPhone asks you to confirm | | Capability           | Kind           | In one line                                                                   |\n| -------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Read and receive SMS | Asks you first | Can see texts on Android only if Google approves the app, and never on iPhone | | Capability | Kind           | In one line                                                             |\n| ---------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Send SMS   | Asks you first | Can send texts on Android once allowed; on iPhone you tap Send yourself | | Capability                    | Kind             | In one line                                                  |\n| ----------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| SIM identifiers (ICCID, IMSI) | Blocked for apps | Can't see SIM identifiers like ICCID or IMSI as a normal app |"},"375":{"title":"Call audio recording","description":"Blocked: neither platform lets normal apps record the other side of a phone call.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Calls and texts"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/telephony-sms/nx-call-audio","content":"<Chips /> Can't record the other side of a phone call Blocked: neither platform lets normal apps record the other side of a phone call. Android narrowed call recording over time (mic limits in 9, VOICE\\_CALL source blocked in 10). iOS never allowed it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked for normal apps\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The phone blocks normal apps from call audio Use the microphone when you allow it, with limits during calls on Android 9 and later Tell that a call is active thro"},"376":{"title":"IMEI, MEID and serial number","description":"Blocked for normal apps: Android needs a privileged permission since 10; iOS has never exposed them.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Calls and texts"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/telephony-sms/nx-imei-serial","content":"<Chips /> Can't see the phone's IMEI, MEID or serial number as a normal app Blocked for normal apps: Android needs a privileged permission since 10; iOS has never exposed them. These IDs are permanent and unique, so platforms closed them to stop tracking. Only carrier, MDM or system apps can read them. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked for normal apps\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. These permanent IDs are blocked from normal apps Use other IDs instead, such as the vendor ID or advertising ID"},"377":{"title":"Own phone number","description":"Android gives it behind a runtime permission; iOS has no API.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Calls and texts"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/telephony-sms/nx-phone-number","content":"<Chips /> Can't get the phone's own number on iPhone; Android allows it behind a restricted permission Android gives it behind a runtime permission; iOS has no API. The number is a strong identity key, so iOS never exposes it and Android gates it behind a restricted permission set. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked on iPhone, gated on Android\" /> **What the user does:** You type your number, or verify it with a text code or Sign in with Apple Ask you to type your number Check it with a text code o"},"378":{"title":"SIM identifiers (ICCID, IMSI)","description":"Blocked for normal apps on Android since 10 and never available on iOS.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Calls and texts"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/telephony-sms/nx-sim-ids","content":"<Chips /> Can't see SIM identifiers like ICCID or IMSI as a normal app Blocked for normal apps on Android since 10 and never available on iOS. SIM IDs identify the subscriber. Only carrier-privileged apps can read them. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked for normal apps\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Only carrier approved apps get these Use them if it holds carrier privileges (Android) Get them in a carrier app with an Apple entitlement (iPhone) See them on Android 10 and later as a normal ap"},"379":{"title":"Call log","description":"Lets the app read or write the list of calls made and received.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Calls and texts"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/telephony-sms/telephony-call-log","content":"<Chips /> Can see your call history on Android, only for apps Google approves Lets the app read or write the list of calls made and received. Shows who a person calls, when and for how long: a full social and professional graph. Google Play restricts it to default dialer apps. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Full call history, once allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow when your dialer or calling app asks about call logs List who you called, when and for how long Add or remove call log entri"},"380":{"title":"Place and answer calls","description":"Lets the app start or answer phone calls without the user pressing Dial.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Calls and texts"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/telephony-sms/telephony-phone-call","content":"<Chips /> Can start calls for you on Android after you allow it; iPhone asks you to confirm Lets the app start or answer phone calls without the user pressing Dial. A direct call costs money and can disclose the number; premium-rate fraud is the abuse case. Normal apps should hand off to the dialer instead. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Calls you start or answer\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a phone number, a Call button, or Dial Place a call directly (Android, with permission) Open the dialer w"},"381":{"title":"Phone state and numbers","description":"Lets the app see call state, the SIM and the phone's number.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Calls and texts"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/telephony-sms/telephony-phone-state","content":"<Chips /> Can see call state, SIM and carrier info on Android; iPhone shows no number Lets the app see call state, the SIM and the phone's number. Reveals carrier, SIM info and in some cases the user's number. Android restricts device IDs such as IMEI to privileged apps since Android 10. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, about calls and SIM\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow on the Phone permission prompt Tell whether a call is active See carrier and SIM information See your number on Andr"},"382":{"title":"Read and receive SMS","description":"Lets the app read stored texts and get a copy of incoming SMS.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Calls and texts"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/telephony-sms/telephony-sms-read","content":"<Chips /> Can see texts on Android only if Google approves the app, and never on iPhone Lets the app read stored texts and get a copy of incoming SMS. Texts contain bank codes, medical reminders and private chats. Google Play restricts SMS access to default handlers, and Android 17 delays OTP texts. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"All your texts, once allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow when your messaging app asks to access SMS List stored texts Get a copy of each incoming SMS Fill one ti"},"383":{"title":"Send SMS","description":"Lets the app send text messages from the user's number.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Calls and texts"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/telephony-sms/telephony-sms-send","content":"<Chips /> Can send texts on Android once allowed; on iPhone you tap Send yourself Lets the app send text messages from the user's number. Can run up charges via premium numbers or spread malware to the contact list. Android warns the user about possible costs for premium short codes. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Texts you send or start\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Send on a message, or tap Allow when the app asks Send text messages from your number (Android, with permission) Open a ready to se"},"384":{"title":"Speed and timing","description":"How fast things load and run. 1 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/timing-performance","content":"How fast things load and run. 1 capabilities in this group. | Capability                        | Kind                 | In one line                                                               |\n| --------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Required Reason: system boot time | Works without asking | Can check how long the phone has been on; iPhone needs an approved reason |"},"385":{"title":"Required Reason: system boot time","description":"iOS uptime reads need 35F9.1, 8FFB.1 or 3D61.1.","breadcrumbs":["Native apps (Android and iPhone)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/mobile/timing-performance/rr-boot-time","content":"<Chips /> Can check how long the phone has been on; iPhone needs an approved reason iOS uptime reads need 35F9.1, 8FFB.1 or 3D61.1. Boot timestamp is a stable fingerprint until reboot. 35F9.1 measures in-app intervals, 8FFB.1 stamps in-app events, 3D61.1 adds it to a user-submitted bug report. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Ongoing, a quiet signal\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app measures time or logs an event Measure time between actions inside the app (reason 35F9.1) Stamp"},"386":{"title":"Camera pan, tilt and zoom control","description":"Lets a site move or zoom a PTZ-capable camera.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen/camera-camera-ptz","content":"<Chips /> Can move or zoom a motorized camera, once the site already has camera access Lets a site move or zoom a PTZ-capable camera. Gives the site physical control of camera direction and zoom on conferencing cameras, so it can aim at people and rooms. Gated by a separate permission on top of camera access. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One camera, while you use the site\" /> **What the user does:** You use pan or zoom controls on a video call or monitoring page and approve the extra prompt Pan, tilt"},"387":{"title":"Camera (video capture)","description":"Asks to turn on the camera and receive live video.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen/camera-camera","content":"<Chips /> Can see and record live video from your camera while the tab is open Asks to turn on the camera and receive live video. Gives the site a live view of the person's face, room and surroundings. Used for calls and photos, but also for identity capture, biometrics and covert recording if the tab stays open. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the tab is open\" /> **What the user does:** You click Start video, Join call, or Scan, then choose Allow in the browser pop up Show live video from your ca"},"388":{"title":"Captured Surface Control (scroll and zoom the shared tab)","description":"Lets an app scroll and zoom the tab it is capturing.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen/camera-captured-surface-control","content":"<Chips /> Can scroll and zoom the browser tab it is sharing, if from the same site Lets an app scroll and zoom the tab it is capturing. Gives a meeting app remote scroll and zoom of the person's own shared tab. Needs a separate prompt because it is a control action on top of a view. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One shared tab only\" /> **What the user does:** You share a tab in a presentation or recording tool, then use its scroll or zoom buttons Scroll the shared tab up and down from the controlling "},"389":{"title":"Screen, window and tab capture","description":"Asks to record or stream a screen, window or browser tab.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen/camera-display-capture","content":"<Chips /> Can see the screen, window or tab you choose to share, until you stop Asks to record or stream a screen, window or browser tab. Gives the site a live copy of whatever the person picks: their whole screen, an app window, or a tab. Can expose private messages, tabs and credentials visible on screen. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"What you pick, while sharing\" /> **What the user does:** You click Share screen or Present, then pick a tab, window or screen in the browser chooser Show a live copy o"},"390":{"title":"enumerateDevices label gating","description":"Lists camera, microphone and speaker counts; names only after permission.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen/camera-enumerate-devices","content":"<Chips /> Can count your cameras, mics and speakers, but names stay hidden until you allow access Lists camera, microphone and speaker counts; names only after permission. Reveals how many cameras, mics and speakers exist (before consent, labels empty), and device names after consent. Counts and kinds are a mild fingerprint and show setup type. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A device count, no content\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a call or recording page loads Tell how many came"},"391":{"title":"MediaRecorder","description":"Records a camera, mic or screen stream to a file in the page.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen/camera-media-recorder","content":"<Chips /> Can save a camera, mic or screen stream to a file, once you allowed it Records a camera, mic or screen stream to a file in the page. Turns a live stream into stored audio or video. Needs a stream the person already allowed; the point is the site can keep a copy, upload it, or hold it in memory. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only streams you allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You click Record on a page that already has camera, mic or screen access Record video or audio from a stream you alre"},"392":{"title":"Microphone (audio capture)","description":"Asks to turn on the microphone and receive live audio.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen/camera-microphone","content":"<Chips /> Can hear and record live audio from your microphone while the tab is open Asks to turn on the microphone and receive live audio. Gives the site live sound from the room: voice, background talk, ambient noise. Used for calls and dictation; also usable for voice biometrics or covert listening if the tab stays open. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the tab is open\" /> **What the user does:** You click Unmute, Record, or Join call, then choose Allow in the browser pop up Capture live audio fr"},"393":{"title":"Picture-in-Picture and automatic PiP","description":"Pops a video into a small floating window above other apps.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen/camera-picture-in-picture","content":"<Chips /> Can float a video in a small window over your other apps Pops a video into a small floating window above other apps. Floats a video over other apps; with camera access, auto PiP can keep a video call visible when the person switches tabs. Chrome gives a separate prompt for camera-based auto PiP. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One video window\" /> **What the user does:** You click the picture in picture button on a video, or switch tabs during a call Pop a video into a small floating window th"},"394":{"title":"Region Capture and Element Capture","description":"Crops a screen capture to part of the page, no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen/camera-region-element-capture","content":"<Chips /> Can crop a screen share to one part of the page, with no extra prompt Crops a screen capture to part of the page, no prompt. Lets an app crop a tab capture to one element and hide the rest, so a recording shows only that element. Cannot reach beyond the tab capture the person already allowed. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A piece of one shared tab\" /> **What the user does:** You are already sharing the tab, then the app limits the view to one panel Crop your shared tab to show only one box o"},"395":{"title":"Audio output device selection (selectAudioOutput)","description":"Lets a site pick which speaker or headphones play audio.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen/camera-speaker-selection","content":"<Chips /> Can send sound to the speaker or headphones you pick Lets a site pick which speaker or headphones play audio. Lets a site list and choose speakers. The chooser shows device names only after a pick, because output names can reveal paired Bluetooth gear and hardware. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One chosen speaker\" /> **What the user does:** You click Choose speaker in a call or music page and pick a device from the list Play audio through the output device you choose Remember which output de"},"396":{"title":"Web Speech recognition","description":"Turns microphone audio into text.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen/camera-speech-recognition","content":"<Chips /> Can turn your spoken words into text while you speak and the tab is open Turns microphone audio into text. Uses the microphone to transcribe speech. In Chrome the audio is sent to a Google service by default, so spoken words leave the device unless on-device recognition is chosen. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While you dictate\" /> **What the user does:** You click the microphone or Dictate button and allow the microphone Turn your speech into text as you talk Use the words to fill a box, se"},"397":{"title":"HTML <usermedia> element (camera and microphone)","description":"A browser-drawn button that asks for camera or microphone; the click is the request.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Camera, mic and screen"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen/camera-usermedia-element","content":"<Chips /> Can ask for camera or microphone through a button the browser draws itself A browser-drawn button that asks for camera or microphone; the click is the request. Same power as getUserMedia, but the request starts from a button the site cannot disguise. Aims to cut prompt spam and make recovery after a block possible. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One click, then the usual prompt\" /> **What the user does:** You click the browser made Use camera or Use microphone button on the page Show a browse"},"398":{"title":"Camera, mic and screen","description":"Seeing, hearing and recording. 12 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen","content":"Seeing, hearing and recording. 12 capabilities in this group. | Capability                                        | Kind           | In one line                                          |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| Audio output device selection (selectAudioOutput) | Asks you first | Can send sound to the speaker or headphones you pick | | Capability             | Kind           | In one line                                                          |\n| ---------------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Camera (video capture) | Asks you first | Can see and record live video from your camera while the tab is open | | Capability                        | Kind           | In one line                                                                  |\n| --------------------------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Camera pan, tilt and zoom control | Asks you first | Can move or zoom a motorized camera, once the site already has camera access | | Capability                                                | Kind           | In one line                                                              |\n| --------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Captured Surface Control (scroll and zoom the shared tab) | Asks you first | Can scroll and zoom the browser tab it is sharing, if from the same site | | Capability                    | Kind          | In one line                                                                             |\n| ----------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| enumerateDevices label gating | Gets silently | Can count your cameras, mics and speakers, but names stay hidden until you allow access | | Capability                                             | Kind           | In one line                                                                |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| HTML &lt;usermedia&gt; element (camera and microphone) | Asks you first | Can ask for camera or microphone through a button the browser draws itself | | Capability    | Kind                 | In one line                                                            |\n| ------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| MediaRecorder | Works without asking | Can save a camera, mic or screen stream to a file, once you allowed it | | Capability                 | Kind           | In one line                                                               |\n| -------------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Microphone (audio capture) | Asks you first | Can hear and record live audio from your microphone while the tab is open | | Capability                           | Kind                          | In one line                                              |\n| ------------------------------------ | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Picture-in-Picture and automatic PiP | Does something on your device | Can float a video in a small window over your other apps | | Capability                         | Kind                 | In one line                                                           |\n| ---------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Region Capture and Element Capture | Works without asking | Can crop a screen share to one part of the page, with no extra prompt | | Capability                     | Kind           | In one line                                                           |\n| ------------------------------ | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Screen, window and tab capture | Asks you first | Can see the screen, window or tab you choose to share, until you stop | | Capability             | Kind           | In one line                                                              |\n| ---------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Web Speech recognition | Asks you first | Can turn your spoken words into text while you speak and the tab is open |"},"399":{"title":"Tracking across apps and sites","description":"IDs and tricks that follow you around. 61 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking","content":"IDs and tricks that follow you around. 61 capabilities in this group. | Capability                                     | Kind              | In one line                                                           |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Ad click IDs (gclid, fbclid, msclkid and more) | Used to track you | Can tag your ad click so a later purchase links back to that exact ad | | Capability                       | Kind              | In one line                                                        |\n| -------------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Ad network retargeting audiences | Used to track you | Can add you to an ad list when you visit a site, so ads follow you | | Capability                          | Kind              | In one line                                                      |\n| ----------------------------------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Attribution Reporting API (retired) | Used to track you | Can't measure which ad click led to a purchase in Chrome anymore | | Capability                  | Kind          | In one line                                                         |\n| --------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Bounce tracking mitigations | Gets silently | Can wipe stored data of sites you only passed through on a redirect | | Capability                                        | Kind          | In one line                                                                            |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| California privacy law (CCPA, AB 566, Delete Act) | Gets silently | Can let California residents opt out of sale and sharing, and require a browser signal | | Capability                | Kind                 | In one line                                                                     |\n| ------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| CHIPS partitioned cookies | Works without asking | Can keep a cookie for an embedded service, separate for each site it appears on | | Capability                             | Kind              | In one line                                                                         |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Click ID and query parameter stripping | Used to track you | Can remove known tracking codes from links before the site sees them, in some modes | | Capability     | Kind              | In one line                                                                  |\n| -------------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| CNAME cloaking | Used to track you | Can hide a tracker as part of the site's own address so it looks first party | | Capability                                | Kind              | In one line                                                               |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Conversions API (server to server events) | Used to track you | Can send your purchases and sign ups to LinkedIn from a company's servers | | Capability                          | Kind              | In one line                                                                       |\n| ----------------------------------- | ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Conversions APIs (server to server) | Used to track you | Can send your purchases and sign ups to ad platforms from the advertiser's server | | Capability     | Kind              | In one line                                                       |\n| -------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Cookie syncing | Used to track you | Can let ad companies swap IDs so each recognizes you across sites | | Capability        | Kind          | In one line                                                 |\n| ----------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| document.referrer | Gets silently | Can tell which page you came from the moment the page loads | | Capability        | Kind              | In one line                                                  |\n| ----------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Email open pixels | Used to track you | Can tell a sender when and roughly where you opened an email | | Capability                                        | Kind              | In one line                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Enhanced matching (hashed email from the browser) | Used to track you | Can send a scrambled version of your email to LinkedIn to match you to a member | | Capability                       | Kind                 | In one line                                                         |\n| -------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Fenced Frames (being phased out) | Works without asking | Can't fully show an ad in a sealed frame, as Chrome makes it a stub | | Capability      | Kind                 | In one lin"},"400":{"title":"Conversions API (server to server events)","description":"Advertisers send purchase and lead events straight from their servers with hashed identifiers.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/tracking-linkedin-conversions-api","content":"<Chips /> Can send your purchases and sign ups to LinkedIn from a company's servers Advertisers send purchase and lead events straight from their servers with hashed identifiers. Moves tracking off the browser where blockers and cookie limits apply. LinkedIn matches events to members using hashed email, click ID, IP hash and other IDs. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Every conversion the advertiser sends\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing on your side. It happens after you buy or sign up on an advertis"},"401":{"title":"LinkedIn ad sync cookies (UserMatchHistory, AnalyticsSyncHistory, lms_ads, li_sugr, _guid)","description":"Cookies that sync LinkedIn IDs with ad partners and match visitors by probability.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/tracking-linkedin-cookies-ad-sync","content":"<Chips /> Can match your browser to ad partners' records so ads can follow you Cookies that sync LinkedIn IDs with ad partners and match visitors by probability. Keep identifier matches between LinkedIn and ad tech partners. li\\_sugr (90 days) is a probabilistic match for visitors who are not logged in; \\_guid (90 days) links to Google Ads. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"A stable ID across sites\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you visit LinkedIn or a site that uses LinkedIn ads Sto"},"402":{"title":"LinkedIn anti-abuse cookies (li_bapfcc, li_odapfcc, li_apfcdc, spectroscopyId, fid, fcookie)","description":"Cookies that control and carry the fingerprint and extension scans, disclosed in LinkedIn's own table.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/tracking-linkedin-cookies-antiabuse","content":"<Chips /> Can check your browser for bots and add a device fingerprint to your visit Cookies that control and carry the fingerprint and extension scans, disclosed in LinkedIn's own table. First party confirmation that LinkedIn collects 'abuse prevention features' from the member's device and checks for extensions. The Cookie Policy text itself does not mention fingerprinting or extensions. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your browser's traits, each visit\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens w"},"403":{"title":"LinkedIn identity and session cookies (bcookie, bscookie, lidc, li_gc, li_mc)","description":"Cookies that give each browser and member a stable ID on LinkedIn and on sites with its tag.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/tracking-linkedin-cookies-identity","content":"<Chips /> Can give your browser and account a steady ID on LinkedIn and partner sites Cookies that give each browser and member a stable ID on LinkedIn and on sites with its tag. bcookie and bscookie identify the browser for one year, lidc routes traffic for 24 hours, li\\_gc and li\\_mc store consent and marketing state for 6 months. They link logged out and logged in activity. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"A stable ID that follows you\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you open Linke"},"404":{"title":"Enhanced matching (hashed email from the browser)","description":"The tag hashes an email address in the browser and sends the hash to LinkedIn to match a member.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/tracking-linkedin-enhanced-matching","content":"<Chips /> Can send a scrambled version of your email to LinkedIn to match you to a member The tag hashes an email address in the browser and sends the hash to LinkedIn to match a member. Turns an anonymous visitor into a known LinkedIn member when their email is on the advertiser's form. SHA-256 is deterministic, so the hash is still an identifier. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your email's hash, once matched\" /> **What the user does:** You type or autofill your email on a site that uses the LinkedIn "},"405":{"title":"HUMAN Security (PerimeterX) hidden iframe","description":"A hidden iframe from li.protechts.net runs HUMAN's bot detection with a hash of LinkedIn's browser ID.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/tracking-linkedin-human-px-iframe","content":"<Chips /> Can run a hidden bot check on LinkedIn pages and tie it to its browser ID A hidden iframe from li.protechts.net runs HUMAN's bot detection with a hash of LinkedIn's browser ID. A third-party vendor runs its own behavior and device checks in the page and sets its own cookies, tied to the person through a hash of LinkedIn's bcookie. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Each LinkedIn page visit\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a LinkedIn page loads Load a hidden frame from a securi"},"406":{"title":"LinkedIn Insight Tag (conversion pixel on other sites)","description":"A script and pixel on advertisers' sites that reports visits and actions back to LinkedIn.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/tracking-linkedin-insight-tag","content":"<Chips /> Can tell LinkedIn when you visit or act on an advertiser's site A script and pixel on advertisers' sites that reports visits and actions back to LinkedIn. Lets LinkedIn see which pages a person visits across other sites, tie it to their member profile if logged in, and use it for ads, retargeting and measurement. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Pages and actions on tagged sites\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you open a page that has the LinkedIn tag Report each page you v"},"407":{"title":"li_fat_id first party click ID","description":"A click ID added to ad landing page URLs and saved as a first party cookie.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/tracking-linkedin-li-fat-id","content":"<Chips /> Can tag your visit from a LinkedIn ad click with an ID the site saves A click ID added to ad landing page URLs and saved as a first party cookie. Survives third party cookie blocking and lets the advertiser's tag and API tie a later purchase to a LinkedIn ad click and member. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"One ad click, kept for weeks\" /> **What the user does:** You click a LinkedIn ad and land on the advertiser's page Add a click ID to the ad link's web address Save that ID as a cookie on th"},"408":{"title":"Matched Audiences (website retargeting and list upload)","description":"Advertisers build ad audiences from site visitors or from uploaded email lists.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/tracking-linkedin-matched-audiences","content":"<Chips /> Can put you on an ad list after a site visit or an uploaded email Advertisers build ad audiences from site visitors or from uploaded email lists. Visits on other sites and email lists are turned into ad audiences. Lists are hashed (SHA-256) in the browser or uploaded as hashes. Needs at least 300 matched members. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Lists built from visits or emails\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you visit a tagged site or give an email to an advertiser Add vi"},"409":{"title":"Merchant Pool device fingerprint script (Microsoft DFP)","description":"Loads mdt.js from merchantpool1.linkedin.com and runs a device fingerprint call.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/tracking-linkedin-merchantpool-dfp","content":"<Chips /> Can collect device traits from a Microsoft fingerprint script on LinkedIn pages Loads mdt.js from merchantpool1.linkedin.com and runs a device fingerprint call. A second device fingerprinting layer, apparently Microsoft's fraud protection technology (inferred from cookie names and a doFpt call), keyed to LinkedIn's browser ID. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your device's traits\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a LinkedIn page loads the script Load a script from a LinkedIn "},"410":{"title":"reCAPTCHA v3 Enterprise on every page","description":"Google reCAPTCHA v3 Enterprise scores each page load without any visible challenge.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/tracking-linkedin-recaptcha-enterprise","content":"<Chips /> Can score every page visit for bot behavior without showing a challenge Google reCAPTCHA v3 Enterprise scores each page load without any visible challenge. Sends page behavior and device signals to Google to get a bot risk score. Invisible to the person; Google sets its own cookies and receives IP and browser data. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Each LinkedIn page visit\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens each time a LinkedIn page loads Watch how the page is used to judge if you a"},"411":{"title":"LinkedIn telemetry endpoint (/li/track)","description":"A single endpoint receives batched interaction and fingerprint events from the page.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/tracking-linkedin-telemetry-li-track","content":"<Chips /> Can send batches of your clicks and device traits from LinkedIn pages to LinkedIn A single endpoint receives batched interaction and fingerprint events from the page. Carries tracking events including AedEvent, SpectroscopyEvent and the encrypted fingerprint in batches, so one request can hold many data points. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your activity on LinkedIn\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as you use LinkedIn Collect your clicks and other page interactions Send them i"},"412":{"title":"Third party ID sync cookies in LinkedIn's table","description":"Ad partner cookies (Adobe, ID5, Xandr, Yahoo, Microsoft, Google) set through LinkedIn's ad stack.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/tracking-linkedin-third-party-id-sync","content":"<Chips /> Can set partner cookies that let ad companies match you to their records Ad partner cookies (Adobe, ID5, Xandr, Yahoo, Microsoft, Google) set through LinkedIn's ad stack. Shows LinkedIn acting as a demand side platform: other ad networks write IDs that LinkedIn uses to resolve people across sites. Observation data is kept at most 90 days per LinkedIn. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"IDs shared across ad partners\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you visit LinkedIn or a site "},"413":{"title":"Hyperlink ping attribute","description":"A link attribute that makes the browser send a POST to extra URLs when the link is clicked.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-a-ping","content":"<Chips /> Can tell extra web addresses when you click a link, without you noticing A link attribute that makes the browser send a POST to extra URLs when the link is clicked. Lets a site or ad network count clicks without a redirect or script. Body is the text PING, with Ping-From and Ping-To headers. Firefox ships it disabled by default, and blockers and Brave drop it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One click at a time\" /> **What the user does:** You click a link that has a ping address attached Send "},"414":{"title":"Ad click IDs (gclid, fbclid, msclkid and more)","description":"Unique IDs ad platforms add to links so a later purchase can be matched to the exact ad click.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-ad-click-ids","content":"<Chips /> Can tag your ad click so a later purchase links back to that exact ad Unique IDs ad platforms add to links so a later purchase can be matched to the exact ad click. A click ID ties one person's click on an ad to later conversions, even across sessions, and is stored in a first-party cookie (for example Google \\_gcl\\_aw about 90 days, Meta \\_fbc about 90 days). iOS ATT era gbraid/wbraid are aggregated. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"One ad click, matched later\" /> **What the user does:** You c"},"415":{"title":"Attribution Reporting API (retired)","description":"Browser measured which ad click led to a purchase. Deprecated Chrome 144; removal under way in 2026.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-attribution-reporting","content":"<Chips /> Can't measure which ad click led to a purchase in Chrome anymore Browser measured which ad click led to a purchase. Deprecated Chrome 144; removal under way in 2026. Matched ad clicks or views to later conversions with noised event reports (3 bits click data for views) or encrypted aggregate reports. Google retired it in Oct 2025 and plans a W3C PATWG attribution standard instead. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Retired, one click and one report\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It used to "},"416":{"title":"Bounce tracking mitigations","description":"Browsers delete the stored data of sites the person only passed through on a redirect.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-bounce-tracking-mitigations","content":"<Chips /> Can wipe stored data of sites you only passed through on a redirect Browsers delete the stored data of sites the person only passed through on a redirect. Defeats the tracker that bounces a person through its own domain to set a first-party cookie. Chrome checks about hourly and spares sites visited with interaction in the past 45 days. Safari has classified bounce trackers since 2017. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Redirect only sites, after a wait\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Your b"},"417":{"title":"California privacy law (CCPA, AB 566, Delete Act)","description":"California rules that let residents opt out of sale and sharing and that require browsers to offer a signal.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-california-privacy-law","content":"<Chips /> Can let California residents opt out of sale and sharing, and require a browser signal California rules that let residents opt out of sale and sharing and that require browsers to offer a signal. CCPA fines are \\$2,663 per violation and \\$7,988 if intentional or involving minors (2025 amounts). AB 566, signed Oct 8 2025, requires browsers to offer an opt out signal from Jan 1 2027. The Delete Act DROP tool opened Jan 2026. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Covers residents who use it\" /> **What "},"418":{"title":"CHIPS partitioned cookies","description":"Lets an embedded service keep a cookie that is private to each top-level site where it is embedded.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-chips-partitioned-cookies","content":"<Chips /> Can keep a cookie for an embedded service, separate for each site it appears on Lets an embedded service keep a cookie that is private to each top-level site where it is embedded. A widget (chat, maps, payments) can remember state per embedding site without a cross-site ID. Capped at 10 KiB per partition, so it cannot build a cross-site profile. Supported on Chrome 114 and later; Firefox also ships it. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One service on one site\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing."},"419":{"title":"Click ID and query parameter stripping","description":"Browsers remove known tracking parameters from URLs before the site sees them, in some modes.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-click-id-stripping","content":"<Chips /> Can remove known tracking codes from links before the site sees them, in some modes Browsers remove known tracking parameters from URLs before the site sees them, in some modes. Controls whether a site can read the ad click ID at all. Safari strips in Private, Mail and Messages; Firefox in ETP Strict and Private; Brave in all modes by default; Chrome does not strip. Measurement teams lose attribution where stripping applies. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Known codes only, some modes\" /> **Wh"},"420":{"title":"CNAME cloaking","description":"A tracker is served from a subdomain of the site (like metrics.site.com) that points at the tracker's servers.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-cname-cloaking","content":"<Chips /> Can hide a tracker as part of the site's own address so it looks first party A tracker is served from a subdomain of the site (like metrics.site.com) that points at the tracker's servers. Makes tracking look first party so the cookie lasts longer and blockers miss it. Safari caps cookies set by such CNAME or mismatched IP hosts to 7 days. Studies found it on a few percent of top sites (Dimova et al 2021). <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"A tracker disguised as the site\" /> **What the user does:"},"421":{"title":"Conversions APIs (server to server)","description":"Advertisers send purchase and signup events from their server straight to ad platforms.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-conversions-api","content":"<Chips /> Can send your purchases and sign ups to ad platforms from the advertiser's server Advertisers send purchase and signup events from their server straight to ad platforms. Meta, TikTok, LinkedIn, Snap and Google accept server events with hashed email, phone, IP and click IDs, bypassing browser blocking. Meta dedups with event\\_id inside 48 hours. The person sees nothing and the browser cannot stop it. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Every event the company sends\" /> **What the user does:** Nothi"},"422":{"title":"Cookie syncing","description":"Ad companies swap IDs through redirects so each can recognise the same person.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-cookie-syncing","content":"<Chips /> Can let ad companies swap IDs so each recognizes you across sites Ad companies swap IDs through redirects so each can recognise the same person. Vendor A redirects the browser to vendor B with its own ID in the URL, so B stores a mapping A to B. Repeated across dozens of partners it builds the ID graph that real time bidding relies on. Academic crawls found it on a large share of sites. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Matched across many sites\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens wh"},"423":{"title":"document.referrer","description":"Script-readable copy of the Referer, available the moment a page loads.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-document-referrer","content":"<Chips /> Can tell which page you came from the moment the page loads Script-readable copy of the Referer, available the moment a page loads. Same previous-site data as the header, readable by any script on the page including third-party tags. Tag managers use it to label traffic sources; Safari 26 and Brave can blank it for classified tracker scripts. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"The one page before\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a page loads after you clicked a link Tell which"},"424":{"title":"Hashed email as identity","description":"A SHA-256 hash of an email lets ad platforms recognise a person without the plain email.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-email-hash-identity","content":"<Chips /> Can recognize you across sites by a scrambled version of your email A SHA-256 hash of an email lets ad platforms recognise a person without the plain email. Normalised lowercase email hashed with SHA-256 is accepted by Meta, Google, LinkedIn and TikTok for matching. Hashing is deterministic, so the same email gives the same value everywhere. The FTC stated in July 2024 that hashing does not make data anonymous. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"A stable ID tied to your email\" /> **What the user "},"425":{"title":"Email open pixels","description":"A one pixel image in an email that tells the sender when and where the message was opened.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-email-open-pixels","content":"<Chips /> Can tell a sender when and roughly where you opened an email A one pixel image in an email that tells the sender when and where the message was opened. The request reveals open time, device type, rough location by IP and a unique recipient ID. Apple Mail Privacy Protection (iOS 15, Sep 2021) preloads images through proxies, so opens from Apple Mail are inflated and hide the real IP. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Each opened email\" /> **What the user does:** You open an email that loads its i"},"426":{"title":"Fenced Frames (being phased out)","description":"A sandboxed ad frame that cannot talk to the page around it. Chrome is turning it into a stub in 2026.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-fenced-frames","content":"<Chips /> Can't fully show an ad in a sealed frame, as Chrome makes it a stub A sandboxed ad frame that cannot talk to the page around it. Chrome is turning it into a stub in 2026. Embedded content that blocks communication with the embedder so ads chosen from private data cannot leak who saw them. Moved to Deprecate and remove on Aug 14 2026; stub in Chrome 156 (stable Oct 20 2026). <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Being phased out\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It would have happened when an ad l"},"427":{"title":"fetch keepalive","description":"A fetch flag that lets the request finish after the page is gone.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-fetch-keepalive","content":"<Chips /> Can finish sending a request after you leave or close the page A fetch flag that lets the request finish after the page is gone. Replaces sendBeacon when a site needs custom headers or methods on exit. The browser holds the request open past unload, with the same 64 KiB cap. Firefox shipped it in version 133 (Nov 2024). <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A final report, as you leave\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you close or leave a page that has a pending report Complete a"},"428":{"title":"fetchLater","description":"Queues a request now that the browser sends later, even if the page closes first.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-fetchlater","content":"<Chips /> Can queue a report now and have the browser send it later, even after you close Queues a request now that the browser sends later, even if the page closes first. Gives analytics a reliable way to report at page end. The browser sends it after a chosen delay or when the page is discarded or goes into bfcache. Chrome shipped it in version 135 (Apr 2025). <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One queued report\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as you use a page that sets up a delayed repo"},"429":{"title":"Form field capture before submit","description":"Scripts read typed or autofilled form values and can send them before the person presses submit.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-form-field-capture","content":"<Chips /> Can see what you type or autofill in a form before you press submit Scripts read typed or autofilled form values and can send them before the person presses submit. Email and name typed into a newsletter box can reach ad trackers on blur or autofill, even if never submitted. Leaky Forms (USENIX Security 2022) found about 1,800 EU and about 3,000 US crawl sites leaking emails to trackers. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Form fields on that page\" /> **What the user does:** You type or autofill i"},"430":{"title":"Global Privacy Control (GPC)","description":"A browser setting that tells every site the person opts out of sale and sharing.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-global-privacy-control","content":"<Chips /> Can tell every site that you opt out of sale and sharing of your data A browser setting that tells every site the person opts out of sale and sharing. Sent as Sec-GPC: 1 and navigator.globalPrivacyControl. Legally binding opt out in California, Colorado (Jul 2024), Connecticut and Texas (2025). California fined Sephora \\$1.2M in 2022 and Healthline \\$1.55M in 2025 for ignoring it. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Every site, if turned on\" /> **What the user does:** You turn on Global Privacy Co"},"431":{"title":"Google enhanced conversions","description":"Google matches a hashed email or phone from a checkout to a Google account to count the sale.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-google-enhanced-conversions","content":"<Chips /> Can match a scrambled email or phone from your checkout to a Google account Google matches a hashed email or phone from a checkout to a Google account to count the sale. Advertiser sends SHA-256 normalised email, phone, name and address after a conversion. Google matches them to signed in accounts that clicked an ad. It does not need third party cookies and it works after the person leaves. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"One checkout, matched to Google\" /> **What the user does:** You enter yo"},"432":{"title":"Google tag (gtag.js)","description":"Google's page script for analytics and ads; sets IDs like _ga and _gcl_au in the browser.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-google-tag","content":"<Chips /> Can tell Google about your visits and actions on a site that uses the tag Google's page script for analytics and ads; sets IDs like \\_ga and \\_gcl\\_au in the browser. \\_ga (2 years, capped at 400 days in Chrome) joins visits into one user, \\_gid lasts 24 hours, \\_gcl\\_au 90 days for ad conversions. Consent Mode v2 changes what is sent if the person declines. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your visits to tagged sites\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you open a page with the"},"433":{"title":"Global Privacy Platform string","description":"A coded record of a person's privacy choices for US states and other regions.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-gpp","content":"<Chips /> Can carry your privacy choices for US states and other regions to ad partners A coded record of a person's privacy choices for US states and other regions. GPP replaces the US Privacy string. Its sections carry US national and state opt outs: section 7 for US national and 8 for California. Ad vendors read it via \\_\\_gpp() to decide whether to sell or share data. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your choices, per site\" /> **What the user does:** You pick choices in a cookie banner or click an op"},"434":{"title":"Heatmaps and scroll depth","description":"A script logs where people click, move and scroll so a color map of attention is built.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-heatmaps","content":"<Chips /> Can log where you click, move and scroll on a page to draw attention maps A script logs where people click, move and scroll so a color map of attention is built. Shows which parts of the page attract clicks, how far people scroll (in 25 percent steps often) and rage clicks. Usually aggregated, but raw events are tied to a visitor ID and can feed session replay. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your actions on that page\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as you use a page with a hea"},"435":{"title":"Universal IDs: UID2, ID5, RampID","description":"Shared advertising IDs derived from login email so ad partners can match people without cookies.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-id-graphs","content":"<Chips /> Can give you a shared ad ID based on your login email, with no cookie Shared advertising IDs derived from login email so ad partners can match people without cookies. A publisher sends a hashed or encrypted email at login; the ID vendor returns a token that ad platforms accept. UID2 (The Trade Desk, open source), ID5 and LiveRamp RampID let a person be recognised across sites and apps whenever they log in. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"A stable ID across many sites\" /> **What the user does:*"},"436":{"title":"IP address and device matching","description":"Sites and ad vendors link visits and devices by shared IP address and basic device traits.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-ip-address-matching","content":"<Chips /> Can link visits and devices that share your network address and basic device traits Sites and ad vendors link visits and devices by shared IP address and basic device traits. A visit's IP reveals city level location and the ISP; matched with user agent and time it joins phone and laptop on the same Wi-Fi into one household. Needs no cookie; breaks with VPN, Private Relay or mobile CGNAT. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Devices on the same network\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens"},"437":{"title":"IP Protection (discontinued)","description":"A planned Chrome proxy to hide IP addresses from trackers. Discontinued per Oct 2025 announcement.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-ip-protection","content":"<Chips /> Can't hide your IP address from trackers in Chrome, since the plan was dropped A planned Chrome proxy to hide IP addresses from trackers. Discontinued per Oct 2025 announcement. Would have routed third-party tracker traffic through two hops of proxies so a site saw a proxy IP, not the person's. The Apr 2025 plan targeted Incognito in Q3 2025. Status page now says Discontinue; the person's IP stays visible to sites. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Discontinued\" /> **What the user does:** Nothin"},"438":{"title":"LinkedIn Insight Tag","description":"LinkedIn's page script that reports visits and conversions on an advertiser's site to LinkedIn.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-linkedin-insight-tag","content":"<Chips /> Can tell LinkedIn about your visits and actions on a site that uses the tag LinkedIn's page script that reports visits and conversions on an advertiser's site to LinkedIn. Sets li\\_fat\\_id (30 days) and reads bcookie (1 year) and lidc (24 hours); can match to a signed in LinkedIn member and send hashed email through its Conversions API. Safari 27 beta is reported to classify it as a fingerprinting tracker. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your visits to tagged sites\" /> **What the user does:** "},"439":{"title":"Meta Pixel","description":"Meta's page script that reports visits and actions on an advertiser's site back to Meta.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-meta-pixel","content":"<Chips /> Can tell Meta about your visits and actions on a site that uses the pixel Meta's page script that reports visits and actions on an advertiser's site back to Meta. Sets \\_fbp (90 days) and \\_fbc (from the fbclid) cookies and sends page, button and form events, plus hashed email or phone if Advanced Matching is on. Lets Meta tie a visit to a Facebook or Instagram account. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your visits to tagged sites\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you open a p"},"440":{"title":"Navigational tracking","description":"Passing IDs through link URLs or redirects when the person moves between sites.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-navigational-tracking","content":"<Chips /> Can pass an ID along when you move between sites by link or redirect Passing IDs through link URLs or redirects when the person moves between sites. Works even with third-party cookies blocked: site A appends an ID to the outbound link, site B reads it and sets a first-party cookie, or a tracker redirect sets its own first-party cookie for a moment. The main workaround to ITP and TCP. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One hop between sites\" /> **What the user does:** You click a link or get redi"},"441":{"title":"Outbound link decoration","description":"Adding an ID to a link's URL so the next site can read it and link the visit.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-outbound-link-decoration","content":"<Chips /> Can add an ID to a link so the next site can recognize your visit Adding an ID to a link's URL so the next site can read it and link the visit. Meta adds fbclid to outbound clicks; Google's linker adds \\_gl with first party cookie IDs valid for a short time (about 2 minutes). The next site turns the ID into its own cookie. Safari caps such cookies at 24 hours, Firefox and Brave strip known params. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One click between sites\" /> **What the user does:** You click a l"},"442":{"title":"pagehide, visibilitychange and unload","description":"Events that tell a page it is being hidden, left or closed so it can save state or send a last report.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-page-lifecycle-events","content":"<Chips /> Can tell a page when you hide or leave it, so it can send a report Events that tell a page it is being hidden, left or closed so it can save state or send a last report. visibilitychange and pagehide are the reliable last chance for a final send. unload is being retired: Chrome is deprecating it with a Permissions-Policy and completing the change in late 2026. beforeunload is unreliable on mobile. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"As you leave a page\" /> **What the user does:** You switch tabs, "},"443":{"title":"Private Aggregation API (removed)","description":"Noisy aggregate reports from Protected Audience and Shared Storage. Removed in Chrome 152 (Aug 2026).","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-private-aggregation","content":"<Chips /> Can't send noisy ad totals from Chrome anymore, since it was removed in August 2026 Noisy aggregate reports from Protected Audience and Shared Storage. Removed in Chrome 152 (Aug 2026). Worklet code could contribute values to histograms that a server summed with differential privacy noise. Retired Oct 2025, deprecated Chrome 144, removed in Chrome 152 (released Aug 25 2026). The Aggregation Service is scheduled for phaseout. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Removed\" /> **What the user does:** N"},"444":{"title":"Private State Tokens","description":"Anonymous tokens that carry a trust signal across sites without identifying the person.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-private-state-tokens","content":"<Chips /> Can carry a trust signal between sites without telling them who you are Anonymous tokens that carry a trust signal across sites without identifying the person. An issuer vouches that a visitor looks human and later redeemers read that single bit, unlinked to identity. Chrome lists it as Continue to support after Oct 2025. Issuer limit: 2 issuers per origin used per top frame (soft cap). <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A trust signal, no name\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when"},"445":{"title":"Protected Audience (FLEDGE, retired)","description":"Browser run ad auctions for retargeting. Retirement announced Oct 2025; removal rolling out to 100% in Oct 2026.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-protected-audience","content":"<Chips /> Can't run ads in Chrome based on your interests, since the plan was retired Browser run ad auctions for retargeting. Retirement announced Oct 2025; removal rolling out to 100% in Oct 2026. Let advertisers store interest groups in the browser and run an on-device auction, keeping the audience list local. Joins fell almost 100x per the Chrome team. Chrome 144 deprecated it; staged removal on stable reaches 100% from Oct 26 2026. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Retired\" /> **What the user does:**"},"446":{"title":"Referer header and Referrer-Policy default","description":"Tells the site which page the person came from, trimmed to the origin for cross-site links by default.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-referer-header","content":"<Chips /> Can tell a site which page you came from, trimmed to the site name cross site Tells the site which page the person came from, trimmed to the origin for cross-site links by default. Reveals the previous site and, for same-origin or same-site hops, the full URL including query strings. Sites use it for attribution; it can leak search terms, IDs and private page paths when sites set weak policies. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"The one page before\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens "},"447":{"title":"Related Website Sets (retired)","description":"A declared group of sites that could share cookies. Removal postponed to Chrome 152 to 153.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-related-website-sets","content":"<Chips /> Can't let a group of related sites share cookies in Chrome anymore A declared group of sites that could share cookies. Removal postponed to Chrome 152 to 153. Let one organisation list a primary plus associated and service sites (up to 5 associated, 3 service) that were treated as same party for storage access prompts. Retired Oct 2025 with requestStorageAccessFor; Related Website Partition discontinued. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Retired\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It would have"},"448":{"title":"Ad network retargeting audiences","description":"Ad pixels add a visitor to lists the site owner can later target with ads elsewhere.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-retargeting-audiences","content":"<Chips /> Can add you to an ad list when you visit a site, so ads follow you Ad pixels add a visitor to lists the site owner can later target with ads elsewhere. A tag fires on page load or product view and adds a cookie or hashed email to an audience (for example Meta Custom Audiences, Google remarketing lists, LinkedIn Matched Audiences). Windows run up to 180 days at Meta and 540 days at Google Ads. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"A list built from your visits\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It "},"449":{"title":"Safari 27 IP based tracker blocking (beta)","description":"Safari 27 beta checks the server IP of outgoing requests and can block known ad tracker ranges.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-safari-27-tracker-ip-blocking","content":"<Chips /> Can block requests to known ad tracker servers by their IP address in Safari 27 beta Safari 27 beta checks the server IP of outgoing requests and can block known ad tracker ranges. Per a July 2026 code review, Safari 27 beta blocks connections by destination IP (found for bat.bing.com) and puts LinkedIn Insight (snap.licdn.com) on the fingerprinting list so it loses query and referrer data. Unverified beyond one secondary report. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Known tracker servers\" /> **What"},"450":{"title":"Search engine referral data loss","description":"Search engines no longer pass the search keywords to the site; only the engine name arrives.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-search-referral-loss","content":"<Chips /> Can't see the search words you used, only the search engine's name Search engines no longer pass the search keywords to the site; only the engine name arrives. Since Oct 2011 Google withholds queries for signed in then all searches ('not provided'). Sites see the origin google.com only and learn keywords through Search Console (up to 1000 rows per query export) or on site search. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Just the engine name\" /> **What the user does:** You click a result on a search eng"},"451":{"title":"navigator.sendBeacon","description":"Sends a small POST that the browser delivers even while the person is leaving the page.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-sendbeacon","content":"<Chips /> Can send a small report that the browser delivers even as you leave the page Sends a small POST that the browser delivers even while the person is leaving the page. Lets analytics report time on page, scroll depth and exit clicks after the tab is closed or navigated away. No response is readable. The person cannot see or stop it, and it carries cookies for the target origin. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A last report, as you leave\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you clo"},"452":{"title":"Server side tagging","description":"The site sends its analytics to its own server first, which relays it to ad vendors.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-server-side-tagging","content":"<Chips /> Can send your activity to the site's own server first, then on to ad vendors The site sends its analytics to its own server first, which relays it to ad vendors. Moves tracking from the browser to the site's cloud container, so blockers cannot see vendor calls and cookies are set by the site's own server. The vendor can still receive IP, user agent, hashed email and click IDs the server chooses to forward. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"What the site chooses to pass on\" /> **What the user doe"},"453":{"title":"Session replay","description":"A script records page changes, mouse, scroll and typing so staff can replay a visit like a video.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-session-replay","content":"<Chips /> Can record your clicks, scrolls and typing on a page so staff can replay it A script records page changes, mouse, scroll and typing so staff can replay a visit like a video. Shows exactly what the person saw and did, including text typed in forms unless masked. A 2017 Princeton study found replay scripts on 482 of the top 50,000 sites leaking data. In 2022 to 2025 US wiretap suits grew. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your whole visit on that site\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happen"},"454":{"title":"Shared Storage API (retired)","description":"Cross-site storage whose values could only be read in a locked worker. Retired Oct 2025.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-shared-storage","content":"<Chips /> Can't store cross site data for ads in Chrome anymore, since it was retired in 2025 Cross-site storage whose values could only be read in a locked worker. Retired Oct 2025. Let scripts write cross-site values (for example a frequency count) and read them only inside a worklet that output through limited channels (selectURL, Private Aggregation). Disabled starting build 153.0.8001.0; code removal follows. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Retired\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It used to ha"},"455":{"title":"Speculation Rules prefetch and prerender","description":"A page can load a likely next page early, so a site may see a request before the person visits.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-speculation-rules-prefetch","content":"<Chips /> Can load a likely next page early, so a site may see a visit first A page can load a likely next page early, so a site may see a request before the person visits. Prefetch or prerender makes the destination server log a hit, and run scripts, before any click, sending Sec-Purpose: prefetch;prerender. Analytics can over count; cookies from a prefetched response apply only on activation. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Pages you might visit next\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens whe"},"456":{"title":"Storage Access API","description":"Lets an embedded frame ask to use its third-party cookies; the browser may prompt or auto-allow.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-storage-access-api","content":"<Chips /> Can ask to use its own cookies when embedded in another site Lets an embedded frame ask to use its third-party cookies; the browser may prompt or auto-allow. Used by embedded SSO, comments and video frames to regain cross-site cookies on Safari, Firefox and Chrome. Safari prompts per embed; Firefox auto-grants after 5 prior interactions and a prompt in other cases; Chrome shows a prompt. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One embedded service\" /> **What the user does:** You click a button in an e"},"457":{"title":"IAB TCF consent string","description":"A coded record of which ad vendors and purposes the person agreed to in the EU consent banner.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-tcf-consent-string","content":"<Chips /> Can see which ad vendors you agreed to in the EU cookie banner A coded record of which ad vendors and purposes the person agreed to in the EU consent banner. Stored in the euconsent-v2 cookie and read through \\_\\_tcfapi. TCF v2.3 requires the disclosedVendors segment, and strings without it were invalid from Feb 28 2026. Consent is valid for up to 13 months. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only choices you made in the banner\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Accept, Reject, or Manage choices"},"458":{"title":"Third-party cookies by browser (Nov 2026)","description":"Whether an embedded site can set and read its own cookies on other sites, which varies by browser.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-third-party-cookies-status","content":"<Chips /> Can recognise you across sites with embedded cookies, unless your browser blocks them Whether an embedded site can set and read its own cookies on other sites, which varies by browser. Third-party cookies are how ad networks recognise the same person across sites. Safari blocks them, Firefox partitions them, Brave blocks them, Chrome leaves them on by default (user choice) and blocks them in Incognito. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Across many sites, unless blocked\" /> **What the user does:*"},"459":{"title":"Topics API (retired)","description":"Chrome's interest-topic API for ads. Deprecated in Chrome 144 and being removed in 2026.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-topics-api","content":"<Chips /> Can't pick your interests from your browsing any more; Chrome is retiring this Chrome's interest-topic API for ads. Deprecated in Chrome 144 and being removed in 2026. Gave ad scripts up to 3 coarse interest topics (one per recent week) from the browsing history of sites that called it. Taxonomy had 469 topics in v2; 5% of results were random noise. Removal rolling out from Chrome 153. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Broad topics only, going away\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It used to"},"460":{"title":"UTM campaign parameters","description":"Tags in the link URL that tell the site which campaign, medium and source sent the person.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Tracking across apps and sites"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/xsite-utm-params","content":"<Chips /> Can see which ad, email, or post sent you, from tags in the link Tags in the link URL that tell the site which campaign, medium and source sent the person. Lets a site tie a visit to a specific ad, email or post. Five standard keys: utm\\_source, utm\\_medium, utm\\_campaign, utm\\_term, utm\\_content. Stored in first-party cookies by analytics tools for the session or longer. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One link, one visit\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a link that carries campaign tags, "},"461":{"title":"Compute Pressure","description":"Reports how busy the device CPU is in four coarse levels.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/compute-compute-pressure","content":"<Chips /> Can tell roughly how busy your device is, only while the page is in view Reports how busy the device CPU is in four coarse levels. Gives nominal, fair, serious, critical CPU pressure states. Lets video-call sites adapt, but also leaks activity on the machine. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Coarse level, while the tab is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. A video call or heavy page may start it while you use the tab Check four coarse busy levels: nominal, fair, serious, critical Slow do"},"462":{"title":"Ad blocker detection","description":"Detects ad blockers via bait elements and filter-list selectors.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-adblock-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if you use an ad blocker and roughly which filter lists Detects ad blockers via bait elements and filter-list selectors. Reveals ad blocker use (about a third of desktop users) and which filter lists (EasyList, AdGuard, Fanboy, regional lists) are on. FingerprintJS probes about 20 lists; list pattern is itself identifying. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A guess, while the page loads\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Test whether bait ads or ad script"},"463":{"title":"Battery Status","description":"Battery level, charging state and time estimates, without a prompt on Chromium.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-battery-status","content":"<Chips /> Can see your battery level and charging state, with no prompt in Chrome Battery level, charging state and time estimates, without a prompt on Chromium. Shows how drained a phone or laptop is. Researchers showed level plus charge time works as a short-term tracking ID. Site could raise prices for low battery or tell laptop from desktop. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Current battery, while the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page runs the check Check how f"},"464":{"title":"Battery status","description":"Reads charge level and charging state where the browser still offers it.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-battery","content":"<Chips /> Can see your battery level and charging state in Chrome and Edge Reads charge level and charging state where the browser still offers it. Level and times (level, charging, chargingTime, dischargingTime) once enabled short term tracking across sites. Firefox removed it; Chrome limits it to secure contexts. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Current battery, while the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. No pop up is shown Check your charge level and whether you are plugged in See time"},"465":{"title":"Browser flavor globals","description":"Checks for window objects only certain browsers define (chrome, safari, __gCrWeb, opr).","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-browser-flavor-globals","content":"<Chips /> Can tell which browser you really use, even when the label is changed Checks for window objects only certain browsers define (chrome, safari, \\_\\_gCrWeb, opr). Identifies Chrome vs Edge vs Opera vs Safari vs in-app webviews even when the UA lies. FingerprintJS tests about 30 flavor names; spoofing tools often miss one. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Browser type, while the page loads\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Check for tell tale items that only Chrome"},"466":{"title":"Client Hints: architecture and bitness","description":"CPU family (x86 or ARM) and 32 or 64 bit, via high-entropy Client Hints.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-ch-arch-bitness","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if your chip is Intel, AMD, or ARM, and 32 or 64 bit CPU family (x86 or ARM) and 32 or 64 bit, via high-entropy Client Hints. Reveals Apple Silicon or Windows on ARM vs Intel or AMD, and bitness. Useful to serve the right binary or WebAssembly build; also splits users into hardware cohorts. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Chip family, only when the site asks\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The site asks and Chromium answers without a prompt See whether you have an Apple Silicon, "},"467":{"title":"Client Hint: form factors","description":"Device class list: Desktop, Mobile, Tablet, XR, Automotive, EInk, Watch.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-ch-form-factors","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if you use a desktop, phone, tablet, watch, or car screen Device class list: Desktop, Mobile, Tablet, XR, Automotive, EInk, Watch. Newer replacement for guessing from the mobile flag and screen size. Lets a site serve car, e-reader, VR or tablet layouts. Some users report more than one factor. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Device type, only when the site asks\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The site asks and Chromium answers without a prompt See device types such as Desktop, Mo"},"468":{"title":"Client Hint: device model","description":"Android phone or tablet model name, only when the site asks.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-ch-model","content":"<Chips /> Can see the model name of your Android phone or tablet when it asks Android phone or tablet model name, only when the site asks. Exposes the exact handset (Pixel 8, SM-S928B). Informs device-specific bugs and ad targeting. iOS never exposes a model; desktop returns an empty string. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Phone model, only when asked\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The site asks and Chrome on Android answers without a prompt Learn the exact model, such as Pixel 8 or a Samsung code"},"469":{"title":"Client Hint: platform version","description":"Exact operating system version, via a high-entropy Client Hint.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-ch-platform-version","content":"<Chips /> Can tell your exact Windows or macOS version when it asks Exact operating system version, via a high-entropy Client Hint. Only way left on Chromium to tell Windows 10 from 11 (major 13 or higher means 11) or macOS 14 from 15. Lets sites ship OS-specific features or target upgrade prompts. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"OS version, only when asked\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The site asks and Chromium answers without a prompt Tell Windows 10 from Windows 11 (major 13 or higher means 1"},"470":{"title":"Codec support probing","description":"canPlayType and isTypeSupported answers across dozens of codecs.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-codec-support","content":"<Chips /> Can test which video and audio formats your browser can play canPlayType and isTypeSupported answers across dozens of codecs. Pattern of supported codecs (H.264, HEVC, AV1, VP9, AC-3, Opus, FLAC) separates browser build and OS. Chrome without proprietary codecs and Chromium builds stand out. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Format list, while the page runs\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a page checks for video or audio support Ask whether your browser plays formats like H."},"471":{"title":"Legacy cpuClass and oscpu","description":"Old non-standard strings naming the CPU class and operating system CPU.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-cpu-class-oscpu","content":"<Chips /> Can sometimes see an old CPU and OS string, mostly in Firefox Old non-standard strings naming the CPU class and operating system CPU. cpuClass (old Internet Explorer) and oscpu (Firefox) mostly return nothing in Chrome, which is itself a signal for which browser engine runs. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One old text string\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a page checks the old browser fields Get the oscpu text in Firefox Notice when Chrome returns nothing, which hints at"},"472":{"title":"CSS system colors and system fonts","description":"Computed values of system color keywords and font names reveal OS and theme.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-css-system-colors-fonts","content":"<Chips /> Can guess your OS and theme from how system colors and fonts look Computed values of system color keywords and font names reveal OS and theme. getComputedStyle on keywords like ActiveText, Canvas, ButtonFace, and system-ui fonts returns OS-specific RGB values and font names. CreepJS uses them to detect OS, theme and spoofing. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"OS look and theme, on page load\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Check the colors of built in items lik"},"473":{"title":"Approximate device RAM","description":"RAM in gigabytes, rounded down to a power of two and capped at 8.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-device-memory","content":"<Chips /> Can see roughly how much RAM you have, rounded and capped at 8 GB RAM in gigabytes, rounded down to a power of two and capped at 8. Lets a site serve a light page to low-memory phones. Rounded to 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4 or 8 GB, so a 32 GB workstation and an 8 GB laptop look identical. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A rounded RAM bucket\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads in Chrome or Edge See memory as 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4, or 8 GB Send a lighter page to low memory p"},"474":{"title":"DevTools open detection","description":"Heuristics that guess when the browser developer tools are open.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-devtools-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can guess when your browser developer tools are open, but often gets it wrong Heuristics that guess when the browser developer tools are open. Used by anti-scraping and anti-cheat code. Methods: window outer minus inner size over about 160 px, console getter traps, debugger timing. Easily false-positive when docked or zoomed. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A guess, while the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It runs in the background while the page is open Guess from window si"},"475":{"title":"Extension presence probing","description":"Detects installed Chrome extensions by fetching their exposed files.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-extension-probing","content":"<Chips /> Can find out which Chrome extensions you have by testing known files Detects installed Chrome extensions by fetching their exposed files. Learns which extensions the person runs: job tools, ad blockers, accessibility aids, religious or political filters. LinkedIn probes about 6,236 IDs, mapping the result to a named member. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Which extensions, without asking\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads in Chrome or Edge Test thousands of ext"},"476":{"title":"Gamepad presence","description":"Lists connected game controllers, but only after a button press on the controller.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-gamepad-presence","content":"<Chips /> Can see a game controller only after you press a button on it Lists connected game controllers, but only after a button press on the controller. Reveals a controller is plugged in or paired (id string includes vendor and product IDs). Hidden until the user presses a button, so it is not a silent probe. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only after a button press\" /> **What the user does:** You press a button on a connected controller while the page is open List connected controllers, including ma"},"477":{"title":"CPU logical core count","description":"Number of logical processors the browser may use.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-hardware-concurrency","content":"<Chips /> Can see how many processor threads your device has Number of logical processors the browser may use. Splits users into low, mid and high end hardware (2, 4, 8, 12, 16 or more threads). Sites use it to size worker pools; trackers use it as a stable hardware trait. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A thread count, on page load\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Count your logical processor threads, such as 4, 8, or 16 Size how much work the page runs at once Sort y"},"478":{"title":"Private browsing detection","description":"Infers whether the browser is in a private or incognito window.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-incognito-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can often tell if you are in a private window, until browsers close the gaps Infers whether the browser is in a private or incognito window. Sites use it for metered paywalls and fraud scoring. Browsers keep closing the leaks (quota size, filesystem API, IndexedDB errors), so any method has a short life. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A guess, on page load\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Check storage size and errors that differ in private mode Block a mete"},"479":{"title":"Installed fonts via measurement","description":"Detects which fonts are installed by measuring text width, no permission needed.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-installed-fonts","content":"<Chips /> Can tell which fonts you have installed by measuring text, with no prompt Detects which fonts are installed by measuring text width, no permission needed. Shows OS, office suites and design tools installed (Adobe, Microsoft Office add fonts). FingerprintJS tests dozens of fonts against 3 fallbacks; list entropy was 13.9 bits in 2016. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Fonts it tests for, silently\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Test dozens of font names to see "},"480":{"title":"Media Capabilities","description":"Which video and audio formats decode smoothly and power-efficiently on this device.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-media-capabilities","content":"<Chips /> Can ask how well your device plays each video and audio format Which video and audio formats decode smoothly and power-efficiently on this device. Reveals hardware decoder generation, HDR support and codecs (AV1, HEVC, VP9, Dolby) and so GPU and OS tier. Sites use it to pick streams; it also fingerprints the device. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Playback abilities, on page load\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a video page or player loads Check which formats play smoothly"},"481":{"title":"Media device kinds and counts","description":"Counts of cameras, microphones and speakers, without names, before any permission.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-mediadevices-count","content":"<Chips /> Can count your cameras, mics, and speakers without names before you allow access Counts of cameras, microphones and speakers, without names, before any permission. Tells a site whether the device has a camera and mic and how many outputs. Without permission labels and device IDs are empty, but kinds and counts leak a hardware profile (laptop vs headset vs desktop). <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Counts only, until you allow it\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a page checks"},"482":{"title":"Legacy navigator properties (appName, appVersion, platform, vendor ...)","description":"Frozen strings such as appName, appVersion, appCodeName, platform, product, productSub, vendor and vendorSub.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-navigator-legacy-properties","content":"<Chips /> Can see old fixed labels naming your browser maker, OS family, and engine Frozen strings such as appName, appVersion, appCodeName, platform, product, productSub, vendor and vendorSub. Mostly frozen for compatibility, but platform and vendor still differ by OS and engine. Used for consistency checks against the user agent. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A few frozen labels\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Check old browser strings like appName, platform, and "},"483":{"title":"navigator.oscpu, vendor, productSub","description":"Legacy engine strings: oscpu (Firefox), vendor, vendorSub, productSub, cpuClass.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-navigator-oscpu-vendor","content":"<Chips /> Can see old engine labels that tell Chrome, Safari, and Firefox apart Legacy engine strings: oscpu (Firefox), vendor, vendorSub, productSub, cpuClass. Quick tells for the rendering engine and OS. vendor is Google Inc., Apple Computer, Inc. or empty (Firefox); productSub is 20030107 on Chromium and Safari, 20100101 on Firefox. Used to catch spoofed browsers. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A few frozen labels\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Check vendor, prod"},"484":{"title":"navigator.platform","description":"Legacy string naming the OS family such as Win32, MacIntel, Linux x86_64 or iPhone.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-navigator-platform","content":"<Chips /> Can see a short label for your OS family, like Win32, MacIntel, or iPhone Legacy string naming the OS family such as Win32, MacIntel, Linux x86\\_64 or iPhone. Coarse OS signal that is deprecated but still returned. Often frozen to a fixed value (Win32 on 64-bit Windows, MacIntel on Apple Silicon), so it carries little entropy but reveals spoofed user agents when it disagrees. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One short OS label\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads "},"485":{"title":"PDF viewer enabled","description":"Whether the browser shows PDFs inline instead of downloading them.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-pdf-viewer-enabled","content":"<Chips /> Can see if your browser shows PDFs inline instead of downloading them Whether the browser shows PDFs inline instead of downloading them. A single boolean that replaced plugin enumeration. False on some mobile browsers and on users who disabled the viewer. Low entropy but common in bot checks. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Check one yes or no setting Decide to embed a PDF or offer a download link Use it with oth"},"486":{"title":"JS heap size","description":"Chromium reports JavaScript heap size limits, which track device RAM.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-performance-memory","content":"<Chips /> Can see how much memory the page can use, which hints at your RAM class Chromium reports JavaScript heap size limits, which track device RAM. jsHeapSizeLimit is about 2 to 4 GB on 64-bit desktops and 0.5 to 1 GB on low-end phones, bucketed in Chrome, so it hints at RAM class even though deviceMemory is capped at 8. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A rough memory class, in Chrome\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page runs in Chrome or Edge Check the JavaScript memory limi"},"487":{"title":"navigator.plugins and mimeTypes","description":"List of browser plugins and MIME types; now a fixed list of 5 PDF viewers.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-plugins-mimetypes","content":"<Chips /> Can see a fixed list of 5 PDF viewers, so it no longer tells you apart List of browser plugins and MIME types; now a fixed list of 5 PDF viewers. Once the strongest fingerprint (15.4 bits, Flash and Java era). Since Chrome 94 and Firefox 99 it returns a hardcoded PDF list, so it now mostly flags headless or odd browsers. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A fixed list for everyone\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Get the same hard coded list of PDF viewers Spot "},"488":{"title":"Secure context and cross-origin isolation","description":"Flags telling whether the page is HTTPS and isolated from other origins.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-secure-context-isolation","content":"<Chips /> Can check whether the page is on HTTPS and isolated, which describes the site, not you Flags telling whether the page is HTTPS and isolated from other origins. isSecureContext and crossOriginIsolated are set by the site itself, so they mostly describe the page, not the person. They gate high-power APIs and timer precision (5 microseconds when isolated, 100 microseconds otherwise in Chrome). <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Page setup, not you\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It describes ho"},"489":{"title":"Speech synthesis voices","description":"Installed text-to-speech voices, which reveal OS, version and installed language packs.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-speech-voices","content":"<Chips /> Can list the speech voices on your device, which hint at your OS and languages Installed text-to-speech voices, which reveal OS, version and installed language packs. macOS lists 100+ voices, Windows 10 to 30, Android per Google TTS pack. Exact list shows OS version and which languages the person installed, a strong stable fingerprint. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Voice list, silently\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads or speaks text List installed voices, f"},"490":{"title":"Storage quota estimate","description":"Reports how much storage the origin may use, which tracks total disk size.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-storage-estimate","content":"<Chips /> Can see how much storage the site may use, which tracks your disk size Reports how much storage the origin may use, which tracks total disk size. Chrome and Safari set the quota near 60% of total disk, so quota reveals disk size within a few GB, and Incognito shows a much smaller number. Also usage shows how much the site has already stored. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A rough disk size class\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads See the storage quota, about 6"},"491":{"title":"Storage and cookie availability flags","description":"Tests whether cookies, localStorage, sessionStorage and IndexedDB work.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-storage-feature-flags","content":"<Chips /> Can check whether cookies and site storage work in your browser Tests whether cookies, localStorage, sessionStorage and IndexedDB work. Four booleans plus openDatabase and addBehavior legacy checks. Almost always all true, so low entropy, but they flag locked-down privacy modes and blocked storage. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A few yes or no checks\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Test cookies, localStorage, sessionStorage, and IndexedDB Spot a locked dow"},"492":{"title":"Touch support","description":"How many simultaneous touches the screen supports and whether touch events exist.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-touch-support","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if your screen takes touch and how many fingers at once How many simultaneous touches the screen supports and whether touch events exist. Separates phone and tablet from desktop, and touch laptops from plain ones. maxTouchPoints is 0 on mouse-only devices, 5 on most phones, 10 on many tablets and touch PCs. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Touch type, on page load\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads See the number of touch points, often 0, 5, or 10 Tell "},"493":{"title":"UA Client Hints, high entropy","description":"Detailed device and browser versions a site can request with Accept-CH or a JavaScript call.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-ua-client-hints-high","content":"<Chips /> Can see detailed device and browser versions, but only if the site asks Detailed device and browser versions a site can request with Accept-CH or a JavaScript call. Returns exact OS version, CPU, bitness, device model and full browser version. Not sent unless the site asks. The browser can decline, but Chrome currently answers without a prompt. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Device detail, only when asked\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The site asks and Chromium answers without a prompt"},"494":{"title":"UA Client Hints, low entropy","description":"Browser brand list, mobile flag and OS name that Chromium sends by default.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-ua-client-hints-low","content":"<Chips /> Can see your browser brand, whether it is mobile, and your OS name Browser brand list, mobile flag and OS name that Chromium sends by default. Three coarse hints (Sec-CH-UA, Sec-CH-UA-Mobile, Sec-CH-UA-Platform) replace most of the old User-Agent. They split the audience into mobile vs desktop and Windows vs macOS vs Android with almost no tracking power. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Three coarse hints\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Chromium sends this with every request See your brow"},"495":{"title":"User-Agent string","description":"The browser's self-description sent on every request and readable in JavaScript.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-user-agent-string","content":"<Chips /> Can see your browser brand, version, OS family, and chip family on every request The browser's self-description sent on every request and readable in JavaScript. Tells the site browser brand and major version, OS family, and CPU family. Used for compatibility and bot filtering. Since UA reduction the string is coarse, so finer detail moved to opt-in Client Hints. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Browser and OS, on every request\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Your browser sends it with eve"},"496":{"title":"User agent string","description":"The text the browser sends naming its browser, version and operating system.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-user-agent","content":"<Chips /> Can see your browser, version, and operating system from the text it sends The text the browser sends naming its browser, version and operating system. Says which browser, version and OS the person uses. Chrome has been reducing detail since 2022, but the string still gates features, and LinkedIn's extension probe only runs when it contains Chrome. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Browser and OS, on every request\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Your browser sends it with every page request"},"497":{"title":"Automation (webdriver) flag","description":"True when the browser is being driven by Selenium, Playwright, Puppeteer or similar.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-webdriver-flag","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if Selenium, Playwright, or Puppeteer is controlling the browser True when the browser is being driven by Selenium, Playwright, Puppeteer or similar. Tells a site a script, not a person, may be on the page. Bot defenses use it with other tells (missing plugins, headless UA) to block or challenge scrapers and agents. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no flag\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Check whether the browser says it is automated Combi"},"498":{"title":"Automation detection (navigator.webdriver)","description":"Tells whether the browser is controlled by automation, plus other marker checks.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-webdriver","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if a script or automation tool is driving your browser Tells whether the browser is controlled by automation, plus other marker checks. True for Selenium, Puppeteer and similar. LinkedIn also looks for PhantomJS and Selenium markers on window and document. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no flag\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Check a flag that is true in Selenium, Puppeteer, and similar tools Look for PhantomJS and Selenium marks on the "},"499":{"title":"WebGL parameters and extensions","description":"About 65 WebGL limits and an extension list that differ by GPU and driver.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-webgl-parameters","content":"<Chips /> Can measure about 65 graphics limits that point to your GPU and driver About 65 WebGL limits and an extension list that differ by GPU and driver. MAX\\_TEXTURE\\_SIZE (4096 to 32768), MAX\\_VERTEX\\_ATTRIBS, shader precision formats and supported extensions together fingerprint the GPU even when the renderer string is hidden. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"GPU traits, silently\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a page draws 3D graphics or tests for it Check texture size limits, "},"500":{"title":"GPU via WebGL unmasked renderer","description":"Reads the graphics card and driver name through WebGL.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-webgl-renderer","content":"<Chips /> Can see your graphics card and driver name through WebGL Reads the graphics card and driver name through WebGL. Exposes GPU model and driver (ANGLE (NVIDIA, GeForce RTX 4070 ...)). Identifies the device tier and OS. Safari reports a generic Apple GPU; Brave and Firefox spoof or trim it. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"GPU name, silently\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a page draws 3D graphics or tests for it See your GPU make and model, like an NVIDIA GeForce See your driv"},"501":{"title":"WebGPU adapter info","description":"GPU vendor, architecture, features and limits through WebGPU.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/device-webgpu-adapter","content":"<Chips /> Can see your GPU maker, design family, and graphics limits through WebGPU GPU vendor, architecture, features and limits through WebGPU. adapter.info gives vendor and architecture (nvidia, ampere); Chrome blanks device and description by default. Limits (maxTextureDimension2D 8192 or 16384) and the feature list still tier the GPU. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"GPU tier, silently\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a page checks for WebGPU See the GPU vendor and architecture, "},"502":{"title":"Device details","description":"Model, memory, battery and other facts about the device. 67 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile","content":"Model, memory, battery and other facts about the device. 67 capabilities in this group. | Capability                   | Kind              | In one line                                                                 |\n| ---------------------------- | ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Active extension probe (AED) | Used to track you | Can check which of thousands of known browser extensions you have installed | | Capability           | Kind          | In one line                                                      |\n| -------------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Ad blocker detection | Gets silently | Can tell if you use an ad blocker and roughly which filter lists | | Capability             | Kind          | In one line                                                       |\n| ---------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Approximate device RAM | Gets silently | Can see roughly how much RAM you have, rounded and capped at 8 GB | | Capability                  | Kind          | In one line                                                               |\n| --------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Automation (webdriver) flag | Gets silently | Can tell if Selenium, Playwright, or Puppeteer is controlling the browser | | Capability                                 | Kind          | In one line                                                     |\n| ------------------------------------------ | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Automation detection (navigator.webdriver) | Gets silently | Can tell if a script or automation tool is driving your browser | | Capability     | Kind          | In one line                                                      |\n| -------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Battery status | Gets silently | Can see your battery level and charging state in Chrome and Edge | | Capability     | Kind          | In one line                                                             |\n| -------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Battery Status | Gets silently | Can see your battery level and charging state, with no prompt in Chrome | | Capability                 | Kind             | In one line                              |\n| -------------------------- | ---------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| Bookmarks and reading list | Blocked for apps | Can't see your bookmarks or reading list | | Capability             | Kind          | In one line                                                           |\n| ---------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Browser flavor globals | Gets silently | Can tell which browser you really use, even when the label is changed | | Capability       | Kind             | In one line                                  |\n| ---------------- | ---------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| Browsing history | Blocked for apps | Can't see which other sites you have visited | | Capability                 | Kind             | In one line                                              |\n| -------------------------- | ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Call log, SMS and messages | Blocked for apps | Can't see your calls, texts, or messages from a web page | | Capability                  | Kind             | In one line                                                       |\n| --------------------------- | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Carrier, SIM and cell tower | Blocked for apps | Can't see your mobile carrier, SIM, or cell tower from a web page | | Capability                | Kind          | In one line                                                         |\n| ------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Client Hint: device model | Gets silently | Can see the model name of your Android phone or tablet when it asks | | Capability                | Kind          | In one line                                                        |\n| ------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Client Hint: form factors | Gets silently | Can tell if you use a desktop, phone, tablet, watch, or car screen | | Capability                    | Kind          | In one line                                               |\n| ----------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Client Hint: platform version | Gets silently | Can tell your exact Windows or macOS version when it asks | | Capability                             | Kind          | In one line                                                   |\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Client Hints: architecture and bitness | Gets silently | Can tell if your chip is Intel, AMD, or ARM, and 32 or 64 bit | | Capability            | Kind          | In one line                                                  |\n| --------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Codec support probing | Gets silently | Can test which video and audio formats your browser can play | | Capability       | Kind           | In one line                                                              |\n| ---------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------"},"503":{"title":"Permissions API","description":"Lets a page ask what each permission is currently set to without prompting.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/meta-permissions-api","content":"<Chips /> Can check whether you allowed or blocked a feature, without asking you Lets a page ask what each permission is currently set to without prompting. navigator.permissions.query() returns granted, denied or prompt for most permissions. It reveals which permissions you have granted or blocked, and a change event lets pages react live. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your choices for this site\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page checks a setting See if a permission is allo"},"504":{"title":"Permissions Policy and iframe allow","description":"HTTP header and iframe allow attribute that decide which features a page or frame may use.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/meta-permissions-policy","content":"<Chips /> Can turn features off for itself and its embedded frames HTTP header and iframe allow attribute that decide which features a page or frame may use. Lets a site disable features for itself and its iframes. MDN lists 51 directives; each can be self, a list of origins, or none. A feature blocked here never reaches the user prompt. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Site rules, not your data\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The site sets rules in its headers or in an iframe Block features like ca"},"505":{"title":"Nearby Bluetooth devices","description":"Sites cannot scan for nearby Bluetooth devices without a picker you approve.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-bluetooth-nearby","content":"<Chips /> Can't scan for nearby Bluetooth devices without a picker you approve Sites cannot scan for nearby Bluetooth devices without a picker you approve. Web Bluetooth (Chromium) requires a user gesture, a chooser prompt and a service filter; the page only gets devices the person picks. Silent scanning does not exist. Safari and Firefox do not support Web Bluetooth. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One device you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect device and pick one from the browser list"},"506":{"title":"Bookmarks and reading list","description":"Sites cannot read your bookmarks or reading list.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-bookmarks","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your bookmarks or reading list Sites cannot read your bookmarks or reading list. Only browser extensions with the bookmarks permission can read them. A web page has no API at all; even installing a PWA is detectable only through display-mode. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Nothing about bookmarks\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this Tell if the page runs as an installed app Show a prompt to add to your home screen Ask you to bookmark it, but only yo"},"507":{"title":"Browsing history","description":"Sites cannot read which other sites you visited.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-browsing-history","content":"<Chips /> Can't see which other sites you have visited Sites cannot read which other sites you visited. :visited styling is limited since Chrome 51 and Firefox 4 (getComputedStyle lies, only color changes) so history sniffing no longer works. Proxies: cache timing, HSTS and partitioned storage are now keyed per site, and referrer shows only the prior page. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only the page before\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this See the one page that sen"},"508":{"title":"Call log, SMS and messages","description":"Web pages cannot read calls, texts or messages on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-call-log-sms","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your calls, texts, or messages from a web page Web pages cannot read calls, texts or messages on the device. No browser API exposes call history or SMS inboxes. WebOTP reads a single matching code. Android native apps need READ\\_SMS and READ\\_CALL\\_LOG; Google Play restricts both heavily. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One code at most\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this Take one matching one time code from a text on Android (WebOTP) Fill a code yo"},"509":{"title":"Carrier, SIM and cell tower","description":"Sites cannot read your mobile carrier, SIM or tower ID.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-cellular-carrier","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your mobile carrier, SIM, or cell tower from a web page Sites cannot read your mobile carrier, SIM or tower ID. Only the Network Information type (cellular) on Android Chromium and the server-side IP to ASN lookup reveal the carrier (about 100 major carriers). No MCC, MNC, SIM or tower API exists on the web. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A guess from IP only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this Check if you are on cellular or Wi-Fi in Chrome on And"},"510":{"title":"Full contact list","description":"Sites cannot read your address book; they only see contacts you pick.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-contacts-list","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your full contact list; only the contacts you pick Sites cannot read your address book; they only see contacts you pick. The Contact Picker (Android Chrome 80, Chrome iOS no) returns only the entries the person ticks, per call. No bulk read exists, unlike native apps that hold the contacts permission. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only contacts you tick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Choose contacts and tick the people you want to share Show a picker in Chrome on Android Get t"},"511":{"title":"CPU temperature, fan and power","description":"No API reads CPU heat; only coarse pressure buckets exist.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-cpu-temperature","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your CPU heat, fan speed, or power use No API reads CPU heat; only coarse pressure buckets exist. Compute Pressure API (Chrome 125, secure context) returns nominal, fair, serious or critical, throttled to at most 1 update per second and capped per origin. Exact degrees, fan speed and wattage are not exposed. Battery level is separate. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Four coarse levels only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this Check four coarse busy l"},"512":{"title":"Serial number, IMEI and device ID","description":"Sites cannot read your serial number, IMEI or advertising ID.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-device-serial-imei","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your serial number, IMEI, or advertising ID from a web page Sites cannot read your serial number, IMEI or advertising ID. No unprompted API exposes hardware identifiers. Managed ChromeOS and enterprise policies expose serial to allowlisted extensions only (chrome.enterprise.deviceAttributes). A regular site gets none; fingerprints are rebuilt from many signals instead. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"No device ID at all\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask fo"},"513":{"title":"Free disk space","description":"Exact free disk space is hidden; a fuzzy quota estimate leaks.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-disk-free-space","content":"<Chips /> Can't see exact free disk space, only a fuzzy storage quota Exact free disk space is hidden; a fuzzy quota estimate leaks. navigator.storage.estimate().quota is about 60% of disk in Chrome and Safari 17, with noise. Exact free space and total disk size are not exposed; quota gives bits of a size class (256 GB vs 512 GB vs 1 TB). <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A rough quota only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a page checks how much it can store See a storage quota, about "},"514":{"title":"Exact RAM size","description":"Only a capped RAM figure exists; exact memory is hidden.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-exact-ram","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your exact RAM size; only a rounded number capped at 8 GB Only a capped RAM figure exists; exact memory is hidden. deviceMemory returns 0.25 to 8 GB (capped, Chromium, secure context, power of two rounding), so a 64 GB workstation looks like 8. Firefox and Safari expose nothing. Memory pressure is not readable. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A rounded, capped number\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads in Chrome or Edge See memory as 0.25 up to 8 GB in"},"515":{"title":"File system paths and directories","description":"Sites cannot list your folders or see where files live.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-file-paths","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your folders or where your files are stored Sites cannot list your folders or see where files live. The File System Access API (Chromium) returns handles after a picker; the real path is withheld (fakepath). OPFS is a private sandbox. Only the file name, size and last modified are readable. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only the file you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Choose file or Open file and pick one in the file picker Open the file you pick, once or for the visit Se"},"516":{"title":"Installed native apps","description":"A site cannot list your installed apps, except to check its own related app.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-installed-apps","content":"<Chips /> Can't list your installed apps; can only check its own related app A site cannot list your installed apps, except to check its own related app. navigator.getInstalledRelatedApps() (Chrome Android 80, Chrome desktop 140 for PWAs) only answers for apps declared in the site manifest related\\_applications. Custom URL scheme probing is blocked or prompts. Roughly 0 bits for arbitrary apps. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only its own app\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the site"},"517":{"title":"Exact iPhone and iPad model","description":"iOS Safari hides the exact device model.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-ios-device-model","content":"<Chips /> Can't see which exact iPhone or iPad you have iOS Safari hides the exact device model. The UA says only iPhone or iPad with a frozen OS string. Proxies: screen size (390x844 etc.) and DPR narrow to a generation, and WebGL renderer says Apple GPU. Exact model such as iPhone 15 vs 16 is often ambiguous, about 2 to 3 bits. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A generation guess only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads See that it is an iPhone or iPad Guess a generation "},"518":{"title":"MAC address","description":"A page cannot read your device's network hardware address.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-mac-address","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your device's network hardware address (MAC) A page cannot read your device's network hardware address. MAC addresses never reach the web platform. Proxies are weak: WebRTC local candidates (mDNS now) and Bluetooth device IDs after a prompt are per-origin and randomized. iOS and Android also randomize Wi-Fi MACs per network. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"No hardware address\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this See random per site IDs from some feat"},"519":{"title":"OS username and home folder","description":"Pages cannot see your computer login name.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-os-username","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your computer login name or home folder Pages cannot see your computer login name. No web API exposes it. Proxies: file-picker display names after the user chooses a file, a downloaded file path in a native dialog, and rare leaks in error messages from extensions or file:// URLs. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Nothing unless you type it\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this See the name of a file you pick, if it includes your name Get your name only "},"520":{"title":"Other tabs and windows content","description":"A page cannot see what other tabs show, only whether its own tab is visible.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-other-tabs","content":"<Chips /> Can't see what other tabs or windows show A page cannot see what other tabs show, only whether its own tab is visible. Same-origin policy and site isolation block it. Proxies: Page Visibility and focus say if this tab is hidden, BroadcastChannel talks to same-origin tabs, and Window Management lists screens (prompt). Number of open tabs is unknown. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only its own tabs\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this Tell if its own tab is vis"},"521":{"title":"Your phone number","description":"A page cannot read the phone number of your phone or SIM.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-phone-number","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your own phone number from a web page A page cannot read the phone number of your phone or SIM. No web API returns the device's own number. Proxies: WebOTP reads an SMS code (needs a prompt-free user flow but only the code, only formatted for the origin), Credential Management and Contact Picker give numbers the person chooses. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only what you type or pick\" /> **What the user does:** You type your number, pick one from a contact picker, or tap Autofill T"},"522":{"title":"Real IP behind a VPN","description":"A page cannot learn your real IP if a VPN works properly.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-real-ip-vpn","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your real IP address when your VPN works properly A page cannot learn your real IP if a VPN works properly. Servers see the VPN exit IP. Leaks come from WebRTC (local IPs now hidden by mDNS), DNS leaks outside the browser, IPv6 bypass and timezone or language mismatch. Browsers expose no 'real IP' API. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"VPN address only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when your device connects to the site See the VPN exit IP address Spot leaks from IPv6,"},"523":{"title":"Saved passwords and autofill data","description":"Pages cannot read passwords or addresses saved in the browser.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-saved-passwords","content":"<Chips /> Can't see passwords or addresses saved in your browser Pages cannot read passwords or addresses saved in the browser. Password managers fill on user action or after an autofill choice. Known weak spot: hidden-field autofill attacks and the :autofill CSS pseudo-class, which reveals that a field was autofilled (a boolean), but never the value before user action. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only what you choose to fill\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a field and choose a saved login or ad"},"524":{"title":"Screen brightness","description":"A site cannot read how bright your screen is set.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-screen-brightness","content":"<Chips /> Can't see how bright your screen is set A site cannot read how bright your screen is set. No API exposes backlight level. Proxies: prefers-color-scheme, dynamic-range and Screen Wake Lock state. Ambient light sensor was removed from stable browsers (Chrome behind a flag, Firefox 122 removed it) for fingerprint and privacy risk. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Light or dark mode only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this See if you use light or dark mode Check H"},"525":{"title":"System volume level","description":"No API tells a page how loud the device volume is.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-system-volume","content":"<Chips /> Can't see how loud your device volume is set No API tells a page how loud the device volume is. Volume is OS state. Proxies exist: autoplay policy shows a muted tab, audio output device changes fire devicechange, and a muted video can still play. A page cannot read a 0 to 100 level. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Own player only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this See if audio is blocked by autoplay rules Tell when your audio output device changes Play muted"},"526":{"title":"Wi-Fi name, BSSID and signal strength","description":"Sites cannot read your Wi-Fi network name, router ID or signal bars.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/notexposed-wifi-ssid","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your Wi-Fi name, router ID, or signal bars Sites cannot read your Wi-Fi network name, router ID or signal bars. Wi-Fi identity is a location tracker (BSSID databases map to street addresses). Browsers expose none of it. Proxies: Network Information type on Android, server-side IP geolocation, and the Geolocation API which uses Wi-Fi servers behind its prompt. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Connection type only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this Se"},"527":{"title":"Active extension probe (AED)","description":"Fetches a hidden file from each of thousands of known extension IDs to learn which ones are installed.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/tracking-linkedin-extension-probe","content":"<Chips /> Can check which of thousands of known browser extensions you have installed Fetches a hidden file from each of thousands of known extension IDs to learn which ones are installed. Reveals installed add-ons, which can imply job search, sales role, religion, health or politics, and a stable identifier for the browser profile. LinkedIn calls it abuse prevention; critics call it covert profiling. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent check, only known extensions\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing"},"528":{"title":"Spectroscopy DOM scan for extension traces","description":"Walks the page's own DOM looking for chrome-extension:// strings that extensions inject.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Device details"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/tracking-linkedin-spectroscopy-dom-scan","content":"<Chips /> Can look through its own page for traces that extensions leave behind Walks the page's own DOM looking for chrome-extension:// strings that extensions inject. Finds extensions that modify the page even when they hide their files from fetch probes. A passive second way to learn which add-ons the person runs while using the site. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only traces left on its own page\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens while the page loads and changes. Scan the page's own t"},"529":{"title":"Color depth, pixel depth and pixel ratio","description":"Reads bits per pixel and the device pixel ratio (zoom or Retina factor).","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-color-depth-pixel-ratio","content":"<Chips /> Can see your screen's color depth and how sharp or zoomed it is Reads bits per pixel and the device pixel ratio (zoom or Retina factor). Pixel ratio shows Retina screens and browser zoom. Together with resolution it identifies display class. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Two numbers, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads or you zoom. Check the color depth number Check how many real pixels sit behind each page pixel (Retina or zoom) Notice when you zoom"},"530":{"title":"Color and pixel depth","description":"Bits per pixel the screen reports; almost always 24.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-color-depth","content":"<Chips /> Can see how many colors your screen reports, almost always 24 bit Bits per pixel the screen reports; almost always 24. Reported colorDepth is 24 on nearly everything (30 on some wide-gamut Safari setups, 16 or 32 rarely). Low entropy but included in every library. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One number, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check the color depth number your screen reports Pick image or video quality to match Add the number to a brow"},"531":{"title":"color-gamut","description":"Widest color space the screen shows: sRGB, P3 or Rec2020.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-color-gamut","content":"<Chips /> Can check whether your screen shows standard, wide P3 or very wide Rec2020 colors Widest color space the screen shows: sRGB, P3 or Rec2020. P3 separates Apple devices and OLED phones from budget screens. A few bits of hardware signal. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Ask whether your screen supports P3 or Rec2020 Serve richer images and colors if it does Add the answer to a browser fingerprint See your"},"532":{"title":"Custom or hidden cursor","description":"Replaces the mouse pointer with an image, or hides it, inside the page.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-custom-cursor","content":"<Chips /> Can swap your mouse pointer for an image, or hide it, only inside the page Replaces the mouse pointer with an image, or hides it, inside the page. A site can change or hide the pointer, helping games and drawing apps. Browsers block cursors that could imitate system pointers or hide over browser UI, so a page cannot fake the cursor outside its own area. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only while over the page\" /> **What the user does:** You move your mouse over the page or a button on it. Show"},"533":{"title":"Device pixel ratio and zoom","description":"How many physical pixels per CSS pixel; also changes with browser zoom.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-device-pixel-ratio","content":"<Chips /> Can see your screen's sharpness factor, which also shifts when you zoom How many physical pixels per CSS pixel; also changes with browser zoom. Values like 1, 1.25, 1.5, 2, 2.625, 3 reveal retina screens, Windows scaling and the person's zoom level (50% to 500%). Zoom shifts DPR so it leaks accessibility settings. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One number, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads or you zoom. Check how many real pixels sit behind each page"},"534":{"title":"display-mode","description":"How the page is shown: browser tab, standalone, fullscreen, PWA.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-display-mode","content":"<Chips /> Can tell whether it is open in a browser tab, a home screen app or fullscreen How the page is shown: browser tab, standalone, fullscreen, PWA. Shows the app was installed as a PWA and whether the person runs it in a window. Useful for install analytics. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One answer, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads or the window changes. Check which display mode it is running in Hide install buttons when it is already an app Change its"},"535":{"title":"Document Picture-in-Picture","description":"Opens a small always-on-top window containing any HTML.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-document-pip","content":"<Chips /> Can open a small floating window with its own content after you click Opens a small always-on-top window containing any HTML. Floats custom page content above other windows (video call controls, timers). A page can keep a floating surface visible while you work elsewhere, so it can also be abused for fake UI. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only after you click\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a button such as Pop out or Mini player. Open a small always-on-top window with any page content, "},"536":{"title":"dynamic-range and video-dynamic-range","description":"Whether the screen can show HDR.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-dynamic-range","content":"<Chips /> Can check whether your screen can show HDR bright and dark detail Whether the screen can show HDR. Tells a site to serve HDR video. Splits users into standard and HDR displays (modern iPhones, MacBook Pro, HDR monitors). <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Ask whether your screen supports high dynamic range Serve HDR video or images if it does Add the answer to a browser fingerprint See your screen's exac"},"537":{"title":"forced-colors","description":"True when Windows High Contrast or similar forces a palette.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-forced-colors","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if Windows High Contrast or a similar mode is forcing your colors True when Windows High Contrast or similar forces a palette. Strong accessibility signal (low vision or migraine users). Mostly Windows contrast themes. A tiny share of users, hence very identifying for those who use it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check whether forced colors are on Change its design so text stays easy to s"},"538":{"title":"Fullscreen API","description":"Lets a page take over the whole screen.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-fullscreen","content":"<Chips /> Can fill your whole screen after you tap, with a clear way out Lets a page take over the whole screen. Needs a click or tap and shows an exit hint. Fullscreen can hide the address bar, which scam pages abuse to fake a browser UI. Esc always exits; iPhone Safari allows only video elements. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While you stay in fullscreen\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Fullscreen, or a video's expand button. Fill the screen with the page, or one part such as a video or game Hide"},"539":{"title":"Embed another page in an iframe","description":"A page can show another site inside a frame unless that site blocks framing.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-iframe-embedding","content":"<Chips /> Can show another site inside its own page, unless that site blocks it A page can show another site inside a frame unless that site blocks framing. A site can embed a third party page (maps, video, ads, payment forms) and, if it frames you without protection, overlay invisible content for clickjacking. Framing headers and sandbox settings control who can do this. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the framed page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You open a page that includes a map, video "},"540":{"title":"inverted-colors","description":"True if the OS inverts colors (Safari Smart Invert).","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-inverted-colors","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if your device inverts screen colors, such as Smart Invert on iPhone True if the OS inverts colors (Safari Smart Invert). Accessibility setting visible only in Safari and WebKit browsers. Used by FingerprintJS as a Safari-only trait. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check whether inverted colors are on Keep images and video looking normal Add the answer to a browser fingerprint Tell why you tu"},"541":{"title":"Keyboard Lock","description":"Lets a fullscreen page capture shortcut keys like Esc, Alt+Tab or Ctrl+W (Chromium only).","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-keyboard-lock","content":"<Chips /> Can capture shortcut keys like Esc or Ctrl+W, but only in fullscreen on Chromium Lets a fullscreen page capture shortcut keys like Esc, Alt+Tab or Ctrl+W (Chromium only). A site can keep keys the browser or OS would normally take, which remote desktops, games and cloud IDEs need. It also lets a hostile fullscreen page swallow the usual escape shortcuts for a moment. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only while fullscreen\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Fullscreen in a game, remote desktop or"},"542":{"title":"Screen orientation","description":"Whether the screen is portrait or landscape and its rotation angle.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-orientation","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if your screen is upright or sideways, and by how many degrees Whether the screen is portrait or landscape and its rotation angle. Shows how a phone is held and whether a tablet is docked. Reports type (portrait-primary etc.) and angle 0, 90, 180 or 270; changes with a rotate event. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Portrait or landscape only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads or you rotate the device. Check portrait or landscape Check the rotation angle"},"543":{"title":"Picture in Picture (video)","description":"Pops a video into a small always-on-top window, usually after a click.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-picture-in-picture","content":"<Chips /> Can pop a video into a small floating window after you tap Pops a video into a small always-on-top window, usually after a click. A site can float a video above all other apps, which keeps attention on its content while the person works elsewhere. The person can close or resize it, and a site cannot place or hide the window. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only after you tap\" /> **What the user does:** You tap the Picture in Picture button on a video. Move a video into a small always-on-top wi"},"544":{"title":"hover, pointer, any-pointer","description":"Whether the main input is a mouse, finger or stylus and whether hover works.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-pointer-hover","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if you use a mouse, finger or stylus, and whether hover works Whether the main input is a mouse, finger or stylus and whether hover works. pointer is coarse for touch, fine for mouse or pen; hover is hover or none. any-\\* variants say if any attached device qualifies, revealing a mouse on a tablet or a touchscreen on a laptop. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Input type only, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check what your main input is Ch"},"545":{"title":"prefers-color-scheme","description":"Whether the OS or browser theme is light or dark.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-prefers-color-scheme","content":"<Chips /> Can tell whether your device theme is light or dark Whether the OS or browser theme is light or dark. Reveals the person's OS appearance setting (dark mode is used by an estimated 40 to 60% of mobile users) and time of day if auto switching. Low entropy; often set at the site by cookie. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One answer, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check whether you chose light or dark Show matching colors Ask for it in a request head"},"546":{"title":"prefers-contrast","description":"OS setting for higher or lower color contrast.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-prefers-contrast","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if you asked your device for more or less contrast OS setting for higher or lower color contrast. Signals low vision or light sensitivity. Values no-preference, more, less, custom. Rare, so each non-default value is highly distinguishing. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One answer, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check whether you chose more, less or normal contrast Change its colors to match Add the answer to a browser fingerprint Tell w"},"547":{"title":"prefers-reduced-data","description":"User wants less data use (data saver).","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-prefers-reduced-data","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if you asked for less data use, where the browser supports it User wants less data use (data saver). Pairs with Save-Data. Still behind a flag in Chrome, so almost always no-preference. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no, rarely on\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check whether a data saver setting is on Send smaller images and skip autoplay video Add the answer to a browser fingerprint See your data plan or how much you have used Get an "},"548":{"title":"prefers-reduced-motion","description":"OS setting to reduce animation.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-prefers-reduced-motion","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if you asked your device to cut down on animation OS setting to reduce animation. Marks people with vestibular disorders or motion sensitivity (a health-adjacent setting). Roughly 5 to 15% enable it (estimates differ by platform). <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check whether reduced motion is on Turn off animations and autoplay Add the answer to a browser fingerprint Tell why you chose it, t"},"549":{"title":"prefers-reduced-transparency","description":"OS setting to reduce translucent effects.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-prefers-reduced-transparency","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if you asked your device to make windows less see through OS setting to reduce translucent effects. Accessibility and low-vision hint. New and rare. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check whether reduced transparency is on Use solid backgrounds instead of frosted ones Add the answer to a browser fingerprint See your other accessibility settings Get an answer in Firefox Change the setting **How"},"550":{"title":"Display refresh rate (measured)","description":"Estimated by timing animation frames; 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-refresh-rate","content":"<Chips /> Can estimate your screen's refresh rate by timing about one second of animation Estimated by timing animation frames; 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz. No API reports refresh rate; scripts measure the gap between requestAnimationFrame callbacks. Distinguishes high-refresh phones and gaming monitors, but is noisy under load. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A guess, taken once\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It runs for about a second when the page loads or an animation starts. Count animation frames "},"551":{"title":"Screen frame (taskbar insets)","description":"Offsets between full screen and usable area reveal taskbar or dock size and position.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-screen-frame","content":"<Chips /> Can tell how big your taskbar or dock is and where it sits Offsets between full screen and usable area reveal taskbar or dock size and position. Shows macOS dock height and side, Windows taskbar edge, notches and system bars. Identifies OS UI settings and multi-monitor layout. Firefox now hides taskbar size by default. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Four numbers, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Compare the full screen size with the usable area Gu"},"552":{"title":"Screen orientation","description":"Reads portrait or landscape and angle.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-screen-orientation","content":"<Chips /> Can check whether your screen is portrait or landscape and its angle Reads portrait or landscape and angle. Shows how the phone or tablet is held and whether a laptop screen is rotated. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Portrait or landscape only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads or you rotate the device. Check portrait or landscape Check the rotation angle Notice when you rotate the device See the device's tilt or movement Rotate your screen for you See how you"},"553":{"title":"Viewport meta and visual viewport","description":"visualViewport reports pinch-zoom scale and on-screen keyboard offsets.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-screen-refresh-and-viewport-meta","content":"<Chips /> Can see your pinch zoom level and when the on screen keyboard pops up visualViewport reports pinch-zoom scale and on-screen keyboard offsets. Scale above 1 tells the person is zoomed in (accessibility); height shrink exposes the on-screen keyboard. Updates live as the user pinches or types. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You pinch to zoom, or tap a box that opens the keyboard. Check your current pinch zoom scale See changes to the visible are"},"554":{"title":"Screen width and height","description":"The monitor or phone screen size in CSS pixels, plus the usable area.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-screen-size","content":"<Chips /> Can see your screen width and height, and the usable area, in page units The monitor or phone screen size in CSS pixels, plus the usable area. Identifies device model families (iPhone 390x844) and monitor setups (1920x1080, 2560x1440, 3440x1440). Safari 26 and Firefox protections spoof or round it for known scripts; Firefox RFP returns the window size. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Size numbers, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check the screen's"},"555":{"title":"Scroll control and hijacking","description":"Moves the page scroll position and can change scrolling behaviour or capture the wheel.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-scroll-control","content":"<Chips /> Can scroll its own page and change how scrolling feels, but nothing else Moves the page scroll position and can change scrolling behaviour or capture the wheel. A site can jump the page, animate scrolling, restore position or block back-bounce. That helps navigation but also supports scrolljacking and trapping scroll inside a panel. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only while on its page\" /> **What the user does:** You scroll or tap a link. Sometimes nothing: it can also move the page on load. "},"556":{"title":"Theme color for browser UI","description":"Tints the browser's address bar or tab strip with a color the page picks.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-theme-color","content":"<Chips /> Can tint your browser's address bar or tab strip with a color it picks Tints the browser's address bar or tab strip with a color the page picks. A site can suggest a colour for the browser toolbar to match its brand. The tag is write only: it reveals nothing about the person, and the browser may ignore it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One color, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Set the color of the address bar or tab strip Pick a different color"},"557":{"title":"update, scripting, monochrome","description":"Screen refresh type, whether JS is enabled, and monochrome depth.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-update-scripting-monochrome","content":"<Chips /> Can tell how your screen refreshes, if scripting is on, and if it is monochrome Screen refresh type, whether JS is enabled, and monochrome depth. update is none for e-ink or print; scripting reports enabled or none; monochrome is 0 on color screens. Mostly identifies e-readers and print contexts. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Three yes or no answers\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check whether the screen updates slowly, like an e-ink reader Check whether"},"558":{"title":"Screen Wake Lock","description":"Keeps the screen from dimming or sleeping while the tab is visible; no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-wake-lock","content":"<Chips /> Can keep your screen awake while the tab is open and showing Keeps the screen from dimming or sleeping while the tab is visible; no prompt. A site can stop the screen going dark, which is useful for recipes, maps and video, but drains battery. The browser drops the lock the moment the tab is hidden, so it cannot run in the background. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the tab is showing\" /> **What the user does:** You start a recipe, a video, map directions or a slideshow. Stop the screen "},"559":{"title":"Window Management (multi-screen)","description":"Asks to see all connected monitors and place windows on them.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-window-management","content":"<Chips /> Can see all your connected screens and place windows on them, if you allow Asks to see all connected monitors and place windows on them. Reveals how many screens you have, their sizes, positions, labels and scale. That is a strong device fingerprint and lets a site open fullscreen windows on a chosen monitor. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While you use it, after Allow\" /> **What the user does:** You click Use all screens or Present on second monitor, then Allow. List your screens with their "},"560":{"title":"Move and resize own window","description":"Moves or resizes a popup the site opened; ignored for normal tabs.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-window-move-resize","content":"<Chips /> Can move or resize only a popup window it opened itself Moves or resizes a popup the site opened; ignored for normal tabs. A site can reshape a popup it created, but the browser blocks changes to tabs and pre-existing windows, so scripts cannot yank your main window around or hide it off screen. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only its own popup\" /> **What the user does:** You click a link that opens a popup or small window. Move a popup it opened Resize a popup it opened Nudge a popup by a sm"},"561":{"title":"Window position and multi-screen flag","description":"Window position on the desktop and whether more than one monitor is attached.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-window-position-multimonitor","content":"<Chips /> Can see where your browser window sits and whether you have two or more screens Window position on the desktop and whether more than one monitor is attached. screenX and screenY can reveal monitor layout; screen.isExtended says if a second display exists, without a prompt. Detailed monitor list (getScreenDetails) does need a permission. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Position and a yes or no\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check the window's position on th"},"562":{"title":"Window inner and outer size","description":"Browser window and viewport dimensions, which change constantly.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Screen and windows"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/display-window-sizes","content":"<Chips /> Can see your browser window and visible page size, as they change Browser window and viewport dimensions, which change constantly. Reveals tiling, maximized vs windowed use, sidebars and devtools. Firefox RFP rounds inner size to 200x100 steps (letterboxing) so everyone shares a few buckets. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Size numbers, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check the window's inner and outer size Notice when you resize or rotate Choose "},"563":{"title":"Screen and windows","description":"Screen size, brightness, full screen, windows. 34 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window","content":"Screen size, brightness, full screen, windows. 34 capabilities in this group. | Capability            | Kind          | In one line                                                       |\n| --------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Color and pixel depth | Gets silently | Can see how many colors your screen reports, almost always 24 bit | | Capability                               | Kind          | In one line                                                     |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Color depth, pixel depth and pixel ratio | Gets silently | Can see your screen's color depth and how sharp or zoomed it is | | Capability  | Kind          | In one line                                                                       |\n| ----------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| color-gamut | Gets silently | Can check whether your screen shows standard, wide P3 or very wide Rec2020 colors | | Capability              | Kind                          | In one line                                                                |\n| ----------------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Custom or hidden cursor | Does something on your device | Can swap your mouse pointer for an image, or hide it, only inside the page | | Capability                  | Kind          | In one line                                                             |\n| --------------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Device pixel ratio and zoom | Gets silently | Can see your screen's sharpness factor, which also shifts when you zoom | | Capability                      | Kind          | In one line                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Display refresh rate (measured) | Gets silently | Can estimate your screen's refresh rate by timing about one second of animation | | Capability   | Kind          | In one line                                                                   |\n| ------------ | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| display-mode | Gets silently | Can tell whether it is open in a browser tab, a home screen app or fullscreen | | Capability                  | Kind                          | In one line                                                           |\n| --------------------------- | ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Document Picture-in-Picture | Does something on your device | Can open a small floating window with its own content after you click | | Capability                            | Kind          | In one line                                                       |\n| ------------------------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| dynamic-range and video-dynamic-range | Gets silently | Can check whether your screen can show HDR bright and dark detail | | Capability                      | Kind                 | In one line                                                           |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Embed another page in an iframe | Works without asking | Can show another site inside its own page, unless that site blocks it | | Capability    | Kind          | In one line                                                                |\n| ------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| forced-colors | Gets silently | Can tell if Windows High Contrast or a similar mode is forcing your colors | | Capability     | Kind                          | In one line                                                    |\n| -------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Fullscreen API | Does something on your device | Can fill your whole screen after you tap, with a clear way out | | Capability                  | Kind          | In one line                                                            |\n| --------------------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| hover, pointer, any-pointer | Gets silently | Can tell if you use a mouse, finger or stylus, and whether hover works | | Capability      | Kind          | In one line                                                                   |\n| --------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| inverted-colors | Gets silently | Can tell if your device inverts screen colors, such as Smart Invert on iPhone | | Capability    | Kind                 | In one line                                                                      |\n| ------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Keyboard Lock | Works without asking | Can capture shortcut keys like Esc or Ctrl+W, but only in fullscreen on Chromium | | Capability                 | Kind                          | In one line                                             |\n| -------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Move and resize own window | Does something on your device | Can move or resize only a popup window it opened itself | | Capability                 | Kind    "},"564":{"title":"Blob URLs (object URLs)","description":"Makes a temporary URL for in-memory data or a File, usable in links, images and downloads.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/files-blob-urls","content":"<Chips /> Can make temporary links to data held in memory, which work only on its own site Makes a temporary URL for in-memory data or a File, usable in links, images and downloads. A site can hand files to itself without a server round trip. The URL works only in that browser and origin and dies when the page unloads or is revoked. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only data it already has\" /> **What the user does:** You pick an image to preview, or tap Download for a file the page built. Turn a file or "},"565":{"title":"Capture attribute (camera or mic from a file input)","description":"Opens the phone camera or microphone directly from a file input; the page gets one finished file.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/files-capture-attribute","content":"<Chips /> Can open your camera or microphone app for one photo, video or clip Opens the phone camera or microphone directly from a file input; the page gets one finished file. A site can skip the gallery and ask for a fresh photo, video or recording. It gets a single file, not a live stream, and the person confirms each capture. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One finished photo or clip\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Take photo, Scan document or Record video on an upload button. Start the phone's c"},"566":{"title":"File System Access: folder picker and blocked folders","description":"Lets the person grant a whole folder for reading and writing; sensitive system folders are refused.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/files-directory-picker","content":"<Chips /> Can open and change files in a whole folder you pick, but not system folders Lets the person grant a whole folder for reading and writing; sensitive system folders are refused. A folder grant shares every file inside it and lets the site edit them. Chrome refuses root, system and the profile home folders so one click cannot expose the whole disk. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One folder you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You click Open folder, choose a folder, then click Allow. List every "},"567":{"title":"Directory upload (webkitdirectory)","description":"Lets the person pick a whole folder; the page gets every file in it with relative paths.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/files-directory-upload","content":"<Chips /> Can access every file inside a folder you choose, in order to upload them Lets the person pick a whole folder; the page gets every file in it with relative paths. One pick shares a full folder tree, often thousands of files. The site sees file names and sub folder structure, so a careless pick can expose more than expected. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One folder you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You click Upload folder and choose one. Receive every file in the folder, with its path insi"},"568":{"title":"Dropping files and folders onto the page","description":"Receives files and whole folders the person drags onto the page, with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/files-drop-files","content":"<Chips /> Can receive files or whole folders you drag onto the page Receives files and whole folders the person drags onto the page, with no prompt. Dropping is consent for exactly what is dropped, including folder trees. The site can read file contents and, in Chrome, ask for write access to the dropped items. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only what you drop\" /> **What the user does:** You drag files from your desktop onto the page and let go. Receive the files and folders you drop Open the contents "},"569":{"title":"File input (choose files to upload)","description":"Opens the system file chooser; the page gets only the files the person picks.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/files-file-input","content":"<Chips /> Can open only the files you pick in the file chooser Opens the system file chooser; the page gets only the files the person picks. The most common way a site reaches a person's files. The person picks exactly what is shared, and the site sees name, size, type and modified time, but not the folder path. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only the files you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Choose file, Upload or Attach, then pick. Open the files you choose, one or several Upload them to its"},"570":{"title":"File metadata (name, size, type, modified time)","description":"Shows name, byte size, MIME type and last modified time of any file the person shares.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/files-file-metadata","content":"<Chips /> Can see the name, size, type and last changed date of files you share Shows name, byte size, MIME type and last modified time of any file the person shares. Metadata alone can reveal a lot: camera model names, document names, dates of work. The MIME type comes from the person's OS file associations, which also differs between machines. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Basic file details only\" /> **What the user does:** You pick or drop a file onto the page. See each file's name and size See its"},"571":{"title":"File System Access: open and save pickers","description":"Lets the person pick files and then read and write them in place; Chromium only.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/files-file-system-access-pickers","content":"<Chips /> Can open files you pick and save changes back to them, in Chrome and Edge Lets the person pick files and then read and write them in place; Chromium only. A site can open a local document, edit it and save over the original, like a desktop app. The person picks the file, and the browser asks again before write access. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only the files you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You click Open file or Save as, pick, and later click Allow. Open the file you pick Save chang"},"572":{"title":"Origin Private File System (OPFS)","description":"Gives the page a private, fast file system inside the browser, with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/files-opfs","content":"<Chips /> Can keep large private files in the browser that you cannot browse yourself Gives the page a private, fast file system inside the browser, with no prompt. A site can store large files and databases on disk invisibly to the person. It is sandboxed per site, but it can fill gigabytes of disk and survives until site data is cleared. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Its own private area only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app stores data such as a database or editor projec"},"573":{"title":"Files and storage","description":"Saving, opening and keeping data. 29 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage","content":"Saving, opening and keeping data. 29 capabilities in this group. | Capability         | Kind          | In one line                                                             |\n| ------------------ | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Back forward cache | Gets silently | Can freeze this page when you leave and bring it back instantly on Back | | Capability              | Kind                 | In one line                                                                      |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Blob URLs (object URLs) | Works without asking | Can make temporary links to data held in memory, which work only on its own site | | Capability       | Kind                 | In one line                                                     |\n| ---------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| BroadcastChannel | Works without asking | Can send messages between open tabs and frames of the same site | | Capability    | Kind                 | In one line                                                           |\n| ------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Cache Storage | Works without asking | Can keep copies of pages and files so the site opens without internet | | Capability                                          | Kind                   | In one line                                                         |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Capture attribute (camera or mic from a file input) | You pick what to share | Can open your camera or microphone app for one photo, video or clip | | Capability                         | Kind                   | In one line                                                               |\n| ---------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Directory upload (webkitdirectory) | You pick what to share | Can access every file inside a folder you choose, in order to upload them | | Capability                               | Kind                 | In one line                                               |\n| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Dropping files and folders onto the page | Works without asking | Can receive files or whole folders you drag onto the page | | Capability                          | Kind                   | In one line                                          |\n| ----------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| File input (choose files to upload) | You pick what to share | Can open only the files you pick in the file chooser | | Capability                                      | Kind          | In one line                                                           |\n| ----------------------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| File metadata (name, size, type, modified time) | Gets silently | Can see the name, size, type and last changed date of files you share | | Capability                                  | Kind           | In one line                                                        |\n| ------------------------------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| File System Access (read and write handles) | Asks you first | Can open files and folders you pick and save changes back in place | | Capability                                            | Kind                   | In one line                                                                  |\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| File System Access: folder picker and blocked folders | You pick what to share | Can open and change files in a whole folder you pick, but not system folders | | Capability                                | Kind                   | In one line                                                               |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| File System Access: open and save pickers | You pick what to share | Can open files you pick and save changes back to them, in Chrome and Edge | | Capability          | Kind                 | In one line                                                           |\n| ------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| First party cookies | Works without asking | Can keep small notes in your browser and get them back on every visit | | Capability                           | Kind          | In one line                                                |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Incognito and private mode detection | Gets silently | Can guess whether you are in a private or Incognito window | | Capability | Kind                 | In one line                                                           |\n| ---------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| IndexedDB  | Works without asking | Can keep gigabytes of organized data for its own site in your browser | | Capability   | Kind                 | In one line                                                     |\n| ----------"},"574":{"title":"Back forward cache","description":"The browser freezes a left page whole and restores it instantly on Back, with scripts and state intact.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-bfcache","content":"<Chips /> Can freeze this page when you leave and bring it back instantly on Back The browser freezes a left page whole and restores it instantly on Back, with scripts and state intact. A person who leaves and presses Back can resume the same JavaScript heap, form values and open connections. Analytics must treat pageshow with persisted=true as a new view. The page can run no code while frozen. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only for the Back button\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Back or Forward t"},"575":{"title":"BroadcastChannel","description":"Lets tabs and frames of the same site send messages to each other instantly.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-broadcastchannel","content":"<Chips /> Can send messages between open tabs and frames of the same site Lets tabs and frames of the same site send messages to each other instantly. A site can see that the same person has several tabs open and sync a login, logout or shopping state. Since partitioning, a tracker embed can no longer message its own copy on a different top site. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only tabs of the same site\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens while you have the same site open in more than one t"},"576":{"title":"Cache Storage","description":"A store of HTTP responses used by service workers to serve pages offline.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-cache-storage","content":"<Chips /> Can keep copies of pages and files so the site opens without internet A store of HTTP responses used by service workers to serve pages offline. Lets a site keep its own pages, scripts and API responses on the device. Shares the origin quota with IndexedDB. Opaque cross-origin responses are padded in quota accounting so sites cannot probe sizes. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Its own saved copies only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you visit or install the site. Save page"},"577":{"title":"First party cookies","description":"Small name and value pairs a site stores in the browser and gets back on every request.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-cookies-first-party","content":"<Chips /> Can keep small notes in your browser and get them back on every visit Small name and value pairs a site stores in the browser and gets back on every request. The base identifier of the web: a random ID in a cookie lets a site recognise a returning browser, keep a login, and join visits into one profile. It is sent automatically with every matching request, so the person never has to act. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, on its own site\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens wh"},"578":{"title":"File System Access (read and write handles)","description":"Lets a site open files and folders and save changes back in place.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-file-system-access","content":"<Chips /> Can open files and folders you pick and save changes back in place Lets a site open files and folders and save changes back in place. Gives a site read and write access to specific files or directories the person picks. Since Chrome 122 a grant can persist across visits, so the site keeps access to your folder until revoked. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only what you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You click Open or Save, pick, then click Allow. Open the files or folders you pick Save chan"},"579":{"title":"IndexedDB","description":"A large browser database that can hold gigabytes of structured data for one site.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-indexeddb","content":"<Chips /> Can keep gigabytes of organized data for its own site in your browser A large browser database that can hold gigabytes of structured data for one site. Stores app data, offline caches and, for trackers, bigger profiles or IDs that survive cookie clearing. Quota is a share of free disk, so a site can fill hundreds of MB without a prompt. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Its own storage only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as the site saves your work or caches data. Store lots of "},"580":{"title":"localStorage","description":"A browser store of up to about 5 MB per site that scripts read at any time and that survives restarts.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-localstorage","content":"<Chips /> Can keep up to about 5 MB of text notes per site, until cleared A browser store of up to about 5 MB per site that scripts read at any time and that survives restarts. Holds settings, drafts, and very often a tracker's visitor ID that is re-created if the cookie is deleted. It is not sent to servers on its own, but every script on the page can read it. A common 'respawn' source for IDs. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Its own notes only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as you use"},"581":{"title":"Origin Private File System","description":"A private, fast file area per site that the person cannot browse and that counts against quota.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-opfs","content":"<Chips /> Can keep fast private files in the browser that count against its storage limit A private, fast file area per site that the person cannot browse and that counts against quota. Hosts SQLite or media caches in the browser. Invisible to the person in the file manager, uses the origin quota, and lasts until site data is cleared. Needs no prompt, unlike the File System Access picker. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Its own private area only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the a"},"582":{"title":"Storage persistence and eviction","description":"A site can ask the browser to keep its data permanently; otherwise it can be evicted when disk is low.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-persistence-eviction","content":"<Chips /> Can ask the browser to keep its saved data when space runs low A site can ask the browser to keep its data permanently; otherwise it can be evicted when disk is low. navigator.storage.persist() asks for protected storage. Chrome grants it from engagement, bookmarks or install, Firefox asks the person. Best effort data is deleted least recently used first. storage.estimate() reveals quota, a coarse signal. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A request, not a guarantee\" /> **What the user does:** Fi"},"583":{"title":"Persistent storage (persist)","description":"Asks the browser not to delete this site's stored data under disk pressure.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-persistent-storage","content":"<Chips /> Can ask the browser not to delete its saved data when disk space is low Asks the browser not to delete this site's stored data under disk pressure. Protects offline data (notes, drafts, downloaded media) from automatic eviction. Chrome and Safari decide silently from engagement; Firefox asks the person. A grant does not raise the quota. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A request, not a guarantee\" /> **What the user does:** In Firefox you click Allow on Store data in persistent storage. Elsewher"},"584":{"title":"Incognito and private mode detection","description":"Guesses whether the browser is in private mode using storage quota behavior.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-private-browsing-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can guess whether you are in a private or Incognito window Guesses whether the browser is in private mode using storage quota behavior. Tells the site when someone is deliberately browsing privately. LinkedIn's script uses the FileSystem quota (Chrome 74 and older) or storage.estimate. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A guess, taken once\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Test how much storage it gets, to guess private mode Change what it shows, such as article"},"585":{"title":"sessionStorage","description":"A per tab store of up to about 5 MB that is cleared when the tab closes.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-sessionstorage","content":"<Chips /> Can keep up to about 5 MB of notes for this tab until you close it A per tab store of up to about 5 MB that is cleared when the tab closes. Used for checkout steps and form state, and for trackers that carry a 'session ID' inside one tab. Different tabs of one site do not share it, so it cannot follow the person across tabs. Copied into a tab opened by window.open. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"This tab only, until closed\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as you move through st"},"586":{"title":"SharedWorker","description":"A background script that all tabs of a site share, so one process keeps state across them.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-sharedworker","content":"<Chips /> Can run one background script shared by all open tabs of the site A background script that all tabs of a site share, so one process keeps state across them. Lets a site keep one connection and a single cross-tab state, which also shows how many tabs the person has. Partitioned like other storage in modern browsers, so a tracker cannot join sites with it. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only tabs of the same site\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens while you have the site open in mo"},"587":{"title":"Storage Access API","description":"Lets an embedded third-party frame ask for its own cookies back.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-storage-access","content":"<Chips /> Can ask to get its own cookies back when shown inside another site Lets an embedded third-party frame ask for its own cookies back. When third-party cookies are blocked, a frame can ask to use its first-party cookies. A grant lets that embed recognise the person across sites where it is embedded. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only its own cookies, after you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in or Allow inside an embedded box, then answer the prompt. Ask for its own cookies in an"},"588":{"title":"Storage feature detection (localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB, openDatabase, addBehavior)","description":"Tests which storage APIs work in this browser.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-storage-feature-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can test which storage tools your browser lets it use Tests which storage APIs work in this browser. Support patterns show browser type and settings such as blocked storage. Old APIs (openDatabase, addBehavior) separate engines. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Yes or no answers, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check whether localStorage, sessionStorage and IndexedDB work Tell old browsers from new ones Guess private mode from the results Add the r"},"589":{"title":"Storage partitioning","description":"Browsers key embedded storage by the top site so a tracker sees a separate store on each site.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-storage-partitioning","content":"<Chips /> Can't use the same embedded storage to recognize you across different sites Browsers key embedded storage by the top site so a tracker sees a separate store on each site. Cuts the main cross-site tracking path: a widget on site A and site B no longer shares one cookie or localStorage. Embeds can still track within one site, and workarounds shift to link decoration, redirects and server side IDs. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Separate storage per site\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Your"},"590":{"title":"Storage quotas and eviction","description":"How much each site may store on the device, per browser.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-storage-quota","content":"<Chips /> Can store only what your browser allows, and may lose it when space is low How much each site may store on the device, per browser. Caps how much a site can keep (IndexedDB, Cache, files). Large quotas let a site store gigabytes; quota values are derived from total disk size, so estimate() was padded to limit fingerprinting. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A rough number, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check roughly how much space it has used and"},"591":{"title":"Top-level storage access (requestStorageAccessFor)","description":"Lets a top-level page request cookie access on behalf of related embedded sites.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-top-level-storage-access","content":"<Chips /> Can ask Chrome to let related embedded sites use their cookies, if you allow Lets a top-level page request cookie access on behalf of related embedded sites. Allows a page to unlock third-party cookies for sites in the same Related Website Set. Chrome only and deprecated; its use hints at cross-site identity joining. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Related sites only, after you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a button on the page, then answer the Chrome prompt if one appears. Ask for"},"592":{"title":"window.name carry over","description":"A string on the tab that survives page loads and was once used to carry data between sites.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/storage-window-name","content":"<Chips /> Can carry a short text string in the tab from one page to the next A string on the tab that survives page loads and was once used to carry data between sites. Browsers now clear or partition it on cross-site navigation, so trackers can no longer hand an ID from one site to the next in a tab. Same-site use for session state still works. Large strings (MBs) were a legacy storage trick. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One short string, per tab\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as yo"},"593":{"title":"Safari 7 day script storage deletion","description":"Safari deletes a site's script written storage after 7 days of Safari use without a visit to the site.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Files and storage"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/xsite-safari-7-day-storage-cap","content":"<Chips /> Can't keep script saved data in Safari after 7 days of use without a visit Safari deletes a site's script written storage after 7 days of Safari use without a visit to the site. Covers localStorage, IndexedDB, Cache, service workers, media keys and script set cookies. The counter is days of Safari use, not calendar days. Installed Home Screen web apps are exempt. Limits long lived tracker storage. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Safari clears it after 7 days\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing"},"594":{"title":"Account profile fields","description":"What the person typed when signing up: name, email, phone, address, birthday and more.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/auth-account-profile-fields","content":"<Chips /> Can keep the details you typed when signing up, such as name, email and address What the person typed when signing up: name, email, phone, address, birthday and more. Once signed in, every action ties to a stored identity. The site holds the typed fields plus order, device and login history. Email and phone are the keys ad platforms and data brokers use to match the person across services. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"What you typed, kept on its servers\" /> **What the user does:** You fill "},"595":{"title":"Apple Hide My Email relay","description":"A random Apple address that forwards mail to the person's real inbox.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/auth-apple-hide-my-email","content":"<Chips /> Can only see a random Apple address that forwards mail to your real inbox A random Apple address that forwards mail to the person's real inbox. A site sees random@privaterelay.appleid.com and cannot match the person across sites by email. Apple removes trackers inside forwarded mail only partly. The address stops working if the person deactivates it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A relay address, not your email\" /> **What the user does:** You choose Hide My Email when signing up with Apple. "},"596":{"title":"Cross device identity linking","description":"A site recognises one person across phone, laptop and tablet when they sign in on each.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/auth-cross-device-linking","content":"<Chips /> Can recognize you as one person across your phone, laptop and tablet when you sign in A site recognises one person across phone, laptop and tablet when they sign in on each. A shared login ID is a deterministic link. Hashed email, phone and shared IP add probabilistic links. Ad platforms with big login bases (Google, Meta, Apple) see the same person across devices; others rent graphs like UID2 or RampID. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Follows you across your devices\" /> **What the user does:*"},"597":{"title":"Device Bound Session Credentials","description":"A device held key that stops stolen session cookies from working on another computer.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/auth-dbsc","content":"<Chips /> Can tie your sign in to this device so copied cookies stop working elsewhere A device held key that stops stolen session cookies from working on another computer. The site gets short lived cookies refreshed with a signature from a key kept in the TPM or secure hardware. Reveals that the session is tied to one device. Chrome 146 on Windows (Apr 2026); GA for Google Workspace on May 25 2026. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your device and your sign in\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happ"},"598":{"title":"Federated Credential Management (FedCM)","description":"A browser mediated sign in that works without third party cookies or hidden redirects.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/auth-fedcm","content":"<Chips /> Can sign you in with the account you pick in a browser prompt A browser mediated sign in that works without third party cookies or hidden redirects. The browser asks the identity provider for account data, shows a prompt, and only after consent shares the ID token with the site. Stops identity providers from tracking silently. Google is moving its sign in to FedCM. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One account, one sign in\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in with Google (or similar) and "},"599":{"title":"Passkeys and WebAuthn sign in","description":"Phishing resistant sign in with a key stored on the device or password manager.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/auth-passkeys","content":"<Chips /> Can sign you in with a passkey after your fingerprint, face or PIN Phishing resistant sign in with a key stored on the device or password manager. The site gets a public key, a credential ID, a user handle and a signed challenge, not a secret. Attestation is usually withheld so the device model is not revealed. FIDO Alliance said in 2025 that over 15 billion accounts could use passkeys. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One sign in, you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in with a "},"600":{"title":"Password manager and Credential Management API","description":"Browsers and password managers fill saved logins and let a site read or store a credential.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/auth-password-credential-management","content":"<Chips /> Can fill your saved login once you pick it, and offer to save new ones Browsers and password managers fill saved logins and let a site read or store a credential. A site learns the username the person chose, not the password manager's other entries. The Credential Management API lets a site request a saved credential after a gesture. Reused passwords across sites are the main account risk. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"The login you choose\" /> **What the user does:** You tap the username box"},"601":{"title":"Session cookies and tokens","description":"The cookie or token that keeps a person signed in between pages.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/auth-session-cookies","content":"<Chips /> Can keep you signed in between pages with a small ID stored in your browser The cookie or token that keeps a person signed in between pages. A random session ID (OWASP: at least 64 bits of entropy) ties each request to an account. Whoever holds it is the person. Timeouts are 2 to 5 minutes idle for high risk apps and up to 30 minutes for low risk, per OWASP. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Whole session, until sign out\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing extra. It starts when you sign in and c"},"602":{"title":"Sign in with Apple","description":"A login where Apple confirms identity and can hide the person's real email address.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/auth-sign-in-with-apple","content":"<Chips /> Can sign you in with Apple and get a real or hidden email address A login where Apple confirms identity and can hide the person's real email address. Site gets a sub ID, email (real or relay) and email\\_verified; name only on first sign in. is\\_private\\_email and real\\_user\\_status (0 unsupported, 1 unknown, 2 likely real) are returned. Required in App Store apps that offer other social logins. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Name and email you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in"},"603":{"title":"Sign in with Google","description":"A one click login where Google tells the site who the person is.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/auth-sign-in-with-google","content":"<Chips /> Can sign you in with Google and learn your name, email and photo A one click login where Google tells the site who the person is. The site gets a signed ID token with sub (stable user ID), email, email\\_verified, name, picture, locale and for Workspace users the hd domain. Extra scopes can add Calendar, Drive or Contacts. The site gets the same sub every visit. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Basic profile, extra on request\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in with Google and pick an ac"},"604":{"title":"Sign in with LinkedIn","description":"A login where LinkedIn confirms the person and shares basic professional profile details.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/auth-sign-in-with-linkedin","content":"<Chips /> Can sign you in with LinkedIn and see your basic profile details A login where LinkedIn confirms the person and shares basic professional profile details. With scopes openid, profile and email the site gets sub, name, given\\_name, family\\_name, picture, locale, email and email\\_verified in an ID token. Work history and connections need separate approved partner APIs. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Basic profile you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in with LinkedIn and approve "},"605":{"title":"Sign in with Microsoft","description":"A login where Microsoft Entra or a personal account tells the site who the person is.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/auth-sign-in-with-microsoft","content":"<Chips /> Can sign you in with Microsoft and learn your name, email and employer A login where Microsoft Entra or a personal account tells the site who the person is. ID token claims include sub, oid (directory object ID), tid (tenant ID), name, preferred\\_username and optionally email. Work accounts reveal the employer's tenant ID. Graph scopes can add mail, files and calendar. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Basic profile, extra on request\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in with Microsoft and"},"606":{"title":"TLS client certificates","description":"A site can ask the browser to present a certificate stored on the computer.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/identity-client-certificates","content":"<Chips /> Can ask your browser to show a certificate stored on your computer A site can ask the browser to present a certificate stored on the computer. Certificates identify a person or company. The browser asks which cert to send; used in banks and enterprise. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One certificate you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You open a bank or company site and pick a certificate in the box that appears Ask you to choose one certificate from those on your device Receive the certifica"},"607":{"title":"Credential Management (password and federated)","description":"Lets a site store and fetch saved passwords through the browser.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/identity-credential-management","content":"<Chips /> Can fetch a saved login after you pick an account in a browser sheet Lets a site store and fetch saved passwords through the browser. Gets a stored username and password after the person picks an account, without scraping form fields. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"The saved login you choose\" /> **What the user does:** You tap the username box or Sign in and choose an account from the sheet Show saved accounts for that site in a chooser Receive the username and password for the account you pi"},"608":{"title":"Digital Credentials API (mDL and wallet IDs)","description":"Lets a site ask for a government ID or other credential from a digital wallet.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/identity-digital-credentials","content":"<Chips /> Can ask for facts from your digital ID, only the ones you approve Lets a site ask for a government ID or other credential from a digital wallet. Requests only fields the person approves (age over 21, name, address) from a wallet such as Google Wallet or Apple Wallet, with a browser prompt. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only the ID fields you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Verify with ID and approve each item in your wallet Ask your wallet for one fact such as age over 21, name o"},"609":{"title":"FedCM (Federated Credential Management)","description":"Lets a site ask the browser to sign the person in with Google, Apple or similar without third party cookies.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/identity-fedcm","content":"<Chips /> Can sign you in with Google, Apple or similar using a browser account picker Lets a site ask the browser to sign the person in with Google, Apple or similar without third party cookies. Gives the site an ID token for a chosen identity provider account; the browser mediates the prompt. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One account, one sign in\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in with Google (or similar) or answer a prompt that appears on the page Show a browser box listing accounts you ha"},"610":{"title":"HTTP authentication (Basic, Digest, Negotiate)","description":"A server can make the browser ask for a username and password.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/identity-http-auth","content":"<Chips /> Can make your browser ask for a username and password in a plain box A server can make the browser ask for a username and password. Browser shows a native login box and then sends credentials on every request to that realm. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While you use that site\" /> **What the user does:** You open a page that is protected, and a login box appears Show a login box from the browser itself Receive the username and password you type Send those details again with every request to "},"611":{"title":"Sign In with LinkedIn (OpenID Connect)","description":"Lets a site sign people in with their LinkedIn account and receive basic profile data.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/identity-linkedin-oidc-signin","content":"<Chips /> Can sign you in with LinkedIn and get your name, photo and email Lets a site sign people in with their LinkedIn account and receive basic profile data. After the person approves, the site gets a stable pairwise ID, name, photo, locale and optionally a verified email. It does not verify real world identity. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Basic profile you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in with LinkedIn and approve the LinkedIn consent screen Receive a stable ID for you that i"},"612":{"title":"Private State Tokens","description":"Lets one site share a trust signal about a visitor with another without identifying them.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/identity-private-state-tokens","content":"<Chips /> Can pass a small trust badge about you between sites without naming you Lets one site share a trust signal about a visitor with another without identifying them. Carries a one bit trust token to other origins so bots can be told from people without a login. Privacy Sandbox wind down affects its future. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One trust signal, no identity\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens quietly in the background when the page loads Store a token after a site decides you"},"613":{"title":"Storage Access API (for embedded SSO)","description":"Lets an embedded iframe ask for its own cookies back on third party pages.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/identity-storage-access-sso","content":"<Chips /> Can ask to get its own cookies back when shown inside another site Lets an embedded iframe ask for its own cookies back on third party pages. Restores a login cookie inside an embed with a browser prompt. Used for SSO widgets and embedded apps. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Its own cookies, on that page\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a sign in or sign up button inside an embedded widget Show a browser prompt asking to let the embedded site use its own cookies Keep you signed in inside t"},"614":{"title":"WebAuthn passkey registration","description":"Creates a passkey or security key credential for the site.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/identity-webauthn-create","content":"<Chips /> Can create a passkey or security key login for one site Creates a passkey or security key credential for the site. Makes a public key login bound to the site's domain. The site never sees the private key or biometrics. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One passkey, one site\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Create a passkey and confirm with your fingerprint, face, phone or key Ask your device to make a new key pair tied to that site Save the public half on its servers so you can sign in later "},"615":{"title":"WebAuthn sign in (get) and conditional mediation","description":"Signs the person in with a passkey, including autofill style suggestions.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/identity-webauthn-get","content":"<Chips /> Can sign you in with a passkey, even from the username box suggestions Signs the person in with a passkey, including autofill style suggestions. Proves who the person is without a password. Conditional UI shows passkeys in the username field. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One sign in, you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Sign in with a passkey, or pick one from the username box Ask your device to prove you hold the passkey for that site Show your passkeys as suggestions in the use"},"616":{"title":"WebAuthn PRF and largeBlob extensions","description":"Derives a secret from a passkey or stores a small blob on it.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/identity-webauthn-prf","content":"<Chips /> Can use your passkey to make an encryption key or store a tiny note on it Derives a secret from a passkey or stores a small blob on it. Lets an app use the passkey as a key for end to end encryption (PRF is a 32 byte HMAC output). largeBlob stores small data on the authenticator. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One secret per passkey tap\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Unlock or Sign in on an app that protects your data with a passkey Ask your passkey to make a secret value, 32 bytes long,"},"617":{"title":"WebAuthn Related Origin Requests","description":"Lets one passkey work across several of a company's domains.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/identity-webauthn-related-origins","content":"<Chips /> Can let one passkey work across a company's own listed websites Lets one passkey work across several of a company's domains. Allows an RP ID to be shared by listed sites so passkeys follow brand domains. Limited labels prevent abuse. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only the company's listed sites\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing extra. It happens when you use a passkey on any of that company's sites Share one passkey across a short list of the company's own domains Let you sign in on a bran"},"618":{"title":"WebAuthn Signal API","description":"Lets a site tell the passkey manager a credential is gone or its name changed.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Sign in and identity"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/identity-webauthn-signal-api","content":"<Chips /> Can tell your passkey manager that a passkey is gone or renamed Lets a site tell the passkey manager a credential is gone or its name changed. Keeps the passkey list clean. Shows no data back to the site. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One note, nothing sent back\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing extra. It happens after you delete an account or change your username Send a note that a passkey no longer works so the manager can hide it Send a new username or display name so your passkey list "},"619":{"title":"Sign in and identity","description":"Face ID, passkeys, accounts and logins. 25 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth","content":"Face ID, passkeys, accounts and logins. 25 capabilities in this group. | Capability             | Kind                 | In one line                                                                     |\n| ---------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Account profile fields | Works without asking | Can keep the details you typed when signing up, such as name, email and address | | Capability                | Kind          | In one line                                                               |\n| ------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Apple Hide My Email relay | Gets silently | Can only see a random Apple address that forwards mail to your real inbox | | Capability                                     | Kind           | In one line                                                          |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Credential Management (password and federated) | Asks you first | Can fetch a saved login after you pick an account in a browser sheet | | Capability                    | Kind              | In one line                                                                           |\n| ----------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Cross device identity linking | Used to track you | Can recognize you as one person across your phone, laptop and tablet when you sign in | | Capability                       | Kind                 | In one line                                                                  |\n| -------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Device Bound Session Credentials | Works without asking | Can tie your sign in to this device so copied cookies stop working elsewhere | | Capability                                   | Kind           | In one line                                                       |\n| -------------------------------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Digital Credentials API (mDL and wallet IDs) | Asks you first | Can ask for facts from your digital ID, only the ones you approve | | Capability                              | Kind           | In one line                                                                  |\n| --------------------------------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| FedCM (Federated Credential Management) | Asks you first | Can sign you in with Google, Apple or similar using a browser account picker | | Capability                              | Kind           | In one line                                                   |\n| --------------------------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Federated Credential Management (FedCM) | Asks you first | Can sign you in with the account you pick in a browser prompt | | Capability                                     | Kind                 | In one line                                                          |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| HTTP authentication (Basic, Digest, Negotiate) | Works without asking | Can make your browser ask for a username and password in a plain box | | Capability                    | Kind                 | In one line                                                        |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Passkeys and WebAuthn sign in | Works without asking | Can sign you in with a passkey after your fingerprint, face or PIN | | Capability                                     | Kind                 | In one line                                                            |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Password manager and Credential Management API | Works without asking | Can fill your saved login once you pick it, and offer to save new ones | | Capability           | Kind                 | In one line                                                             |\n| -------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Private State Tokens | Works without asking | Can pass a small trust badge about you between sites without naming you | | Capability                 | Kind                 | In one line                                                                 |\n| -------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Session cookies and tokens | Works without asking | Can keep you signed in between pages with a small ID stored in your browser | | Capability         | Kind                 | In one line                                                       |\n| ------------------ | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Sign in with Apple | Works without asking | Can sign you in with Apple and get a real or hidden email address | | Capability          | Kind                 | In one line                                                      |\n| ------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Sign in with Google | Works without asking | Can sign you in with Google and learn your name, email and photo | | Capabili"},"620":{"title":"Websites (7 browsers)","description":"483 capabilities of websites, with support in 7 browsers.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web","content":"483 capabilities of websites, with support in 7 browsers. <Card title=\"Location and motion\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/location-motion/\">Where you are and how you move. 9 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Camera, mic and screen\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/camera-mic-screen/\">Seeing, hearing and recording. 12 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Contacts and personal data\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/personal-data-contacts/\">Your people, photos, calendar and more. 6 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Sign in and identity\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/identity-auth/\">Face ID, passkeys, accounts and logins. 25 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Payments and shopping\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/payments-commerce/\">Paying, wallets and purchases. 8 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Notifications and background\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/\">Alerts and work that runs when the app is closed. 14 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Files and storage\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/files-storage/\">Saving, opening and keeping data. 29 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Hardware and accessories\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/\">Bluetooth, USB, NFC, controllers, sensors. 22 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Network and connection\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/\">Wi-Fi, cell signal, internet speed. 39 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Device details\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/device-system-profile/\">Model, memory, battery and other facts about the device. 67 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Screen and windows\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/display-window/\">Screen size, brightness, full screen, windows. 34 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Typing and clipboard\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/\">Keyboard, copy and paste, touch and mouse. 16 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Language and settings\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/locale-preferences/\">Language, time zone, dark mode, text size. 10 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Speed and timing\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/\">How fast things load and run. 29 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"On-device AI\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/on-device-ai/\">AI that runs right on your device. 10 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Home screen and system links\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/\">Installing, widgets, shortcuts, sharing. 15 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Tracking across apps and sites\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/cross-site-tracking/\">IDs and tricks that follow you around. 61 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Graphics and fingerprinting\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/\">Drawing and the hidden device clues it leaks. 38 capabilities.</Card> <Card title=\"Things a page can do\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/\">Actions a website can take for you. 39 capabilities.</Card>"},"621":{"title":"Typing and clipboard","description":"Keyboard, copy and paste, touch and mouse. 16 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard","content":"Keyboard, copy and paste, touch and mouse. 16 capabilities in this group. | Capability           | Kind           | In one line                                                   |\n| -------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Async Clipboard read | Asks you first | Can see what you last copied, after you allow it or tap Paste | | Capability            | Kind                          | In one line                                                    |\n| --------------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Async Clipboard write | Does something on your device | Can put text or images on your clipboard when you tap a button | | Capability                                     | Kind                          | In one line                                                           |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Copy and cut events (rewrite what gets copied) | Does something on your device | Can change what ends up on your clipboard when you copy from its page | | Capability                      | Kind                 | In one line                                                 |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Editable regions and spellcheck | Works without asking | Can turn parts of a page into a text editor you can type in | | Capability                      | Kind                 | In one line                                                               |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| EditContext (custom text input) | Works without asking | Can run its own text editor that still gets your keyboard and emoji input | | Capability              | Kind          | In one line                                         |\n| ----------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| Keyboard and IME events | Gets silently | Can see each key you press while the page has focus | | Capability          | Kind                 | In one line                                                       |\n| ------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Keyboard layout map | Works without asking | Can learn which keyboard layout you use, such as QWERTY or AZERTY | | Capability    | Kind                          | In one line                                                            |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Keyboard Lock | Does something on your device | Can capture keys like Escape and Alt+Tab while the page is full screen | | Capability                 | Kind          | In one line                                                             |\n| -------------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Keyboard Map (layout leak) | Gets silently | Can tell your keyboard layout, which hints at your country and language | | Capability        | Kind           | In one line                                                       |\n| ----------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Local Font Access | Asks you first | Can list the fonts installed on your computer, after you allow it | | Capability                                | Kind                 | In one line                                                       |\n| ----------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Paste event (read what the person pastes) | Works without asking | Can get what you paste, but only when you press paste on its page | | Capability                                | Kind          | In one line                                                             |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Pointer events (mouse, pen, touch detail) | Gets silently | Can see where your mouse, finger or pen is and how it moves on the page | | Capability   | Kind                          | In one line                                                                |\n| ------------ | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Pointer Lock | Does something on your device | Can hide your mouse pointer and capture its movement for games or 3D views | | Capability               | Kind          | In one line                                                            |\n| ------------------------ | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Text selection and caret | Gets silently | Can see which of its own words you highlight and move your text cursor | | Capability                         | Kind          | In one line                                                                |\n| ---------------------------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Touch events and touch point count | Gets silently | Can see your finger touches and how many touch points your device supports | | Capability           | Kind                 | In one line                                                                |\n| -------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Virtual Keyboard API | Works without asking | Can control how the on screen keyboard moves the page, and show or hi"},"622":{"title":"Async Clipboard read","description":"Asks to read what is on the clipboard.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-clipboard-read","content":"<Chips /> Can see what you last copied, after you allow it or tap Paste Asks to read what is on the clipboard. Can show anything the person copied: passwords, addresses, personal messages. Chromium asks permission; Firefox and Safari show a one-time Paste menu so the person approves each read. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Each time you allow or paste\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Paste on the page, or allow clipboard access when the browser asks See text, images or other items you copied, one r"},"623":{"title":"Async Clipboard write","description":"Puts text or images onto the clipboard.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-clipboard-write","content":"<Chips /> Can put text or images on your clipboard when you tap a button Puts text or images onto the clipboard. Can overwrite what the person copied, for example swapping a crypto address. Allowed on a click without a prompt in all browsers. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One copy, when you tap\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Copy link, Copy code or a similar button Copy text, links or images onto your clipboard after a tap or click Replace whatever you had copied before Work without any pop up in"},"624":{"title":"Editable regions and spellcheck","description":"Makes any element editable; the browser spellcheck may run on what is typed.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-contenteditable","content":"<Chips /> Can turn parts of a page into a text editor you can type in Makes any element editable; the browser spellcheck may run on what is typed. A site can build rich editors from plain HTML. With a cloud assisted spellcheck on, typed text can leave the device, so editing in a web page is not always private. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only what you type there\" /> **What the user does:** You click into an editable box and start typing Let you type, format and edit text right on the page Run browse"},"625":{"title":"Copy and cut events (rewrite what gets copied)","description":"Lets a page change what the person's copy or cut puts on the clipboard.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-copy-event","content":"<Chips /> Can change what ends up on your clipboard when you copy from its page Lets a page change what the person's copy or cut puts on the clipboard. When the person presses copy, the site can append a credit line, strip formatting, or substitute other text. It cannot see other apps' copies; it only controls copies made inside its own page. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only copies made on that page\" /> **What the user does:** You press copy or cut on text inside the page Add a credit line or link t"},"626":{"title":"EditContext (custom text input)","description":"Lets canvas or custom editors receive IME and keyboard text input without a hidden text box.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-edit-context","content":"<Chips /> Can run its own text editor that still gets your keyboard and emoji input Lets canvas or custom editors receive IME and keyboard text input without a hidden text box. A page can run its own editor surface and still get full IME and emoji input. It sees the same typing data as a normal text field, so the privacy picture is unchanged. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"What you type in its editor\" /> **What the user does:** You click into a custom editor and type Receive your typing, including fore"},"627":{"title":"Keyboard and IME events","description":"Sees every key pressed while the page has focus, plus IME composition text.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-keyboard-events","content":"<Chips /> Can see each key you press while the page has focus Sees every key pressed while the page has focus, plus IME composition text. A page that has focus gets each keydown with the key and its physical code, and exact timing. Typing rhythm can identify a person; composition events reveal the language they type in. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Every key while the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as you type while the page is open and in front See which key you pressed"},"628":{"title":"Keyboard layout map","description":"Reads which character each physical key makes, so the page can tell the keyboard layout.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-keyboard-layout-map","content":"<Chips /> Can learn which keyboard layout you use, such as QWERTY or AZERTY Reads which character each physical key makes, so the page can tell the keyboard layout. The layout map shows if the person types on QWERTY, AZERTY, Dvorak or a language specific keyboard. That narrows country and language, and helps shortcuts, but it is also a fingerprint hint. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One layout check\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page or app loads Check which letter each phys"},"629":{"title":"Keyboard Lock","description":"Captures keys like Escape and Alt+Tab while in fullscreen.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-keyboard-lock","content":"<Chips /> Can capture keys like Escape and Alt+Tab while the page is full screen Captures keys like Escape and Alt+Tab while in fullscreen. Lets games and remote desktops receive keys the browser normally keeps, so a site can trap Escape or Ctrl+W. Works only in fullscreen; a long-press of Escape always exits. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While in full screen\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a full screen button in a game or remote desktop Take over keys the browser normally keeps, such as Escape "},"630":{"title":"Keyboard Map (layout leak)","description":"Reveals the physical-to-printed key mapping, i.e. the keyboard layout.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-keyboard-map","content":"<Chips /> Can tell your keyboard layout, which hints at your country and language Reveals the physical-to-printed key mapping, i.e. the keyboard layout. Shows which keyboard layout the person uses (QWERTY, AZERTY, Dvorak, language), a strong locale and fingerprint signal. No prompt. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One layout check\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page or app loads Check which character each physical key produces Guess your language or region from that layout Add "},"631":{"title":"Local Font Access","description":"Asks to list every font installed on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-local-fonts","content":"<Chips /> Can list the fonts installed on your computer, after you allow it Asks to list every font installed on the device. Reveals the full list of installed fonts and raw font files, which identifies the device and installed software. Gated by prompt because font lists were a long-standing fingerprint. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Once you allow it for that site\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a font picker button and choose Allow when Chrome asks See the names of all installed fonts Open the "},"632":{"title":"Paste event (read what the person pastes)","description":"Gives the page the clipboard contents, but only when the person presses paste inside the page.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-paste-event","content":"<Chips /> Can get what you paste, but only when you press paste on its page Gives the page the clipboard contents, but only when the person presses paste inside the page. A paste is consent for that one read: the site gets text, HTML or files the person chose to paste. It cannot peek at the clipboard at other times, which blocks silent snooping. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One paste, when you press it\" /> **What the user does:** You press Ctrl+V, Cmd+V, or use Paste from the menu on the page Receive"},"633":{"title":"Pointer events (mouse, pen, touch detail)","description":"Reports pointer position and, for pens and touch, pressure, tilt and contact size.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-pointer-events","content":"<Chips /> Can see where your mouse, finger or pen is and how it moves on the page Reports pointer position and, for pens and touch, pressure, tilt and contact size. Mouse paths, speed and hesitation are strong behavioral signals (bot detection, attention tracking). Pen and touch fields add pressure and tilt that can tell devices apart. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Every move while the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens while you move or tap over the page Follow the path and "},"634":{"title":"Pointer Lock","description":"Hides and captures the mouse for games and 3D views.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-pointer-lock","content":"<Chips /> Can hide your mouse pointer and capture its movement for games or 3D views Hides and captures the mouse for games and 3D views. Gives raw relative mouse movement with unlimited range while the cursor is hidden. With unadjustedMovement the site gets unaccelerated input, a small hardware hint. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While you use that view\" /> **What the user does:** You click inside a game or 3D view Hide the pointer and track raw mouse movement Let you turn a camera or view without hi"},"635":{"title":"Text selection and caret","description":"Lets the page see what is selected inside itself and move the cursor; nothing outside the page.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-selection","content":"<Chips /> Can see which of its own words you highlight and move your text cursor Lets the page see what is selected inside itself and move the cursor; nothing outside the page. A site knows which of its own words the person highlights, which reveals interest and reading focus. It cannot see a selection in another tab or app. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only text on its own page\" /> **What the user does:** You highlight text or click in a box on the page See the text you select on the page Move the c"},"636":{"title":"Touch events and touch point count","description":"Reports finger positions and the number of touch points the device supports.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-touch-events","content":"<Chips /> Can see your finger touches and how many touch points your device supports Reports finger positions and the number of touch points the device supports. navigator.maxTouchPoints separates phones, tablets and touch laptops from plain desktops, a common fingerprint input. Touch radius and force add small device level detail. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Touches on that page\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you touch the screen, and the touch count is checked on load See whe"},"637":{"title":"Virtual Keyboard API","description":"Lets a page control whether the on-screen keyboard resizes the page or overlays it, and show or hide it.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Typing and clipboard"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/input-clipboard/input-virtual-keyboard","content":"<Chips /> Can control how the on screen keyboard moves the page, and show or hide it Lets a page control whether the on-screen keyboard resizes the page or overlays it, and show or hide it. A web app can take over keyboard layout handling and learn the keyboard rectangle. Keyboard height hints at device type and which keyboard (emoji, numeric) is open. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the keyboard is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap into a text box on a phone or tablet Choose whether the ke"},"638":{"title":"Language and settings","description":"Language, time zone, dark mode, text size. 10 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/locale-preferences","content":"Language, time zone, dark mode, text size. 10 capabilities in this group. | Capability                      | Kind              | In one line                                                                       |\n| ------------------------------- | ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Accessibility settings inferred | Used to track you | Can guess you may use accessibility settings by combining several browser signals | | Capability       | Kind          | In one line                                           |\n| ---------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| Browser language | Gets silently | Can see which language your browser asks pages to use | | Capability                         | Kind          | In one line                                                                |\n| ---------------------------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Browser zoom and default text size | Gets silently | Can guess your page zoom and your default text size from how pages lay out | | Capability   | Kind          | In one line                                                         |\n| ------------ | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Do Not Track | Gets silently | Can see whether you turned on Do Not Track, which most sites ignore | | Capability        | Kind          | In one line                                |\n| ----------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| Do Not Track flag | Gets silently | Can see whether you turned on Do Not Track | | Capability             | Kind          | In one line                                                 |\n| ---------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Global Privacy Control | Gets silently | Can see that you asked sites not to sell or share your data | | Capability          | Kind          | In one line                                                  |\n| ------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Intl locale formats | Gets silently | Can see the date, number and clock formats your browser uses | | Capability          | Kind          | In one line                                                     |\n| ------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Keyboard layout map | Gets silently | Can tell your keyboard layout, such as QWERTY, AZERTY or QWERTZ | | Capability                                          | Kind          | In one line                                                         |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Languages (Accept-Language and navigator.languages) | Gets silently | Can see the languages you prefer, in order, with every page request | | Capability               | Kind          | In one line                                              |\n| ------------------------ | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Time zone and UTC offset | Gets silently | Can tell your time zone, roughly which region you are in |"},"639":{"title":"Languages (Accept-Language and navigator.languages)","description":"Ordered list of the person's preferred languages, sent on every request.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/locale-preferences/locale-accept-language","content":"<Chips /> Can see the languages you prefer, in order, with every page request Ordered list of the person's preferred languages, sent on every request. Shows native language, second languages and country (en-US vs en-GB, es-419). A multilingual list narrows identity sharply: 5.9 bits in 2016. Sites localize with it; trackers fingerprint it. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Sent with every page request\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Your browser sends it every time a page loads See your first languag"},"640":{"title":"Accessibility settings inferred","description":"Combining contrast, motion, forced colors, zoom and font size to guess a disability.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/locale-preferences/locale-accessibility-inference","content":"<Chips /> Can guess you may use accessibility settings by combining several browser signals Combining contrast, motion, forced colors, zoom and font size to guess a disability. No single API says disabled, but together prefers-contrast, forced-colors, reduced-motion, large fonts and zoom flag vision, vestibular or motor needs. Under GDPR or similar this may be special-category data. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Settings your browser reveals\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens quietly whil"},"641":{"title":"Do Not Track","description":"Header and property saying the person asked not to be tracked; mostly ignored.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/locale-preferences/locale-dnt","content":"<Chips /> Can see whether you turned on Do Not Track, which most sites ignore Header and property saying the person asked not to be tracked; mostly ignored. Reveals a privacy preference (about 1 in 4 desktop Chrome and Firefox users enabled it at its peak) and ironically adds a fingerprint bit. Safari removed it in 12.1; Firefox is removing the setting. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no setting\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Your browser sends it with each page request if the setting i"},"642":{"title":"Global Privacy Control","description":"Browser signal that the person opts out of sale or sharing of data.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/locale-preferences/locale-gpc","content":"<Chips /> Can see that you asked sites not to sell or share your data Browser signal that the person opts out of sale or sharing of data. Legally binding opt-out in California, Colorado, Connecticut and more, so sites must honor it. Also tells the site this user runs privacy-focused settings or a browser like Firefox, DuckDuckGo or Brave. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no setting\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Your browser sends it with each page request if the setting is on Check the "},"643":{"title":"Intl locale formats","description":"Number, date, calendar, numbering system and hour cycle formats the browser would use.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/locale-preferences/locale-intl-formats","content":"<Chips /> Can see the date, number and clock formats your browser uses Number, date, calendar, numbering system and hour cycle formats the browser would use. resolvedOptions() exposes calendar (gregory, buddhist, japanese), numberingSystem (latn, arab), hourCycle (h12 or h23), and decimal and thousands marks. Reveals regional OS settings beyond language. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One check of regional formats\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page or app loads Check the cale"},"644":{"title":"Keyboard layout map","description":"Which character each physical key produces, revealing QWERTY, AZERTY, QWERTZ, Dvorak etc.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/locale-preferences/locale-keyboard-layout","content":"<Chips /> Can tell your keyboard layout, such as QWERTY, AZERTY or QWERTZ Which character each physical key produces, revealing QWERTY, AZERTY, QWERTZ, Dvorak etc. Tells native keyboard layout and so likely country and language (French AZERTY, German QWERTZ, Colemak enthusiasts). Chromium returns about 48 key codes, no prompt. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One layout check\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page or app loads Check which letter each physical key types, about 48 ke"},"645":{"title":"Browser language","description":"Reads the language the browser asks pages to use.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/locale-preferences/locale-language","content":"<Chips /> Can see which language your browser asks pages to use Reads the language the browser asks pages to use. Reveals language, region and sometimes nationality. Order of preferred languages narrows identity. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One browser setting\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads See your preferred language and region Show the page in that language Guess your country or background from the order of languages See your exact location from it Know languag"},"646":{"title":"Time zone and UTC offset","description":"IANA time zone name plus UTC offset from Intl and Date.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/locale-preferences/locale-timezone","content":"<Chips /> Can tell your time zone, roughly which region you are in IANA time zone name plus UTC offset from Intl and Date. Narrows physical location to a region (about 400 IANA zones; America/Chicago). Mismatch with IP location is a VPN tell. 3 bits at the offset level, more with the full zone name. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A region, not a place\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page or app loads See your time zone name, such as America/Chicago, and its offset from UTC Show"},"647":{"title":"Browser zoom and default text size","description":"Infers page zoom and the person's default font size from layout measurements.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/locale-preferences/locale-zoom-text-size","content":"<Chips /> Can guess your page zoom and your default text size from how pages lay out Infers page zoom and the person's default font size from layout measurements. Zoom (50% to 500%) from devicePixelRatio and window size; default font size (usually 16 px, but 12 to 24 px settable) from computed em. Large values suggest low vision or older users. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One layout check\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as the page measures itself while loading Guess your zoom, from "},"648":{"title":"Do Not Track flag","description":"Reads whether the person turned on Do Not Track.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Language and settings"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/locale-preferences/privacy-do-not-track","content":"<Chips /> Can see whether you turned on Do Not Track Reads whether the person turned on Do Not Track. A setting few sites honor; LinkedIn says it does not generally respond to it. The collector reads it but excludes it from the hash. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One yes or no setting\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It is checked when the page or app loads Check your Do Not Track setting Leave it out of the data used to recognise your browser, in some tools Make sites stop tracking you; few honor"},"649":{"title":"Location and motion","description":"Where you are and how you move. 9 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/location-motion","content":"Where you are and how you move. 9 capabilities in this group. | Capability                                              | Kind           | In one line                                               |\n| ------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| AbsoluteOrientationSensor and RelativeOrientationSensor | Asks you first | Can find which way your device is facing and tilted in 3D | | Capability                                             | Kind           | In one line                                                     |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Accelerometer, LinearAccelerationSensor, GravitySensor | Asks you first | Can feel how your device moves and rests while the page is open | | Capability         | Kind           | In one line                                                 |\n| ------------------ | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| AmbientLightSensor | Asks you first | Can't use your device's light sensor in most browsers today | | Capability                                | Kind           | In one line                                                                      |\n| ----------------------------------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| DeviceOrientation and DeviceMotion events | Asks you first | Can sense how your phone is tilted and shaken, sometimes only after you allow it | | Capability  | Kind           | In one line                                                   |\n| ----------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Geolocation | Asks you first | Can find where your device is, once or while the page is open | | Capability             | Kind           | In one line                                                                 |\n| ---------------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Geolocation in iframes | Asks you first | Can let an embedded map ask for your location, only if the host page allows | | Capability | Kind           | In one line                                                |\n| ---------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Gyroscope  | Asks you first | Can feel how fast your device turns while the page is open | | Capability                       | Kind           | In one line                                                                |\n| -------------------------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| HTML &lt;geolocation&gt; element | Asks you first | Can show a browser made button that asks for your location when you tap it | | Capability   | Kind           | In one line                                                     |\n| ------------ | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Magnetometer | Asks you first | Can sense the magnetic field around your device, like a compass |"},"650":{"title":"DeviceOrientation and DeviceMotion events","description":"Tilt and shake data from the phone; iOS asks first, Android Chrome does not.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/location-motion/location-device-orientation","content":"<Chips /> Can sense how your phone is tilted and shaken, sometimes only after you allow it Tilt and shake data from the phone; iOS asks first, Android Chrome does not. Reveals how the device is held and moved: tilt, walking cadence, vehicle vibration. Research has inferred PINs and keystrokes from tilt patterns, and it hints at device class. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You open a tilt based page, like a game or AR view, and tap Allow on iPhone Measu"},"651":{"title":"HTML <geolocation> element","description":"A browser-drawn button that asks for location; the click itself is the request.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/location-motion/location-geolocation-element","content":"<Chips /> Can show a browser made button that asks for your location when you tap it A browser-drawn button that asks for location; the click itself is the request. Same data as Geolocation, but the request starts from a trusted button the site cannot disguise, so prompts only appear when the person asked. Chrome can also re-ask after a block. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only after you tap, then while allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap the Use my location button drawn by the browser Ask for"},"652":{"title":"Geolocation in iframes","description":"Lets an embedded frame ask for location only if the parent page allows it.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/location-motion/location-geolocation-iframe","content":"<Chips /> Can let an embedded map ask for your location, only if the host page allows Lets an embedded frame ask for location only if the parent page allows it. Controls whether ad, map or widget frames can see where the person is. The default blocks cross-origin frames so third parties cannot request location through a host page. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Find me or Use my location inside an embedded map or widget Ask for location from in"},"653":{"title":"Geolocation","description":"Asks the person for their device location, precise or approximate.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/location-motion/location-geolocation","content":"<Chips /> Can find where your device is, once or while the page is open Asks the person for their device location, precise or approximate. Tells the site where the person physically is, down to a few meters outdoors: home, workplace, travel, daily routine. Repeated fixes reveal patterns of life. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Use my location, Find stores near me or a map button, then choose Allow Find your position, precise or approximate where"},"654":{"title":"Accelerometer, LinearAccelerationSensor, GravitySensor","description":"Reads device acceleration, with or without gravity, as a stream.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/location-motion/location-sensor-accelerometer","content":"<Chips /> Can feel how your device moves and rests while the page is open Reads device acceleration, with or without gravity, as a stream. Shows how the phone moves and rests: walking cadence, vehicle vibration, on a table or in a pocket. Sensor noise can fingerprint the hardware. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing in most browsers. Chrome allows it by default unless you changed Motion sensors in site settings Measure movement with or without gravi"},"655":{"title":"AmbientLightSensor","description":"Reads the light level (lux) around the device; not shipped by default anywhere.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/location-motion/location-sensor-ambient-light","content":"<Chips /> Can't use your device's light sensor in most browsers today Reads the light level (lux) around the device; not shipped by default anywhere. Shows whether the person is indoors, outdoors or in a dark room. Research showed it can leak visited links and cross-origin pixels, so Chrome keeps it behind a flag and Firefox removed it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Off by default almost everywhere\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Chrome keeps it off by default behind a setting, and Firefox remove"},"656":{"title":"Gyroscope","description":"Reads how fast the device is rotating around three axes.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/location-motion/location-sensor-gyroscope","content":"<Chips /> Can feel how fast your device turns while the page is open Reads how fast the device is rotating around three axes. Shows how fast the device turns. Gyroscope noise has been used to eavesdrop on nearby speech (Gyrophone, 2014) and to fingerprint devices, which is why browsers cap the rate. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing in most browsers. Chrome allows it by default unless you changed Motion sensors in site settings Measure turning spe"},"657":{"title":"Magnetometer","description":"Reads the magnetic field around the device, like a compass.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/location-motion/location-sensor-magnetometer","content":"<Chips /> Can sense the magnetic field around your device, like a compass Reads the magnetic field around the device, like a compass. Gives compass heading and can reveal nearby metal and electronics. Indoor magnetic fields form a rough fingerprint of a building or room. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing in most browsers. Chrome allows it by default unless you changed Motion sensors in site settings Find which way your device points, like a compas"},"658":{"title":"AbsoluteOrientationSensor and RelativeOrientationSensor","description":"Fused device orientation as a quaternion or rotation matrix.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Location and motion"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/location-motion/location-sensor-orientation","content":"<Chips /> Can find which way your device is facing and tilted in 3D Fused device orientation as a quaternion or rotation matrix. Gives exact device attitude relative to Earth (absolute) or to its start pose (relative). Same privacy class as DeviceOrientation but easier to use for 3D scenes. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing in most browsers. Chrome allows it by default unless you changed Motion sensors in site settings Find the device's tilt and f"},"659":{"title":"Network and connection","description":"Wi-Fi, cell signal, internet speed. 39 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity","content":"Wi-Fi, cell signal, internet speed. 39 capabilities in this group. | Capability                | Kind          | In one line                                                              |\n| ------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| ASN, ISP and hosting type | Gets silently | Can tell which internet company you use and if you look like a home user | | Capability                        | Kind          | In one line                                                          |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Bot detection and CAPTCHA signals | Gets silently | Can combine many browser and network clues to guess if you are a bot | | Capability                            | Kind          | In one line                                                                        |\n| ------------------------------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Client Hints (Accept-CH, Critical-CH) | Gets silently | Can ask your browser for extra device details such as model, OS version and screen | | Capability        | Kind          | In one line                                              |\n| ----------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Client IP address | Gets silently | Can see the public internet address your connection uses | | Capability                                  | Kind          | In one line                                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Device client hints (DPR, Viewport, Memory) | Gets silently | Can learn your screen density, window width, memory and light or dark mode in Chromium browsers | | Capability                | Kind           | In one line                                                                                |\n| ------------------------- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Direct Sockets (TCP, UDP) | Asks you first | Can open raw network connections only inside signed, installed apps, never on normal sites | | Capability             | Kind          | In one line                                                                |\n| ---------------------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| DNS resolver leak test | Gets silently | Can find which DNS service your device uses by having it look up odd names | | Capability             | Kind          | In one line                                                                       |\n| ---------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| ECH and DoH visibility | Gets silently | Can hide the site you visit from your internet provider, not from the site itself | | Capability                      | Kind              | In one line                                                                       |\n| ------------------------------- | ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| ETag and HTTP cache supercookie | Used to track you | Can try to tag your browser with a hidden cached ID, though browsers now limit it | | Capability                          | Kind              | In one line                                                                    |\n| ----------------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Favicon cache supercookie (F-CACHE) | Used to track you | Can try to tag your browser with a hidden icon cache ID, though fixes limit it | | Capability       | Kind              | In one line                                                                    |\n| ---------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| HSTS supercookie | Used to track you | Can try to tag your browser with a hidden secure site ID, though it is limited | | Capability                   | Kind              | In one line                                                              |\n| ---------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| HTTP header order and casing | Used to track you | Can tell which browser or tool you use from the order of request headers | | Capability                             | Kind              | In one line                                                               |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| HTTP/2 connection fingerprint (Akamai) | Used to track you | Can tell which browser or tool you use from how your connection starts up | | Capability                  | Kind              | In one line                                                                       |\n| --------------------------- | ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| HTTP/3 and QUIC fingerprint | Used to track you | Can tell which browser or tool you use from the first packet of a fast connection | | Capability     | Kind          | In one line                                                  |\n| -------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| IP geolocation | Gets silently | Can guess your country, region and city from your IP address | | Capability                                     | Kind          | In one line                                      "},"660":{"title":"ASN, ISP and hosting type","description":"The IP maps to an ISP or hosting company, which tells if the visitor is a home, mobile or cloud user.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-asn-isp-hosting","content":"<Chips /> Can tell which internet company you use and if you look like a home user The IP maps to an ISP or hosting company, which tells if the visitor is a home, mobile or cloud user. ASN databases label IPs as residential, mobile, business, hosting or education. Datacenter ASNs (AWS, GCP, OVH) trigger bot and fraud rules. Residential proxies are harder to spot. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Every connection to the site\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when your device connects to the "},"661":{"title":"Bot detection and CAPTCHA signals","description":"Anti-bot services combine many browser and network signals to guess if the visitor is automated.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-bot-detection-signals","content":"<Chips /> Can combine many browser and network clues to guess if you are a bot Anti-bot services combine many browser and network signals to guess if the visitor is automated. Inputs include navigator.webdriver, headless user agents and CDP side effects, JA3/JA4 and HTTP/2 mismatches, canvas and WebGL consistency, mouse and key timing, IP reputation and challenge solving (Turnstile, reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha). <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Many signals, on every visit\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It"},"662":{"title":"Client Hints (Accept-CH, Critical-CH)","description":"Servers ask the browser for extra device details through request headers.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-client-hints","content":"<Chips /> Can ask your browser for extra device details such as model, OS version and screen Servers ask the browser for extra device details through request headers. A server sends Accept-CH to receive hints such as Sec-CH-UA-Platform-Version, Arch, Bitness, Model, Device-Memory, DPR and Viewport-Width. Critical-CH forces a retry with them. Chromium only; Firefox and Safari do not send them. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Details sent with page requests\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens "},"663":{"title":"Client IP address","description":"Every request carries the sender's public IP address.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-client-ip","content":"<Chips /> Can see the public internet address your connection uses Every request carries the sender's public IP address. The server sees the IP (or the proxy or relay address). IPv4 gives about 32 bits at most; an IPv6 /64 gives up to 64. It is the base for geolocation, ISP and VPN checks, and rate limits. Shared NAT and CGNAT blur it. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Every connection, a stable ID\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Every site you visit gets it when you connect See your IP address, or y"},"664":{"title":"DNS resolver leak test","description":"A page makes the browser look up unique hostnames so the site learns which DNS resolver the visitor uses.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-dns-resolver-leak","content":"<Chips /> Can find which DNS service your device uses by having it look up odd names A page makes the browser look up unique hostnames so the site learns which DNS resolver the visitor uses. The page requests random-subdomain.example. The authoritative server logs which recursive resolver asked, revealing the ISP, a public DNS (Google, Cloudflare), or a VPN leak. DoH hides it from the local network, not from the site. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One quiet check\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. I"},"665":{"title":"ECH and DoH visibility","description":"Encrypted Client Hello and DNS over HTTPS hide which site a visitor connects to from the local network.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-ech-encrypted-sni-doh","content":"<Chips /> Can hide the site you visit from your internet provider, not from the site itself Encrypted Client Hello and DNS over HTTPS hide which site a visitor connects to from the local network. ECH encrypts the SNI and the rest of the ClientHello using a key from DNS HTTPS records (RFC 9849 in Chrome 117 and Firefox 118 era). DoH encrypts DNS lookups. They hide hostnames from ISPs but not from the site, and they weaken JA3/JA4 on the wire. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Protects you from the network\""},"666":{"title":"ETag and HTTP cache supercookie","description":"A tracker stores an ID in the cache validators of a file, which come back on every request.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-etag-cache-supercookie","content":"<Chips /> Can try to tag your browser with a hidden cached ID, though browsers now limit it A tracker stores an ID in the cache validators of a file, which come back on every request. A server sets a unique ETag or Last-Modified on a resource; the browser sends If-None-Match back, identifying the visitor. Since cache partitioning (Chrome 86, Firefox 85, Safari earlier) the ID is only valid per top-level site. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Same site only, after limits\" /> **What the user does:** Nothin"},"667":{"title":"Favicon cache supercookie (F-CACHE)","description":"A site encodes an ID into which favicons were cached, which survives clearing cookies.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-favicon-cache-supercookie","content":"<Chips /> Can try to tag your browser with a hidden icon cache ID, though fixes limit it A site encodes an ID into which favicons were cached, which survives clearing cookies. NDSS 2021 (Solomos et al.) showed a persistent ID stored in the favicon cache across subdomains, surviving cookie clearing and even private browsing in some browsers. Vendors patched after disclosure. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Mostly blocked by fixes\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you visit a site that "},"668":{"title":"HSTS supercookie","description":"A site stores an ID in which subdomains the browser has been told to load only over HTTPS.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-hsts-supercookie","content":"<Chips /> Can try to tag your browser with a hidden secure site ID, though it is limited A site stores an ID in which subdomains the browser has been told to load only over HTTPS. Each bit is a subdomain set to HSTS or not. The tracker reads it back by probing http:// URLs and seeing which upgrade (Kamkar 2015). Safari's ITP and clearing site data restrict it; HSTS is mostly cleared with history. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Small ID, mostly cleared by history\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It "},"669":{"title":"HTTP header order and casing","description":"The set and order of request headers is different for each browser and HTTP library.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-http-header-order","content":"<Chips /> Can tell which browser or tool you use from the order of request headers The set and order of request headers is different for each browser and HTTP library. Chrome, Firefox and Safari send Accept, Accept-Language, Accept-Encoding and Sec-\\* headers in distinct orders; scripted clients (requests, Go, Node fetch) differ. Mismatches versus the User-Agent flag bots. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Sent with every request\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens with every page request Comp"},"670":{"title":"HTTP/2 connection fingerprint (Akamai)","description":"HTTP/2 settings, window updates and header order differ by browser and are readable by the server.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-http2-akamai-fingerprint","content":"<Chips /> Can tell which browser or tool you use from how your connection starts up HTTP/2 settings, window updates and header order differ by browser and are readable by the server. Akamai's fingerprint (Black Hat EU 2017) joins SETTINGS values, WINDOW\\_UPDATE, PRIORITY frames and pseudo-header order. Chrome sends m,a,s,p, Firefox m,p,a,s and Safari m,s,p,a; Go and Python clients differ visibly. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Every secure connection\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as y"},"671":{"title":"HTTP/3 and QUIC fingerprint","description":"QUIC's first packet carries a readable ClientHello, giving the same fingerprints as TLS plus transport parameters.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-http3-quic-fingerprint","content":"<Chips /> Can tell which browser or tool you use from the first packet of a fast connection QUIC's first packet carries a readable ClientHello, giving the same fingerprints as TLS plus transport parameters. The QUIC Initial packet is encrypted with a public key, so middleboxes can read the TLS ClientHello and QUIC transport parameters. JA4 has a QUIC variant (prefix q). Connection ID length and version also differ per stack. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Every HTTP/3 connection\" /> **What the user doe"},"672":{"title":"IP geolocation","description":"The server converts the visitor's IP into an approximate country, region and city.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-ip-geolocation","content":"<Chips /> Can guess your country, region and city from your IP address The server converts the visitor's IP into an approximate country, region and city. Commercial databases (MaxMind, IPinfo, IP2Location) map IPs to places. Vendors claim about 99.8% country accuracy, but city accuracy is much lower and mobile or VPN IPs often point to the carrier hub or exit. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Country is close, city is rough\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when your device connects to the "},"673":{"title":"IP masking (Private Relay, IP Protection, Tor)","description":"Some browsers route traffic through relays so sites do not see the visitor's real IP.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-ip-masking","content":"<Chips /> Can see only a relay's address when you browse with Private Relay, IP Protection or Tor Some browsers route traffic through relays so sites do not see the visitor's real IP. iCloud Private Relay (Safari, iCloud+ subscribers) uses two hops with a coarse egress location. Chrome IP Protection proxies selected third-party traffic in Incognito for known trackers. Tor routes everything over 3 hops. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only the relay's address\" /> **What the user does:** You turn on iClou"},"674":{"title":"IPv6 prefix and interface ID stability","description":"IPv6 addresses can stay stable for weeks, making them a long lived ID even if cookies are cleared.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-ipv6-prefix-stability","content":"<Chips /> Can tell your home network apart by its stable IPv6 address, even after clearing cookies IPv6 addresses can stay stable for weeks, making them a long lived ID even if cookies are cleared. The /64 prefix delegated by an ISP often persists for weeks or months, while privacy extensions rotate the interface ID (about daily). Servers key on the /64 or /56 to identify a household. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Follows your home across weeks\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when your"},"675":{"title":"JA3 TLS client fingerprint","description":"The TLS handshake order of ciphers and extensions identifies the client software.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-ja3-tls-fingerprint","content":"<Chips /> Can guess which browser or app you use from how your connection starts The TLS handshake order of ciphers and extensions identifies the client software. JA3 (Salesforce, 2017) is an MD5 of version, ciphers, extensions, curves and point formats in the ClientHello. Chrome 110 randomized extension order (Jan 2023), so one browser now yields many JA3s; JA4 sorts them out. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Quietly, on every connection\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens the moment your br"},"676":{"title":"JA4+ network fingerprint family","description":"A newer family of fingerprints for TLS, HTTP, TCP and more, built to survive randomization.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-ja4-fingerprint-family","content":"<Chips /> Can recognize your browser or app from several quiet network clues, even when shuffled A newer family of fingerprints for TLS, HTTP, TCP and more, built to survive randomization. JA4 (FoxIO, 2023) sorts ciphers and extensions so Chrome's shuffle gives one value. JA4S (server), JA4H (HTTP), JA4L (latency), JA4X (cert), JA4SSH and JA4T (TCP) extend it. JA4 is BSD licensed; the rest use FoxIO License 1.1. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Quietly, on every connection\" /> **What the user does:** Not"},"677":{"title":"Local Network Access (LNA) permission","description":"Chrome asks the user before a public website can reach devices on their local network.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-local-network-access","content":"<Chips /> Can connect to devices on your home network, only if you allow it in Chrome Chrome asks the user before a public website can reach devices on their local network. Chrome 142 added a prompt for requests to private IPs. Chrome 145 split it into local-network and loopback-network permissions, and 147 extended it to WebSocket and WebTransport. It replaced Private Network Access preflights. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your local network, if allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You open a site an"},"678":{"title":"Localhost and LAN port scanning","description":"A page probes ports on the visitor's own machine or home network to find running software.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-localhost-port-scanning","content":"<Chips /> Can try to find software running on your own computer, though Chrome now blocks it A page probes ports on the visitor's own machine or home network to find running software. JavaScript uses fetch, WebSocket or image timing to test localhost ports (eBay's 2020 check, Meta Pixel and Yandex on Android in 2025 messaging via localhost). Chrome LNA prompts since 142 and PNA aim to stop this. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your own device, if not blocked\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happe"},"679":{"title":"Private Access Tokens and PACT","description":"A signed token proves the visitor is probably a real device without revealing who they are.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-private-access-tokens-pact","content":"<Chips /> Can check that you are probably a real device without learning who you are A signed token proves the visitor is probably a real device without revealing who they are. Privacy Pass (RFC 9576 to 9578) tokens issued after device or account attestation. Apple PAT ships in Safari and Cloudflare Turnstile consumes tokens. PACT was announced on 2026-06-22 by Cloudflare, Google, Microsoft, Mozilla and Shopify; Apple was not listed. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One check, no identity\" /> **What the "},"680":{"title":"Referer and Origin headers","description":"Requests tell the destination site which page the visitor came from.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-referrer-origin","content":"<Chips /> Can tell which site you came from, usually just the site name, not the page Requests tell the destination site which page the visitor came from. The default policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin (Chrome 85, Firefox 87) sends only the origin cross-site. Safari has capped cross-site referrers to the origin since 12.1. Full URLs still go same-site. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One step back, mostly the site name\" /> **What the user does:** You click a link or load a page that pulls in another"},"681":{"title":"Reverse DNS (PTR) of the client IP","description":"A reverse lookup of the IP can return an ISP hostname that hints at location or line type.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-reverse-dns","content":"<Chips /> Can look up your internet address to learn your provider and sometimes your area A reverse lookup of the IP can return an ISP hostname that hints at location or line type. PTR records such as pool-1-2-3-4.nycmny.fios.verizon.net expose ISP, city codes and sometimes dynamic vs static addressing. Servers do this for logs and bot checks (Googlebot verification). <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Rough area and provider\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the site's server logs or c"},"682":{"title":"Sec-CH-UA low entropy hints and reduced User-Agent","description":"Chromium sends brand, mobile flag and platform in every request by default, with a frozen User-Agent string.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-sec-ch-ua","content":"<Chips /> Can see your browser brand, major version, phone or computer type, and system in Chromium browsers Chromium sends brand, mobile flag and platform in every request by default, with a frozen User-Agent string. Sec-CH-UA lists browser brands and major versions (with a GREASE brand), Sec-CH-UA-Mobile and Sec-CH-UA-Platform. The User-Agent string is frozen to a reduced form in Chrome 110 and later. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Browser type on every request\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Ch"},"683":{"title":"Sec-Fetch-* request metadata","description":"Browsers add headers saying where a request came from and why, which servers use to block cross-site abuse.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-sec-fetch-headers","content":"<Chips /> Can tell why a request was sent, such as a click or a script Browsers add headers saying where a request came from and why, which servers use to block cross-site abuse. Sec-Fetch-Site, Mode, Dest and User tell the server if a request is same-origin, a navigation, or user triggered. They are not user identifying but help servers tell real browsers from scripts. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One label per request\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Your browser adds it to every request automa"},"684":{"title":"TCP/IP passive OS fingerprint (p0f, JA4T)","description":"The TCP handshake reveals the operating system through TTL, window size, MSS and option order.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-tcp-ip-passive-os-fingerprint","content":"<Chips /> Can guess your operating system from how your device opens a connection The TCP handshake reveals the operating system through TTL, window size, MSS and option order. p0f (Zalewski) and JA4T read SYN fields: initial TTL (64 or 128), window size, MSS, window scale and option order. Windows, Linux, iOS and macOS differ, and a mismatch with the User-Agent signals spoofing or a proxy. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Quietly, on every connection\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when "},"685":{"title":"TLS session resumption tracking","description":"Reuse of a TLS session ticket can link a visitor's connections across sites that share a server.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-tls-session-resumption-tracking","content":"<Chips /> Can link your visits across sites on one server if your browser reuses a ticket Reuse of a TLS session ticket can link a visitor's connections across sites that share a server. A session ticket or PSK is a stable ID, so two domains behind one CDN or server can see the same resumption. Sy et al. (ACSAC 2018) found browsers reusing tickets across origins. Chrome partitioned TLS session caches by network isolation key. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Short lived link across sites\" /> **What the u"},"686":{"title":"VPN, proxy and Tor detection","description":"Sites guess whether the visitor hides behind a VPN, proxy or Tor.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-vpn-proxy-tor-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can guess whether you are hiding behind a VPN, proxy or Tor, but not always correctly Sites guess whether the visitor hides behind a VPN, proxy or Tor. Methods: IP lists (Tor exit list, hosting ASNs), latency versus claimed geography, timezone and language versus IP, WebRTC leaks, open ports, MTU and TCP quirks. Residential VPN IPs often slip through. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A guess, not proof\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you load a page, sign in, or watch video"},"687":{"title":"WebRTC ICE host candidates (mDNS)","description":"WebRTC used to expose the device's local network IP; browsers now hide it behind a random .local name.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-webrtc-host-candidates","content":"<Chips /> Can try to find your device's home network address, but browsers now hide it WebRTC used to expose the device's local network IP; browsers now hide it behind a random .local name. RTCPeerConnection gathers ICE host candidates. Since 2019 and 2020 Chrome and Firefox replace private IPs with mDNS .local names, but granting getUserMedia (camera or microphone) restores real addresses. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Hidden unless camera is allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You join a video call "},"688":{"title":"WebRTC STUN public IP discovery","description":"A STUN request lets a page discover the public IP of the real network path, even behind some VPNs.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/net-webrtc-stun-public-ip","content":"<Chips /> Can find the public internet address of your real connection, even when some VPNs hide it A STUN request lets a page discover the public IP of the real network path, even behind some VPNs. ICE gathering sends UDP to a STUN server and learns the server reflexive address. Proxies and browser VPN extensions that cannot proxy UDP leak the real IP. Chrome WebRTC IP handling policies and Brave default settings block this. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your public address, if leaked\" /> **What the "},"689":{"title":"Network Information (downlink, rtt, effectiveType)","description":"Reads estimated connection speed, type and latency.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/network-connection-info","content":"<Chips /> Can see roughly how fast and what type your connection is, in Chrome and Edge Reads estimated connection speed, type and latency. Reveals if the person is on Wi-Fi, cellular or slow links; changes over time so useful for short term session linking. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads and while you stay on it Check if your connection looks like Wi-Fi, cellular or slow See rough speed and delay figures, rounde"},"690":{"title":"Device client hints (DPR, Viewport, Memory)","description":"Opt-in headers carrying DPR, viewport width, device memory and color scheme.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/network-device-client-hints","content":"<Chips /> Can learn your screen density, window width, memory and light or dark mode in Chromium browsers Opt-in headers carrying DPR, viewport width, device memory and color scheme. Sec-CH-DPR, Sec-CH-Viewport-Width, Sec-CH-Width, Sec-CH-Device-Memory and Sec-CH-Prefers-Color-Scheme let servers pick image sizes and themes before first paint, and also hand the same fingerprint bits to every server that asks. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Repeats on every request\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Th"},"691":{"title":"Direct Sockets (TCP, UDP)","description":"Raw TCP and UDP sockets from a page, for installed isolated apps only.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/network-direct-sockets","content":"<Chips /> Can open raw network connections only inside signed, installed apps, never on normal sites Raw TCP and UDP sockets from a page, for installed isolated apps only. Lets an installed Isolated Web App open arbitrary TCP and UDP connections and servers. Not available to normal websites because it would bypass same-origin and CORS protections. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Only inside installed apps\" /> **What the user does:** An organization installs a signed Isolated Web App on your computer Ope"},"692":{"title":"Network client hints (ECT, RTT, Downlink)","description":"Server-side headers with the effective connection type, RTT and downlink.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/network-ect-client-hints","content":"<Chips /> Can see your rough connection speed and delay in each request, if it asks first Server-side headers with the effective connection type, RTT and downlink. Same numbers as the Network Information API but delivered on requests so the server can adapt before sending HTML. Requires Accept-CH opt-in, with the same 25 ms and 25 kbps rounding. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Repeats on every request\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens after the site opts in, on each page load Receive speed"},"693":{"title":"Local Network Access (local-network)","description":"Asks to let a public site talk to devices on your home or office network.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/network-local-network","content":"<Chips /> Can find and connect to devices on your home or office network, if you tap Allow Asks to let a public site talk to devices on your home or office network. Blocks a public website from reaching private IP addresses (printers, routers, smart devices) unless you allow it. Closes the old way for sites to scan or attack your LAN from the browser. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your network, if you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You visit a site that needs your printer, router or smart device an"},"694":{"title":"Loopback network access (loopback-network)","description":"Asks to let a public site talk to services on this very computer.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/network-loopback-network","content":"<Chips /> Can talk to programs on your own computer, only if you allow it in Chrome Asks to let a public site talk to services on this very computer. Controls access to localhost services (dev servers, local agents, desktop apps). Sites used to probe localhost ports to detect installed software, so access now needs consent. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your own device, if allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You visit a site that pairs with a desktop app and tap Allow Connect to helper apps or develop"},"695":{"title":"Network Information API","description":"Estimated connection type, speed and latency, plus a data-saver flag.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/network-network-information","content":"<Chips /> Can see roughly how fast your connection is and whether you turned on data saving Estimated connection type, speed and latency, plus a data-saver flag. Lets a site pick light pages on slow links. effectiveType buckets: slow-2g, 2g, 3g, 4g. downlink rounds to 25 kbps steps (capped near 10 Mbps), rtt to 25 ms steps. Type (wifi or cellular) is Android only. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads and while you stay"},"696":{"title":"Online and offline state","description":"Tells the page whether the device believes it has a network.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/network-online-offline","content":"<Chips /> Can tell if your device thinks it is online, and when that changes Tells the page whether the device believes it has a network. Boolean only; true can still mean a captive portal or no internet. Events fire when the device loses or regains connection, revealing travel, elevators, flights. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Yes or no, while page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens while the page is open and your connection changes See a yes or no answer for being online Notice "},"697":{"title":"Save-Data header","description":"Request header showing the person turned on data saving.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/network-save-data-header","content":"<Chips /> Can see that you turned on data saving, in Chrome, Edge and Samsung Internet Request header showing the person turned on data saving. Save-Data: on indicates Lite mode on a metered or slow connection, often in lower-income or roaming contexts. Sites should send lighter pages. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A single on or off flag\" /> **What the user does:** You turn on Data saver or Lite mode, then browse See a Save-Data flag on each request when you have data saving on Send smaller images an"},"698":{"title":"WebRTC local IP discovery","description":"Uses a WebRTC peer connection to learn the device's local and public IP addresses.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Network and connection"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/network-connectivity/network-webrtc-local-ip","content":"<Chips /> Can try to learn your device's home network address and public address through a call setup Uses a WebRTC peer connection to learn the device's local and public IP addresses. Can reveal a real IP behind a VPN and LAN addresses. Browsers now often hide local addresses behind mDNS names. LinkedIn uses stun.l.google.com:19302 and an IP regex. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Mostly hidden, can leak a VPN\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. A page can start a call setup without a pop up Ask an out"},"699":{"title":"Notifications and background","description":"Alerts and work that runs when the app is closed. 14 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background","content":"Alerts and work that runs when the app is closed. 14 capabilities in this group. | Capability       | Kind                 | In one line                                                                      |\n| ---------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Background Fetch | Works without asking | Can download or upload big files after you leave the tab, with a progress notice | | Capability      | Kind                 | In one line                                                                     |\n| --------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Background Sync | Works without asking | Can retry sending queued data after you close the tab, once you are back online | | Capability  | Kind                 | In one line                                                  |\n| ----------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Badging API | Works without asking | Can show a number or dot on the icon of an installed web app | | Capability                                        | Kind           | In one line                                                                |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Chrome and OS defenses against notification abuse | Asks you first | Can't keep sending spam if you block it; Chrome quiets and removes abusers | | Capability                    | Kind                 | In one line                                                                         |\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Declarative Web Push (Safari) | Works without asking | Can show you a notification from Safari's new push format without running site code | | Capability     | Kind           | In one line                                                      |\n| -------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Idle Detection | Asks you first | Can tell when you step away or lock your screen, if you allow it | | Capability                        | Kind                 | In one line                                                                   |\n| --------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Notification Triggers (abandoned) | Works without asking | Can't schedule a notification for later without a server; no browser ships it | | Capability    | Kind           | In one line                                                                  |\n| ------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Notifications | Asks you first | Can show messages on your screen outside the browser tab, once you tap Allow | | Capability               | Kind           | In one line                                                                            |\n| ------------------------ | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Periodic Background Sync | Asks you first | Can refresh an installed web app now and then while closed, at times the browser picks | | Capability          | Kind           | In one line                                                     |\n| ------------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Push API (Web Push) | Asks you first | Can send you notifications after you leave, once you allow them | | Capability                               | Kind                 | In one line                                                                                |\n| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Push, periodic sync and background fetch | Works without asking | Can wake a site's background script after you leave, through push, timed sync or downloads | | Capability       | Kind                 | In one line                                                                  |\n| ---------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Screen Wake Lock | Works without asking | Can keep your screen on while the page is showing, but not in the background | | Capability              | Kind                 | In one line                                                                       |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Service worker lifetime | Works without asking | Can keep a background helper script for a site that stays after you close the tab | | Capability       | Kind             | In one line                                                                    |\n| ---------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| System Wake Lock | Blocked for apps | Can't keep your device awake with the screen off; no browser ever shipped this |"},"700":{"title":"Background Fetch","description":"Downloads large files after the person leaves, with a progress notification.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/notify-background-fetch","content":"<Chips /> Can download or upload big files after you leave the tab, with a progress notice Downloads large files after the person leaves, with a progress notification. Lets a site download or upload big files outside the tab. The browser shows progress UI so the person can see and cancel it. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"One download, in the background\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Download for a movie, podcast or large file and close the tab Keep a large download or upload going after you leave"},"701":{"title":"Background Sync","description":"Retries a request later when the connection returns, after the tab closes.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/notify-background-sync","content":"<Chips /> Can retry sending queued data after you close the tab, once you are back online Retries a request later when the connection returns, after the tab closes. Lets a site send queued data (a message, a form, a like) once the device is back online even if the person has left. Quiet and automatic, so no prompt is shown. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Queued sends, after you leave\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Send or Save while offline, then leave the page Send a saved message, form or like l"},"702":{"title":"Badging API","description":"Shows a number or dot on an installed app icon.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/notify-badging","content":"<Chips /> Can show a number or dot on the icon of an installed web app Shows a number or dot on an installed app icon. Puts an unread count on the app icon of an installed web app. No prompt on desktop, but iOS needs notification permission and an installed app. It shows the person that the app has attention to give. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A badge on one icon\" /> **What the user does:** You install a web app and, on iPhone, allow notifications Show an unread count or dot on the app icon Update "},"703":{"title":"Idle Detection","description":"Tells a site when the person is away from keyboard and mouse or the screen is locked.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/notify-idle-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can tell when you step away or lock your screen, if you allow it Tells a site when the person is away from keyboard and mouse or the screen is locked. Reveals when the person is present: active or idle (at least 60 seconds) and whether the screen is locked. Useful for chat presence; also shows daily schedule, sleep and work hours. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While open, if you allow\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a button like Show my status and tap Allow See whether you are active or"},"704":{"title":"Notification Triggers (abandoned)","description":"Scheduled local notifications that fire without a server; not shipped.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/notify-notification-triggers","content":"<Chips /> Can't schedule a notification for later without a server; no browser ships it Scheduled local notifications that fire without a server; not shipped. Would have let a site schedule notifications for a future time without push. Chrome ran an origin trial and then dropped it, so no browser ships it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Not available today\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Chrome tried an experiment, then dropped it Show a normal notification when a server sends one, which is a diff"},"705":{"title":"Notifications","description":"Asks to show system notifications outside the browser tab.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/notify-notifications","content":"<Chips /> Can show messages on your screen outside the browser tab, once you tap Allow Asks to show system notifications outside the browser tab. Lets a site put messages on the person's desktop or lock screen. Reaches the person after they leave the page; abuse is spam, scams and fake alerts, so browsers add quiet prompts and auto-revocation. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, until you block it\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow on the site's notification pop up Show alerts on your deskto"},"706":{"title":"Periodic Background Sync","description":"Wakes an installed site every so often to refresh data.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/notify-periodic-background-sync","content":"<Chips /> Can refresh an installed web app now and then while closed, at times the browser picks Wakes an installed site every so often to refresh data. Lets an installed web app fetch updates on a schedule while closed. The browser, not the site, picks the actual times from engagement and battery. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Occasional refresh, installed apps\" /> **What the user does:** You install the web app and use it often Fetch fresh news, mail or podcasts on a schedule while the app is closed"},"707":{"title":"Chrome and OS defenses against notification abuse","description":"Browser rules that quiet, throttle and auto-revoke abusive notification sites.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/notify-push-abuse-defenses","content":"<Chips /> Can't keep sending spam if you block it; Chrome quiets and removes abusers Browser rules that quiet, throttle and auto-revoke abusive notification sites. Shows how the browser limits what an ignored or abusive site can do: a quiet prompt chip, auto-revocation, server-side throttling at FCM. A site that is ignored keeps no permission and sends nothing. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Limits what a site can do\" /> **What the user does:** You ignore the prompt, tap Block, or tap Unsubscribe on a "},"708":{"title":"Push API (Web Push)","description":"Lets a server wake the site in the background and show a notification.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/notify-push-api","content":"<Chips /> Can send you notifications after you leave, once you allow them Lets a server wake the site in the background and show a notification. Reaches the person after they leave the site. Every message must show a visible notification (userVisibleOnly), but the site learns when a device is online and which browser vendor push service it uses. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, until you block it\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow on the notification pop up, or add the site to your iPhone"},"709":{"title":"Declarative Web Push (Safari)","description":"A push format that shows a notification without running site code.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/notify-push-declarative-safari","content":"<Chips /> Can show you a notification from Safari's new push format without running site code A push format that shows a notification without running site code. The browser renders the notification straight from JSON, so no service worker runs. Cuts battery use and removes the old silent-push abuse path; the site still learns when a device is online. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, until you block it\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow on the notification pop up in Safari Show a notificat"},"710":{"title":"Screen Wake Lock","description":"Keeps the screen on while the page is visible.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/notify-screen-wake-lock","content":"<Chips /> Can keep your screen on while the page is showing, but not in the background Keeps the screen on while the page is visible. Stops the display from dimming or locking, which can drain battery and expose the screen. Browsers drop it when the page is hidden or battery is low, so it cannot hold the screen on in the background. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only while the page is visible\" /> **What the user does:** You open a recipe, video or presentation and the page asks to stay awake Stop the "},"711":{"title":"System Wake Lock","description":"A way to keep the CPU awake while the screen is off; never shipped.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/notify-system-wake-lock","content":"<Chips /> Can't keep your device awake with the screen off; no browser ever shipped this A way to keep the CPU awake while the screen is off; never shipped. The spec once listed a system lock that would keep the machine awake with the screen off. No browser shipped it because of battery abuse risk. Proxies are audio playback, background sync and push. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Not available\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It is not available Use workarounds such as audio playing, push or back"},"712":{"title":"Push, periodic sync and background fetch","description":"Ways a site can wake its service worker after the person leaves: push messages, timed sync, long downloads.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/xsite-background-wake-apis","content":"<Chips /> Can wake a site's background script after you leave, through push, timed sync or downloads Ways a site can wake its service worker after the person leaves: push messages, timed sync, long downloads. Push needs a notification permission; Periodic Background Sync needs an installed PWA and site engagement, with a minimum of about 12 hours between wakes; Background Fetch shows a download notice. Each lets a site run code with no tab open. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Brief wakes, if you allow\""},"713":{"title":"Service worker lifetime","description":"A background script registered by a site that persists after the tab is closed.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Notifications and background"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/notifications-background/xsite-service-worker-lifetime","content":"<Chips /> Can keep a background helper script for a site that stays after you close the tab A background script registered by a site that persists after the tab is closed. It can intercept every request, serve cached content, and wake for push or sync even when no page is open. The browser stops it after about 30 seconds idle, but the registration stays until cleared. Safari deletes it after 7 days of non use. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Registered until cleared\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. "},"714":{"title":"Built in AI model download and local data","description":"The browser downloads a Gemini Nano model and keeps prompts on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/on-device-ai/ai-gemini-nano-requirements","content":"<Chips /> Can tell that your browser downloaded a local AI model, which hints at your hardware The browser downloads a Gemini Nano model and keeps prompts on the device. The model needs about 22 GB free space and a capable GPU or CPU; availability states hint at hardware class. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One hint about your hardware\" /> **What the user does:** You use a page feature that asks Chrome to download its local AI model Check if the local model is ready, downloading or not available Guess"},"715":{"title":"Language Detector API","description":"Guesses the language of text on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/on-device-ai/ai-language-detector","content":"<Chips /> Can guess the language of text on your device, without sending it away Guesses the language of text on the device. Local detection with confidence scores. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One text at a time\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a button like Translate this page or Detect language Guess the language of a text snippet with a confidence score Choose the right language before translating Download the small model the first time Start without a click or tap on the page Send the text to"},"716":{"title":"Prompt API (LanguageModel, Gemini Nano)","description":"Runs a local language model in Chrome and lets a page prompt it.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/on-device-ai/ai-prompt-api","content":"<Chips /> Can run a small AI model on your device with text you give the page Runs a local language model in Chrome and lets a page prompt it. Page text and the person's input stay on the device. The page can summarise, classify or chat without sending data to a server. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a button like Summarize, Reply or Ask AI on the page Send the page text or your message to a local AI model Get summaries, labels, smart replies o"},"717":{"title":"Proofreader API","description":"Checks spelling and grammar on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/on-device-ai/ai-proofreader-api","content":"<Chips /> Can check spelling and grammar of text you typed, on your device, in Chrome testing Checks spelling and grammar on the device. Local proofreading of what the person typed. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One text, in testing only\" /> **What the user does:** You type in a form or comment box on a site in the Chrome trial Check spelling and grammar locally Suggest fixes for text you typed Keep your text on your device Work for normal visitors yet, since it is a developer or origin trial Work in "},"718":{"title":"Rewriter API","description":"Rewrites text in a different tone or length on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/on-device-ai/ai-rewriter-api","content":"<Chips /> Can rewrite text you typed in a new tone or length, on your device, in testing Rewrites text in a different tone or length on the device. Edits what the person typed locally. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One text, in testing only\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a button like Make shorter or Make friendlier on a site in the trial Change the tone or length of text you typed Work on your device without a server Offer a few versions to pick from Work for normal visitors yet, since it is an "},"719":{"title":"Summarizer API","description":"Summarises long text on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/on-device-ai/ai-summarizer-api","content":"<Chips /> Can summarize a long text on your device, without sending it away Summarises long text on the device. Produces key points, TL;DR, teaser or headline from text without sending it off the device. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One summary at a time\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a button like Summarize, TL;DR or Key points Make key points, a TL;DR, a teaser or a headline from text Run on your device after the model downloads once Summarize page text or text you pasted Start before a click "},"720":{"title":"Translator API","description":"Translates text on the device between languages.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/on-device-ai/ai-translator-api","content":"<Chips /> Can translate text on your device between languages, without sending it away Translates text on the device between languages. Page text stays local. Model downloads per language pair. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One language pair at a time\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a button like Translate this page Translate page text or what you typed between two languages Download a model for each language pair the first time Keep the text on your device Start before a click or tap Translate to"},"721":{"title":"WebGPU compute","description":"Lets a page run general GPU compute shaders.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/on-device-ai/ai-webgpu-compute","content":"<Chips /> Can use your graphics chip for heavy math, and learn what kind of chip you have Lets a page run general GPU compute shaders. Used for local AI and rendering. Reveals GPU class and limits. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your graphics chip, while open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a page loads a game, an AI feature or a 3D view Run AI models and 3D graphics on your graphics chip Learn the rough class and limits of your chip Run mining scripts that use your chip, which is "},"722":{"title":"WebNN (neural network API)","description":"Runs machine learning models on the device's NPU, GPU or CPU.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/on-device-ai/ai-webnn","content":"<Chips /> Can run AI models on your device's chips, but only in Chrome and Edge testing Runs machine learning models on the device's NPU, GPU or CPU. Lets a page run AI locally. Reveals hardware class through speed and supported ops. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your chips, in testing only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a page in the trial loads an AI feature Run AI models on your device's AI chip, graphics chip or main chip Tell your hardware class from speed and supported step"},"723":{"title":"Writer API","description":"Writes new text from a prompt on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","On-device AI"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/on-device-ai/ai-writer-api","content":"<Chips /> Can draft new text from a prompt on your device, only in Chrome and Edge testing Writes new text from a prompt on the device. Generates drafts locally; the page controls tone and length. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One draft, in testing only\" /> **What the user does:** You type a short instruction and tap a button like Draft for me Draft new text from a prompt you give the page Match a tone and length the page sets Keep the text on your device Work for normal visitors yet, since it is an o"},"724":{"title":"On-device AI","description":"AI that runs right on your device. 10 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/on-device-ai","content":"AI that runs right on your device. 10 capabilities in this group. | Capability                                | Kind          | In one line                                                                          |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Built in AI model download and local data | Gets silently | Can tell that your browser downloaded a local AI model, which hints at your hardware | | Capability            | Kind                 | In one line                                                            |\n| --------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Language Detector API | Works without asking | Can guess the language of text on your device, without sending it away | | Capability                              | Kind                 | In one line                                                         |\n| --------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Prompt API (LanguageModel, Gemini Nano) | Works without asking | Can run a small AI model on your device with text you give the page | | Capability      | Kind                 | In one line                                                                         |\n| --------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Proofreader API | Works without asking | Can check spelling and grammar of text you typed, on your device, in Chrome testing | | Capability   | Kind                 | In one line                                                                    |\n| ------------ | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Rewriter API | Works without asking | Can rewrite text you typed in a new tone or length, on your device, in testing | | Capability     | Kind                 | In one line                                                       |\n| -------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Summarizer API | Works without asking | Can summarize a long text on your device, without sending it away | | Capability     | Kind                 | In one line                                                                  |\n| -------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Translator API | Works without asking | Can translate text on your device between languages, without sending it away | | Capability     | Kind                 | In one line                                                                     |\n| -------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| WebGPU compute | Works without asking | Can use your graphics chip for heavy math, and learn what kind of chip you have | | Capability                 | Kind                 | In one line                                                                   |\n| -------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| WebNN (neural network API) | Works without asking | Can run AI models on your device's chips, but only in Chrome and Edge testing | | Capability | Kind                 | In one line                                                                      |\n| ---------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Writer API | Works without asking | Can draft new text from a prompt on your device, only in Chrome and Edge testing |"},"725":{"title":"Home screen and system links","description":"Installing, widgets, shortcuts, sharing. 15 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install","content":"Installing, widgets, shortcuts, sharing. 15 capabilities in this group. | Capability    | Kind                 | In one line                                                                     |\n| ------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| App shortcuts | Works without asking | Can add quick actions to an installed app icon's long press or right click menu | | Capability  | Kind                          | In one line                                                             |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Badging API | Does something on your device | Can show a number or dot on an installed app's icon, but can't check it | | Capability                  | Kind                 | In one line                                                                        |\n| --------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| File Handling (launchQueue) | Works without asking | Can open files from your file manager in an installed web app, after you choose it | | Capability                                                | Kind                 | In one line                                                      |\n| --------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Install prompt (beforeinstallprompt) and manifest install | Works without asking | Can offer to install the site as an app, but only you can accept | | Capability        | Kind                 | In one line                                                                          |\n| ----------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Isolated Web Apps | Works without asking | Can run as a signed, packaged app with extra system features, on managed Chrome only | | Capability     | Kind                 | In one line                                                                            |\n| -------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Launch Handler | Works without asking | Can choose whether links reopen your installed app in its existing window or a new one | | Capability                          | Kind                 | In one line                                                       |\n| ----------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Note taking (manifest note\\_taking) | Works without asking | Can list an installed web app as a note taking choice on ChromeOS | | Capability        | Kind                 | In one line                                                  |\n| ----------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Protocol Handlers | Works without asking | Can open links like mailto: in the site after you approve it | | Capability      | Kind                          | In one line                                                     |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Run on OS login | Does something on your device | Can start an installed app when you sign in, only if you choose | | Capability                 | Kind             | In one line                                                                   |\n| -------------------------- | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| SMS, calendar and call log | Blocked for apps | Can't send or see your text messages, calendar or call history from a website | | Capability                          | Kind                 | In one line                                                            |\n| ----------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Web Install API (navigator.install) | Works without asking | Can offer to install a web app with one call, but you still approve it | | Capability                  | Kind                 | In one line                                                                           |\n| --------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Web Locks and Media Session | Works without asking | Can coordinate work across your open tabs and show media controls on your lock screen | | Capability | Kind                          | In one line                                                                    |\n| ---------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Web Share  | Does something on your device | Can open your phone's share sheet with a link, text or files, only after a tap | | Capability       | Kind                 | In one line                                                                          |\n| ---------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Web Share Target | Works without asking | Can appear in your device's share sheet and receive links, text or files you send it | | Capability                              | Kind                 | In one line                                                                  |\n| --------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Window Controls Overlay and Tabbed mode | Works without asking | Can draw into the title bar of an installed app, like showing tabs or search |"},"726":{"title":"Badging API","description":"Shows a number or dot on the app icon.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-badging","content":"<Chips /> Can show a number or dot on an installed app's icon, but can't check it Shows a number or dot on the app icon. Alerts the person to unread items without a notification. Cannot read badge state. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A badge on one icon\" /> **What the user does:** You install the web app and, on iPhone, allow notifications Show an unread count or dot on the app icon Update it when something new arrives Clear it once you open the item Work for a site that is not installed as an app Che"},"727":{"title":"File Handling (launchQueue)","description":"Lets an installed web app open files of a given type from the file manager.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-file-handling","content":"<Chips /> Can open files from your file manager in an installed web app, after you choose it Lets an installed web app open files of a given type from the file manager. Registers the PWA as handler for file types; the file arrives as a handle once the person opens it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One file you choose\" /> **What the user does:** You install the web app, then open a file with Open with and pick it Appear in the Open with list for file types it supports Open the one file you picked and s"},"728":{"title":"Install prompt (beforeinstallprompt) and manifest install","description":"Lets a site offer to install itself as an app on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-install-prompt","content":"<Chips /> Can offer to install the site as an app, but only you can accept Lets a site offer to install itself as an app on the device. An installed app gets its own window and icon, and unlocks install only features (badging, file handling, shortcuts). <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One install, if you say yes\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Install app, or Add to Home Screen on iPhone Show an install offer after you tap a button on the page Give the site its own window and icon once you accept Un"},"729":{"title":"Isolated Web Apps","description":"A packaged, signed web app with extra system APIs.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-isolated-web-apps","content":"<Chips /> Can run as a signed, packaged app with extra system features, on managed Chrome only A packaged, signed web app with extra system APIs. Needs a signed bundle and, today, enterprise managed Chrome. Unlocks Direct Sockets and Controlled Frame. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Only in managed installed apps\" /> **What the user does:** Your organization installs the signed app on your computer Run from a signed bundle instead of a normal web address Use raw network connections and controlled frames"},"730":{"title":"Launch Handler","description":"Controls how an installed app reopens when a link is clicked.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-launch-handler","content":"<Chips /> Can choose whether links reopen your installed app in its existing window or a new one Controls how an installed app reopens when a link is clicked. Picks reuse of the existing window or a new one. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One link, one window\" /> **What the user does:** You click a link that opens in your installed web app Reuse the app's open window when you click a link Open a new window instead Pass the link to the app that is already running Work for a site that is not installed Op"},"731":{"title":"SMS, calendar and call log","description":"A site cannot send or read SMS, read the calendar, or see call history.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-not-exposed-sms-calendar","content":"<Chips /> Can't send or see your text messages, calendar or call history from a website A site cannot send or read SMS, read the calendar, or see call history. sms: and tel: links only open the user's own app. Calendars arrive as .ics downloads or OAuth sign in. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only opens your own apps\" /> **What the user does:** You tap an sms: link, tel: link, or a calendar file or sign in button Open your own messaging or phone app with a number or message filled in Offer an .ics cale"},"732":{"title":"Note taking (manifest note_taking)","description":"Marks an installed app as a note taking app on ChromeOS.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-note-taking","content":"<Chips /> Can list an installed web app as a note taking choice on ChromeOS Marks an installed app as a note taking app on ChromeOS. Appears in the lock screen note chooser. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One chooser entry\" /> **What the user does:** You install the web app and pick it in your ChromeOS lock screen note settings Appear in the lock screen note chooser Open a new note from the lock screen once you pick it Work only for installed apps Work on Windows, Mac, Android or iPhone Work in Firefox"},"733":{"title":"Protocol Handlers","description":"Lets a site or installed app handle links like mailto: or web+music:.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-protocol-handlers","content":"<Chips /> Can open links like mailto: in the site after you approve it Lets a site or installed app handle links like mailto: or web+music:. Opens links with that scheme in the site after the person approves. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One link type, if you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a mailto: or similar link and approve the site as its handler Open that kind of link in the site after you approve Handle custom link types like web+music: Ask again if you change your mind in settings"},"734":{"title":"Run on OS login","description":"An installed app can start when the person logs in to their computer.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-run-on-os-login","content":"<Chips /> Can start an installed app when you sign in, only if you choose An installed app can start when the person logs in to their computer. Set by the person, not the site, via about://apps, or by IT policy. No site can enable it silently. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Each sign in, if you choose\" /> **What the user does:** You turn on Start app when you sign in, or your IT team sets it Start the app when you sign in once you turn it on Start through an IT policy on managed computers Stay off unti"},"735":{"title":"App shortcuts","description":"Adds quick actions to the app icon's long press or right click menu.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-shortcuts","content":"<Chips /> Can add quick actions to an installed app icon's long press or right click menu Adds quick actions to the app icon's long press or right click menu. Offers up to a few deep links from the OS launcher. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A few shortcuts on one icon\" /> **What the user does:** You install the app, then press and hold or right click its icon Show a few links like Compose or New tab on the icon menu Jump straight into part of the app Use up to a handful of shortcuts Add many shortcuts"},"736":{"title":"Web Install API (navigator.install)","description":"Lets a page install another web app, or itself, with one call.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-web-install-api","content":"<Chips /> Can offer to install a web app with one call, but you still approve it Lets a page install another web app, or itself, with one call. Turns a link into an install button; the user still approves. Still an origin trial on desktop Chromium. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One install, if you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap an Install button on a page and confirm in the dialog Show an install dialog for itself or for another web app Turn a link into an install button Work only on site"},"737":{"title":"Web Locks and Media Session","description":"Lets a page coordinate tabs and show media controls on the lock screen.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-web-locks-media-session","content":"<Chips /> Can coordinate work across your open tabs and show media controls on your lock screen Lets a page coordinate tabs and show media controls on the lock screen. Media Session exposes title and artwork to OS media UI. Web Locks coordinates work across tabs. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While media is playing\" /> **What the user does:** You play a podcast or video, or open the same site in two tabs Show the title and artwork in your lock screen and notification controls Let play and pause button"},"738":{"title":"Web Share Target","description":"Lets an installed web app appear in the system share sheet.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-web-share-target","content":"<Chips /> Can appear in your device's share sheet and receive links, text or files you send it Lets an installed web app appear in the system share sheet. Receives text, URLs and files other apps share, which is how a PWA becomes a share destination. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"What you choose to share\" /> **What the user does:** You install the web app, then pick it from the share sheet in another app Show up as a choice in the share sheet Receive text, links or files you share to it Open the app w"},"739":{"title":"Web Share","description":"Opens the phone's native share sheet with text, a link or files.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-web-share","content":"<Chips /> Can open your phone's share sheet with a link, text or files, only after a tap Opens the phone's native share sheet with text, a link or files. The person picks the target app. The site does not learn which one. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One share, one tap\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a Share button on the page Open the share sheet with text, a link or files Let you pick the app to send to Share one item at a time Open the share sheet without a tap See which app you picked Share a"},"740":{"title":"Window Controls Overlay and Tabbed mode","description":"Lets an installed app draw into the title bar, or show tabs.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Home screen and system links"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/os-integration-install/os-window-controls-overlay","content":"<Chips /> Can draw into the title bar of an installed app, like showing tabs or search Lets an installed app draw into the title bar, or show tabs. Customises the app window chrome on desktop. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only its own window\" /> **What the user does:** You install the web app on a computer and open it Place buttons or a search box in the title bar of the app window Draw tabs there Keep the system's close and minimize buttons Work for a site that is not installed Hide the close, minim"},"741":{"title":"Autoplay audio and video","description":"Starts video or sound automatically; muted video is allowed, sound usually needs prior interaction.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-autoplay","content":"<Chips /> Can start muted video on its own, and sound only after you have interacted Starts video or sound automatically; muted video is allowed, sound usually needs prior interaction. A site can start media without a click. Browsers allow silent autoplay but block surprise sound unless the person has engaged with the site, which shapes ad video and background audio. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Muted video, sound after you interact\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing for muted video. For sound, you "},"742":{"title":"Leave-page confirmation (beforeunload)","description":"Shows a generic Leave site? dialog, only if the person interacted with the page.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-beforeunload","content":"<Chips /> Can show a generic Leave site? warning when you close or leave, after you have interacted Shows a generic Leave site? dialog, only if the person interacted with the page. A site can ask the person to confirm closing or leaving, often to protect unsaved work. Browsers hide the site's own text, so it cannot scare people with custom messages. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One warning per attempt\" /> **What the user does:** You click, tap or type on the page, then try to close or leave it Show a"},"743":{"title":"Compression Streams","description":"Compresses or decompresses data in the page with built-in gzip, deflate or deflate-raw.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-compression-streams","content":"<Chips /> Can squeeze or unpack data in the page with the browser's built in compression Compresses or decompresses data in the page with built-in gzip, deflate or deflate-raw. A site can shrink or expand data without a library. It also lets trackers pack fingerprint payloads before sending them out. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Data the page already has\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a site's script compresses logs, files or data Compress data with gzip or deflate before sendin"},"744":{"title":"Cookie Store API","description":"Reads and changes cookies asynchronously and gets change events, including in service workers.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-cookie-store","content":"<Chips /> Can watch and change the site's own cookies, but not hidden ones or other sites' cookies Reads and changes cookies asynchronously and gets change events, including in service workers. A site can watch and edit its own cookies without parsing document.cookie. It sees only non-HttpOnly cookies for its own origin. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"The site's own cookies, ongoing\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the site's script checks or sets a cookie List and change the site's"},"745":{"title":"Cross-origin isolation (SharedArrayBuffer, COOP, COEP)","description":"A site can opt into an isolated mode that unlocks SharedArrayBuffer, precise timers and Wasm threads.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-cross-origin-isolation","content":"<Chips /> Can switch on a locked down mode that unlocks fast shared memory for the page A site can opt into an isolated mode that unlocks SharedArrayBuffer, precise timers and Wasm threads. Isolation trades openness (no cross-origin popups or unmarked embeds) for power. Sites with it get memory sharing and 5 microsecond timers, which are also better side channel tools. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only inside its own page\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the site loads with the ri"},"746":{"title":"alert, confirm and prompt dialogs","description":"Blocks the page with a modal alert, confirm or prompt box.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-dialogs","content":"<Chips /> Can freeze the page with a pop up message box until you answer it Blocks the page with a modal alert, confirm or prompt box. A site can interrupt the person with a message or a text box. Dialogs freeze the page script, are easy to abuse, and are blocked in cross-origin iframes and background tabs. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One page, one box at a time\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page runs a script, or after an action like Delete. Show an alert, a yes or no que"},"747":{"title":"Trigger file downloads","description":"Saves a file to the device; repeated automatic downloads ask permission.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-downloads","content":"<Chips /> Can save a file to your device, but repeated automatic downloads ask first Saves a file to the device; repeated automatic downloads ask permission. A site can drop files into Downloads (reports, exports, installers). Browsers guard with multi-download prompts and safe browsing checks because a downloaded file is how malware arrives. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One file, then it asks again\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing for the first one. It happens when the page runs a download or you"},"748":{"title":"Drag and drop","description":"Lets people drag items within or into the page, and lets the page start drags.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-drag-and-drop","content":"<Chips /> Can let you drag items on the page, or drop files onto it Lets people drag items within or into the page, and lets the page start drags. A site can start a drag with text, links or files, and react when people drop things on it. Data in a drag is readable only on drop, so the page cannot spy on a drag it does not own. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only what you drop\" /> **What the user does:** You drag a file, text or picture onto the page, or drag an item inside it. Accept files you drop on"},"749":{"title":"Fenced frames","description":"Embeds content that cannot talk to or be read by the page around it.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-fenced-frames","content":"<Chips /> Can embed a box of content that the page around it cannot look into Embeds content that cannot talk to or be read by the page around it. Used by ad auctions so the embedding page cannot see which ad rendered. It separates the frame's storage and messaging from the parent to block link-back tracking. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A sealed box inside the page\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads, usually where an ad or a widget sits. Show an ad or widget inside t"},"750":{"title":"Focus and window raise","description":"Can move focus between elements; cannot pull the window to the front or steal focus from other apps.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-focus-stealing","content":"<Chips /> Can move the cursor between boxes on its page, but can't pull its window forward Can move focus between elements; cannot pull the window to the front or steal focus from other apps. A page can focus its own elements, but browsers stop it from grabbing focus from the address bar, another tab or another app, which blocks keystroke capture and pop-over scams. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only inside its own page\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as you use the page, or when it fi"},"751":{"title":"Fullscreen","description":"Takes over the whole screen for a page element after a click.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-fullscreen","content":"<Chips /> Can make a video or page area fill your screen, after you tap Takes over the whole screen for a page element after a click. A site can hide the browser UI, which helps video and games but also lets a malicious page draw a fake desktop or browser bar. Esc always exits. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While you watch or play\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a Full screen button, or press a key like F on a video. Fill the screen with a video, a game or a photo after a tap or key press Hide the"},"752":{"title":"Push history entries (back button limits)","description":"Adds fake history entries so Back does less than expected; browsers skip entries made without a click.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-history-pushstate","content":"<Chips /> Can add extra Back button steps, but browsers skip steps made without a tap Adds fake history entries so Back does less than expected; browsers skip entries made without a click. A site can change the address bar and history without loading a page. Misused, it traps the back button and keeps the person on the site, so browsers now skip entries added with no user interaction. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Inside its own site only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as you move aro"},"753":{"title":"Navigate or redirect the tab","description":"Sends the current tab to a different page by script, meta refresh or server redirect.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-navigate-top","content":"<Chips /> Can send your current tab to another page by itself Sends the current tab to a different page by script, meta refresh or server redirect. A site can move the person to another URL at any time, including after they click something. Cross-origin iframes get tighter limits, which is why ad frames cannot hijack the whole tab. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"The tab it is running in\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens on load, after a timer, or when you submit a form. Send you to a new "},"754":{"title":"Navigation API","description":"Gives a single place to listen to and control same-document navigations.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-navigation-api","content":"<Chips /> Can watch and control page changes inside the app, such as when you press Back Gives a single place to listen to and control same-document navigations. A single page app can intercept link clicks and back or forward, and list its own history entries. It sees only same-origin entries, so it cannot read browsing history. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only inside its own site\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as you click links, press Back, or move around the app. Notice when you "},"755":{"title":"Open popup or new window","description":"Opens a new tab or popup window, but only right after a click or tap.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-open-popup","content":"<Chips /> Can open a new tab or window, but only right after you tap or click Opens a new tab or popup window, but only right after a click or tap. A site can put another page in front of the person (ads, login flows, payment pages). Popup blockers decide whether it happens, so it is the main way a site can steer attention to a second origin. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One window per tap\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a link, a button, or Share, Print or Open in new tab. Open a new tab or smal"},"756":{"title":"Background tab timer throttling","description":"Hidden tabs get slower timers: 1 per second, then 1 per minute after 5 minutes in Chrome.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-page-visibility-throttling","content":"<Chips /> Can't keep its timers running fast once you switch to another tab Hidden tabs get slower timers: 1 per second, then 1 per minute after 5 minutes in Chrome. Tells the site whether the tab is visible and caps how much it can do while hidden. Useful for battery, but sites also detect tab switches via visibilitychange. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Slows down when hidden\" /> **What the user does:** You switch to another tab or app and leave the page. Run its code once a second while the tab is h"},"757":{"title":"Popover and modal dialog","description":"Shows overlays on top of everything on the page using the top layer.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-popover-dialog","content":"<Chips /> Can show a box over everything else on the page Shows overlays on top of everything on the page using the top layer. A site can draw modal dialogs and popovers above all other content, including iframes. They are page content, not browser UI, so well made fakes can mimic prompts. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Over its own page only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing for most boxes. It happens when you tap a menu, a help bubble or Open details. Show a menu, tooltip or confirm box on top of t"},"758":{"title":"Open the print dialog","description":"Opens the system print dialog for the page, no click required in most browsers.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-print","content":"<Chips /> Can open your print window, without a click in most browsers Opens the system print dialog for the page, no click required in most browsers. A site can ask to print itself or a hidden frame. It cannot choose a printer or print silently, and it cannot read what the person does in the dialog. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Opens the dialog, you decide\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing in most cases. It happens when the page runs a print command, or you tap Print. Open the print dialog for the"},"759":{"title":"Task scheduling (postTask, yield)","description":"Lets a page prioritize its own tasks and yield to input so it stays responsive.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-scheduler","content":"<Chips /> Can sort its own tasks so the page stays quick when you tap Lets a page prioritize its own tasks and yield to input so it stays responsive. A site can order its work and split long tasks. It cannot run when the tab is hidden any faster; it only improves smoothness inside the page. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only its own page work\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens while the page runs and you scroll or tap. Run important page tasks before less important ones Pause its own long"},"760":{"title":"Beacons and keepalive requests","description":"Sends data after the person leaves or closes the page, with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-send-beacon","content":"<Chips /> Can send data to a server as you leave or close the page Sends data after the person leaves or closes the page, with no prompt. A site can finish sending analytics even as the tab closes. It is the standard way for page leave metrics, and the reason tab closing does not stop data from being sent. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"At the moment you leave\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you close the tab, switch away or tap a link. Send a small report to its server when you le"},"761":{"title":"Service Worker offline caching","description":"Installs a script that caches pages and answers requests when offline.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-service-worker-cache","content":"<Chips /> Can save pages on your device so the site works offline Installs a script that caches pages and answers requests when offline. A site can keep running its own code between visits and serve content from a local cache. Safari deletes script-written storage after 7 days without interaction; Chrome keeps it until cleared. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Stays on your device between visits\" /> **What the user does:** You open the site once. It installs by itself, with no pop up. Save pages, images "},"762":{"title":"Speculation Rules (prefetch and prerender)","description":"A page can tell the browser to load and render the next page in the background.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-speculation-rules","content":"<Chips /> Can tell your browser to load the likely next page before you tap A page can tell the browser to load and render the next page in the background. A site can preload other URLs the person may visit. Prerendered pages run scripts before the person arrives, so analytics and counters may fire on a visit that never happens. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only its own likely next pages\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as you hover over or get close to a link. Load the next page in th"},"763":{"title":"Speech synthesis (text to speech)","description":"Speaks text aloud with the device's voices; needs a prior interaction.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-speech-synthesis","content":"<Chips /> Can speak text out loud, after you have tapped on the page Speaks text aloud with the device's voices; needs a prior interaction. A site can read text out loud with built-in voices. The voice list differs by device, so it also hints at the OS, language packs and installed software. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Listen or Play aloud, or tap the page before it speaks. Speak text out loud using voices on your device Pause, resume or sto"},"764":{"title":"Change tab title and icon","description":"Changes the tab title and icon at any time, including while the tab is in the background.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-tab-title-favicon","content":"<Chips /> Can change its tab name and small icon at any time Changes the tab title and icon at any time, including while the tab is in the background. A site can animate the title, add unread counts, or swap the favicon. It is a no-prompt way to draw attention from a hidden tab; scammers also use it to mimic other sites. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Its own tab only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as you use the page, even when the tab is hidden. Change the tab name, such as to show a"},"765":{"title":"Scroll to text fragment","description":"Highlights and scrolls to quoted text in a page when a link includes #:~:text=.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-text-fragments","content":"<Chips /> Can scroll to and highlight text on a page when you open a special link Highlights and scrolls to quoted text in a page when a link includes \\#:\\~:text=. A link can point at one sentence on any page. The browser, not the page, does the highlighting, and the page cannot see the fragment, so it cannot learn what the visitor was searching for. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One link, one highlight\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a link that ends with a text highlight, like a link shared from"},"766":{"title":"Vibrate the device","description":"Triggers the phone's vibration motor in short pulses.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-vibrate","content":"<Chips /> Can buzz the phone in short pulses, after you have tapped the page Triggers the phone's vibration motor in short pulses. A site can buzz the device for feedback or alerts. It is Android only, needs a tap first, and cannot run from hidden frames or background tabs. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Short buzz, after a tap\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a button, win a game, or touch the page before it buzzes. Buzz your Android phone in a pattern after a tap Stop a buzz it started Give touch "},"767":{"title":"View Transitions","description":"Animates between page states, including across same-origin navigations.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-view-transitions","content":"<Chips /> Can animate smoothly between two page screens on its own site Animates between page states, including across same-origin navigations. A site can capture a screenshot of the old and new state and morph between them. Cross-document transitions work only within one origin, so it cannot reveal another site's pixels. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only its own screens\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you tap a link or the page updates. Fade or slide between two screens Animate "},"768":{"title":"Web Audio playback","description":"Plays generated sound; the audio context stays silent until the person has interacted.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-web-audio","content":"<Chips /> Can play and mix sound inside the page, after you have tapped Plays generated sound; the audio context stays silent until the person has interacted. A site can synthesize and mix sound at sample precision (games, music tools). Autoplay rules keep it quiet until a click, but once resumed it can run for as long as the tab lives. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Play, Start or anything on the page before sound begins. Play sounds, music or"},"769":{"title":"Web Crypto (non-extractable keys)","description":"Provides built-in cryptography; keys can be stored in the browser and marked non-exportable.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-web-crypto","content":"<Chips /> Can lock and unlock data inside the page, with keys it can't hand out Provides built-in cryptography; keys can be stored in the browser and marked non-exportable. A site can encrypt data, sign with keys it cannot export, and store them in IndexedDB. Non-extractable keys stop theft by script but do not stop a malicious page using them. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Inside the page only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page signs in, saves data or checks a file. Create "},"770":{"title":"Web Workers","description":"Runs scripts on background threads with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-web-workers","content":"<Chips /> Can run extra code in the background while the page is open Runs scripts on background threads with no prompt. A site can use multiple CPU cores while the tab is open. Workers cannot touch the DOM but can run timers, network and compute, which is how miners and heavy trackers hide from the UI thread. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads or starts a heavy job. Run heavy work on a second thread so the page stay"},"771":{"title":"WebAssembly (threads, SIMD, memory limits)","description":"Runs compiled code at near native speed inside the page, with up to 4 GB of memory (16 GB with memory64 in Chrome).","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-webassembly","content":"<Chips /> Can run fast compiled code inside the page, within memory limits Runs compiled code at near native speed inside the page, with up to 4 GB of memory (16 GB with memory64 in Chrome). A site can run heavy code (games, codecs, Photoshop-class tools) with no install. The same speed makes in-browser miners and side channel timing code practical. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Inside the page's sandbox\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads, such as a game, editor or vid"},"772":{"title":"WebCodecs (video and audio encode and decode)","description":"Gives direct access to the browser's video and audio codecs with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-webcodecs","content":"<Chips /> Can open and make video and sound files by itself inside the page Gives direct access to the browser's video and audio codecs with no prompt. A site can encode or decode media frames in code, often with hardware acceleration. Great for editors, but also lets a page probe which codecs and accelerators the device has. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Media it already has\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page plays, edits or records media. Decode video and audio frame by fr"},"773":{"title":"WebRTC data channels (peer to peer)","description":"Sends data directly between browsers without a server, with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-webrtc-data-channel","content":"<Chips /> Can send data straight between two browsers, with no pop up Sends data directly between browsers without a server, with no prompt. A site can connect two people peer to peer. Setup exchanges network candidates, which can expose the person's public IP address to the other peer or to the site. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing for data. A video call asks for camera permission only when you use it. Send text, files or game moves straight to"},"774":{"title":"WebSockets","description":"Opens a two-way live connection to a server, with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-websockets","content":"<Chips /> Can keep a live two way link to a server, with no pop up Opens a two-way live connection to a server, with no prompt. A site can keep a persistent channel to its server for chat, live data or telemetry. It is not covered by CORS, and the connection ends when the tab closes. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads, like chat, live scores or games. Send and get messages in both directions at once Keep the link ope"},"775":{"title":"WebTransport","description":"Opens low-latency streams and datagrams to an HTTP/3 server, with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-webtransport","content":"<Chips /> Can open fast two way streams to a server, with no pop up Opens low-latency streams and datagrams to an HTTP/3 server, with no prompt. A site can send and receive many parallel streams and unreliable datagrams over QUIC. Faster than WebSockets, but also a flexible channel for covert upload. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads, like a live video or game. Send and get many streams of data at once Send small qu"},"776":{"title":"Close a window from script","description":"Closes windows the script opened; ordinary tabs cannot be closed by script.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/actions-window-close","content":"<Chips /> Can close windows it opened itself, but not your regular tabs Closes windows the script opened; ordinary tabs cannot be closed by script. A page can close a popup it created (for example after sign in), but it cannot kill the tab the person opened, which prevents sites from trapping or hiding. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only windows it opened\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing for its own windows. It happens when you finish a sign in or tap Done. Close a popup or window that the page ope"},"777":{"title":"Things a page can do","description":"Actions a website can take for you. 39 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions","content":"Actions a website can take for you. 39 capabilities in this group. | Capability           | Kind          | In one line                                                                 |\n| -------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Ad blocker detection | Gets silently | Can guess whether you use an ad blocker, by checking if its bait ads vanish | | Capability                        | Kind                          | In one line                                                       |\n| --------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| alert, confirm and prompt dialogs | Does something on your device | Can freeze the page with a pop up message box until you answer it | | Capability               | Kind                          | In one line                                                                |\n| ------------------------ | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Autoplay audio and video | Does something on your device | Can start muted video on its own, and sound only after you have interacted | | Capability                      | Kind                 | In one line                                                       |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Background tab timer throttling | Works without asking | Can't keep its timers running fast once you switch to another tab | | Capability                     | Kind                 | In one line                                              |\n| ------------------------------ | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Beacons and keepalive requests | Works without asking | Can send data to a server as you leave or close the page | | Capability                | Kind                          | In one line                                        |\n| ------------------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| Change tab title and icon | Does something on your device | Can change its tab name and small icon at any time | | Capability                 | Kind                          | In one line                                                   |\n| -------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Close a window from script | Does something on your device | Can close windows it opened itself, but not your regular tabs | | Capability          | Kind                 | In one line                                                                    |\n| ------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Compression Streams | Works without asking | Can squeeze or unpack data in the page with the browser's built in compression | | Capability       | Kind                 | In one line                                                                              |\n| ---------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Cookie Store API | Works without asking | Can watch and change the site's own cookies, but not hidden ones or other sites' cookies | | Capability                                             | Kind                 | In one line                                                                   |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Cross-origin isolation (SharedArrayBuffer, COOP, COEP) | Works without asking | Can switch on a locked down mode that unlocks fast shared memory for the page | | Capability    | Kind                          | In one line                                               |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Drag and drop | Does something on your device | Can let you drag items on the page, or drop files onto it | | Capability    | Kind                 | In one line                                                         |\n| ------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Fenced frames | Works without asking | Can embed a box of content that the page around it cannot look into | | Capability             | Kind                          | In one line                                                                      |\n| ---------------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Focus and window raise | Does something on your device | Can move the cursor between boxes on its page, but can't pull its window forward | | Capability | Kind                          | In one line                                                   |\n| ---------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Fullscreen | Does something on your device | Can make a video or page area fill your screen, after you tap | | Capability                             | Kind                          | In one line                                                                               |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Leave-page confirmation (beforeunload) | Does something on your device | Can show a generic Leave site? warning when you close or leave, after you have interacted | | Capability                       | Kind          | In one line                            "},"778":{"title":"Ad blocker detection","description":"Creates bait elements to see whether an ad blocker hides them.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/page-adblock-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can guess whether you use an ad blocker, by checking if its bait ads vanish Creates bait elements to see whether an ad blocker hides them. Shows when someone uses content blockers, which also marks privacy tool users. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A guess when the page loads\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check if a hidden bait element gets hidden or blocked Show a message asking you to turn off your blocker Count how many visitors use blockers See which"},"779":{"title":"Loaded script list (javascripts)","description":"Lists which scripts the page loaded.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/page-javascripts","content":"<Chips /> Can list the scripts loaded on its own page Lists which scripts the page loaded. A list of script URLs (up to 2,200 chars) can reveal injected scripts from extensions or tools. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only the page itself\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads or when a tool scans the page. Find which scripts its page loaded, including ones from other companies Spot tracking or ad scripts on its own page Check that a script loaded as expected See scripts on"},"780":{"title":"Page URL (location)","description":"Reads the current page's full address.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Things a page can do"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/page-actions/page-page-url","content":"<Chips /> Can see the full address of the page you are on Reads the current page's full address. The URL (up to 1500 chars) shows what profile or page the person is looking at, which is sensitive on a social network. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"The current page only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as soon as the page loads. See the whole address, including any search words or codes in it Use bits of the address to show the right content Send the address to its own server or to other "},"781":{"title":"Purchase history and loyalty data","description":"A signed in shop knows exactly what the person bought, when, and how much they paid.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/payments-commerce/commerce-purchase-history","content":"<Chips /> Can see what you bought, when and for how much, once you are signed in A signed in shop knows exactly what the person bought, when, and how much they paid. Order records show size, health, family and hobby clues and are the most valuable first party data for retail media networks. Tax law has retailers keep records 3 to 7 years in the US. Shared with ad partners only if permitted. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Everything you buy from that shop\" /> **What the user does:** You sign in to a sho"},"782":{"title":"Payments and shopping","description":"Paying, wallets and purchases. 8 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/payments-commerce","content":"Paying, wallets and purchases. 8 capabilities in this group. | Capability                             | Kind                 | In one line                                                              |\n| -------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Apple Pay on the web (ApplePaySession) | Works without asking | Can start an Apple Pay payment that you confirm with Face ID or Touch ID | | Capability                                | Kind                 | In one line                                                       |\n| ----------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Digital Goods API (Play Billing in a TWA) | Works without asking | Can sell in app items in a Play Store web app through Google Play | | Capability             | Kind                 | In one line                                                         |\n| ---------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Google Pay for the web | Works without asking | Can start a Google Pay payment that you pick a card for and approve | | Capability          | Kind                 | In one line                                                                      |\n| ------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Payment Handler API | Works without asking | Can offer its own pay option in the browser's payment sheet, once you install it | | Capability          | Kind                 | In one line                                                              |\n| ------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Payment Request API | Works without asking | Can open the browser's pay sheet with saved cards and addresses you pick | | Capability                        | Kind          | In one line                                                            |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Purchase history and loyalty data | Gets silently | Can see what you bought, when and for how much, once you are signed in | | Capability                  | Kind                 | In one line                                                               |\n| --------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Secure Payment Confirmation | Works without asking | Can confirm a card payment with a passkey that you approve in the browser | | Capability                      | Kind                 | In one line                                                           |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Stored payment and address data | Works without asking | Can fill in saved cards and addresses at checkout, if you choose them |"},"783":{"title":"Apple Pay on the web (ApplePaySession)","description":"Pays with Apple Pay in Safari or via the Payment Request API.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/payments-commerce/payments-apple-pay-js","content":"<Chips /> Can start an Apple Pay payment that you confirm with Face ID or Touch ID Pays with Apple Pay in Safari or via the Payment Request API. The site gets a payment token, billing and shipping contact only after Face ID or Touch ID approval. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One payment, once you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap the Apple Pay button at checkout. Show the Apple Pay sheet with your cards and shipping address Send a one time payment code to the store after you approve Ask for "},"784":{"title":"Digital Goods API (Play Billing in a TWA)","description":"Lets a Play Store web app sell in app items through Google Play.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/payments-commerce/payments-digital-goods","content":"<Chips /> Can sell in app items in a Play Store web app through Google Play Lets a Play Store web app sell in app items through Google Play. Lists products and past purchases of the account inside a TWA; needs Play account. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One purchase, once you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Buy on an in app item inside a Play Store web app. Show the items for sale and their prices from Google Play Open the Google Play payment sheet Check which items you already own Charge "},"785":{"title":"Google Pay for the web","description":"Pays with a Google Pay card through a Google button or Payment Request.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/payments-commerce/payments-google-pay","content":"<Chips /> Can start a Google Pay payment that you pick a card for and approve Pays with a Google Pay card through a Google button or Payment Request. Returns a tokenized card to the merchant's PSP after the person approves. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One payment, once you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap the Google Pay button at checkout and pick a card. Show the Google Pay sheet with your saved cards and addresses Send the store a one time payment code once you approve Share your name, "},"786":{"title":"Payment Handler API","description":"Lets a payment app run inside the browser as a payment option.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/payments-commerce/payments-payment-handler","content":"<Chips /> Can offer its own pay option in the browser's payment sheet, once you install it Lets a payment app run inside the browser as a payment option. Lets a wallet website register as a payment method; the site sees only the payment response. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One payment, once you pick it\" /> **What the user does:** You pick that payment app in the browser's payment sheet, after it was added. Show up as a payment choice in the browser pay sheet Open its own window to take payment deta"},"787":{"title":"Payment Request API","description":"Opens the browser's native pay sheet with saved cards and addresses.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/payments-commerce/payments-payment-request","content":"<Chips /> Can open the browser's pay sheet with saved cards and addresses you pick Opens the browser's native pay sheet with saved cards and addresses. The site sees only what the person approves: card or wallet token, shipping, email and phone if requested. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One payment, once you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Pay or a wallet button at checkout. Show a pay sheet with your saved cards, wallets and addresses Ask for your name, email, phone and shipping address "},"788":{"title":"Secure Payment Confirmation","description":"Authenticates a card payment with a passkey inside the browser.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/payments-commerce/payments-secure-payment-confirmation","content":"<Chips /> Can confirm a card payment with a passkey that you approve in the browser Authenticates a card payment with a passkey inside the browser. Binds a WebAuthn credential to a payment so banks can confirm the person approved this exact amount. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One payment, once you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Pay, then confirm with your fingerprint, face or device PIN. Show the shop name and amount before you confirm Check you with a passkey that your bank set up Send"},"789":{"title":"Stored payment and address data","description":"Saved cards and addresses a browser, wallet or the site itself can fill at checkout.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Payments and shopping"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/payments-commerce/payments-stored-payment-address","content":"<Chips /> Can fill in saved cards and addresses at checkout, if you choose them Saved cards and addresses a browser, wallet or the site itself can fill at checkout. Browser autofill fills a saved card only after the person picks it, and often asks for the CVC. Apple Pay and Google Pay give the merchant a tokenised card, not the card number. Sites that store cards keep tokens from a payment provider. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"What you pick at checkout\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a field at "},"790":{"title":"Web MIDI (midi and midi sysex)","description":"Asks to talk to connected music gear such as keyboards and controllers.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/hardware-midi","content":"<Chips /> Can talk to your connected music gear, with a prompt for deeper control Asks to talk to connected music gear such as keyboards and controllers. Two permission levels: plain MIDI sees note and control data; sysex also sends vendor system messages that can reprogram a device. Reveals which music devices you own. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect keyboard or Connect MIDI on a music site. Listen to notes from a keyboard or controller"},"791":{"title":"Web NFC","description":"Asks to read or write NFC tags with your phone.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/hardware-nfc","content":"<Chips /> Can scan NFC tags or save to them with your phone, after you allow it Asks to read or write NFC tags with your phone. Lets a page scan NDEF tags and write to them. Only while the page is visible and focused. Android Chrome only. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Scan tag or Save to tag, then hold your phone near it. Scan an NFC tag when your phone touches it Save text or a link onto a tag after a tap Scan only while the page is open and "},"792":{"title":"Hardware and accessories","description":"Bluetooth, USB, NFC, controllers, sensors. 22 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware","content":"Bluetooth, USB, NFC, controllers, sensors. 22 capabilities in this group. | Capability                         | Kind             | In one line                                                    |\n| ---------------------------------- | ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Bulk photo library and file system | Blocked for apps | Can't browse your whole photo library or your files on its own | | Capability       | Kind          | In one line                                                   |\n| ---------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Compute Pressure | Gets silently | Can see how busy your device's chip is, but not exact numbers | | Capability                                     | Kind          | In one line                                                                             |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| enumerateDevices (camera, mic, speakers count) | Gets silently | Can count your cameras, microphones and speakers, but names stay hidden until you allow | | Capability | Kind                   | In one line                                                    |\n| ---------- | ---------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| EyeDropper | You pick what to share | Can pick one color from anywhere on your screen when you click | | Capability              | Kind          | In one line                                                               |\n| ----------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Gamepad API and haptics | Gets silently | Can see your game controller after you press a button, and make it rumble | | Capability       | Kind           | In one line                                                      |\n| ---------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Presentation API | Asks you first | Can show itself on a second screen you choose, like a Chromecast | | Capability      | Kind           | In one line                                                      |\n| --------------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Remote Playback | Asks you first | Can play a video or audio on a cast or AirPlay device you choose | | Capability              | Kind                          | In one line                                                            |\n| ----------------------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Screen Orientation lock | Does something on your device | Can lock your screen to portrait or landscape, but only in full screen | | Capability                       | Kind                 | In one line                                                |\n| -------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Shape Detection: BarcodeDetector | Works without asking | Can find barcodes and QR codes in a picture or camera view | | Capability                                     | Kind                 | In one line                                                                      |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Shape Detection: FaceDetector and TextDetector | Works without asking | Can find faces and printed text in a picture, but only with a special setting on | | Capability | Kind                          | In one line                                                        |\n| ---------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Vibration  | Does something on your device | Can buzz the phone in a pattern, after you have tapped on the page | | Capability           | Kind           | In one line                                                     |\n| -------------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Web Bluetooth (GATT) | Asks you first | Can connect to one nearby Bluetooth device you pick from a list | | Capability | Kind           | In one line                                                |\n| ---------- | -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Web MIDI   | Asks you first | Can send and get notes with music gear, after you allow it | | Capability                     | Kind           | In one line                                                             |\n| ------------------------------ | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Web MIDI (midi and midi sysex) | Asks you first | Can talk to your connected music gear, with a prompt for deeper control | | Capability | Kind           | In one line                                                           |\n| ---------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Web NFC    | Asks you first | Can scan NFC tags or save to them with your phone, after you allow it | | Capability | Kind           | In one line                                                    |\n| ---------- | -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Web NFC    | Asks you first | Can scan NFC tags and save to them when your phone touches one | | Capability | Kind           | In one line                                                 |\n| ---------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Web Serial | Asks you first | Can open one serial port you pick and exchange data with it | | Capability | Kind           | In"},"793":{"title":"Shape Detection: BarcodeDetector","description":"Finds and reads barcodes and QR codes in an image or camera frame.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-barcode-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can find barcodes and QR codes in a picture or camera view Finds and reads barcodes and QR codes in an image or camera frame. Turns pixels into decoded strings (product codes, URLs, tickets). Runs on-device with no prompt, once the page has the image. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Photos or camera while open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Scan QR code and allow your camera, or you pick a photo. Find a barcode or QR code in a photo Scan live from your camera, once you allow it Tell the p"},"794":{"title":"Compute Pressure","description":"Reports how stressed the CPU is (nominal, fair, serious, critical).","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-compute-pressure","content":"<Chips /> Can see how busy your device's chip is, but not exact numbers Reports how stressed the CPU is (nominal, fair, serious, critical). Coarse load level; shows if the device is struggling. Can leak what else is running. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a heavy app or call is open, if the site is allowed. Learn if your chip is in one of four states: nominal, fair, serious or critical Slow a video call or game when the device "},"795":{"title":"enumerateDevices (camera, mic, speakers count)","description":"Lists the kinds and counts of cameras, microphones and speakers (labels only after permission).","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-enumerate-devices","content":"<Chips /> Can count your cameras, microphones and speakers, but names stay hidden until you allow Lists the kinds and counts of cameras, microphones and speakers (labels only after permission). Counts of cameras and microphones separate laptops from desktops and show headset use. Without permission labels and IDs are empty. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A count, names after you allow\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads, like a video call lobby. Count how many cameras, m"},"796":{"title":"EyeDropper","description":"Lets the person pick a colour from anywhere on screen.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-eyedropper","content":"<Chips /> Can pick one color from anywhere on your screen when you click Lets the person pick a colour from anywhere on screen. Returns one pixel colour the person points at, including outside the page. Cannot capture other pixels. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One spot you click\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Pick color and click a spot on the screen. Show a magnifier so you can choose a color Get the color code of the one spot you click Work on any part of the screen, even outside the page Take"},"797":{"title":"Shape Detection: FaceDetector and TextDetector","description":"Finds faces and printed text in an image (experimental flag only).","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-face-text-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can find faces and printed text in a picture, but only with a special setting on Finds faces and printed text in an image (experimental flag only). Face boxes and text blocks from images without server calls. Experimental and off by default; not a stable surface. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A picture it already has\" /> **What the user does:** You turn on a flag in Chrome, then pick or load a picture. Find where faces are in a picture, without naming them Find where printed text sits in a p"},"798":{"title":"Gamepad API and haptics","description":"Reads buttons and sticks of a connected game controller; can trigger rumble.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-gamepad","content":"<Chips /> Can see your game controller after you press a button, and make it rumble Reads buttons and sticks of a connected game controller; can trigger rumble. Shows that a controller exists and exactly how it is being used. Controller id strings leak make and model. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You press any button on a connected controller while the page is open. See which buttons you press and how far you move the sticks Make a controller rumble "},"799":{"title":"Bulk photo library and file system","description":"A site cannot browse your photo library or disk by itself.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-not-exposed-camera-roll","content":"<Chips /> Can't browse your whole photo library or your files on its own A site cannot browse your photo library or disk by itself. Only files the person picks (or a folder they grant) are visible. No silent photo, music or Downloads scan. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only what you pick, each time\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Upload and pick files or a folder yourself each time. Open the files you pick in a file chooser Open a folder you pick, in browsers that allow it Remember a folder you pi"},"800":{"title":"Presentation API","description":"Lets a page show itself on a second screen such as a Chromecast.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-presentation-api","content":"<Chips /> Can show itself on a second screen you choose, like a Chromecast Lets a page show itself on a second screen such as a Chromecast. Sees which nearby display receivers exist only after the person picks one; lets a page cast to TVs. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A screen you choose\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Cast and pick a screen from the list. List nearby screens you can show the page on Show a page or video on the screen you pick Send messages to the second screen while it runs Pick"},"801":{"title":"Remote Playback","description":"Lets a video or audio element play on a nearby cast or AirPlay device.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-remote-playback","content":"<Chips /> Can play a video or audio on a cast or AirPlay device you choose Lets a video or audio element play on a nearby cast or AirPlay device. Shows whether cast targets exist; the person chooses where playback goes. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A device you choose\" /> **What the user does:** You tap the cast or AirPlay icon on the video and pick a device. Show the list of cast or AirPlay devices Play the video or audio on the one you pick Pause, play or stop it from the page Pick a device for you"},"802":{"title":"Screen Orientation lock","description":"Forces the screen into portrait or landscape.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-screen-orientation-lock","content":"<Chips /> Can lock your screen to portrait or landscape, but only in full screen Forces the screen into portrait or landscape. Controls how the person holds the phone. It reads nothing, but it can trap a page in one orientation. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While in full screen or installed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Full screen or open an installed web app, then start a game or video. Lock the screen to portrait or landscape Unlock it again when you leave full screen Work in installed web a"},"803":{"title":"Vibration","description":"Makes the phone buzz in a pattern.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-vibration","content":"<Chips /> Can buzz the phone in a pattern, after you have tapped on the page Makes the phone buzz in a pattern. Gives tactile feedback only; it cannot read anything. Sites can annoy people or use it for cues, but it also signals the device is a phone. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Short buzz after a tap\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a button or touch the page before it buzzes. Buzz your Android phone in a pattern Stop a buzz it started Give touch feedback in games, timers and alerts Buzz before "},"804":{"title":"Web Bluetooth (GATT)","description":"Lets a site connect to a nearby Bluetooth LE device the person picks.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-web-bluetooth","content":"<Chips /> Can connect to one nearby Bluetooth device you pick from a list Lets a site connect to a nearby Bluetooth LE device the person picks. Reads sensors (heart rate, battery, thermometers) and controls BLE gadgets. Reveals device names and service data; used for fitness, IoT setup and beacons. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One device you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect device and choose a gadget in the list, then press Pair. Show nearby Bluetooth LE devices that match what the si"},"805":{"title":"Web MIDI","description":"Lets a site send and receive MIDI messages to music gear.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-web-midi","content":"<Chips /> Can send and get notes with music gear, after you allow it Lets a site send and receive MIDI messages to music gear. Exposes the names of connected MIDI ports and live note and control data from keyboards and pads. With sysex it can reconfigure or flash devices. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect keyboard and allow MIDI access in the pop up. Listen for notes from your keyboard or controller Send notes and lights to your music gear"},"806":{"title":"Web NFC","description":"Lets a site read and write NFC tags when the phone touches one.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-web-nfc","content":"<Chips /> Can scan NFC tags and save to them when your phone touches one Lets a site read and write NFC tags when the phone touches one. Reads tag messages (URLs, text, ids) and can write or lock them. A site can learn which tags the person tapped, for payments tags, tickets or inventory. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Scan or Save, allow NFC, then hold your phone near a tag. Scan a tag your phone touches Save text or a link onto a tag Work onl"},"807":{"title":"Web Serial","description":"Lets a site open a serial port the person picks and exchange bytes with it.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-web-serial","content":"<Chips /> Can open one serial port you pick and exchange data with it Lets a site open a serial port the person picks and exchange bytes with it. Gives a page direct read and write to microcontrollers, 3D printers, GPS modules and Bluetooth serial adapters. Port identity (USB vendor and product id) can be read once granted. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One port you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect device and pick a port in the chooser. Show the available ports, filtered by device type"},"808":{"title":"WebHID","description":"Lets a site read and write raw reports from a keyboard, pad or other HID device the person picks.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-webhid","content":"<Chips /> Can talk to one keyboard, pad or other device you pick from a list Lets a site read and write raw reports from a keyboard, pad or other HID device the person picks. Gives full access to exotic input hardware (Stream Decks, game controllers, 3D mice, medical devices) beyond what the OS exposes. The device list and report data can identify the hardware and its state. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One device you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect device and pick one from the choos"},"809":{"title":"WebUSB","description":"Lets a site talk directly to a USB device the person picks.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-webusb","content":"<Chips /> Can talk to one USB device you pick from a list Lets a site talk directly to a USB device the person picks. Reveals which USB gadgets are plugged in (vendor, product, serial number) and lets the site control them. Used for dev boards, label printers and security keys; a path to device fingerprinting. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One device you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect device, pick one in the chooser and click Connect. Show USB devices that match what the site asks fo"},"810":{"title":"WebXR hand tracking","description":"Reads the position of each finger joint in a headset session.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-webxr-hand-tracking","content":"<Chips /> Can see where each of your fingers is inside a headset session Reads the position of each finger joint in a headset session. Gives 25 joints per hand every frame, enough to read gestures and sometimes identify a person by hand shape and motion. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the VR session runs\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Enter VR on a site in a headset and allow tracking. Track your finger joints during a VR session Let you point, pinch and grab with your hands Work only while "},"811":{"title":"WebXR immersive VR and AR sessions","description":"Starts a full VR or AR session on a headset or phone.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-webxr-immersive","content":"<Chips /> Can start a full VR or AR session after you allow it Starts a full VR or AR session on a headset or phone. Streams head and controller pose every frame and, in AR, shows the camera view of the room. Reveals body movement, height and surroundings. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the session runs\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Enter VR or View in AR and allow the pop up. Fill your view with a VR scene Show AR items placed in your room Track your head and device movement while the sess"},"812":{"title":"WebXR world sensing (hit test, anchors, depth, planes, camera)","description":"Lets an AR session see surfaces, depth and even raw camera frames of the room.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Hardware and accessories"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/peripherals-hardware/peripherals-webxr-world-sensing","content":"<Chips /> Can detect surfaces and depth in your room, and even see camera frames Lets an AR session see surfaces, depth and even raw camera frames of the room. Maps the person's real surroundings: floors, tables, walls, depth and, with camera access, pixels of the room. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the AR session runs\" /> **What the user does:** You tap View in AR and allow the AR and camera prompts. Find floors, walls and tables, so items sit on them Place items that stay in one spot Measure d"},"813":{"title":"Browser saved address and card autofill","description":"Chrome and Safari offer saved addresses and cards to fill checkout forms.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/personal-data-contacts/contacts-address-payment-autofill","content":"<Chips /> Can offer your saved addresses and cards to fill a form, if you pick them Chrome and Safari offer saved addresses and cards to fill checkout forms. Gives a site a user's address, phone and card after the person picks them in a browser prompt. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"What you pick at checkout\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a form field and choose a saved address or card from the list. Fill a name, address or phone number from your saved ones Fill a saved card number in a checkout f"},"814":{"title":"Autofill and autocomplete tokens","description":"Form fields can ask the browser to fill saved name, email, phone, address and birthday.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/personal-data-contacts/contacts-autofill-tokens","content":"<Chips /> Can ask your browser to fill saved name, email, phone and address Form fields can ask the browser to fill saved name, email, phone, address and birthday. Browser autofill puts personal data into inputs. Pages see it only after fill (often after user interaction on Chrome). <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"What you pick at each form\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a form field and choose a suggestion, or click first on some sites. Fill name, email, phone, address and birthday from your saved"},"815":{"title":"Contact Picker","description":"Lets a person choose contacts to share with a site; Chrome on Android only.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/personal-data-contacts/contacts-contact-picker","content":"<Chips /> Can see only the contacts you pick, and only the details it asks for Lets a person choose contacts to share with a site; Chrome on Android only. The site gets only the names, emails, phones, addresses or icons of the contacts the person ticks. No bulk read, no desktop, no Firefox, no Safari release. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only the contacts you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Choose contacts, search and select people, and press Done. Show a list of your contacts that you can s"},"816":{"title":"getInstalledRelatedApps","description":"Lets a site check whether its own native app is installed.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/personal-data-contacts/contacts-get-installed-related-apps","content":"<Chips /> Can check if its own app is installed, but not see your other apps Lets a site check whether its own native app is installed. Reveals only whether the site's declared related app is installed, to hide install banners. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only its own linked apps\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads, to show Open in app or Get the app. Check if its own app is installed on your device Show Open in app instead of Get the app Check apps that the site has "},"817":{"title":"Whole address book or call log","description":"A site cannot read your whole contact list, call history or SMS.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/personal-data-contacts/contacts-not-exposed-address-book","content":"<Chips /> Can't see your whole contact list, call history or text messages A site cannot read your whole contact list, call history or SMS. The only contact route is the picker, one chosen entry at a time. No web API reads call logs, SMS, voicemail or calendar events. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked in every browser\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. No browser lets a site do this, even if you tap Allow Ask you to pick contacts one at a time, on browsers that offer a contact picker Receive onl"},"818":{"title":"WebOTP (one time code from SMS)","description":"Lets a page read the one time code from an SMS the person receives.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Contacts and personal data"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/personal-data-contacts/contacts-webotp","content":"<Chips /> Can see one text message code meant for that site, after you allow it Lets a page read the one time code from an SMS the person receives. Reads one matching SMS code that is tied to the site's domain; not the whole SMS inbox. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One code, one moment\" /> **What the user does:** You ask to log in or confirm a phone number, then tap Allow on the sheet Receive the one time code from a text message that names the site's web address Fill in that code for you, so you do n"},"819":{"title":"Contacts and personal data","description":"Your people, photos, calendar and more. 6 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/personal-data-contacts","content":"Your people, photos, calendar and more. 6 capabilities in this group. | Capability                       | Kind                 | In one line                                                       |\n| -------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Autofill and autocomplete tokens | Works without asking | Can ask your browser to fill saved name, email, phone and address | | Capability                              | Kind                 | In one line                                                               |\n| --------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Browser saved address and card autofill | Works without asking | Can offer your saved addresses and cards to fill a form, if you pick them | | Capability     | Kind                   | In one line                                                          |\n| -------------- | ---------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Contact Picker | You pick what to share | Can see only the contacts you pick, and only the details it asks for | | Capability              | Kind          | In one line                                                        |\n| ----------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| getInstalledRelatedApps | Gets silently | Can check if its own app is installed, but not see your other apps | | Capability                      | Kind           | In one line                                                           |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| WebOTP (one time code from SMS) | Asks you first | Can see one text message code meant for that site, after you allow it | | Capability                     | Kind             | In one line                                                      |\n| ------------------------------ | ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Whole address book or call log | Blocked for apps | Can't see your whole contact list, call history or text messages |"},"820":{"title":"Spoofing and lie detection (signals)","description":"Checks whether language, resolution, OS or browser values were faked and whether an ad blocker is on.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/fingerprint-spoofing-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can check whether your browser settings were faked or an ad blocker is on Checks whether language, resolution, OS or browser values were faked and whether an ad blocker is on. Compares claimed values with probed ones to spot people using privacy tools or anti-detect browsers. The result becomes a fingerprint signal in itself. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent check on every visit\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Compare your claimed language, screen size"},"821":{"title":"Graphics and fingerprinting","description":"Drawing and the hidden device clues it leaks. 38 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint","content":"Drawing and the hidden device clues it leaks. 38 capabilities in this group. | Capability                   | Kind              | In one line                                                                        |\n| ---------------------------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Anti-spoof and lie detection | Used to track you | Can tell when a privacy tool or anti detect browser changed your browser's answers | | Capability                                                | Kind              | In one line                                                                    |\n| --------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| APFC / DNA device fingerprint (abuse prevention features) | Used to track you | Can collect about 48 device traits and send them in secret with later requests | | Capability                                    | Kind          | In one line                                            |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Apple Pay and Private Click Measurement flags | Gets silently | Can check if Apple Pay is set up and if you use Safari | | Capability         | Kind          | In one line                                                          |\n| ------------------ | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Audio base latency | Gets silently | Can measure your audio delay to guess your system and audio hardware | | Capability                              | Kind          | In one line                                                        |\n| --------------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Audio fingerprint (OfflineAudioContext) | Gets silently | Can make a silent test sound to tell your device apart from others | | Capability                       | Kind          | In one line                                                       |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| AudioContext hardware properties | Gets silently | Can see your audio setup, such as sample rate and output channels | | Capability                         | Kind              | In one line                                                          |\n| ---------------------------------- | ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| AudioContext rendering fingerprint | Used to track you | Can run a silent sound test and turn the result into a device number | | Capability     | Kind              | In one line                                                                |\n| -------------- | ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Brave farbling | Used to track you | Can't track you across sites by device traits when Brave adds random noise | | Capability            | Kind              | In one line                                                               |\n| --------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Canvas 2D fingerprint | Used to track you | Can turn a hidden drawing into a device number that works without cookies | | Capability         | Kind          | In one line                                                                    |\n| ------------------ | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Canvas fingerprint | Gets silently | Can draw a hidden picture and use tiny pixel differences to tell devices apart | | Capability                                      | Kind              | In one line                                                             |\n| ----------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Chrome and Edge: no default fingerprint defense | Used to track you | Can't stop sites from using device traits to spot you in Chrome or Edge | | Capability                                     | Kind              | In one line                                                                      |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Composite fingerprint (FingerprintJS, CreepJS) | Used to track you | Can combine many device traits into one visitor ID that survives cleared cookies | | Capability                          | Kind          | In one line                                                  |\n| ----------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| CPU architecture via float behavior | Gets silently | Can run number tests to tell whether your chip is ARM or x86 | | Capability                          | Kind              | In one line                                                            |\n| ----------------------------------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| CSS feature and media query probing | Used to track you | Can find your browser version by testing which style features it knows | | Capability                         | Kind              | In one line                                                    |\n| ---------------------------------- | ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| DOMRect and getClientRects metrics | Used to track you | Can measure text and emoji boxes to spot your fonts and system | | Capability                | Kind              | In one line                  "},"822":{"title":"AudioContext hardware properties","description":"Reads the audio output sample rate, latency and channel count with no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-audio-context-properties","content":"<Chips /> Can see your audio setup, such as sample rate and output channels Reads the audio output sample rate, latency and channel count with no prompt. Sample rate (44100 or 48000), base and output latency and maximum output channels reveal the audio hardware and OS audio stack, such as wired headphones, Bluetooth or a studio interface. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, device traits only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Check the sample rate, usually 44100 or "},"823":{"title":"Audio fingerprint (OfflineAudioContext)","description":"Renders a sound offline and reads tiny numeric differences.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-audio-context","content":"<Chips /> Can make a silent test sound to tell your device apart from others Renders a sound offline and reads tiny numeric differences. Audio stack differences give a device specific value without ever playing sound or asking permission. LinkedIn uses OfflineAudioContext(1,44100,44100). <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, while the page runs\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Process a short sound in memory without playing it Measure tiny number differences that dep"},"824":{"title":"AudioContext rendering fingerprint","description":"Renders a silent tone through an audio graph offline and sums the output samples.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-audio-offline-fingerprint","content":"<Chips /> Can run a silent sound test and turn the result into a device number Renders a silent tone through an audio graph offline and sums the output samples. Tiny floating point differences in the audio stack (DynamicsCompressor, oscillator math, CPU SIMD) give a number that is stable per browser build and hardware. It never plays sound. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Repeatable on every visit\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Render a quiet tone in memory and add u"},"825":{"title":"Canvas 2D fingerprint","description":"Draws text and shapes to a hidden canvas, then hashes the pixels the device produced.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-canvas-2d-fingerprint","content":"<Chips /> Can turn a hidden drawing into a device number that works without cookies Draws text and shapes to a hidden canvas, then hashes the pixels the device produced. GPU, driver, OS font rasterizer and anti-aliasing leave tiny pixel differences. The hash is a stable device-class identifier that works with no cookies and no prompt. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Repeatable across visits\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Draw hidden text and shapes, then check the pi"},"826":{"title":"Canvas fingerprint","description":"Draws text and shapes in a hidden canvas and reads back the pixels.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-canvas","content":"<Chips /> Can draw a hidden picture and use tiny pixel differences to tell devices apart Draws text and shapes in a hidden canvas and reads back the pixels. Tiny differences in fonts, antialiasing and GPU drivers make the image almost unique per device and software stack. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent, repeatable device number\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Draw text and shapes you never see Check how your screen and fonts turned them into pixels Turn that i"},"827":{"title":"Composite fingerprint (FingerprintJS, CreepJS)","description":"Combines dozens of signals into one visitor ID that survives cookie clearing.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-composite-fingerprint","content":"<Chips /> Can combine many device traits into one visitor ID that survives cleared cookies Combines dozens of signals into one visitor ID that survives cookie clearing. Open and commercial libraries hash 50 to 100 signals, then match drifting fingerprints to past visitors. Research found 90% of fingerprints are unique on desktop but breaking up over weeks, so linking needs ML. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Follows your device across visits\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page "},"828":{"title":"Brave farbling","description":"Brave adds small per-site random noise to canvas, WebGL, audio and other readouts.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-countermeasure-brave-farbling","content":"<Chips /> Can't track you across sites by device traits when Brave adds random noise Brave adds small per-site random noise to canvas, WebGL, audio and other readouts. Farbling changes the last bits of fingerprint outputs with a seed per site per session, so tracking sites cannot link a person across sites while pages still work. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Protection that Brave applies for you\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Brave does it for you by default Add small random changes to canvas, "},"829":{"title":"Chrome and Edge: no default fingerprint defense","description":"Chrome and Edge do not randomize or block fingerprinting APIs by default.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-countermeasure-chrome-none","content":"<Chips /> Can't stop sites from using device traits to spot you in Chrome or Edge Chrome and Edge do not randomize or block fingerprinting APIs by default. Chrome only reduced passive signals (User-Agent Reduction, Client Hints, partitioned storage). Privacy Budget never shipped, so canvas, WebGL, audio and fonts stay fully readable. Chrome has the largest fingerprinting surface. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Little default protection\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It is how Chrome and Edge are "},"830":{"title":"Firefox Fingerprinting Protection and RFP","description":"Firefox randomizes canvas readback and spoofs other values in strict modes and Private Browsing.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-countermeasure-firefox-fpp","content":"<Chips /> Can't be fingerprinted as easily in Firefox strict mode or Private Browsing Firefox randomizes canvas readback and spoofs other values in strict modes and Private Browsing. FPP (since 119) targets known fingerprinting scripts. Firefox 145 adds more protections in Private Browsing and ETP Strict, claimed to halve the share of unique-looking users. Full resistFingerprinting (RFP) unifies values but breaks sites. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Strict or private mode only\" /> **What the user does"},"831":{"title":"Safari Advanced Fingerprinting Protection","description":"Safari limits what known fingerprinting scripts can read and perturbs canvas and audio readback.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-countermeasure-safari-afp","content":"<Chips /> Can't use many device traits in Safari to tell you apart Safari limits what known fingerprinting scripts can read and perturbs canvas and audio readback. Safari 17 enabled it in Private Browsing. Safari 26 (iOS 26, macOS 26) extends it to all browsing by default, restricting known fingerprinting scripts from reading screen size, core count, voices, Web Audio and 2D canvas. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Built in protection\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Safari does it by default, in all"},"832":{"title":"Tor Browser uniformity and letterboxing","description":"Tor Browser makes all users look the same: fixed fonts, letterboxed window, canvas prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-countermeasure-tor-letterbox","content":"<Chips /> Can't tell Tor Browser users apart, since everyone looks the same Tor Browser makes all users look the same: fixed fonts, letterboxed window, canvas prompt. Standardizes fonts, screen size in 200x100 steps, UTC timezone and en-US locale. Canvas readback needs a prompt. Goal is one crowd, not noise, which yields about zero bits when users keep defaults. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Same look for all Tor users\" /> **What the user does:** You open Tor Browser. It works by default Show every vi"},"833":{"title":"CSS feature and media query probing","description":"Tests which CSS properties, at-rules and media queries the browser understands.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-css-feature-probing","content":"<Chips /> Can find your browser version by testing which style features it knows Tests which CSS properties, at-rules and media queries the browser understands. Each CSS feature has a first-supported version, so a handful of CSS.supports calls pin the browser version within 1 to 2 releases and expose spoofed user agents. Media queries add color gamut, HDR, contrast and motion settings. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, browser version only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the p"},"834":{"title":"DOMRect and getClientRects metrics","description":"Measures element boxes at subpixel precision, including text, emoji and ranges.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-domrect-clientrects","content":"<Chips /> Can measure text and emoji boxes to spot your fonts and system Measures element boxes at subpixel precision, including text, emoji and ranges. Sizes of text and emoji boxes depend on installed fonts, text shaping, zoom and OS, giving a hash that works even when canvas is blocked. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent, repeatable device number\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Measure the size of hidden text and emoji down to tiny fractions Turn the sizes into"},"835":{"title":"Emoji and glyph rendering","description":"Renders emoji and rare Unicode characters and measures or hashes how they look.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-emoji-rendering","content":"<Chips /> Can draw emoji to tell your system and version apart Renders emoji and rare Unicode characters and measures or hashes how they look. Each OS and version ships a different emoji font and Unicode level, so the rendered glyph set pins the OS, its version and sometimes the vendor skin on Android. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, system traits only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Draw emoji and rare symbols out of sight Check how they look, since each syst"},"836":{"title":"Error message and stack formats","description":"Triggers errors and reads their message text and stack trace format.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-error-stack-formats","content":"<Chips /> Can trigger small errors to learn which browser engine you really use Triggers errors and reads their message text and stack trace format. Error wording and stack layout differ between V8, SpiderMonkey and JavaScriptCore, and shift per version, revealing real engine under a spoofed user agent. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, browser engine only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Cause harmless errors on purpose and look at the wording Tell Chrome, Firef"},"837":{"title":"Font enumeration by text metrics","description":"Tests hundreds of font names by comparing text width against a fallback font.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-font-enumeration-metrics","content":"<Chips /> Can test hundreds of font names to learn which fonts are on your device Tests hundreds of font names by comparing text width against a fallback font. Installed fonts reveal OS, Office or Adobe apps, languages and sometimes profession (design, dev). The list is stable and shared by few people, one of the strongest fingerprint inputs. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent, repeatable device number\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Measure text width for each fo"},"838":{"title":"Installed font detection (and Flash fonts)","description":"Measures text width to guess which fonts are installed.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-fonts","content":"<Chips /> Can measure text widths to guess which fonts are on your device Measures text width to guess which fonts are installed. Font lists reflect installed apps, languages and OS version. Flash font enumeration was another method before Flash ended in 2020. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent, repeatable device number\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Compare text width across many font names Guess which fonts you installed Learn your system, languages and sometim"},"839":{"title":"GPU timing fingerprint (DrawnApart)","description":"Times many identical GPU draw calls to expose per-chip speed differences.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-gpu-timing-drawnapart","content":"<Chips /> Can time many GPU drawing jobs to tell apart even identical graphics chips Times many identical GPU draw calls to expose per-chip speed differences. Manufacturing variation in execution units makes even identical GPU models slightly different. DrawnApart separated identical GPUs after only 150 ms of drawing and remained stable for weeks. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Hard to hide, follows your hardware\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Run many identical dra"},"840":{"title":"Intl and locale formatting quirks","description":"Reads Intl resolved options and formatting outputs for many locales and calendars.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-intl-quirks","content":"<Chips /> Can check language, calendar and number format settings to spot your browser build Reads Intl resolved options and formatting outputs for many locales and calendars. Beyond timezone and language, ICU data version, supported calendars and numbering systems differ per browser build and OS, enabling version and cloak detection. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, settings and version\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Test how your browser formats dates, numbe"},"841":{"title":"JS and Web API feature detection (version pinning)","description":"Checks which JavaScript, WebAssembly and Web APIs exist to estimate the exact browser version.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-js-engine-feature-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can test which web features exist to guess your exact browser version Checks which JavaScript, WebAssembly and Web APIs exist to estimate the exact browser version. New APIs appear on known dates, so a map of 200 to 1,000 properties pins the version within 1 to 2 releases and exposes bots, spoofed user agents and privacy modes. Client Hints and UA reduction make this the main version signal. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, browser version only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It ha"},"842":{"title":"Math function precision","description":"Evaluates Math.tan, cosh, expm1 and similar at odd inputs and compares the exact digits.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-math-precision","content":"<Chips /> Can run maths tests and use the last digits to tell your browser and chip apart Evaluates Math.tan, cosh, expm1 and similar at odd inputs and compares the exact digits. JS engines and CPU math libraries round the last bits differently, so a few results separate V8, SpiderMonkey and JavaScriptCore and sometimes the OS and CPU architecture. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, browser and chip traits\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Compute a few unusual mat"},"843":{"title":"Media codec and capability probing","description":"Asks which audio and video codecs, containers and DRM systems the browser supports.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-media-codec-probing","content":"<Chips /> Can ask which video, audio and copy protection formats your device supports Asks which audio and video codecs, containers and DRM systems the browser supports. Hardware decoders for HEVC, AV1 and DRM (Widevine, FairPlay, PlayReady) differ by OS and chip, so the map reveals device class and licensing. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, device capability list\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Ask if your device plays formats such as HEVC and AV1 Check for c"},"844":{"title":"Plugins and MIME types","description":"Lists installed plugins and supported MIME types.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-plugins-mimetypes","content":"<Chips /> Can list your browser plugins and file types, which now adds little Lists installed plugins and supported MIME types. Once a strong fingerprint. Since Chrome 94 the list is a fixed set of PDF viewers, so it now adds little. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A small, shared trait\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads List the PDF viewers your browser has List the file types it can open Use that list as one small trait of your browser List other plugins, since modern "},"845":{"title":"speechSynthesis voice list","description":"Lists the text-to-speech voices installed on the device.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-speechsynthesis-voices","content":"<Chips /> Can list the spoken voices installed on your device Lists the text-to-speech voices installed on the device. Voices reveal OS, installed languages, downloaded premium voices and Google or Microsoft cloud voices, which hints at language and accessibility use. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, voice list only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads, or when a page offers to speak text List the text to speech voices on your device Learn your system, languages and"},"846":{"title":"SVG text metrics","description":"Measures SVG text extents and glyph positions.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-svg-text-metrics","content":"<Chips /> Can measure picture text sizes to learn about your fonts and system Measures SVG text extents and glyph positions. SVG layout uses a separate text path from HTML, so metrics add fonts and rasterizer detail even under HTML-layout defenses. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent, repeatable device number\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Measure how wide and tall text drawn in pictures is Learn font and drawing differences even when normal text checks are blocke"},"847":{"title":"Anti-spoof and lie detection","description":"Detects when privacy tools or anti-detect browsers have replaced browser functions.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-tamper-lie-detection","content":"<Chips /> Can tell when a privacy tool or anti detect browser changed your browser's answers Detects when privacy tools or anti-detect browsers have replaced browser functions. Checks native toString, prototype descriptors and cross-signal consistency (WebGL renderer versus user agent). Finds noise injection, so being protected becomes a fingerprint of its own. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent check on every visit\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Check if built i"},"848":{"title":"WebGL parameters and extensions","description":"Reads the GPU vendor and model string, hardware limits and the list of supported WebGL extensions.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-webgl-parameters-extensions","content":"<Chips /> Can see your graphics card model, limits and which 3D features it supports Reads the GPU vendor and model string, hardware limits and the list of supported WebGL extensions. Exposes the exact GPU model and driver family (for example 'ANGLE (Apple, M2)'), which is a strong device class signal and reveals gaming, laptop or phone hardware. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent, hardware model\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Get the graphics chip name, for exam"},"849":{"title":"WebGL render hash","description":"Renders a fixed 3D scene with custom shaders and hashes the output pixels.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-webgl-render-hash","content":"<Chips /> Can draw a hidden 3D scene and use the pixels to tell your device apart Renders a fixed 3D scene with custom shaders and hashes the output pixels. Floating point and rasterization differences across GPU, driver and ANGLE backend produce a hash that separates devices better than the renderer string, even hardware with the same label. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent, repeatable device number\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Draw a fixed 3D scene out of s"},"850":{"title":"WebGL shader precision formats","description":"Reads float and integer precision ranges the GPU shader units support.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-webgl-shader-precision","content":"<Chips /> Can check how precise your graphics chip maths is, to guess your chip family Reads float and integer precision ranges the GPU shader units support. Mobile GPUs report different mediump and highp precision, so the numbers separate desktop, Apple, Adreno, Mali and PowerVR families. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, chip family only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Check number precision limits in the graphics chip Tell desktop, Apple, Adreno, Mali and Pow"},"851":{"title":"WebGL renderer and parameters","description":"Reads the graphics card name and GPU capability values.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-webgl","content":"<Chips /> Can see your graphics card name and its capability limits Reads the graphics card name and GPU capability values. Reveals the GPU vendor and model and a set of limits, which together identify a hardware class and often a precise device. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent, hardware model\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Get your graphics card maker and model Get a set of hardware limits Often narrow you to a small group of devices Ask permission first See "},"852":{"title":"WebGPU adapter info","description":"Asks the browser which GPU vendor and architecture WebGPU will run on.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-webgpu-adapter-info","content":"<Chips /> Can ask which graphics maker and design your device will use for 3D work Asks the browser which GPU vendor and architecture WebGPU will run on. Returns vendor, architecture and sometimes device and driver description, a cleaner GPU label than WebGL's masked string. Chrome rounds it, but features and limits still expose the hardware tier. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent, hardware label\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page uses WebGPU Get the graphics maker and ch"},"853":{"title":"WebGPU features and limits","description":"Reads which optional WebGPU features and numeric limits the GPU supports.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/render-webgpu-features-limits","content":"<Chips /> Can list which optional graphics features and limits your device supports Reads which optional WebGPU features and numeric limits the GPU supports. About 30 limits and 15 optional features (for example shader-f16, timestamp-query) form a fine-grained hardware and driver signature, usable for model-size gating and for fingerprinting. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent, hardware profile\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page uses WebGPU List about 30 hardware limits an"},"854":{"title":"Apple Pay and Private Click Measurement flags","description":"Safari-only flags: Apple Pay availability and ad attribution support.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/rendering-applepay-pcm-state","content":"<Chips /> Can check if Apple Pay is set up and if you use Safari Safari-only flags: Apple Pay availability and ad attribution support. ApplePaySession.canMakePayments reveals whether Apple Pay is set up on the device (a financial hint) and PCM support marks Safari. Both are tested by FingerprintJS. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, Safari only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Tell if Apple Pay can be used on your device Tell if ad attribution features exist, whic"},"855":{"title":"Audio base latency","description":"AudioContext.baseLatency reveals the audio stack and hardware.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/rendering-audio-base-latency","content":"<Chips /> Can measure your audio delay to guess your system and audio hardware AudioContext.baseLatency reveals the audio stack and hardware. Typical values 0.005 to 0.04 seconds depending on OS and driver (macOS about 0.01, Windows 0.01 to 0.03, Android up to 0.04). Mild hardware signal; read without playing sound. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, mild hardware clue\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Check the audio delay, usually 0.005 to 0.04 seconds Guess your"},"856":{"title":"CPU architecture via float behavior","description":"Telling ARM from x86 by how Float32 values behave in JavaScript.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/rendering-float-architecture","content":"<Chips /> Can run number tests to tell whether your chip is ARM or x86 Telling ARM from x86 by how Float32 values behave in JavaScript. FingerprintJS writes a Float32Array and reads bytes to detect endianness-like behavior. Chromium reduced UA hides CPU, but such probes can infer the architecture class anyway. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, chip family only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Store numbers in memory and check how they behave Guess your chip famil"},"857":{"title":"Math function precision","description":"Tiny differences in Math.tan, acosh, sinh and others across engines and CPUs.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/rendering-math-engine","content":"<Chips /> Can run maths tests and use the last digits to tell your browser apart Tiny differences in Math.tan, acosh, sinh and others across engines and CPUs. Results of about 10 transcendental functions differ in the last digits between V8, SpiderMonkey, JavaScriptCore and OS math libraries. Identifies engine version and sometimes CPU family. Firefox 145 made some math more uniform. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, browser engine only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page"},"858":{"title":"APFC / DNA device fingerprint (abuse prevention features)","description":"Collects about 48 device and browser traits, encrypts them, and attaches them to later requests.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Graphics and fingerprinting"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/rendering-fingerprint/tracking-linkedin-apfc-fingerprint","content":"<Chips /> Can collect about 48 device traits and send them in secret with later requests Collects about 48 device and browser traits, encrypts them, and attaches them to later requests. Builds a device fingerprint from hardware, display, locale, rendering and storage traits. Because it is encrypted with a LinkedIn key and sent as a header, people and researchers cannot easily see what leaves the browser. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Follows your device across visits\" /> **What the user does:** Nothin"},"859":{"title":"Speed and timing","description":"How fast things load and run. 29 capabilities.","url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance","content":"How fast things load and run. 29 capabilities in this group. | Capability                             | Kind          | In one line                                                        |\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Battery Status API and drain inference | Gets silently | Can see your battery level and charging state in Chromium browsers | | Capability                    | Kind              | In one line                                                             |\n| ----------------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Cache timing history sniffing | Used to track you | Can time how fast something loads to guess if you visited a site before | | Capability                                | Kind              | In one line                                                       |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Clock skew and crystal timing fingerprint | Used to track you | Can compare your device clocks over many seconds to tell it apart | | Capability           | Kind          | In one line                                                        |\n| -------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Compute Pressure API | Gets silently | Can see how busy your device's processor is, in four coarse levels | | Capability                        | Kind              | In one line                                                 |\n| --------------------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| CPU benchmarking and device class | Used to track you | Can run a short speed test to guess how fast your device is | | Capability                                          | Kind          | In one line                                                               |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Cross-origin isolation and SharedArrayBuffer clocks | Gets silently | Can use a very fine clock and shared memory, but only if the site opts in | | Capability                       | Kind          | In one line                                         |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| Display refresh rate measurement | Gets silently | Can count frames to find your screen's refresh rate | | Capability           | Kind          | In one line                                                              |\n| -------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Event Timing and INP | Gets silently | Can time how fast the page responds to your taps, clicks and key presses | | Capability                      | Kind          | In one line                                                         |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| High resolution timer precision | Gets silently | Can use a clock whose sharpness shows your browser and privacy mode | | Capability     | Kind                 | In one line                                                 |\n| -------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Idle callbacks | Works without asking | Can wait until the browser is idle to run low priority jobs | | Capability         | Kind              | In one line                                           |\n| ------------------ | ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| Keystroke dynamics | Used to track you | Can time your key presses to learn your typing rhythm | | Capability                   | Kind          | In one line                                            |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Long Animation Frames (LoAF) | Gets silently | Can see slow page frames and which scripts caused them | | Capability                       | Kind          | In one line                                           |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| measureUserAgentSpecificMemory() | Gets silently | Can check how much memory the page and its embeds use | | Capability                                                                   | Kind              | In one line                                                                    |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Microarchitectural timing attacks (Spectre, DrawnApart, cache side channels) | Used to track you | Can use very exact timing to learn how your processor or graphics chip behaves | | Capability              | Kind              | In one line                                                           |\n| ----------------------- | ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Mouse movement dynamics | Used to track you | Can track how your cursor moves on the page, to tell people from bots | | Capability                     | Kind          | In one line                                            |\n| ------------------------------ | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Navigation and Resource Timing | Gets silently | Can see how fast the page and each file loaded for you | | Capability                            | Kind          | In one line                                               "},"860":{"title":"Battery Status API and drain inference","description":"Reads battery level, charging state and time estimates in Chromium browsers.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-battery-status","content":"<Chips /> Can see your battery level and charging state in Chromium browsers Reads battery level, charging state and time estimates in Chromium browsers. Level and discharge time can short-term link visits across sites. Remaining time infers device load. Removed from Firefox and never added to Safari because of the tracking risk. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, Chromium browsers only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads, or when a page checks power use Check your b"},"861":{"title":"Cache timing history sniffing","description":"Times how fast a resource loads to guess whether the person already visited a site.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-cache-timing-history-sniffing","content":"<Chips /> Can time how fast something loads to guess if you visited a site before Times how fast a resource loads to guess whether the person already visited a site. A cached image or script loads in milliseconds, an uncached one in tens. Probing resources from other sites, or the status of a login, reveals browsing history. Cache partitioning mostly ended the cross-site variant. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Mostly blocked by split caches\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page "},"862":{"title":"Compute Pressure API","description":"Reports the device CPU load as nominal, fair, serious or critical.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-compute-pressure","content":"<Chips /> Can see how busy your device's processor is, in four coarse levels Reports the device CPU load as nominal, fair, serious or critical. A PressureObserver reports coarse CPU state and thermal throttling (four levels), limited to secure contexts and top level or permission policy frames. Replaces CPU benchmarking for adaptive apps, but exposes load state. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page asks, on a secure page Lea"},"863":{"title":"CPU benchmarking and device class","description":"Runs short compute loops to estimate how fast the device is.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-cpu-benchmark","content":"<Chips /> Can run a short speed test to guess how fast your device is Runs short compute loops to estimate how fast the device is. A 50 to 500 ms JS or WebAssembly benchmark, plus worker scaling to test the real core count, ranks a phone or laptop into speed tiers. Used for adaptive quality, bot detection (headless VMs) and as a fingerprint component. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, short speed test\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads, and it takes about 50 to 500"},"864":{"title":"Cross-origin isolation and SharedArrayBuffer clocks","description":"Sites that send COOP and COEP headers unlock finer timers and SharedArrayBuffer.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-cross-origin-isolation-timers","content":"<Chips /> Can use a very fine clock and shared memory, but only if the site opts in Sites that send COOP and COEP headers unlock finer timers and SharedArrayBuffer. Gives a page a 5 microsecond clock and shared memory, both needed for wasm threads. A worker counter on SharedArrayBuffer is a high resolution clock that also powers Spectre-class attacks. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only when the site opts in\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The site sets special page headers when it loads Get a clo"},"865":{"title":"Clock skew and crystal timing fingerprint","description":"Measures tiny clock speed differences between a device's timers.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-crystal-timing-fingerprint","content":"<Chips /> Can compare your device clocks over many seconds to tell it apart Measures tiny clock speed differences between a device's timers. Each device has slightly different oscillator frequencies. Comparing performance.now, Date, requestAnimationFrame and server timestamps over many seconds yields a device-specific drift signature, researched as time-based fingerprinting. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent, while the page stays open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens while the page s"},"866":{"title":"Event Timing and INP","description":"Measures how long clicks, taps and key presses take to produce the next frame.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-event-timing-inp","content":"<Chips /> Can time how fast the page responds to your taps, clicks and key presses Measures how long clicks, taps and key presses take to produce the next frame. Records each interaction's input delay, processing time and presentation delay (8 ms granularity) so sites can compute Interaction to Next Paint (INP), and also learns when and how fast people interact. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While you use the page\" /> **What the user does:** You tap, click or press a key on the page Measure delay from"},"867":{"title":"User activation and idle inference","description":"Tells whether the person has clicked or typed recently, and in Chromium whether they are idle.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-idle-detection-user-activation","content":"<Chips /> Can see if you recently clicked or typed, and in Chromium if you are idle Tells whether the person has clicked or typed recently, and in Chromium whether they are idle. navigator.userActivation shows if a click or key happened. IdleDetector (permission gated, Chromium) reports user active or idle and screen locked. Without it, sites infer idle from the absence of input events. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You click or type on the page. Idle"},"868":{"title":"Keystroke dynamics","description":"Records key press and release times to build a typing rhythm profile.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-keystroke-dynamics","content":"<Chips /> Can time your key presses to learn your typing rhythm Records key press and release times to build a typing rhythm profile. Dwell times (key held) and flight times (between keys) for password and free text identify people with 1 to 5% error. Persists across devices and accounts, used for continuous authentication and fraud. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Follows you across devices\" /> **What the user does:** You type in a box on the page Time how long you hold each key Time the gap between ke"},"869":{"title":"Paint timing (FCP, LCP) and Core Web Vitals","description":"Reports when content first appeared and the largest element rendered.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-lcp-paint-timing","content":"<Chips /> Can check when content first showed and how long the biggest item took Reports when content first appeared and the largest element rendered. PerformancePaintTiming and LargestContentfulPaint give render timings that reflect network, CPU and GPU. Cross-origin images need Timing-Allow-Origin for full detail. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, while the page loads\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as the page loads Get time to first content and time to the largest picture or bl"},"870":{"title":"Long Animation Frames (LoAF)","description":"Reports frames that took over 50 ms and which scripts caused them.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-long-animation-frames","content":"<Chips /> Can see slow page frames and which scripts caused them Reports frames that took over 50 ms and which scripts caused them. Shows slow frames with script URLs, durations, forced layout and blocking time. Gives site owners (and third party scripts) a view of device speed and which embedded scripts exist on the page. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, while the page runs\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as the page runs List frames that took over 50 ms Name which script or link"},"871":{"title":"measureUserAgentSpecificMemory()","description":"Asks for a breakdown of memory the page and its iframes use.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-measure-user-agent-memory","content":"<Chips /> Can check how much memory the page and its embeds use Asks for a breakdown of memory the page and its iframes use. Reports total bytes and per-origin attribution in cross-origin isolated contexts. A noisy but accurate measure of memory use, useful for leaks and for inferring device memory pressure. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The site calls it while the page is open, in a protected page mode Get the total memory the page uses Get "},"872":{"title":"Microarchitectural timing attacks (Spectre, DrawnApart, cache side channels)","description":"Uses precise timing to read CPU cache or GPU execution quirks.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-microarchitectural-timing","content":"<Chips /> Can use very exact timing to learn how your processor or graphics chip behaves Uses precise timing to read CPU cache or GPU execution quirks. Browser JS can run cache side channels (prime+probe), Spectre variants and GPU timing fingerprints (DrawnApart, 2022) that identify a physical GPU by execution unit speeds. Defended by timer coarsening and site isolation. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Hard to hide, follows hardware\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page runs, and"},"873":{"title":"Mouse movement dynamics","description":"Records cursor paths, speed and acceleration.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-mouse-dynamics","content":"<Chips /> Can track how your cursor moves on the page, to tell people from bots Records cursor paths, speed and acceleration. Movement curvature, jitter and click timing separate humans from bots and can identify repeat users. Coalesced events give up to 1000 Hz traces on gaming mice. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You move or click the mouse on the page Record cursor paths, speed and how it speeds up Time your clicks Tell humans from bots by how smoot"},"874":{"title":"Navigation and Resource Timing","description":"Reports DNS, connect, TLS and download timings for the page and each resource.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-navigation-resource-timing","content":"<Chips /> Can see how fast the page and each file loaded for you Reports DNS, connect, TLS and download timings for the page and each resource. Reveals connection speed and CDN behavior, whether a request hit the cache, protocol (h2, h3) and TLS reuse. Cross-origin detail needs the Timing-Allow-Origin header, otherwise sizes and phases are zeroed. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, while the page loads\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as the page loads Get timings for DNS, connection"},"875":{"title":"Network Information API and Save-Data","description":"Exposes estimated connection type, speed and data saver preference.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-network-information","content":"<Chips /> Can see a rough guess of your connection type, speed and data saver setting Exposes estimated connection type, speed and data saver preference. navigator.connection returns effectiveType (4g, 3g...), rtt rounded to 25 ms, downlink rounded to 25 kbps steps (max 10 Mbps) and saveData. Chrome on Android adds type (wifi, cellular); other browsers do not support it. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, rough connection info\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads, or "},"876":{"title":"Page Visibility, focus and blur","description":"Tells a page when its tab is hidden, backgrounded or unfocused.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-page-visibility-focus","content":"<Chips /> Can tell when you switch tabs, minimise the browser or lock the screen Tells a page when its tab is hidden, backgrounded or unfocused. Sites learn when the person switches tabs, minimizes or locks the screen. Used for attention tracking, exam proctoring and ad viewability, and by bot detectors that expect focus events. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You switch tabs, minimise the window or lock the screen Learn when the tab is shown or hidden "},"877":{"title":"performance.memory (legacy heap size)","description":"Reads JavaScript heap sizes in Chrome.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-performance-memory","content":"<Chips /> Can see how much memory the page uses, in Chrome and Edge Reads JavaScript heap sizes in Chrome. Exposes usedJSHeapSize, totalJSHeapSize and jsHeapSizeLimit, which cluster by device RAM and OS (about 4 GB on 64-bit desktop, 2 GB on mobile). Values are quantized. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, rough device memory\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page asks Get used, total and limit sizes for the page's memory Guess your device memory, around 4 GB on desktop and 2"},"878":{"title":"Display refresh rate measurement","description":"Counts animation frames per second to find the monitor's refresh rate.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-refresh-rate-measurement","content":"<Chips /> Can count frames to find your screen's refresh rate Counts animation frames per second to find the monitor's refresh rate. requestAnimationFrame timing reveals 60, 90, 120, 144 or 165 Hz displays, and variable refresh. Combined with devicePixelRatio and screen size it narrows the device model (e.g. ProMotion iPads and iPhones at 120 Hz). <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, screen traits only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads, or when animations run Learn i"},"879":{"title":"Server-Timing header","description":"Servers send timing metrics to the page through a response header.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-server-timing","content":"<Chips /> Can pass timing numbers from the server to page scripts Servers send timing metrics to the page through a response header. Lets a backend or CDN expose database, cache and edge timings to client JavaScript, for monitoring or for leaking server state when cross-origin readable. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Server numbers only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as the page loads Send database, cache and edge time numbers in a response header Let page code see those numbers Show s"},"880":{"title":"High resolution timer precision","description":"performance.now() returns a clock whose coarseness depends on browser and isolation.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-timer-precision","content":"<Chips /> Can use a clock whose sharpness shows your browser and privacy mode performance.now() returns a clock whose coarseness depends on browser and isolation. Timer resolution is a deliberate defense against timing attacks. The resolution itself tells a site the browser and whether privacy mode or cross-origin isolation is on, and limits attacks like cache timing. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, browser clue only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Check how f"},"881":{"title":"Scroll and touch dynamics","description":"Records scroll speed, swipe shape and touch pressure and size.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-typing-cadence-scroll","content":"<Chips /> Can track how you scroll and swipe, plus touch size and pressure Records scroll speed, swipe shape and touch pressure and size. Touch events expose contact radius, rotation and force (where hardware supports it); scroll and swipe timing is device and person specific. Used in mobile behavioral biometrics and bot checks. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"While you use the page\" /> **What the user does:** You scroll, swipe or touch the screen See how fast you scroll See the shape of your swipes See"},"882":{"title":"Visited link (:visited) history sniffing","description":"Used to detect which links the person had visited by styling :visited links.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/perf-visited-link-sniffing","content":"<Chips /> Can't find which links you visited by testing link colours anymore Used to detect which links the person had visited by styling :visited links. Browsers lie to getComputedStyle for :visited since 2010, but timing, scroll and rendering side channels remained. Chrome 136 partitions :visited links, ending cross-site history probing. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked in current browsers\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Chrome 136 and other browsers already block it Show visited links in "},"883":{"title":"Page Lifecycle and back/forward cache","description":"Tells a page when it is frozen, discarded or restored from the back/forward cache.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/timing-page-lifecycle-bfcache","content":"<Chips /> Can tell when your tab sleeps, is discarded or returns from the Back button Tells a page when it is frozen, discarded or restored from the back/forward cache. A site can see when a tab goes to sleep, was discarded to free memory, or came back instantly from the back button, which reveals how you navigate and how much memory the device has. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open or asleep\" /> **What the user does:** You switch away, leave the tab idle, or press Back Learn when a"},"884":{"title":"Reporting API (crash, CSP and deprecation reports)","description":"Sends the browser's own error, crash and policy violation reports to a site endpoint.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/timing-reporting-api","content":"<Chips /> Can get the browser's crash and error reports sent to the site Sends the browser's own error, crash and policy violation reports to a site endpoint. A site can learn when its page crashes, runs out of memory, hits a blocked resource or uses a deprecated feature on your device, and gets those reports even after the page closed. No prompt is shown. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Silent, even after you leave\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens if the page crashes or breaks a site rul"},"885":{"title":"requestAnimationFrame timing","description":"Runs code before each screen refresh, and reveals the display's refresh rate.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/timing-request-animation-frame","content":"<Chips /> Can run code before each screen refresh and learn your refresh rate Runs code before each screen refresh, and reveals the display's refresh rate. A site can learn your monitor's refresh rate (60, 120, 144 Hz) by counting frames, and can tell when the tab is hidden because callbacks stop. It is the core of smooth animation. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is visible\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page shows animation Run smooth animations and games Count"},"886":{"title":"Idle callbacks","description":"Runs low priority work when the browser is idle; no prompt.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/timing-request-idle-callback","content":"<Chips /> Can wait until the browser is idle to run low priority jobs Runs low priority work when the browser is idle; no prompt. A site can wait until the page is quiet before running analytics or prefetching. It also reveals roughly how busy your device is, since idle windows are shorter on slow devices or busy tabs. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Silent, only when idle\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads and the browser is quiet Run analytics or preload files when thi"},"887":{"title":"Timestamp seed (tsSeed)","description":"Records the browser's clock value when collection starts.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/timing-timestamp-seed","content":"<Chips /> Can note your browser's clock when it starts collecting data Records the browser's clock value when collection starts. Time value used to order events and to check clock drift against the server. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One clock value, one moment\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when collection starts on the page Save the current clock value Put events in order Check for clock drift against the server Know your exact location Ask permission first See your files Know who"},"888":{"title":"User Timing marks and measures","description":"Lets a page stamp named points in time and measure the gaps between them.","breadcrumbs":["Websites (7 browsers)","Speed and timing"],"url":"/docs/capabilities/web/timing-performance/timing-user-timing","content":"<Chips /> Can mark points in time and measure gaps between them inside the page Lets a page stamp named points in time and measure the gaps between them. A site can time its own code in detail and send the numbers home, so it learns how fast the person's device and network really are. The marks live only in this tab and are not shared across sites. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as the page runs Stamp named moments in its own code M"},"889":{"title":"Contributing","description":"Fork, edit or add one JSON file, validate it, and open a pull request.","url":"/docs/contributing","content":"The best contributions are small: fix a fact, add a missing browser version, add a source, or write a new capability. You do not need to write code. You only edit JSON. Fork ever-just/platform-ontology on GitHub and clone your fork. Every capability page on this site ends with an **Edit this data on GitHub** link that opens the exact file. To add a new capability, create a file at `data/<space>/<group>/<slug>.json` where the slug is the id without its `mob.` or `web.` prefix. The easiest start is to copy a neighbor in the same group and change the values. Run the build in strict mode. It checks the schema, ids, folders, links between capabilities and the writing rules, and it stops with an error if anything is off. Push your branch and open a pull request. Say what you changed and add a source link for any new fact. A reviewer will check the facts, not your formatting. A stable `id` that starts with `mob.` or `web.` and matches the file name. A `kind` and `group` from the data model, and a folder that matches the group. Shared facts once at the top level. Per platform facts only under `platforms.android`, `platforms.ios` or `platforms.web`. `limits.can` and `limits.cannot` with 2 to 5 short items each, and a `reach` from 1 to 4. At least one entry in `refs`, with a link to official documentation or a spec, and an honest `confidence`. Links in `related` and `web_equivalents` that point to ids that exist. Plain language is the point of this project. Write for someone who has never read a spec. Use short sentences and everyday words. Explain a term the first time you use it. Say what a person sees and what an app can do, not how the API is shaped. **Never use em dashes or en dashes.** Rewrite the sentence with a period, comma, colon or parentheses. Write ranges as \"7 to 15\". The build rejects dashes in data files. Use an ordinary hyphen only inside a real compound word, such as \"one-click\". Keep each item in `can` and `cannot` to one idea. Be neutral about abuse. Describe what is possible and what was reported, with a source. Do not copy long text from other sources. Summarize and link. Not ready to edit? Open an issue with the capability id, what is wrong and a source if you have one."},"890":{"title":"Data model","description":"Every field, the 7 kinds, 24 groups, lifecycle stages, reach levels, scores and status codes.","url":"/docs/data-model","content":"Every capability is one JSON object checked by `schema/capability.schema.json`. This page explains each field and every fixed list of values. | Field | What it holds                                                                                |\n| ----- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `id`  | Stable id. `mob.*` for native apps (Android and iPhone), `web.*` for websites. Never reused. | | Field  | What it holds       |\n| ------ | ------------------- |\n| `name` | A short plain name. | | Field   | What it holds                        |\n| ------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| `plain` | One plain sentence about what it is. | | Field     | What it holds                       |\n| --------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| `meaning` | Why it matters, in a few sentences. | | Field  | What it holds             |\n| ------ | ------------------------- |\n| `kind` | One of the 7 kinds below. | | Field               | What it holds                                             |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `group`, `subgroup` | One of the 24 groups below, plus a finer label inside it. | | Field   | What it holds                          |\n| ------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| `space` | `mobile` or `web`. Matches the folder. | | Field  | What it holds       |\n| ------ | ------------------- |\n| `tags` | Short search words. | | Field       | What it holds                                           |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `lifecycle` | When it happens. A list of stages from the table below. | | Field       | What it holds                                  |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `platforms` | The facts that differ per platform. See below. | | Field  | What it holds                                                                                                    |\n| ------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `data` | What comes back: `type`, `metric`, `update` rate and a `fields` list with name, type, unit, range and precision. | | Field    | What it holds                                                                                    |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `limits` | The plain bounds: `headline`, `user_action`, `can`, `cannot`, `reach`, `reach_label`, `differs`. | | Field   | What it holds                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `story` | Reader friendly text: `what_it_is`, a Lucide `icon` name, `for_you`, `for_makers`, `use_cases`, `risks`, vendor `marketing` links and short `android_note` and `ios_note`. | | Field              | What it holds                                                                 |\n| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `technical_bounds` | Hard technical limits, for example \"Only works on cameras that advertise it\". | | Field    | What it holds                                                |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `scores` | `sensitivity`, `utility`, `friction` and `fingerprint_bits`. | | Field   | What it holds                   |\n| ------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `value` | `legit_uses` and known `abuse`. | | Field        | What it holds                                                                |\n| ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `case_study` | A real example. Currently a LinkedIn note when the capability is used there. | | Field     | What it holds                                                                        |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `history` | Dated platform changes, each with `date`, the OS or browser, `version` and `change`. | | Field     | What it holds                |\n| --------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `related` | Ids of related capabilities. | | Field             | What it holds                                           |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `web_equivalents` | Ids of the same idea on the other side (native or web). | | Field  | What it holds                                         |\n| ------ | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| `refs` | Sources, each with a type `t`, a `label` and a `url`. | | Field        | What it holds                                                      |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `confidence` | `verified`, `docs` or `inferred`. How well the facts were checked. | For `mob.*` capabilities, `platforms.android` and `platforms.ios` hold: | Field | Meaning                 |\n| ----- | ----------------------- |\n| `s`   | Status code, see below. | | Field | Meaning                       |\n| ----- | ----------------------------- |\n| `v`   | First OS version that has it. | | Field | Meaning                           |\n| ----- | --------------------------------- |\n| `api` | Android API level (Android only). | | Field        | Meaning                                               |\n| ------------ | ----------------------------------------------------- |\n| `permission` | The permission string, entitlement or Info.plist key. | | Field        | Meaning                                   "},"891":{"title":"Getting started","description":"Download the JSON bundles and use them in JavaScript or Python.","url":"/docs/getting-started","content":"The whole map is published as plain JSON files. No account, no key, no rate limit beyond normal web courtesy. Pick the bundle that fits your job. | Bundle     | URL                                               | What is in it                                            |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Everything | `https://platformontology.lol/data/ontology.json` | All 844 capabilities with every field, plus the taxonomy | | Bundle      | URL                                            | What is in it                                                                             |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Light index | `https://platformontology.lol/data/index.json` | One short row per capability: id, name, kind, group, headline, reach, status per platform | | Bundle   | URL                                               | What is in it                                                                   |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Taxonomy | `https://platformontology.lol/data/taxonomy.json` | Labels, icons and descriptions for kinds, groups, lifecycle stages and browsers | | Bundle  | URL                                                       | What is in it                                                        |\n| ------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Android | `https://platformontology.lol/data/platform/android.json` | The 361 native capabilities, with the Android block under `platform` | | Bundle | URL                                                   | What is in it                                                       |\n| ------ | ----------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| iPhone | `https://platformontology.lol/data/platform/ios.json` | The 361 native capabilities, with the iPhone block under `platform` | | Bundle | URL                                                   | What is in it                                                   |\n| ------ | ----------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Web    | `https://platformontology.lol/data/platform/web.json` | The 483 web capabilities, with browser support under `platform` | Every bundle starts with the same small header: `name`, `version`, `built`, `license`, `homepage` and `count`. Load `index.json` first. It is much smaller than `ontology.json`, and it has enough to build a list or a search box. Fetch the full bundle only when you need details. In the full bundle each capability is one object. In a platform bundle the `platforms` field is replaced by a single `platform` block for that platform. Read the data model for what every field means. Clone the repository and run the build. It validates every file first, so it also works as a check on your own edits. The bundles land in `dist/`. There are no dependencies to install. Each capability has a stable `id`. Use it in your own data so you can always find the page again. For example, the page for `mob.app.associated-domains` lives under capabilities in the app group, and the simulator opens it at `/lab/#mob-app-associated-domains` (dots become hyphens)."},"892":{"title":"How the data is stored","description":"One JSON file per capability, folders that carry meaning, and a build step that compiles per platform bundles.","url":"/docs/how-data-is-stored","content":"The data is plain files in a Git repository. Nothing hides behind a database, so every change is a normal pull request that anyone can read. <Card title=\"One file per capability\">Small files make reviews easy and merge conflicts rare.</Card> <Card title=\"Folders carry meaning\">The path tells you the space and the group before you open the file.</Card> <Card title=\"Shared once, different per platform\">A concept is never duplicated across operating systems.</Card> <Card title=\"Bundles are built\">A build step validates everything and writes ready to use JSON.</Card> Every capability lives in its own file: `space` is `mobile` or `web`. `group` is a key from `data/taxonomy.json`, for example `camera-mic-screen`. `slug` is the `id` without its `mob.` or `web.` prefix. The id `web.camera.camera-ptz` is stored as `data/web/camera-mic-screen/camera.camera-ptz.json`. The build checks that the folder, the `space` field and the `group` field agree, so a file can never sit in the wrong place. A native capability such as precise location is one idea on Android and iPhone. Its name, meaning, data fields, limits and history are stored **once**, at the top level of the file. Only what truly differs sits in per platform blocks: This is a simplified example: Web capabilities keep one support record per browser under `platforms.web.support`. A native capability and its web twin are connected with `web_equivalents`. Labels, icons and descriptions for kinds, groups, lifecycle stages and browsers live in `data/taxonomy.json`. Renaming a label never touches the 844 capability files. `scripts/build-data.mjs` reads every file, validates it, and writes `dist/`: | Output               | What it is                    |\n| -------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `dist/ontology.json` | Everything, plus the taxonomy | | Output            | What it is                               |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| `dist/index.json` | A light list for search boxes and tables | | Output               | What it is              |\n| -------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| `dist/taxonomy.json` | Labels and descriptions | | Output                       | What it is                                                            |\n| ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `dist/platform/android.json` | Native capabilities with the Android block flattened under `platform` | | Output                   | What it is                                                           |\n| ------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `dist/platform/ios.json` | Native capabilities with the iPhone block flattened under `platform` | | Output                   | What it is                                             |\n| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `dist/platform/web.json` | Web capabilities with browser support under `platform` | The website serves them under `/data/`. See Getting started for the URLs. The build also checks that ids are unique, that every `related` and `web_equivalents` link points to a real capability, that groups and kinds exist, and that no text contains an em or en dash. Run it with `STRICT=1` and any problem stops the build with a non zero exit code. `schema/capability.schema.json` describes one capability. Point your editor at it to get completion and live errors while you edit a file: This site is generated from the same files. A script reads each JSON file and writes one page per capability, so the docs can never drift from the data. Every page ends with a link back to the file on GitHub."},"893":{"title":"Platform.Ontology","description":"An open, plain language map of what apps and websites can do on your phone and in your browser.","url":"/docs","content":"Platform.Ontology is an open map of **844 capabilities**: the things an app or a website can do, get, or ask for on a phone or in a browser. Each one has a plain language page that says what it is, what it can and cannot reach, what the user sees, and how Android, iPhone and the web differ. **361 native app capabilities** cover Android and iPhone side by side on one page, so you see the same idea on both systems without reading two lists. **483 web capabilities** cover 7 browsers: Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari, Safari on iPhone, Chrome on Android and Samsung Internet. **24 groups** sort them, from location and camera to payments, tracking and on-device AI. **7 kinds** say how each one behaves: asks first, you pick, works without asking, does something, gets silently, used to track you, or blocked. <Card title=\"Simulator\" href=\"/lab/\">Try a capability on a pretend phone or browser and see what a site really gets.</Card> <Card title=\"Wiki\" href=\"/wiki/\">Browse the same map as a plain language wiki, one page per capability.</Card> <Card title=\"Browse all 844 capabilities\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/\">Every capability with limits, platform tables, history and sources.</Card> <Card title=\"Use the data\" href=\"/docs/getting-started/\">Download the JSON bundles and use them in your own project.</Card> <Card title=\"Curious people\">Find out what an app can actually see before you tap Allow.</Card> <Card title=\"Makers and designers\">Check what you can ask for, what users will see, and which platforms support it.</Card> <Card title=\"Privacy and security teams\">Compare sensitivity, reach and known abuse across Android, iPhone and the web.</Card> <Card title=\"Researchers and tool builders\">Pull clean, validated JSON and build on it. The data is CC BY 4.0.</Card> Start with the one line summary and the reach meter. The meter runs from 1 (one thing, one moment) to 4 (broad, anytime). Then read what it can and cannot do, and open the platform table for the exact permission names, prompts and first versions. New here? Read Getting started to grab the data. Want the vocabulary? Read the data model. Curious how files are laid out? Read how the data is stored. Want to fix or add something? Read contributing."},"894":{"title":"License","description":"Code is MIT. The data is CC BY 4.0.","url":"/docs/license","content":"Platform.Ontology is open source. There are two licenses, one for the code and one for the data. | Part | License | What it covers                                                     |\n| ---- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Code | MIT     | Build scripts, the simulator, the wiki and this documentation site | | Part | License   | What it covers                                                     |\n| ---- | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Data | CC BY 4.0 | Everything under `data/`, `schema/` and the published JSON bundles | You may copy, share and build on the data, including for commercial work, as long as you give credit. A good credit line looks like this: > Data from Platform.Ontology (https://platformontology.lol), licensed CC BY 4.0. Link back to the site or to a specific capability page when you can. If you change the data, say so. The MIT license lets you use, copy, modify and distribute the code with very few limits. Keep the copyright notice and license text with any copy. Capability pages link to the official documentation, specifications and research they are based on. Those sources keep their own licenses and copyrights. Names such as Android, iPhone, Chrome, Safari and Firefox belong to their owners and are used here only to describe what their products do."},"895":{"title":"Platforms","description":"Android, iPhone and the web, with how many capabilities of each kind they cover.","url":"/docs/platforms","content":"The map covers three platforms. Native apps are described once for Android and iPhone together. Websites are described once with support for 7 browsers. <Card title=\"Android\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/\">361 capabilities. Permissions, protection levels, API levels, Settings paths and Play policy.</Card> <Card title=\"iPhone (iOS)\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/mobile/\">361 capabilities. Info.plist keys, entitlements, prompts, Settings paths and App Store policy.</Card> <Card title=\"Web browsers\" href=\"/docs/capabilities/web/\">483 capabilities. Support in Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari, Safari on iPhone, Chrome on Android and Samsung Internet.</Card> | Kind                          | What it means             | Android and iPhone | Web |\n| ----------------------------- | ------------------------- | ------------------ | --- |\n| Asks you first (`permission`) | A pop up asks for your OK | 66                 | 55  | | Kind                              | What it means              | Android and iPhone | Web |\n| --------------------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------ | --- |\n| You pick what to share (`picker`) | You choose the exact items | 6                  | 7   | | Kind                                | What it means                   | Android and iPhone | Web |\n| ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------- | ------------------ | --- |\n| Works without asking (`capability`) | A useful feature with no pop up | 151                | 107 | | Kind                                     | What it means        | Android and iPhone | Web |\n| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------ | --- |\n| Does something on your device (`action`) | Makes the device act | 7                  | 37  | | Kind                     | What it means                    | Android and iPhone | Web |\n| ------------------------ | -------------------------------- | ------------------ | --- |\n| Gets silently (`signal`) | A detail apps get with no prompt | 72     