e user does:** Nothing. It happens when you touch the screen, and the touch count is checked on load See whe"],[798,"<Chips /> Can see your game controller after you press a button, and make it rumble Reads buttons and sticks of a connected game controller; can trigger rumble. Shows that a controller exists and exactly how it is being used. Controller id strings leak make and model. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You press any button on a connected controller while the page is open. See which buttons you press and how far you move the sticks Make a controller rumble "],[501,"<Chips /> Can see your GPU maker, design family, and graphics limits through WebGPU GPU vendor, architecture, features and limits through WebGPU. adapter.info gives vendor and architecture (nvidia, ampere); Chrome blanks device and description by default. Limits (maxTextureDimension2D 8192 or 16384) and the feature list still tier the GPU. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"GPU tier, silently\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a page checks for WebGPU See the GPU vendor and architecture, "],[500,"<Chips /> Can see your graphics card and driver name through WebGL Reads the graphics card and driver name through WebGL. Exposes GPU model and driver (ANGLE (NVIDIA, GeForce RTX 4070 ...)). Identifies the device tier and OS. Safari reports a generic Apple GPU; Brave and Firefox spoof or trim it. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"GPU name, silently\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a page draws 3D graphics or tests for it See your GPU make and model, like an NVIDIA GeForce See your driv"],[848,"<Chips /> Can see your graphics card model, limits and which 3D features it supports Reads the GPU vendor and model string, hardware limits and the list of supported WebGL extensions. Exposes the exact GPU model and driver family (for example 'ANGLE (Apple, M2)'), which is a strong device class signal and reveals gaming, laptop or phone hardware. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent, hardware model\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Get the graphics chip name, for exam"],[851,"<Chips /> Can see your graphics card name and its capability limits Reads the graphics card name and GPU capability values. Reveals the GPU vendor and model and a set of limits, which together identify a hardware class and often a precise device. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Silent, hardware model\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads Get your graphics card maker and model Get a set of hardware limits Often narrow you to a small group of devices Ask permission first See "],[149,"<Chips /> Can see your heart rate and similar body signals while the app is open Asks to read heart rate and similar body sensor data while the app is in use. Heart rate and related vitals reveal fitness, stress and health conditions. Android 16 replaces this permission with granular health permissions for apps targeting 36. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the app is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Start workout or Measure heart rate and then Allow See live heart rate from the phone or a "],[188,"<Chips /> Can see your language list and region, with no prompt Reads the person's ordered language list and region with no prompt. Tells native language, diaspora, expat status and often nationality. Apps localize with it; ad tech uses it for targeting and as a fingerprint bit. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Your language settings\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app or page loads Get your ordered list of languages (usually up to about 5) Find your region and country format Sho"],[150,"<Chips /> Can see your medical records, such as conditions and allergies, if you share them Reads medical records such as conditions, allergies and immunizations in FHIR form. Lab results, diagnoses and vaccines are among the most sensitive data. Both platforms use separate permissions. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Medical records you share\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect health records and approve a separate sheet See conditions, allergies and immunizations you choose to share Use record"],[134,"<Chips /> Can see your own music library and play your songs once you tap Allow Reads the person's own music library and listening history. Reveals taste, play counts and playlists; on iOS it also gates Apple Music playback control. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your music library, once allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow when a music or player app asks for your library List songs and playlists in your music library Play your music inside the app Use Apple Music playback on iPhone after "],[209,"<Chips /> Can see your phone's home network address and DNS servers, with limits in Android 17 Reads the phone's LAN addresses and DNS servers; Android 17 adds a local network permission. LAN IP and DNS often reveal the router and network type; local network access also lets apps scan other devices at home (50 Ways to Leak Your Data). <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Home network info, with limits\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing, or you tap Allow when the app asks to find devices on your network See "],[110,"<Chips /> Can see your phone's system version and security patch date, with no prompt Reads OS version, API level and security patch date with no prompt. Shows how up to date the phone is and its exposure to known bugs. A strong fingerprint bit and a fraud and compatibility signal. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Always available, simple facts\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it opens, often to show an update message See your Android version, its build level and the security patc"],[553,"<Chips /> Can see your pinch zoom level and when the on screen keyboard pops up visualViewport reports pinch-zoom scale and on-screen keyboard offsets. Scale above 1 tells the person is zoomed in (accessibility); height shrink exposes the on-screen keyboard. Updates live as the user pinches or types. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You pinch to zoom, or tap a box that opens the keyboard. Check your current pinch zoom scale See changes to the visible are"],[692,"<Chips /> Can see your rough connection speed and delay in each request, if it asks first Server-side headers with the effective connection type, RTT and downlink. Same numbers as the Network Information API but delivered on requests so the server can adapt before sending HTML. Requires Accept-CH opt-in, with the same 25 ms and 25 kbps rounding. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Repeats on every request\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens after the site opts in, on each page load Receive speed"],[112,"<Chips /> Can see your screen refresh rate and whether it shows HDR, with no prompt Reads the screen refresh rate and HDR support with no prompt. 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz plus HDR format list separates device tiers and models. Used for animation pacing. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Always available, simple facts\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Games and video apps check when they start See the refresh rate, such as 60, 90, 120 or 144 Hz See which refresh rate options your screen supports on Android"],[114,"<Chips /> Can see your screen size, sharpness and scale, with no prompt Reads pixel size, density and scale factor with no prompt. Identifies the device model almost uniquely on iPhone and narrows it on Android. Needed for layout; also a long-lived fingerprint field. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Always available, simple facts\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The app checks when it opens, to lay out its screens See screen width and height, in pixels on Android and points on iPhone See sharpness, 1"],[282,"<Chips /> Can see your screen when you call the assistant, if it is your chosen one Lets the assistant see what is on screen to answer questions. The system can capture the screen, or a camera view, and hand it to an assistant. Apps can opt out of capture on Android and iOS. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only when you ask the assistant\" /> **What the user does:** You set an assistant as default, or use Circle to Search or Visual Intelligence Look at the screen content to answer a question about it Sha"],[554,"<Chips /> Can see your screen width and height, and the usable area, in page units The monitor or phone screen size in CSS pixels, plus the usable area. Identifies device model families (iPhone 390x844) and monitor setups (1920x1080, 2560x1440, 3440x1440). Safari 26 and Firefox protections spoof or round it for known scripts; Firefox RFP returns the window size. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Size numbers, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads. Check the screen's"],[529,"<Chips /> Can see your screen's color depth and how sharp or zoomed it is Reads bits per pixel and the device pixel ratio (zoom or Retina factor). Pixel ratio shows Retina screens and browser zoom. Together with resolution it identifies display class. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Two numbers, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads or you zoom. Check the color depth number Check how many real pixels sit behind each page pixel (Retina or zoom) Notice when you zoom"],[533,"<Chips /> Can see your screen's sharpness factor, which also shifts when you zoom How many physical pixels per CSS pixel; also changes with browser zoom. Values like 1, 1.25, 1.5, 2, 2.625, 3 reveal retina screens, Windows scaling and the person's zoom level (50% to 500%). Zoom shifts DPR so it leaks accessibility settings. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One number, no prompt\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads or you zoom. Check how many real pixels sit behind each page"],[196,"<Chips /> Can see your steps and whether you walk, run, drive or cycle, after you allow Reads step counts and whether the person is walking, running, driving or cycling. Reveals daily routine, commute mode and exercise habits, and works as a coarse movement log even without location. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your movement, while allowed\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow for Motion and Fitness (iPhone) or Physical activity (Android) Count your steps and walking distance Tell whether you wa"],[185,"<Chips /> Can see your text size setting, which may hint at age or vision needs Reads the system text size setting, which hints at age or low vision. Large text settings correlate with age and visual impairment, a sensitive inference the person never chose to share. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One size setting\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app lays out text Check how large your system text is Make its own text larger or smaller to match Use the size as one clue in a device"],[190,"<Chips /> Can see your timezone and its offset from UTC, with no prompt Reads the timezone and UTC offset with no prompt. A coarse location proxy (about 400 IANA zones; usually the country or region) that survives a location permission denial. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A timezone name\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app shows dates and times Find your timezone name, such as America/Chicago See your offset from UTC in minutes Show times in your local zone See your exact loc"],[346,"<Chips /> Can see your whole address book once you tap Allow, or only some on iPhone Lets the app read the whole address book. Exposes names, numbers, emails, and who a person knows. Apps use it to match friends, which also lets them upload the entire social graph of the person's family and colleagues. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Everything in your contacts\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Find friends or Match my contacts List names, numbers and emails Upload the full list to match friends Take "],[224,"<Chips /> Can see your Wi-Fi name, but only with location access and extra approval Reads the name and ID of the Wi-Fi network the phone is on, which gives away place. SSID and BSSID are lookup keys for geolocation databases, so apps need location permission to read them. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Your Wi-Fi name, with location\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow Location when the app asks to see your Wi-Fi See the Wi-Fi name (SSID) and BSSID on Android after Location is allowed See the curr"],[339,"<Chips /> Can see, add and change every event in all your calendars once you allow full access Reads and edits every event in the person's calendars. Calendars show meetings, attendees, travel, doctor visits and routines. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"All your events, anytime\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Allow Full Access when the app asks about your calendar List meetings, places and attendees Change or delete events that already exist Add new events Check your calendar again later without ask"],[311,"<Chips /> Can sell digital items and subscriptions, after you approve each purchase Sells digital goods and subscriptions through the platform store. The platform handles payment, receipts and refunds, and takes a commission. The store knows who buys what in every app. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"One purchase at a time\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Buy or Subscribe and confirm with Face ID, a fingerprint or a passcode Charge you through the App Store or Google Play after you confirm Offer subs"],[784,"<Chips /> Can sell in app items in a Play Store web app through Google Play Lets a Play Store web app sell in app items through Google Play. Lists products and past purchases of the account inside a TWA; needs Play account. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One purchase, once you approve\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Buy on an in app item inside a Play Store web app. Show the items for sale and their prices from Google Play Open the Google Play payment sheet Check which items you already own Charge "],[404,"<Chips /> Can send a scrambled version of your email to LinkedIn to match you to a member The tag hashes an email address in the browser and sends the hash to LinkedIn to match a member. Turns an anonymous visitor into a known LinkedIn member when their email is on the advertiser's form. SHA-256 is deterministic, so the hash is still an identifier. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your email's hash, once matched\" /> **What the user does:** You type or autofill your email on a site that uses the LinkedIn "],[451,"<Chips /> Can send a small report that the browser delivers even as you leave the page Sends a small POST that the browser delivers even while the person is leaving the page. Lets analytics report time on page, scroll depth and exit clicks after the tab is closed or navigated away. No response is readable. The person cannot see or stop it, and it carries cookies for the target origin. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"A last report, as you leave\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you clo"],[255,"<Chips /> Can send alerts to an Android phone through Google, up to 4 KB per message Google's push channel: 4 KB payload, up to 4 weeks held, delivered through Play services. A server can nudge an Android install with small data. Limits shape what a marketer can do: little payload, and queued messages expire or collapse, so apps fetch the real content after the nudge. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, whenever the app's server sends\" /> **What the user does:** You allow notifications on Android 1"],[250,"<Chips /> Can send alerts to an iPhone through Apple, up to 4 KB of data per message Apple's push channel: 4 KB payload (5 KB VoIP), priority 10 or 5, optional expiry and collapse id. Apple holds only the latest push per collapse id and can drop or delay low priority ones. The company learns delivery only as best effort, so apps pair pushes with a fetch on open. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Ongoing, whenever the app's server sends\" /> **What the user does:** You allow notifications when the app asks,"],[805,"<Chips /> Can send and get notes with music gear, after you allow it Lets a site send and receive MIDI messages to music gear. Exposes the names of connected MIDI ports and live note and control data from keyboards and pads. With sysex it can reconfigure or flash devices. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect keyboard and allow MIDI access in the pop up. Listen for notes from your keyboard or controller Send notes and lights to your music gear"],[220,"<Chips /> Can send and receive shared messages to many devices on your local network Sends and receives multicast and broadcast traffic on the local network. Needed for custom device discovery; also a way to enumerate LAN devices, so Apple gates it by approval. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Local network broadcasts\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing, or you use device discovery or casting inside the app Listen for broadcast announcements on the local network Find devices that announce themselves (pri"],[411,"<Chips /> Can send batches of your clicks and device traits from LinkedIn pages to LinkedIn A single endpoint receives batched interaction and fingerprint events from the page. Carries tracking events including AedEvent, SpectroscopyEvent and the encrypted fingerprint in batches, so one request can hold many data points. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Your activity on LinkedIn\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens as you use LinkedIn Collect your clicks and other page interactions Send them i"],[773,"<Chips /> Can send data straight between two browsers, with no pop up Sends data directly between browsers without a server, with no prompt. A site can connect two people peer to peer. Setup exchanges network candidates, which can expose the person's public IP address to the other peer or to the site. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"While the page is open\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing for data. A video call asks for camera permission only when you use it. Send text, files or game moves straight to"],[760,"<Chips /> Can send data to a server as you leave or close the page Sends data after the person leaves or closes the page, with no prompt. A site can finish sending analytics even as the tab closes. It is the standard way for page leave metrics, and the reason tab closing does not stop data from being sent. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"At the moment you leave\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when you close the tab, switch away or tap a link. Send a small report to its server when you le"],[575,"<Chips /> Can send messages between open tabs and frames of the same site Lets tabs and frames of the same site send messages to each other instantly. A site can see that the same person has several tabs open and sync a login, logout or shopping state. Since partitioning, a tracker embed can no longer message its own copy on a different top site. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Only tabs of the same site\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens while you have the same site open in more than one t"],[304,"<Chips /> Can send or receive content through the share menu, after you pick where it goes Receives or sends content through the system share menu. Any app can offer to take shared files or text. The system shows suggested people, so the app can learn who the sender usually shares with. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only what you share, one time\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Share and choose an app or person Send a link, picture or file to another app Receive things other apps share with it Show"],[277,"<Chips /> Can send quiet notifications without asking first, which you can keep or turn off Delivers notifications quietly without asking first, for the person to keep or turn off. Inverts the prompt: app earns trust by showing value first. Android 13 prompts but defaults channels. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Quiet alerts only, until you choose\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Your 