et by the site's own server. The vendor can still receive IP, user agent, hashed email and click IDs the server chooses to forward. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"What the site chooses to pass on\" /> **What the user doe"],[36,"<Chips /> Can send your app activity to Meta and match it to your account Meta's SDK logs installs and in-app events and can send hashed email or phone to match people. Ties activity to a Meta account for ads. Can send hashed email or phone (advanced matching). Logs events even for signed-out Facebook users. <Reach level=\"4\" label=\"Ongoing, tied to a Meta account\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the app opens or you do something in it Send installs and in app actions, such as"],[753,"<Chips /> Can send your current tab to another page by itself Sends the current tab to a different page by script, meta refresh or server redirect. A site can move the person to another URL at any time, including after they click something. Cross-origin iframes get tighter limits, which is why ad frames cannot hijack the whole tab. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"The tab it is running in\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens on load, after a timer, or when you submit a form. Send you to a new "],[421,"<Chips /> Can send your purchases and sign ups to ad platforms from the advertiser's server Advertisers send purchase and signup events from their server straight to ad platforms. Meta, TikTok, LinkedIn, Snap and Google accept server events with hashed email, phone, IP and click IDs, bypassing browser blocking. Meta dedups with event\\_id inside 48 hours. The person sees nothing and the browser cannot stop it. <Reach level=\"3\" label=\"Every event the company sends\" /> **What the user does:** Nothi"],[400,"<Chips /> Can send your purchases and sign ups to LinkedIn from a company's servers Advertisers send purchase and lead events straight from their servers with hashed identifiers. Moves tracking off the browser where blockers and cookie 