ecord the other side of a phone call. Android narrowed call recording over time (mic limits in 9, VOICE\\_CALL source blocked in 10). iOS never allowed it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked for normal apps\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The phone blocks normal apps from call audio Use the microphone when you allow it, with limits during calls on Android 9 and later Tell that a call is active thro"],[50,"<Chips /> Can't run ad tools in a separate box on Android, since Google retired it Would have run ad SDKs in their own process so they could not read the host app's data. Isolation of third-party SDKs from host app memory and identifiers. Never reached general availability; retired in the Oct 2025 announcement. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Retired\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It was a background feature that never reached wide release Have kept ad tools away from the app's data (Android 14, n"],[445,"<Chips /> Can't run ads in Chrome based on your interests, since the plan was retired Browser run ad auctions for retargeting. Retirement announced Oct 2025; removal rolling out to 100% in Oct 2026. Let advertisers store interest groups in the browser and run an on-device auction, keeping the audience list local. Joins fell almost 100x per the Chrome team. Chrome 144 deprecated it; staged removal on stable reaches 100% from Oct 26 2026. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Retired\" /> **What the user does:**"],[245,"<Chips /> Can't run as much in the background if you rarely open the app Android sorts apps into five buckets that cap job time, alarms and network by how recently they were used. Rarely opened apps get tiny background budgets, so a retention team sees its sync silently slow down for dormant users. Active apps in the top bucket have almost none. <Reach level=\"2\" label=\"Limits grow tighter if unused\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Android sorts apps into groups by how recently you opened the"],[505,"<Chips /> Can't scan for nearby Bluetooth devices without a picker you approve Sites cannot scan for nearby Bluetooth devices without a picker you approve. Web Bluetooth (Chromium) requires a user gesture, a chooser prompt and a service filter; the page only gets devices the person picks. Silent scanning does not exist. Safari and Firefox do not support Web Bluetooth. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One device you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Connect device and pick one from the browser list"],[704,"<Chips /> Can't schedule a notification for later without a server; no browser ships it Scheduled local notifications that fire without a server; not shipped. Would have let a site schedule notifications for a future time without push. Chrome ran an origin trial and then dropped it, so no browser ships it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Not available today\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Chrome tried an experiment, then dropped it Show a normal notification when a server sends one, which is a diff"],[513,"<Chips /> Can't see exact free disk space, only a fuzzy storage quota Exact free disk space is hidden; a fuzzy quota estimate leaks. navigator.storage.estimate().quota is about 60% of disk in Chrome and Safari 17, with noise. Exact free space and total disk size are not exposed; quota gives bits of a size class (256 GB vs 512 GB vs 1 TB). <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A rough quota only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when a page checks how much it can store See a storage quota, about "],[524,"<Chips /> Can't see how bright your screen is set A site cannot read how bright your screen is set. No API exposes backlight level. Proxies: prefers-color-scheme, dynamic-range and Screen Wake Lock state. Ambient light sensor was removed from stable browsers (Chrome behind a flag, Firefox 122 removed it) for fingerprint and privacy risk. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Light or dark mode only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this See if you use light or dark mode Check H"],[525,"<Chips /> Can't see how loud your device volume is set No API tells a page how loud the device volume is. Volume is OS state. Proxies exist: autoplay policy shows a muted tab, audio output device changes fire devicechange, and a muted video can still play. A page cannot read a 0 to 100 level. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Own player only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this See if audio is blocked by autoplay rules Tell when your audio output device changes Play muted"],[354,"<Chips /> Can't see other apps' logs, only its own Blocked: apps can read only their own logs. System logs once leaked other apps' data. Android 4.1 limited apps to their own logs; iOS gives a normal app only its own process logs. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only its own logs\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The phone limits each app to its own logs See the app's own log messages Check its own process logs on iPhone (iOS 15 and later) See logs from other apps Use the old log permission as a norm"],[523,"<Chips /> Can't see passwords or addresses saved in your browser Pages cannot read passwords or addresses saved in the browser. Password managers fill on user action or after an autofill choice. Known weak spot: hidden-field autofill attacks and the :autofill CSS pseudo-class, which reveals that a field was autofilled (a boolean), but never the value before user action. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only what you choose to fill\" /> **What the user does:** You tap a field and choose a saved login or ad"],[378,"<Chips /> Can't see SIM identifiers like ICCID or IMSI as a normal app Blocked for normal apps on Android since 10 and never available on iOS. SIM IDs identify the subscriber. Only carrier-privileged apps can read them. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked for normal apps\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Only carrier approved apps get these Use them if it holds carrier privileges (Android) Get them in a carrier app with an Apple entitlement (iPhone) See them on Android 10 and later as a normal ap"],[344,"<Chips /> Can't see the Notes field on contacts on iPhone without Apple's special approval Apps cannot read free-text notes on contacts without an Apple entitlement. Notes often hold passwords, door codes and medical details. Apple walled them off after reports of misuse. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked on iPhone without approval\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It is blocked when the app asks for notes without the entitlement Open names, numbers and emails if you allowed contacts See notes "],[376,"<Chips /> Can't see the phone's IMEI, MEID or serial number as a normal app Blocked for normal apps: Android needs a privileged permission since 10; iOS has never exposed them. These IDs are permanent and unique, so platforms closed them to stop tracking. Only carrier, MDM or system apps can read them. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked for normal apps\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. These permanent IDs are blocked from normal apps Use other IDs instead, such as the vendor ID or advertising ID"],[450,"<Chips /> Can't see the search words you used, only the search engine's name Search engines no longer pass the search keywords to the site; only the engine name arrives. Since Oct 2011 Google withholds queries for signed in then all searches ('not provided'). Sites see the origin google.com only and learn keywords through Search Console (up to 1000 rows per query export) or on site search. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Just the engine name\" /> **What the user does:** You click a result on a search eng"],[227,"<Chips /> Can't see the Wi-Fi networks or passwords your phone has saved Blocked: apps cannot list the networks the phone has joined before. Past networks reveal places the person has been. Android 10 removed list access; iOS never exposed it. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked, own networks only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Apps may try during network setup, but it fails See only networks the app itself suggested See an empty list on Android 10 and newer See only networks the app set up it"],[520,"<Chips /> Can't see what other tabs or windows show A page cannot see what other tabs show, only whether its own tab is visible. Same-origin policy and site isolation block it. Proxies: Page Visibility and focus say if this tab is hidden, BroadcastChannel talks to same-origin tabs, and Window Management lists screens (prompt). Number of open tabs is unknown. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only its own tabs\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this Tell if its own tab is vis"],[96,"<Chips /> Can't see when you open an email in Apple Mail, and some link tags get stripped Apple Mail preloads email images through a proxy and Safari and Mail strip known tracking parameters from links. Open pixels look like opens even when no human saw the email, so email-to-app funnels are noisy. Link Tracking Protection (iOS 17) removes known click IDs from URLs in Mail, Messages and Safari private browsing. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocks some tracking, not all\" /> **What the user does:** Not"],[517,"<Chips /> Can't see which exact iPhone or iPad you have iOS Safari hides the exact device model. The UA says only iPhone or iPad with a frozen OS string. Proxies: screen size (390x844 etc.) and DPR narrow to a generation, and WebGL renderer says Apple GPU. Exact model such as iPhone 15 vs 16 is often ambiguous, about 2 to 3 bits. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A generation guess only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads See that it is an iPhone or iPad Guess a generation "],[507,"<Chips /> Can't see which other sites you have visited Sites cannot read which other sites you visited. :visited styling is limited since Chrome 51 and Firefox 4 (getComputedStyle lies, only color changes) so history sniffing no longer works. Proxies: cache timing, HSTS and partitioned storage are now keyed per site, and referrer shows only the prior page. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only the page before\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this See the one page that sen"],[506,"<Chips /> Can't see your bookmarks or reading list Sites cannot read your bookmarks or reading list. Only browser extensions with the bookmarks permission can read them. A web page has no API at all; even installing a PWA is detectable only through display-mode. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Nothing about bookmarks\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this Tell if the page runs as an installed app Show a prompt to add to your home screen Ask you to bookmark it, but only yo"],[508,"<Chips /> Can't see your calls, texts, or messages from a web page Web pages cannot read calls, texts or messages on the device. No browser API exposes call history or SMS inboxes. WebOTP reads a single matching code. Android native apps need READ\\_SMS and READ\\_CALL\\_LOG; Google Play restricts both heavily. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"One code at most\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this Take one matching one time code from a text on Android (WebOTP) Fill a code yo"],[519,"<Chips /> Can't see your computer login name or home folder Pages cannot see your computer login name. No web API exposes it. Proxies: file-picker display names after the user chooses a file, a downloaded file path in a native dialog, and rare leaks in error messages from extensions or file:// URLs. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Nothing unless you type it\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this See the name of a file you pick, if it includes your name Get your name only "],[511,"<Chips /> Can't see your CPU heat, fan speed, or power use No API reads CPU heat; only coarse pressure buckets exist. Compute Pressure API (Chrome 125, secure context) returns nominal, fair, serious or critical, throttled to at most 1 update per second and capped per origin. Exact degrees, fan speed and wattage are not exposed. Battery level is separate. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Four coarse levels only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this Check four coarse busy l"],[518,"<Chips /> Can't see your device's network hardware address (MAC) A page cannot read your device's network hardware address. MAC addresses never reach the web platform. Proxies are weak: WebRTC local candidates (mDNS now) and Bluetooth device IDs after a prompt are per-origin and randomized. iOS and Android also randomize Wi-Fi MACs per network. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"No hardware address\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this See random per site IDs from some feat"],[514,"<Chips /> Can't see your exact RAM size; only a rounded number capped at 8 GB Only a capped RAM figure exists; exact memory is hidden. deviceMemory returns 0.25 to 8 GB (capped, Chromium, secure context, power of two rounding), so a 64 GB workstation looks like 8. Firefox and Safari expose nothing. Memory pressure is not readable. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A rounded, capped number\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when the page loads in Chrome or Edge See memory as 0.25 up to 8 GB in"],[515,"<Chips /> Can't see your folders or where your files are stored Sites cannot list your folders or see where files live. The File System Access API (Chromium) returns handles after a picker; the real path is withheld (fakepath). OPFS is a private sandbox. Only the file name, size and last modified are readable. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only the file you pick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Choose file or Open file and pick one in the file picker Open the file you pick, once or for the visit Se"],[510,"<Chips /> Can't see your full contact list; only the contacts you pick Sites cannot read your address book; they only see contacts you pick. The Contact Picker (Android Chrome 80, Chrome iOS no) returns only the entries the person ticks, per call. No bulk read exists, unlike native apps that hold the contacts permission. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only contacts you tick\" /> **What the user does:** You tap Choose contacts and tick the people you want to share Show a picker in Chrome on Android Get t"],[509,"<Chips /> Can't see your mobile carrier, SIM, or cell tower from a web page Sites cannot read your mobile carrier, SIM or tower ID. Only the Network Information type (cellular) on Android Chromium and the server-side IP to ASN lookup reveal the carrier (about 100 major carriers). No MCC, MNC, SIM or tower API exists on the web. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"A guess from IP only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this Check if you are on cellular or Wi-Fi in Chrome on And"],[521,"<Chips /> Can't see your own phone number from a web page A page cannot read the phone number of your phone or SIM. No web API returns the device's own number. Proxies: WebOTP reads an SMS code (needs a prompt-free user flow but only the code, only formatted for the origin), Credential Management and Contact Picker give numbers the person chooses. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only what you type or pick\" /> **What the user does:** You type your number, pick one from a contact picker, or tap Autofill T"],[226,"<Chips /> Can't see your phone's real hardware address; apps get a fixed placeholder Blocked: apps see a fixed placeholder; Wi-Fi uses a randomized MAC per network. A MAC is a permanent hardware ID. Android returns 02:00:00:00:00:00 since 6.0 and randomizes per network since 10; iOS 14 added Private Wi-Fi Address. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked, a placeholder only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Apps may try when they set up networking, but it fails See a placeholder address (02:00:00:00:0"],[522,"<Chips /> Can't see your real IP address when your VPN works properly A page cannot learn your real IP if a VPN works properly. Servers see the VPN exit IP. Leaks come from WebRTC (local IPs now hidden by mDNS), DNS leaks outside the browser, IPv6 bypass and timezone or language mismatch. Browsers expose no 'real IP' API. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"VPN address only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It happens when your device connects to the site See the VPN exit IP address Spot leaks from IPv6,"],[512,"<Chips /> Can't see your serial number, IMEI, or advertising ID from a web page Sites cannot read your serial number, IMEI or advertising ID. No unprompted API exposes hardware identifiers. Managed ChromeOS and enterprise policies expose serial to allowlisted extensions only (chrome.enterprise.deviceAttributes). A regular site gets none; fingerprints are rebuilt from many signals instead. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"No device ID at all\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask fo"],[31,"<Chips /> Can't see your texts or call history unless it is your default texting or phone app Apps can read SMS or call logs only if they are the default handler or qualify for a narrow exception. Since 9 March 2019 Google limits these permissions to default SMS, Phone or Assistant apps and a few declared exceptions. It stops mass harvesting for contact lists and ads. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked, unless default app\" /> **What the user does:** You set the app as your default SMS or Phone app,"],[817,"<Chips /> Can't see your whole contact list, call history or text messages A site cannot read your whole contact list, call history or SMS. The only contact route is the picker, one chosen entry at a time. No web API reads call logs, SMS, voicemail or calendar events. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked in every browser\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. No browser lets a site do this, even if you tap Allow Ask you to pick contacts one at a time, on browsers that offer a contact picker Receive onl"],[526,"<Chips /> Can't see your Wi-Fi name, router ID, or signal bars Sites cannot read your Wi-Fi network name, router ID or signal bars. Wi-Fi identity is a location tracker (BSSID databases map to street addresses). Browsers expose none of it. Proxies: Network Information type on Android, server-side IP geolocation, and the Geolocation API which uses Wi-Fi servers behind its prompt. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Connection type only\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Pages have no way to ask for this Se"],[443,"<Chips /> Can't send noisy ad totals from Chrome anymore, since it was removed in August 2026 Noisy aggregate reports from Protected Audience and Shared Storage. Removed in Chrome 152 (Aug 2026). Worklet code could contribute values to histograms that a server summed with differential privacy noise. Retired Oct 2025, deprecated Chrome 144, removed in Chrome 152 (released Aug 25 2026). The Aggregation Service is scheduled for phaseout. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Removed\" /> **What the user does:** N"],[731,"<Chips /> Can't send or see your text messages, calendar or call history from a website A site cannot send or read SMS, read the calendar, or see call history. sms: and tel: links only open the user's own app. Calendars arrive as .ics downloads or OAuth sign in. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Only opens your own apps\" /> **What the user does:** You tap an sms: link, tel: link, or a calendar file or sign in button Open your own messaging or phone app with a number or message filled in Offer an .ics cale"],[829,"<Chips /> Can't stop sites from using device traits to spot you in Chrome or Edge Chrome and Edge do not randomize or block fingerprinting APIs by default. Chrome only reduced passive signals (User-Agent Reduction, Client Hints, partitioned storage). Privacy Budget never shipped, so canvas, WebGL, audio and fonts stay fully readable. Chrome has the largest fingerprinting surface. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Little default protection\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It is how Chrome and Edge are "],[454,"<Chips /> Can't store cross site data for ads in Chrome anymore, since it was retired in 2025 Cross-site storage whose values could only be read in a locked worker. Retired Oct 2025. Let scripts write cross-site values (for example a frequency count) and read them only inside a worklet that output through limited channels (selectURL, Private Aggregation). Disabled starting build 153.0.8001.0; code removal follows. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Retired\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It used to ha"],[56,"<Chips /> Can't tell ad tools your interest topics on Android, since Google retired it Android would have told ad SDKs a few interest topics from the person's recent app use. Coarse interest labels (about 470 topics) for ad targeting without an ID. Retired in Oct 2025 after low adoption. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Retired\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. It was a background feature, retired in October 2025 Have told ad tools a few topics from about 470 labels (Android 13 and later) Have worked "],[832,"<Chips /> Can't tell Tor Browser users apart, since everyone looks the same Tor Browser makes all users look the same: fixed fonts, letterboxed window, canvas prompt. Standardizes fonts, screen size in 200x100 steps, UTC timezone and en-US locale. Canvas readback needs a prompt. Goal is one crowd, not noise, which yields about zero bits when users keep defaults. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Same look for all Tor users\" /> **What the user does:** You open Tor Browser. It works by default Show every vi"],[22,"<Chips /> Can't track children or share their data with ad companies in kids apps Child directed apps get stricter rules: no ads tracking, no personal data to third parties, and certified SDKs. Apple's Kids category bans behavioral ads and limits third-party analytics. Play Families requires certified ad SDKs, no AAID use, and a safe design. Both align with COPPA and similar laws. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Rules, not data\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The rules apply when a child uses a kid"],[828,"<Chips /> Can't track you across sites by device traits when Brave adds random noise Brave adds small per-site random noise to canvas, WebGL, audio and other readouts. Farbling changes the last bits of fingerprint outputs with a seed per site per session, so tracking sites cannot link a person across sites while pages still work. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Protection that Brave applies for you\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Brave does it for you by default Add small random changes to canvas, "],[361,"<Chips /> Can't turn on powerful settings for apps installed outside stores until you allow it Android blocks some powerful toggles for apps installed outside stores. Accessibility, notification listener and similar options are greyed out for sideloaded apps until the person opens App Info and allows restricted settings. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocked until you unlock it\" /> **What the user does:** You open App info, tap the menu and choose Allow restricted settings Install outside a store on A"],[29,"<Chips /> Can't use fingerprint prone phone details on iPhone without an approved reason Apple lists APIs that can be used to fingerprint a device and requires the app to state an approved reason. Five categories: file timestamp, system boot time, disk space, active keyboards, and UserDefaults. An unapproved reason is a rejection, so fingerprinting uses are fenced in. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Rules, not data\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Apple checks the reasons when the developer submits "],[831,"<Chips /> Can't use many device traits in Safari to tell you apart Safari limits what known fingerprinting scripts can read and perturbs canvas and audio readback. Safari 17 enabled it in Private Browsing. Safari 26 (iOS 26, macOS 26) extends it to all browsing by default, restricting known fingerprinting scripts from reading screen size, core count, voices, Web Audio and 2D canvas. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Built in protection\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. Safari does it by default, in all"],[367,"<Chips /> Can't use many risky features when Lockdown Mode or Advanced Protection is on An extreme protection mode that turns off risky features for high risk people. Cuts attack surface at the cost of features. Apps may see features missing. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Blocks features for you\" /> **What the user does:** You switch on Lockdown Mode (iPhone) or Advanced Protection (Android) in Settings Keep working with fewer features Notice that some features are missing Use just in time code and mo"],[72,"<Chips /> Can't use the camera or mic without a dot or chip showing A dot or chip that shows the camera or mic is active. Warns the user in real time. Android 12 shows a green chip; iOS 14 shows green and orange dots. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Shown while in use\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The dot or chip shows when an app uses the camera or microphone Show a green chip for the camera on Android 12 and later; tap it for the app name Show a green dot for the camera and orange dot for the m"],[74,"<Chips /> Can't use the camera or mic without a green or orange dot showing A green or orange dot shows when an app is using the camera or microphone. System drawn, so apps cannot hide it. Android also adds hardware toggles that cut access. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Shown while in use\" /> **What the user does:** Nothing. The dot shows by itself when the camera or mic is in use Show a green dot for the camera and an orange dot for the mic Show the app name in Control Center (iPhone) Show a chip wit"],[589,"<Chips /> Can't use the same embedded storage to recognize you across different sites Browsers key embedded storage by the top site so a tracker sees a separate store on each site. Cuts the main cross-site tracking path: a widget on site A and site B no longer shares one cookie or localStorage. Embeds can still track within one site, and workarounds shift to link decoration, redirects and server side IDs. <Reach level=\"1\" label=\"Separate storage