pabilities/\">Every capability with limits, platform tables, history and sources.</Card> <Card title=\"Use the data\" href=\"/docs/getting-started/\">Download the JSON bundles and use them in your own project.</Card> <Card title=\"Curious people\">Find out what an app can actually see before you tap Allow.</Card> <Card title=\"Makers and designers\">Check what you can ask for, what users will see, and which platforms support it.</Card> <Card title=\"Privacy and security teams\">Compare sensitivity, reach and known abuse across Android, iPhone and the web.</Card> <Card title=\"Researchers and tool builders\">Pull clean, validated JSON and build on it. The data is CC BY 4.0.</Card> Start with the one line summary and the reach meter. The meter runs from 1 (one thing, one moment) to 4 (broad, anytime). Then read what it can and cannot do, and open the platform table for the exact permission names, prompts and first versions. New here? Read Getting started to grab the data. Want the vocabulary? Read the data model. Curious how files are laid out? Read how the data is stored. Want to fix or add something? Read contributing."],[894,"Platform.Ontology is open source. There are two licenses, one for the code and one for the data. | Part | License | What it covers                                                     |\n| ---- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Code | MIT     | Build scripts, the simulator, the wiki and this documentation site | | Part | License   | What it covers                                                     |\n| ---- | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Data | CC BY 4.0 | Everything under `data/`, `schema/` and the published JSON bundles | You may copy, share and build on the data, including for commercial work, as long as you give credit. A good credit line looks like this: > Data from Platform.Ontology (https://platformontology.lol), licensed CC BY 4.0. 